GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:5894:   "note": "*** COMMITTED 2026-09-12 as 4dfc716 (local only).  gate 14 PASS 910 checks at the 43 pin (regress/artifacts/armbdt/killtable_20260911h_floor/gate_arm_43rows.log); all four trees cleared both parts after cpu_arm_instr.c was propagated (three trees held an older comment).  Pass-2 receipt in regress/pass2_ledger.tsv.  State stays `held`: the closure records are owed. ***\n\n*** GATE PIN RAISED 39 -> 43 (2026-09-12).  THE GATE MUST BE RE-RUN; ITS LAST GREEN WAS AT 39. ***\n\nTable 7 added four rows to close the floor -- the three store-side modes that had NO ROW AT ALL\n(`ARM_STM_IB_R7`, `ARM_STM_DA_R7`, `ARM_STM_S_R7`) and the tenth cell (`ARM_STM_PC_DB_R7`) -- so\nthe probe is 43 rows and the literal moved by hand with its reason.  **A pin that follows the probe\ncannot tell a probe that GAINED rows from one that LOST them**, which is why it is a literal.\n\nOWED FOR `#524`, IN ORDER:\n  1. re-run `gate_arm.sh` at the 43 pin;\n  2. re-add the `arm_bdt_probe.py` MANIFEST entry to `check_probe_wiring.py` **in the same commit\n     as the probe** -- the exact line is preserved in a comment there.  The entry without the file\n     is a GHOST; the file without the entry is UNCLASSIFIED.  Both are red, in opposite\n     directions, and I hit each in turn today;\n  3. restore the `#524` CHANGELOG block (`python _scratchpad/land_shared.py round524`) and record\n     table 7 in it -- the block currently describes the closure at 39 rows;\n  4. commit, AFTER `#525`.\n\n---\n\n*** TABLE 7 CLOSES THE CHAIN: ALL TEN FLOOR CELLS KILL, AND THE ONLY ESCAPE IS ONE THE CONTRACT\nNAMED IN ADVANCE.  2026-09-12 02:32. ***  Cited: regress/artifacts/armbdt/killtable_20260911h_floor/SCORES.txt\n\n    BASE            43/43  CONTROL=OK  FAILING none\n    -- regression, the six from table g: all still die --\n    M_POPP_NONE     41/43  IB_P2, DB_P2         M_POPP_WEAK  41/43  IB_P2, DB_P2\n    M_NPMASK        41/43  IB_P2, DB_P2         M_SBIT_NONE  (dies)\n    M_POPW_WEAK     40/43  DB_P2, S_P2, MULTI_P2_R1          M_POPU_WEAK  (dies)\n    -- the six NEW floor mutants: ALL DIE --\n    M_PUSHS_NONE    42/43  ARM_STM_S_R7          <- E1, the MEASURED L2 breach\n    M_POPP_MASK2    41/43  IB_P2, DB_P2          <- the ~2 gap, live until residue 7\n    M_PUSH_IB_WEAK  42/43  ARM_STM_IB_R7         <- a mode with NO ROW AT ALL\n    M_PUSH_DA_WEAK  42/43  ARM_STM_DA_R7         <- likewise\n    M_PUSHPC_NONE   42/43  ARM_STM_PC_DB_R7      <- the tenth cell\n    M_PUSHP_OVER7   41/43  ARM_STM_IB_R7, ARM_STM_PC_DB_R7   <- E4\n    -- disclosed under clause (e), EXPECTED to escape --\n    D_PUW_WEAK      43/43  FAILING none          <- ESCAPED, exactly as disclosed\n    D_POPSW_WEAK    42/43  ARM_LDM_S_P2          <- DIED, better than disclosed\n    RESTORE         cmp-vs-SEC CLEAN, binary FRESH, SENTINEL CLEARED (both parts)\n\n**`D_POPSW_WEAK` dying is the contract being CONSERVATIVE rather than wrong**: upgrading `S_P2` to\n`LDMIA r0!,{r1}^` at residue 7 closed the (s&&w) joint for free, which the PRETABLE predicted.\n\n*** WHAT MAKES THIS TABLE DIFFERENT FROM THE SIX BEFORE IT IS WHERE THE ROWS CAME FROM. ***  It is\nthe first whose row set is enumerated from the HELPERS' OWN INPUTS rather than from the mutant\nlist -- which is what the class-closure rule always said, and what pass C invoked but did not do.\nEvery earlier repair rowed at the residue of the mutant it had just found, so each pass\nnecessarily discovered the next strength.  **Residue 7 (mod 8) discriminates `~1`, `~2`, `~3` AND\n`~7` from ONE access**, so no future strength needs a new residue.\n\nThe `pc` row also settled a question its designing seat left open: it could not establish\nunconditional cold-path reachability from source (it depends on `host_store[0x21] == NULL` and on\nthe decoder not substituting an optimised handler).  **`ARM_STM_PC_DB_R7` is GREEN on BASE, so the\ncold path was taken** -- the measurement answered what the reading could not.\n\n---\n\n*** THE FLOOR IS TEN CELLS, DERIVED FROM THE HELPERS' INPUTS -- NOT FROM THE MUTANT LIST. ***\nAdjudicated 2026-09-11; full text `regress/artifacts/armbdt/passD/fable_floor_20260911.md`.\n\n    FLOOR = {cold first access} x {pop, push} x {IA, IB, DA, DB}\n          + {push} x {s = 1}\n          + {push} x {pc in list}          = TEN CELLS, SEVEN OPEN\n    one cold DATA row per cell, access address = 7 (mod 8)\n\n**RESIDUE 7 MOD 8 IS THE WHOLE GEOMETRIC FIX**: it discriminates `~1`, `~2`, `~3` and `~7` at once,\nso no future strength needs a new residue.  Every previous repair placed its rows at the FOUND\nMUTANT'S residue, which is why each pass found the next one.\n\nOPEN: pop IB / DA / DB for the `~2` strength (all three closure rows sit at `0x20006`, bit 0 CLEAR);\npush IB and push DA (NO ROWS AT ALL -- STM is IA, Thumb PUSH is DB); push s=1 (**E1, MEASURED,\nL2**); push pc-in-list (which also closes E4 for free).\n\n---\n\n*** PASS D DID NOT CLOSE THE CHAIN EITHER. FIVE MORE ESCAPES, MEASURED AT 39/39. THIS IS THE\nFOURTH CONSECUTIVE PASS TO FIND A BREACH, AND THE COUNT IS RISING: 1, 2, 5, 5. ***\n\n    E1 M_PUSHS_NONE   s_bit ? addr : (addr & ~3)               PUSH only   39/39\n    E2 M_POPSW_WEAK   addr & ((s_bit && w_bit) ? ~1 : ~3)      POP         39/39\n    E3 M_POPS_OVER7   addr & (s_bit ? ~7 : ~3)                 POP         39/39\n    E4 M_PUSHP_OVER7  addr & (p_bit ? ~7 : ~3)                 PUSH only   39/39\n    E5 M_PUW_WEAK     addr & ((p&&u&&w) ? ~1 : ~3)             BOTH        39/39\n\n**THE SHAPE IS THE SAME EVERY TIME AND IT IS MY CLOSURE'S OWN SHAPE: I CLOSED THE LOAD SIDE, AT ONE\nRESIDUE, AT ONE STRENGTH.**  E1 and E4 are the PUSH-side halves; E3 is the same axis at a\nBIT2-SET residue; E2 and E5 are joints.  Pass C closed `{site}` for the p axis and left `s`; pass D\nfinds `s` at the other site, and `p` at the other strength.\n\nGOVERNING FACT, CHECKED NOT ASSUMED: **all four bits are used in CONDITIONS by BOTH bodies** --\n`arm_pop` has `if (s_bit)`, `if (p_bit)`, `if (!p_bit)`, `u_bit? :` and `w_bit`; `arm_push`\nlikewise.  So they are BODY-TESTED PARTITIONS and the flagship's `xscaleinten` ruling today (a\nparameter that touches nothing the body reads is clause (e)) does NOT rescue them.\n\n**PROPOSED SPLIT, PUT TO THE ADJUDICATOR RATHER THAN DECIDED HERE:** single-axis cells (E1, E3, E4)\nare FLOOR, L2, close in-round; multi-axis JOINTS (E2, E5) are clause-(e) DEBT on the pass-11\nprecedent.  If that holds, the remaining floor is `{p,u,s,w} x {pop,push} x {none, ~1, ~7}` minus\nwhat is rowed -- finite.  **The second question asked of the adjudicator is whether this chain is\nCONVERGING AT ALL, or has become the `#392` shape wearing the class-closure rule's clothes.**\n\n*** ONE OF MY SEVEN CLOSURE ROWS IS VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION, and the reason is sharper than the\n'kills nothing' I disclosed: `ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_R2` CANNOT fail under a slow-path mutant, because\nTHE FIRST COLD ACCESS WARMS THE PAGE and the second register's access takes the FAST PATH. ***\nMeasured by kimi; glm independently called the table *\"internally impossible as stated\"* from the\nsame evidence without knowing why.  **A reading seat and a measuring seat converging on one defect\nfrom opposite ends is the panel working as designed.**\n\n---\n\n*** GATE GREEN AT THE NEW 39-ROW PIN, 2026-09-11 19:4x. ***  `/tmp/gate_arm_524g.log` (100,681 b),\nrun UNPIPED with no timeout wrapper (a pipe makes the exit status the filter's; a timeout kills the\ngate while reporting a clean completion -- both are documented traps here).\n\n    GATE_EXIT=0\n    ok   bdt rows run       39      <- the class-closure pin, moved 32 -> 39 by hand\n    ok   bdt rows correct   39\n    ^FAIL rows: 0     SKIP occurrences: 0     <- no silent coverage loss\n    gate 14: ARM/Thumb flags (#311/#312/#319-#322/#328/#329): PASS (910 checks)\n\nSTILL OWED BEFORE COMMIT: **PASS D on the closure diff.**  Passes A, B and C each found a breach\n(one, then two, then five), so the class closure owes its own pass -- and the chain closes only on\nthe first VALID pass finding ZERO breaches **with its denominator**.  A bare zero does not close\nit.\n\n---\n\n*** THE CLASS CLOSURE IS MEASURED AND ALL FIVE PASS-C ESCAPES ARE DEAD. 2026-09-11 23:37. ***\nCited: regress/artifacts/armbdt/killtable_20260911g_closure/SCORES.txt  (frozen 39-row probe, one instrument for every leg)\n\n    LEG            was      now      killed by\n    BASE                    39/39    -- CONTROL=OK, FAILING none\n    M_POPP_NONE    32/32    37/39    ARM_LDM_IB_P2, ARM_LDM_DB_P2\n    M_POPP_WEAK    32/32    37/39    ARM_LDM_IB_P2, ARM_LDM_DB_P2\n    M_POPW_WEAK    32/32    37/39    ARM_LDM_DB_P2, ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_R1\n    M_NPMASK       32/32    37/39    ARM_LDM_IB_P2, ARM_LDM_DB_P2\n    M_SBIT_NONE    32/32    38/39    ARM_LDM_S_P2\n    RESTORE                          cmp-vs-SEC=CLEAN, binary=FRESH, SENTINEL CLEARED\n\n**Every mutant that was a TOTAL ESCAPE now reds with NAMED rows, and BASE is clean at 39/39.**\nSeven rows closed the load side of the p/u/s/w x site product: `ARM_LDM_IB_P2` (p=1,u=1),\n`ARM_LDM_DA_P2` (u=0), `ARM_LDM_DB_P2` (p=1,u=0), `ARM_LDM_S_P2` (s=1), and the three-assertion\n`ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_*` leg.  **The multi leg is the bargain: ONE session, THREE assertions, closing\nw_bit x residue-2 AND register-list-length together.**  It had to sit at residue 2 -- at the\nexisting `WB_UNAL2` base (0x20001/0x20005) the low bits are `01`, where `~1` and `~3` AGREE, so\nthat row is structurally blind to a w_bit-conditioned weakening.\n\n*** THREE OF THE SEVEN NEW ROWS KILL NOTHING IN THIS TABLE, AND THAT IS RECORDED RATHER THAN\nGLOSSED: `ARM_LDM_DA_P2`, `ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_R2`, `ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_WB`. ***  Their channels are\nreal but no BUILT mutant varies them -- `DA_P2` exists for the `u_bit=0` cell, which the pass-C\nseat ENUMERATED and explicitly did NOT build (*\"derived (same legs)\"*).  A condemning prediction\ngets built, so `M_POPU_WEAK` -- `addr & (u_bit ? 0xfffffffc : 0xfffffffe)` -- WAS BUILT AND\nMEASURED: **37/39, FAILING ARM_LDM_DA_P2 and ARM_LDM_DB_P2.**  `DA_P2` is therefore\nDISCHARGED BY A MEASURED KILL and the u_bit cell is closed rather than derived.\n*Six mutants built, six dead.*  The other two are secondary assertions on a leg whose primary assertion\nalready kills; they are disclosed under clause (e), not claimed as coverage.\n\nTHE REPAIR-CHAIN STATUS: passes A, B and C each found a breach (M_MASK1; M_MASK7 + M_PBITCOND;\nthen five at once).  **This diff is the CLASS CLOSURE the rule demands after two consecutive\npasses of one class -- the full enumeration in ONE diff rather than another point row -- and it\nowes its own pass.  A zero does not close the chain unless it carries its denominator; the\ndenominator is the table above plus the enumeration the pass-C seat swept (mask value, placement,\nsite, predicate x 2 sites x 4 bits x 2 strengths = 16 cells, operand, joint (p,u), reg-list\nlength, byte order, null medium).**\n\n---\n\n*** PASS C DID NOT CLOSE THE CHAIN. 2026-09-11 17:52. FOUR SEATS OF SIX FOUND A CLAUSE-(d)\nESCAPE; TWO DID NOT.  `#524` DOES NOT COMMIT. ***\n\n**`M_POP_PBITCOND` -- found INDEPENDENTLY by codex and grok**, the read-side counterpart of the\nmeasured `M_PBITCOND`:\n    /*  at the arm_pop() read call, fix present at BOTH sites, then:  */\n    addr & (p_bit ? 0xfffffffe : 0xfffffffc)\n\n**Predicted 32/32, a TOTAL SWEEP**, because every tested load carries p_bit=0 -- established by\nDECODING the probe's literal instruction words (`0xe8900002`, `0xe8b00002`, `0xe8b00006`, all IA;\nThumb `0xbc02`), not by assertion.\n\n*** THIS IS THE `#499` SHAPE FOR THE THIRD TIME IN ONE DAY, AND THE FLAGSHIP ADJUDICATION PREDICTED\nIT HOURS EARLIER: *\"Each pass today found a new cell; extrapolate.\"* ***  Pass A found `M_MASK1`;\npass B found `M_MASK7`/`M_PBITCOND`; pass C finds the SYMMETRIC CELL OF PASS B'S OWN REPAIR -- the\nclass closure repaired the store-side cell `M_PBITCOND` used and left the load-side open.\n\n**grok named the enumeration that was missing, in one sentence:** *\"Mask-width (~1 / ~7 / ~15) is\nCLOSED; the predicate x site product is NOT.\"*  So the owed closure is\n**{unconditional, p_bit-conditioned} x {arm_pop, arm_push}** in ONE diff, not another single cell --\nthe class-closure rule, which pass B already invoked and applied to only one axis.\n\n**AN EQUIVALENT-MUTANT TRAP INSIDE THE MUTANT, from grok:** the form must be truthy `p_bit ?`, NOT\n`p_bit == 1`.  `bdt_load` passes `iw & 0x01000000`, so `== 1` is DEAD on every ARM encoding and\nwould measure EQUIVALENT for the wrong reason.\n\n*** IT IS A PREDICTION AND IS RECORDED AS OWED, NOT AS A SCORE. ***  Both seats said so plainly --\ngrok: *\"I could not exec a scratch rebuild (WSL was blocked in this seat)... treat the 32/32 as a\npredicted sweep that still owes a clone run.\"*  Per the flagship ruling on predictions: **a\nCONDEMNING prediction gets BUILT FIRST** (a wrong condemnation costs one build; a wrong excuse ships\na hole).  Build `M_POP_PBITCOND`, score it, and if it sweeps that is **L2 -- repair in-round**.\n\nOpus 5 was fired on the same brief and is still running; agy deliberately not fired.\n\n---\n\n*** 2026-09-11 INVESTIGATION -- **CONFIRMED AT HEAD, AND THE SPLICE IS TRACED TO THE memcpy, NOT ASSERTED.  The row is right in every particular and the artifact genuinely does not exist.** ***\n\n**THE MECHANISM, read at HEAD (`cpu_arm_instr.c`, unmodified in `git status`):**\n  * `arm_pop()` `:1602-1764` and `arm_push()` `:1770-1859` each hold one running `addr = *np`, the RAW base-register value, and branch on host page residency per register.\n  * **FAST path** (page resident): `p32[(addr & 0xfff) >> 2]` -- the `>>2` truncates the low two bits, so it is **architecturally correct** for the implemented A=0/U=0 state (DDI0100I LDM(1) p.A4-37 / STM(1) p.A4-190: *ignore the least significant two bits of the address*).\n  * **SLOW path** (cold): `cpu->memory_rw(..., addr, data, 4, ...)` with the **RAW, UNMASKED addr** (`:1653-1654`, `:1842-1843`).  **Traced into `memory_rw.c:728-782`: `offset = paddr & offset_mask` is PAGE-granularity, NOT word-rounded, and the transfer is a raw `memcpy` at `:770-772`/`:780-782`.  So an unaligned addr genuinely SPLICES BYTES ACROSS AN ARCHITECTURAL WORD BOUNDARY** -- confirmed at the lowest level rather than inferred.\n\n> **The same instruction, the same unaligned base, produces two different byte patterns purely according to HOST PAGE-CACHE WARMTH -- a condition invisible to the guest architecture.**\n\n*** AND THE WRITEBACK IS CORRECT AS IT STANDS, WHICH IS WHY THE FIX SHAPE MATTERS: *** `if (w_bit) *np = addr;` uses the unmasked running addr, and **Mode 4 writeback DERIVES from unmasked Rn**.  **So the repair must mask ONLY at the two `memory_rw` call sites and NEVER at entry** -- masking at entry corrupts writeback.\n\n**THE IN-TREE PRECEDENT FOR EXACTLY THAT SHAPE ALREADY EXISTS:** `src/cpus/generate_arm_multi.c:117` emits `addr &= 0xffc;` -- the code-generated fast dispatch already masks the ACCESS while leaving writeback unmasked.  *A transferred warrant, verified at the cited line.*\n\n**FOUR CALLERS, all confirmed:** `X(bdt_load)` `:1889`, `X(bdt_store)` `:1921`, and Thumb PUSH/POP at `cpu_arm.c:1670`/`:1677`.  **Thumb inherits the defect and has ZERO probe coverage of any kind.**\n\n=== NO ARTIFACT EXISTS.  SEARCHED BY THE DEFECT'S VOCABULARY, NEVER BY GUESSING A FILENAME ===\n*(the brief warned against filename invention because I did it twice today; the search was done by function names and mnemonics instead)*\n  * filename patterns `*armbdt*`, `*bdt*`, `*block*transfer*`, `*blockdata*`: **0 hits**\n  * **127** artifact directories: none for this item\n  * content-grep of all **93** `regress/*.py` for LDM/STM mnemonics: **2 files**, both testing something else\n  * `bdt_load|bdt_store|arm_pop|arm_push`: **1 hit**, a comment\n  * `CHANGELOG.md`: **0** occurrences -- **no round has ever shipped against it**\n\n**GATE 14's 358 IS CORRECT AND CURRENT**, confirmed two independent ways -- `gate_arm.sh:561-563`'s comment AND commit `6014b7e`'s message recording the gate's own run output.  *(The carrier records this figure was stale once at 261, so both were treated as claims.)*  **And it does not cover this defect under any name:**\n  * `arm_writeback_probe.py` scopes itself explicitly to *the single-register path* -- a different generator entirely;\n  * `arm_endian_probe.py` DOES reach `arm_pop`/`arm_push`, but only on byte order and **with a word-aligned base (`0x10000`), where masking is a no-op** -- so it cannot discriminate this defect whichever path a row takes;\n  * measured corroboration: three binaries (HEAD / mask-at-access / mask-at-entry) all scored **22/22 identical** on `arm_writeback_probe.py`, **including the entry-mask mutant that corrupts writeback**;\n  * and the regress adjudication of `6014b7e` states it plainly -- *gate_arm passed 16/16 WITH armbdt PRESENT.*\n\n*** THE MANDATORY ROW: a WRITEBACK row threading an unaligned base through a W=1 transfer and asserting the base register equals the UNMASKED arithmetic result. ***  It is the row `arm_writeback_probe.py` is structurally blind to, and **the only one that discriminates the correct fix from the plausible-but-wrong mask-at-entry mutant.**\n\n**CITATION DRIFT, noted rather than silently fixed:** the 2026-08-19 entries cite `:1563`/`:1752`; HEAD has the same code at `:1653`/`:1842` -- about 90 lines, from unrelated intervening edits.  The mechanism is unchanged; only the numbers moved.\n\n**STILL LIVE AT HEAD**, checked from source rather than from the row: `git log -10` on that file shows the recent touches are all unrelated to the `memory_rw` calls.\n\n*Investigated by a SONNET subagent while both Anthropic seats were walled -- see [[agentmodelwall]].  The work is of the right standard for a READING task, which is the half of that row's claim this supports; it says nothing about measuring equivalence.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 WITNESS + KILL TABLE (agy 3.8, measuring seat, independent transport).  **THE WITNESS EXISTS.  RUNG 3, UNMODIFIED testarm, 17/25 PRE-FIX AND 25/25 POST-FIX -- after three weeks in which this row's own record said the artifact genuinely did not exist.** ***\n\n**THE DEFECT IS UNCHANGED AND IS NOW MEASURED RATHER THAN DERIVED.**  The fast path (`p32[(addr & 0xfff) >> 2]`) truncates the low two bits and is architecturally CORRECT per DDI 0100I LDM(1) p.A4-37 / STM(1) p.A4-190; the slow path passes the **RAW, UNMASKED `addr`** to `memory_rw`, whose `offset = paddr & offset_mask` is PAGE-granularity and whose transfer is a raw `memcpy`.  **So the identical instruction at the identical unaligned base yields DIFFERENT BYTES according to host page-cache warmth -- a condition invisible to the guest architecture.**\n\n=== THE WITNESS, AND IT CLEARS EVERY LADDER CLAUSE ===\n**Rung 3, cold interactive debugger, on the UNMODIFIED in-tree `testarm`** (`gxemul -V -A -T -E testarm -M 64`) driven through a pty.  **No `device_add`, no custom machine -- which is the laundering clause, and it is met by construction rather than by assertion.**\n\n*** THE PAGE-CACHE CONDITIONING IS THE PART THAT MAKES IT A WITNESS AT ALL, and it turns on a fact this project got BACKWARDS once and had two shipped rounds leaning on the wrong version: *** `put b` writes byte-wise with `CACHE_NONE | NO_EXCEPTIONS`, populating physical RAM **without** updating the translation tables, so `host_load`/`host_store` stay NULL -- the COLD seed; `put w` writes words with `CACHE_DATA` and **WARMS** the host page -- the WARM seed.  *The carrier records `put w` WARMS and `put b` does not; the witness uses exactly that asymmetry as its instrument.*\n\n**AND IT VERIFIES THE PATH RATHER THAN ASSUMING IT:** before every transfer the probe runs `tlbdump 0,r` to confirm the target is genuinely ABSENT from the host cache on a cold run and RESIDENT on a warm one.  **That is the row establishing which path each run took -- without it the core comparison proves nothing**, which is the one thing the brief insisted on.\n\n=== SCORES, and the artifact for every one of them is named ===\n*(`regress/artifacts/armbdt/killtable_20260911/`, `KILLTABLE_DONE` sentinel present, each score read from its own `probe_<name>.raw`)*\n\n| leg | score | reading |\n|---|---|---|\n| `BASE` (fix applied) | **25/25** | clean |\n| `HEAD` (committed, pre-fix) | **17/25** | all four cold-data rows AND all four divergence checks red -- *test-first satisfied: the symptom is shown on the COMMITTED build and goes green post-fix with the same script* |\n| `M_ENTRY` (mask at entry) | **20/25** | *** KILLED, AND KILLED BY THE WRITEBACK ROWS SPECIFICALLY *** |\n| `M_POP_ONLY` (one site masked) | 21/25 | killed |\n| `M_PUSH_ONLY` (other site masked) | 21/25 | killed |\n| `M_NULL_MEDIUM` (identity) | **6/25** | 19 rows red -- the rows genuinely observe block transfer, discharging clause (c) |\n| `RESTORED` | **17/25** | *** IDENTICAL TO `HEAD`, which is the clean-restore proof: the clone came back to the committed baseline exactly. *** |\n\n*** `M_ENTRY` IS THE VINDICATION OF THE MANDATORY ROW. ***  The brief said the writeback row *'is the only one that discriminates the correct fix from the plausible-but-wrong mask-at-entry mutant'*, and predicted `arm_writeback_probe.py` is structurally blind to it -- which had already been measured: three binaries (HEAD / mask-at-access / mask-at-entry) all scored **22/22 IDENTICAL** on that probe, **including the entry-mask mutant that corrupts writeback.**  The new instrument kills it 20/25.  *A row specified from the defect's own control flow, before any table ran, and it is the row that carries the whole discrimination.*\n\n**ORDER PROVEN BY MTIME, not asserted:** `PRETABLE.out` written **06:46:22**, first table run `probe_BASE.raw` **06:50:06** -- 3m44s earlier, so the pre-table block cannot have been fitted to the answers.\n\n=== THE FIX SHAPE IS CONFIRMED, AND THE TRAP WITH IT ===\n**Mask ONLY at the two `memory_rw` call sites** -- `addr & ~3` at `cpu_arm_instr.c:1653` and `:1842`.  **NEVER at entry:** `if (w_bit) *np = addr;` (`:1715`, `:1858`) uses the unmasked running `addr`, and Mode 4 writeback derives from unmasked `Rn`, so an entry mask corrupts writeback -- which is precisely what `M_ENTRY` measures.  In-tree precedent verified at the cited line: `generate_arm_multi.c:117` emits `addr &= 0xffc;`, masking the ACCESS while leaving writeback alone.\n\n*** HOUSE RULES OBSERVED, VERIFIED BY ME RATHER THAN TAKEN FROM THE REPORT: `git status` shows `src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c` UNMODIFIED in GXEMUL-SEC. ***  The seat worked in `/tmp/gxarmbdt_clone` and wrote only into `regress/artifacts/armbdt/`, which is the measuring-seat rule met exactly.  The live `/tmp/gxop519p2` tail was not touched.\n\n**STATUS: the fix is SPECIFIED AND MEASURED BUT NOT APPLIED TO SEC.**  What remains is the edit itself, propagation into the build trees, a pass-2 panel on the real diff, and the gates -- none of which could be done in this window, because the `#519` regression tail holds the machine and this project's gates carry load-sensitive wall-clock oracles.  *Deliberately left rather than rushed: a measurement taken against a loaded host is the false-FAIL this project has already paid for once.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 IMPLEMENTED.  **FIX APPLIED TO SEC AS `#524`, MASKING THE ACCESS AND NOT THE RUNNING addr** ***\n\n**Round number `#524`, chosen after checking: `#523` is STAKED by `regress/artifacts/wdcerrfamily/codex_design523_20260911.txt` even though it appears in no CHANGELOG entry, source file or commit.**  *An artifact filename is a claim on a number, and taking a staked one would have collided with a round already in design.*\n\n**THE EDIT, both sites, `uint32_t addr` masked with a hex literal to match the file's own idiom (`addr & 0xfff`, `addr &= 0xffc`):**\n    cpu_arm_instr.c  arm_pop()  : cpu->memory_rw(..., addr & 0xfffffffc, data, ..., MEM_READ, ...)\n    cpu_arm_instr.c  arm_push() : cpu->memory_rw(..., addr & 0xfffffffc, data, ..., MEM_WRITE, ...)\n\n**THE TWO WRITEBACK SITES WERE VERIFIED UNTOUCHED AFTER THE EDIT** -- `if (w_bit) *np = addr;` still reads the bare unmasked running value at both.  *That is the whole discrimination: an entry-mask scores 20/25 on this round's detector and **22/22 -- identical to a correct build -- on the pre-existing `arm_writeback_probe.py`.*  The comment records that measurement at the site, so a future edit tempted to hoist the mask meets the evidence rather than the rule.\n\n*Other `memory_rw(..., addr, ...)` calls in this file (the single-register load/store paths and the instruction fetch) were left alone -- checked by grep after the edit: 7 remain bare and none is on the block-data-transfer path.*\n\n**PROPAGATION, and it is deliberately PARTIAL:**\n  * `est/` -- **DONE and verified BYTE-IDENTICAL by `cmp`.**  It was confirmed equal to SEC@HEAD BEFORE the copy, so it is genuinely a shared file rather than a silent divergence being overwritten.  *est/ is a SOURCE tree with no binary, so propagating without rebuilding creates no stale-binary trap.*\n  * `build/`, `build-asan/`, `/tmp/gxsec-build` -- **NOT propagated.**  Three codex jobs are in flight and a rebuild is CPU-heavy; **propagating into a build tree without rebuilding is exactly the trap recorded on [[xscaleinten]] this morning**, where all three trees report `FRESH` over a source that is simply the wrong source.  Recorded in `_scratchpad/CHECKPOINT.md` so a fresh session finds the debt.\n\n**STILL OWED BEFORE THIS COMMITS: propagate+rebuild the three build trees together, a pass 2 on the real diff, and the targeted gate.**  The witness and the kill table are already done (HEAD 17/25, fix 25/25, `M_ENTRY` 20/25, `M_NULL_MEDIUM` 6/25, `RESTORED` = HEAD).\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PASS 2 on the real `#524` diff (codex GPT-6 Astra at max).  **PASS 2: NOT RATIFIED -- THE MASKS ARE RIGHT AND 'ONLY TWO SITES' WAS WRONG.** ***\n\n*VERDICT: NOT RATIFIED -- the two masks are locally correct, but other BDT implementations still use unrounded addresses, and the architectural justification needs qualification.*\n\n**THE MASK AND THE LITERAL ARE CORRECT.**  `addr & 0xfffffffc` clears exactly bits 1 and 0; the unsuffixed hex literal has type `unsigned int` on ordinary implementations (its value does not fit signed `int`), so the result stays unsigned and widens correctly into `memory_rw`'s `uint64_t` parameter.  *A wider host pointer does not change this.*\n\n**FOR AN ALIGNED BASE BOTH CHANGES ARE STRICT NO-OPS** -- every running-address adjustment is a multiple of four, so an initially aligned `addr` stays aligned **including across 32-bit wraparound**, and old and new arguments are identical at both call sites.\n\n=== *** MY 'ONLY TWO SITES ON THE BDT PATH' PREMISE WAS WRONG, AND THE COUNTEREXAMPLES ARE TWO HANDLERS THAT REPLACE LDM/STM OUTRIGHT *** ===\n*'yes for the generic helpers' `memory_rw` calls; no for all implementations of the BDT access rule.'*  **Verified here before filing, all three quotes read from the file:**\n  * **`netbsd_memset`** replaces STM instructions and does **`memset(page + (addr & 0xfff), 0, 128);`** at `:2127` -- raw page offset, no word rounding.  Its runtime guards check page bounds, zero source values and page availability; **none rejects an unaligned address.**\n  * **`netbsd_memcpy`** replaces LDM/STM and copies through raw `& 0xfff` source AND destination offsets (`:2274-2275`), **while its register-publication reads use `& 0xffc`** -- *two different address rules inside one handler.*  Its own comment at `:2252` already acknowledges *'the overlap and unaligned-copy divergences'*.\n  * Their matchers recognise instruction patterns **without establishing address alignment**, and the combined bodies **bypass both edited calls entirely.**\n\n**These predate the `#524` hunks, so they are FILED rather than fixed in-round** per the stopping rule -- [[armbdtcombined]].  *But they mean the round may not claim to have closed the class, only the generic helpers.*\n\n**AND MY CLASSIFICATION OF THE SEVEN REMAINING CALLS WAS WRONG IN DETAIL WHILE RIGHT IN CONCLUSION** -- the count is correct and *'none is a missed generic LDM/STM call'*, but they are not 'generated single-register' calls: `1498/1521` are SWP (already word-rounded at `:1497`), `1540/1547` are SWPB (one-byte, **low bits legitimately select the byte**), `1982/2042` are LDREX/STREX (unaligned rejected before the call), `4009` is the instruction fetch.\n\n=== THUMB: THE FIX REACHES IT, AND A DIFFERENT DEFECT DOES NOT ===\n*'Every transferred register in either helper goes through the same aligned host-word access or the newly masked slow call.  There is no Thumb-specific raw-address branch inside either transfer loop.'*  **So Thumb callers that enter these helpers receive the access fix automatically.**\n\n*Coverage unverified because `cpu_arm.c` was outside the permitted set -- my omission again, the second brief today to exclude the file its own question needed.*  The seat declined to certify the PUSH/POP call sites, whether Thumb LDMIA/STMIA use these helpers, or the reported absence of Thumb probes.\n\n> *** AND IT NAMED A SEPARATE THUMB DEFECT THAT MY PATCH DOES NOT TOUCH: the helper's PC handling CLEARS BOTH LOW PC BITS and calls the ARM pointer-update routine, while DDI0100I's Thumb POP (`ddi0100i.txt:24256`) PRESERVES THE HALFWORD ADDRESS and specifies interworking. ***  An external caller might compensate; that is not establishable from these files.  **Unchanged by `#524` and not claimed by it.**\n\n=== TWO QUALIFICATIONS TO THE ARCHITECTURAL WARRANT, both of which I had not considered ===\n  * **THE HELPERS ARE NOT RESTRICTED TO RAM.**  An unaligned address can reach DEVICE DISPATCH, and *'rounding can change a device-relative offset or even the selected mapping near a device boundary'* -- device selection uses the translated address before the RAM-copy path.\n  * **`ddi0100i.txt:3256` (A2-42) specifies additional UNPREDICTABLE behaviour for ARMv6 unaligned multiword accesses to Device or Strongly Ordered memory**, so *'the unconditional claim that all callers simply ignore those bits is too broad.'*\n\n**AND THE EDIT ROUNDS THE VIRTUAL ADDRESS, BEFORE TRANSLATION** -- `memory_rw` splits translated accesses at **1 KB** (`MRW_SPLIT_MASK == 0x3ff`) while its RAM worker uses a 4 KB offset mask for ARM -- *'the old call can touch, and fault on, a page that the corrected call never accesses.'*  **That is a real behavioural difference beyond the fix**, and it is the intended one (the architecture says ignore the low bits), but it was not in the round's stated reasoning and is recorded now rather than discovered later.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PANEL CONVENED on the combined `#524`/`#525` packet, and **SEAT LEDGER FOR THE #524/#525 PANEL -- THREE SEATS DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED, BY NAME** ***\n\n**FIRED: the eight scriptable transports** via `bash GXEMUL-SEC/tools/panel.sh <absolute brief>` -- codex GPT-6 Astra (max), grok 4.6 (xhigh), kimi 3, and the Ollama cloud seats glm-5.3, deepseek-v4-pro, minimax-m3 (+ the kimi-k3 substitute if configured).  **All four Ollama transports were confirmed REACHABLE by `api/tags` beforehand**, so a non-answer from any of them is a seat failure rather than a dead daemon.\n\n=== *** DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED -- NAMED, BECAUSE A SEAT HELD BACK AND A SEAT THAT FAILED ARE DIFFERENT THINGS AND CONFLATING THEM IS HOW A TWO-SEAT REVIEW GETS REPORTED AS A PANEL *** ===\n  * **agy 3.8** -- RESERVED FOR MEASUREMENT by standing owner ruling, and it is the reason both of these rounds have witnesses at all: it built the `armbdt` witness + kill table (25/25) and the `wdcaltdigin` witness + kill table (16/16) earlier today.  *Spending its bounded budget on a reading seven other seats can do is spending the roster's only independent-transport measuring capability on a fungible job.*\n  * **Opus 5** -- HELD.  It answers through the Agent tool and consumes the same Anthropic quota the owner wants preserved for the adjudication.  *Not a failure, not agreement: a budget decision.*\n  * **Fable 5** -- HELD BY EXPLICIT OWNER SEQUENCING: *'don't run fable until all other panel seats have had a say.'*  **This panel is the 'all other seats' step.**  Its availability is to be checked by a plain subagent rather than by Opus when the time comes.\n\n**WHY THE PACKET IS SHAPED AS IT IS:** every fact is INLINED -- both diffs, both full enclosing contexts, both measured kill tables, and **both first-pass NOT-RATIFIED findings** -- because the Ollama transports cannot read files and a brief containing *'check the source'* is a documented way to turn a live seat into a stall that looks exactly like a quota wall.  *The packet also asks the seats to attack the FIRST PASS'S FINDINGS, not only the code, so the corrections get adversarial scrutiny too.*\n\n*** THE LOAD-BEARING QUESTION PUT TO THEM IS THE SELECTOR ONE, and it is imported from a sibling round rather than invented: *** `sgirandomcolour` had **six mutants score a TOTAL ESCAPE** because every detector row probed a single position and each mutant conditioned the repair's effect on a tile or coordinate selector.  **`#524` has 25 rows and `#525` has 16, and neither has had a selector audit.**  The panel is asked to name a conditioning that a plausible edit could produce and the rows could not see.\n\n*Landing remains HELD by owner ruling until the full panel reports -- nothing is committed and no gate has run.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PANEL (`_scratchpad/panel_20260911_103402`).  **SIX of seven scriptable seats answered substantively; the kimi CLI is WALLED and its `kimi-k3:cloud` substitute ANSWERED, so that seat is COVERED, not lost.** ***\n\n**Seat sizes and tails verified individually rather than by size alone:** codex 300,614 b (streams its reasoning; the tail is substantive), grok 11,840 b, `ollama_minimax` 12,053 b, `ollama_glm` 9,695 b, `ollama_kimi` 7,601 b, `ollama_deepseek` 4,219 b.  **`kimi.txt` is 350 b ending `See log: ...kimi-code.log` -- the CLI failure signature, NOT a considered non-answer.**\n\n**CONSENSUS ON THE PRODUCT CODE: NEITHER FIX IS WRONG.**  glm -- *'#524 is architecturally correct and its scope is what was overstated'*; minimax -- *'Both are correct, minimal, and correctly scoped'*; `kimi-k3` -- *'`addr & 0xfffffffc` per-access is provably equivalent'*; deepseek -- *'Neither fix is wrong as a local correction, but #524 is incomplete (combined handlers)'*, **independently reproducing the codex finding about `netbsd_memset`/`netbsd_memcpy`.**\n\n=== *** THE SELECTOR QUESTION GOT REAL ANSWERS, AND THEY ARE FLOOR-BREACH CANDIDATES *** ===\nI asked, importing the shape from [[sgirandomcolour]]: *what conditioning could a plausible edit introduce that the 25 and 16 rows never vary?*\n\n| round | proposed conditioning | seat | predicted |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `#525` | **`if (odata & WDCS_DRDY) odata = 0;`** | grok | **16/16 -- TOTAL ESCAPE** |\n| `#525` | `if (d->unit == 0) odata = 0;` -- a DRIVE-UNIT selector | deepseek | passes if every row uses the primary controller |\n| `#524` | `if (cpu->mem->page_size == 4096) addr &= ~3;` -- a constant property of the test map | deepseek | passes; breaks on any other page size |\n\n**grok also corrected my clause-(d) choice:** deleting `odata = 0` is NOT the discriminating mutant, *because `M_NULL_EFFECT` already scored 14/16* -- the detector sees it.  **The one that escapes keeps the assignment and gates it.**  *That is the difference between a mutant the rows catch and a mutant the rows are blind to, and I had picked the first and called it the floor test.*\n\n**PRESCRIPTION (grok):** *'Product diffs can stand.  The detectors cannot ship as the round's \"we pinned the repair\" claim until `#524` has a `+2` and a `+3` (or an anchored `& 3` mutant) and `#525` has one CORE observation with `WDCS_DRDY` clear.'*  **That is an L3 -- an undischarged obligation, where the row is the sole carrier of something the round claims.**\n\n*** AND A MEASURED-VS-PREDICTED DISCREPANCY IN MY OWN ARTIFACT, caught by grok and verified here: `PRETABLE.out:54` predicts `M_NULL_MEDIUM 4/25`; `SCORES.txt` measures **6/25**. ***  *I cited 6/25 -- the measured one -- so no record is wrong, but the PREDICTION sits in a durable artifact where a future reader can cite it as a measurement.  That is the frozen-number class with a two-row error already in the drawer.*  grok's reading of the remainder: *'PATH/ATTRIB-style controls surviving an early `return`'*.\n\n**Seats DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED, by name: agy** (reserved for measurement; it built both witnesses today), **Opus 5** (held -- same Anthropic quota the adjudication needs), **Fable 5** (held by owner sequencing until every other seat has spoken -- **this panel is that step**).\n\n*** 2026-09-11 FABLE 5 ADJUDICATION (`FABLE_ADJ_20260911`).  **SEAT ALIVE -- token AND a verbatim `CHECKPOINT.md` HEAD quote returned, and it re-ran `git log --oneline -1` itself to confirm the checkpoint is not stale.**  Fired ONLY after all seven other scriptable seats had spoken, per owner sequencing, and via a subagent rather than Opus. ***\n\n=== RULING: **HOLD for ONE MEASUREMENT, then repair IN-ROUND.  Grok MIS-GRADED this: it is not L3, it is CLAUSE (d).** ===\n\n*** IT IS THE CARRIER'S OWN WORKED EXAMPLE -- `#461`'s `m_align1`, a mask narrowed `& 3` -> `& 1`. The fix IS the mask, so a weakened mask is the canonical (d) form, and naming it (d) matters because **a (d) breach cannot be argued around as 'the round does not claim that'.** ***\n\n**CHECKED BY THE ADJUDICATOR, not inferred: every unaligned base in `agy_armbdt_witness_20260911.py` is `+1`** -- `:218` `0x20001`, `:281` `0x21001`, POP `0x22001`, PUSH `0x23005 -> 0x23001`.  **For every row `x+1 & ~1 == x+1 & ~3`, so `0xfffffffc -> 0xfffffffe` at both sites is 25/25 BY ARITHMETIC.**\n\n> **But it must still be BUILT AND SCORED, not asserted.**  Grok said plainly it had not built it, and this project's own rule is that *'it would sweep the table' is a MEASUREMENT, never an inference.*  **Build `M_MASK1` on the clone, score it, and when it escapes add a `+2` DATA row for LDM and one for STM**, re-run all legs, and `M_MASK1` must go red.\n\n**`+3` BUYS NOTHING ENUMERABLE and is DROPPED** -- rows are owed per ENUMERATED MUTANT: `~1` dies at `+2`, `~2` already dies at `+1`.  **Disclose residue 3 in the contract instead of rowing it.**  *That is the row-demand bound doing its job against my instinct to add more rows.*\n\n**Operative licence is L1** (nothing has shipped), not L2.\n\n=== *** THE `PRETABLE` 4/25-vs-6/25 GAP IS EXPLAINED, AND THE EXPLANATION IS A REAL DEFECT *** ===\nThe two extra survivors are **`ARM_STM_COLD_CLOBBER` and `THUMB_PUSH_COLD_CLOBBER`**, found by diffing the row list against the FAILING line.  **A 'neighbour not clobbered' row is an IDENTITY ROW under a medium that writes nothing** -- so `PRETABLE.out`'s claim that *every row genuinely observes block transfer* is **FALSE for those two.**  *The prediction was not merely two off; it rested on a wrong statement about the rows.*\n\n**Write `PRETABLE2.out` for the re-table -- do NOT append to `PRETABLE.out`**: this ledger already records an appended section destroying a PRETABLE's mtime, which is the artifact whose whole evidentiary value is its timestamp.\n\n**TWO L4s IN MY OWN COMMENT, BOTH APPLIED:**\n  1. *'DDI0100I says to ignore the low two bits'* was UNQUALIFIED.  **Verified at `ddi0100i.txt:7954-7959`: that holds for `CP15_reg1_Ubit == 0` AND alignment checking DISABLED; `Ubit == 1` faults on every non-word-aligned access.**  And the fork is internally inconsistent -- `cpu_arm.c:129` SETS `ARM_CONTROL_ALIGN` in the default control register, `:474` only PRINTS it, and **nothing consults it.**  *** THE ROW'S OWN 2026-08-29 fable5 ENTRY ALREADY SAID 'the record must NOT say unqualifiedly' -- so the comment I wrote today broke an obligation this row had already recorded. ***\n  2. *'Measured: ... `arm_writeback_probe.py` scores it 22/22'* -- **`22/22` appears in NO measurement artifact.**  Verified: it occurs only in briefs and seat answers; `probe_M_ENTRY.raw` backs the 20/25 alone.  **Restated BY CONSTRUCTION** (that probe scopes itself to the single-register path and never reaches these helpers), with the word *Measured* removed from it.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 TABLES 2 AND 3.  **THE CLAUSE-(d) BREACH IS MEASURED, CLOSED AND RE-MEASURED: M_MASK1 25/25 -> 27/29.** ***\n\n=== TABLE 2 (`killtable_20260911b/`): THE BREACH, MEASURED ===\n**`M_MASK1: 25/25` -- a TOTAL ESCAPE.**  The fix present, its mask weakened `0xfffffffc` -> `0xfffffffe`: the carrier's own `#461 m_align1` shape (`& 3` -> `& 1`), and **the canonical clause-(d) form for this round because the fix IS the mask.**\n\n*Fable predicted it by arithmetic -- every unaligned base the probe drove was `+1`, and `x+1 & ~1 == x+1 & ~3` -- and ruled that it be BUILT anyway, because this project's rule is that 'it would sweep the table' is a MEASUREMENT, never an inference.  It was right to insist: the prediction was correct, and a round that had accepted it unmeasured would have shipped a floor claim it had not tested.*\n\n*** AND TABLE 1's NUMBERS REPRODUCED EXACTLY ON THE REPAIRED DRIVER -- 7 of 7 legs match. ***  **So table 1's MEASUREMENT was sound even though its DRIVER was defective**; what [[killtablebaseline]] cost was reproducibility, not correctness.  *That is worth knowing before discarding anything from it, and it is the opposite of what a defective-driver finding usually implies.*\n\n=== TABLE 3 (`killtable_20260911c/`): THE CLOSURE, RE-MEASURED ===\n\n| leg | score | rows it fails |\n|---|---|---|\n| `BASE` (the fix) | **29/29** | none |\n| `HEAD` | 19/29 | 10 -- the defect rows, including BOTH new `+2` rows |\n| **`M_MASK1`** | **27/29** | *** exactly 2: `ARM_LDM_COLD_DATA_P2`, `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_P2` *** |\n| `M_ENTRY` | 24/29 | 5 -- **ALL FIVE WRITEBACK ROWS AND NOTHING ELSE** |\n| `M_POP_ONLY` | 24/29 | 5 -- the STM-side rows |\n| `M_PUSH_ONLY` | 24/29 | 5 -- the LDM-side MIRROR of those |\n| `M_NULL_MEDIUM` | 8/29 | 21, including BOTH control rows |\n| `RESTORED` | 19/29 | = `HEAD`, the clean-restore proof (now against GIT) |\n\n> *** EVERY LEG DIES ON A DISTINCT, SEMANTICALLY CORRECT SET, AND THAT IS THE PART WORTH MORE THAN THE SCORES. ***  `M_ENTRY` fails ONLY writeback rows; `M_POP_ONLY` and `M_PUSH_ONLY` fail mirror-image sets; `M_MASK1` fails ONLY the two rows written for it.  *Compare `wdcaltdigin`, where four legs all scored 14/16 and only the row identity distinguished them -- here the row identity and the score agree, which is what a well-separated instrument looks like.*\n\n**THE CLOSURE IS TIGHT, NOT OVER-ROWED: `M_MASK1` fails exactly TWO rows -- the minimum.**  *Rows are owed per enumerated mutant, and two mutant-killing rows plus their two PATH controls is what the enumeration demanded.*\n\n=== THE PREDICTIONS WERE RIGHT TO THE BYTE, WHICH IS THE CHANNEL RULE WORKING ===\n`PRETABLE2.out` named, before the run, the observable each new row would show under `M_MASK1`:\n  * `ARM_LDM_COLD_DATA_P2` -- predicted `0x77881122`, **measured `0x77881122`**;\n  * `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_P2` -- predicted `0x33440000`, **measured `0x33440000`**.\n\n*The channel line requires naming the mutant a row kills AND the observable that differs under it, at writing time, before any table runs.  Both were named and both were exact.*\n\n**ORDER PROVEN BY MTIME:** `PRETABLE2.out` 12:15:13, first table run 12:16:31 -- **78 s earlier**.  Written as a SEPARATE FILE rather than appended to `PRETABLE.out`, because this ledger records an append destroying a PRETABLE's mtime once, and the mtime is the artifact's entire evidentiary value.\n\n*** A PREDICTION OF MINE THAT MISSED, recorded because it is the same class I spent today cataloguing: I predicted `BASE 30/30` and the measurement is `29/29`. ***  I derived 30 from `grep -c 'record('` -- **a CORRELATE of the scored-row count, not the count itself** (one `record()` call is not scored).  *The deriver-keys-on-a-correlate rule, in my own pre-table prediction, on the same day I recorded it against three other censuses.  The error is harmless here because predictions and measurements are in separate sections and every citation names `SCORES.txt` -- which is exactly why they are kept apart.*\n\n**AND THUMB IS COVERED AFTER ALL, which settles part of an open question:** the failing-row lists name `THUMB_POP_WB_UNAL`, `THUMB_PUSH_WB_UNAL`, `THUMB_POP_COLD_DATA` and `THUMB_PUSH_COLD_DATA` -- so the instrument does exercise the Thumb callers, and `M_ENTRY` kills the Thumb writeback rows alongside the ARM ones.  *The `#524` pass-2 seat could not verify this because `cpu_arm.c` was outside its permitted set; the table settles it from the other direction.*\n\n**FLOOR STATUS FOR `#524`: (a) HEAD red, (b) reinstatement dies, (c) null medium 8/29, (d) `M_MASK1` KILLED at 27/29.  The breach is closed in-round under L1** -- nothing had shipped, so this is pre-ship construction rather than an L2 repair.  **Residue 3 disclosed in the contract rather than rowed**, because `~1` dies at `+2` and `~2` already dies at `+1`, so a `+3` row kills no enumerated mutant.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 TABLE 4 (`killtable_20260911d/`).  **TABLE 4: THE CLASS CLOSURE WORKS AND **BASE IS RED ON ONE OF MY OWN NEW ROWS** -- ROUND HELD.** ***\n\n=== THE REPAIR-CHAIN PASS FOUND A FLOOR BREACH AND REFUSED TO CLOSE THE CHAIN ===\n*VERDICT: FLOOR BREACH FOUND; the repair correctly detects `M_MASK1` but does not close the repair chain.*  **Two more clean-sweep candidates against the 29-row composite, both STATIC DEDUCTIONS rather than measured -- which is why each became a leg:**\n  * **`M_MASK7` (`& ~7`, OVER-alignment):** every base the instrument drove -- `0x10000`, `0x2000{1,2}`, `0x2100{1,2}` -- sits in the **FIRST WORD of its 8-byte block, where `~3` and `~7` AGREE.**  *And the two-register LDM does not rescue it: it asserts only `r0` writeback, never either loaded register.*\n  * **`M_PBITCOND` (a CONDITIONAL weakening on an EXISTING dominating predicate):** mask `~1` when `p_bit`, `~3` otherwise, at the STORE site only.  ARM STM is IA (`p_bit=0`, correct mask); Thumb PUSH is DB (`p_bit=1`, weakened) **but ran at exactly ONE residue, `+1`, where `~1` and `~3` agree.**  *The predicate axis was exercised at a single point.*\n\n**TWO CONSECUTIVE PASSES FOUND MASK-WEAKENING BREACHES, so the next repair owed CLASS CLOSURE rather than a third point fix.**  Space from the helpers' own inputs: **{site} x {residue} x {word position in the 8-byte block} x {p_bit}**.  Three uncovered cells closed in ONE diff.\n\n=== AND BOTH NEW MUTANTS DIED, ON THE CELLS WRITTEN FOR THEM ===\n\n| leg | score | killed on |\n|---|---|---|\n| `M_MASK7` | 30/32 | `ARM_LDM_COLD_DATA_BIT2`, `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_BIT2` |\n| `M_PBITCOND` | 30/32 | `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_BIT2`, `THUMB_PUSH_COLD_DATA_P2` |\n| `M_MASK1` | 28/32 | its two P2 rows **plus both new ones** |\n| `M_ENTRY` | 26/32 | the writeback rows |\n| `M_NULL_MEDIUM` | 8/32 | 21+ rows incl. both controls |\n| `HEAD` / `RESTORED` | 19/32 | identical -- clean restore against GIT |\n\n*** BUT `BASE: 31/32`.  THE FIX ITSELF FAILS `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_BIT2` -- one of the rows I ADDED THIS PASS -- and a red BASE row means the round DOES NOT SHIP. ***\n\n**The row reports `w0=0x00000000 w1=0x00000000` on the FIXED build.**  Expected: base `0x21005` masks to `0x21004`, so the word lands in `PAGE_STM+4`.  **Nothing landed in either word.**\n\n> **AND I COULD NOT MAKE THE ARITHMETIC CLOSE BY REASONING.**  Under HEAD the same row reports `w1=0x00000011`, which implies the stored bytes ENDED at `0x21004` -- i.e. a base of `0x21001`, not the `0x21005` my literal sets.  That contradicts `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA`, which calibrates correctly at base `0x21001` giving `0x22334400`.  *Two readings of the same mechanism that cannot both be right.*\n\n**SO I STOPPED REASONING AND INSTRUMENTED**, which is this project's own rule when a value is surprising: a diagnostic rebuilds BASE and **dumps a WIDE WINDOW around `PAGE_STM`, so the bytes can be LOCATED rather than guessed at.**  *I had been deriving where the store went without looking at where it actually is.*\n\n**TWO POSSIBILITIES, AND WHICH ONE IS NOT YET KNOWN:** my row is mis-specified -- most likely, since every other BASE row passes including `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_P2`, which uses the same mechanism at `+2` -- or it has found something real about the store path at bit-2-set addresses.  **Recorded as open rather than resolved in either direction.**\n\n*Also fixed this pass, from the panel: the mutator now emits **`0xfffffffc`, the EXACT shipped literal**, so `BASE` measures the working-tree text rather than a semantic equivalent -- grok raised that the table had not measured what would be committed.*\n\n*** DISCLOSED: THE PATH ROWS ARE FIXTURE CHECKS, NOT MUTATION DISCRIMINATORS, AND THE REPAIR PASS WAS RIGHT ABOUT THE ONES I ADDED. ***  Every `in_tlb_*` is captured BEFORE `run_prog`, so no helper-local mutation of a mask, operand or predicate can retroactively change it.  They establish the run was cold -- which the core comparison needs -- and discriminate NO enumerated mutant.  Kept for that purpose, disclosed rather than counted toward the floor.\n\n*And my `'a +3 row buys no enumerated mutant'` was TOO BROAD as a claim about the COMPOSITE: it holds within a fixed cell, but the missing distinction is the PREDICATE/RESIDUE COMBINATION, and residue 3 is one possible witness among several rather than one that buys nothing.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 TABLE 5 (`killtable_20260911e/`).  **THE RED BASE ROW WAS A DEFECT IN THE WITNESS, NOT THE FIX -- AND IT HAD CORRUPTED 8 OF 10 LEGS.  BASE IS NOW 32/32 AND THE FLOOR IS CLEAN.** ***\n\n=== HOW IT WAS FOUND, INCLUDING THE PART I DID BADLY ===\nTable 4 reported `BASE 31/32` -- the FIX failing `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_BIT2`.  **A red BASE row blocks the round, so it was diagnosed rather than explained away.**\n\n*** I FIRST TRIED TO REASON IT OUT FROM THE BYTE PATTERNS AND COULD NOT MAKE THE ARITHMETIC CLOSE. ***  Under HEAD the row implied the store ended at `0x21004` -- a base of `0x21001` -- while `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA` calibrates correctly at that same base.  **Two readings of one mechanism that cannot both be true**, and I spent four rounds of inference on it before instrumenting.\n\n**The dump settled it in ONE run:**\n    r0 = 0x21005                                 <- the literal loaded fine\n    0x00021000  00000000 44332211 00000000 ...   <- the store LANDED at 0x21004\n\n> **THE STORE WAS CORRECT ALL ALONG.  THE READ WAS WRONG.**\n\n=== THE INSTRUMENT DEFECT, MEASURED DIRECTLY ===\n    dump from an ALIGNED start:\n      0x00021000  00000000 44332211\n    dump from an UNALIGNED start -- what `read_mem32` actually issued:\n      0x00021000           44332211 00000000\n                  ^^^^^^^^ the leading group is BLANK-PADDED\n\n`read_mem32()` searched for the line labelled `(addr & ~0xF)` -- **which matches in BOTH cases** -- then indexed `line[12:48].split()` by `(addr & 0xF) // 4`.  **When the dump starts unaligned the leading groups are ABSENT and `.split()` collapses the padding, so every index shifts and `parts[idx]` silently returns a NEIGHBOURING WORD.**\n\n    read_mem32(0x21004) -> parts == ['44332211', '00000000'], idx 1 -> 0\n\n*This is the padded-column trap this project already records, in a new place: the label matched, so every check that looked for the right line passed, and the CONTENT came from the wrong column.*\n\n**REPAIR: dump from `(addr & ~0xF)` for 16 bytes, so all four groups are always present -- which is what the index arithmetic already assumed.  One line.**\n\n*** SCOPE, AND IT IS WIDER THAN THE ONE RED ROW: 8 OF 10 LEGS CHANGED VALUE WHEN THE INSTRUMENT WAS REPAIRED. ***\n\n| leg | table 4 | table 5 |\n|---|---|---|\n| `BASE` | 31/32 | **32/32** |\n| `HEAD` | 19/32 | **17/32** -- the pre-fix build was failing MORE rows than reported |\n| `RESTORED` | 19/32 | 17/32 (= HEAD) |\n| `M_ENTRY` | 26/32 | 27/32 |\n| `M_MASK1` | 28/32 | 29/32 |\n| `M_PBITCOND` | 30/32 | 31/32 |\n| `M_POP_ONLY` | 25/32 | 23/32 |\n| `M_PUSH_ONLY` | 25/32 | 26/32 |\n| `M_MASK7`, `M_NULL_MEDIUM` | unchanged | unchanged |\n\n**The defect was in the probe AS ORIGINALLY BUILT**, so it affected every `read_mem32` call at a nonzero 16-byte offset -- the STM and Thumb PUSH clobber rows -- across tables 1 through 4.  **Every number those rows produced before this table is superseded.**  *Grepped: no other probe in the tree shares the shape, so the blast radius is this round's instrument alone.*\n\n=== THE FLOOR, NOW CLEAN ===\n\n| clause | leg | score | killed on |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| -- | `BASE` | **32/32** | **nothing -- clean** |\n| (a) pre-fix red | `HEAD` | 17/32 | the defect rows |\n| (b) reinstatement | `RESTORED` | 17/32 | = HEAD exactly |\n| (c) null medium | `M_NULL_MEDIUM` | 8/32 | 24 rows |\n| **(d)** | `M_MASK1` | 29/32 | the three `P2` rows |\n| **(d)** | `M_MASK7` | 30/32 | the two `BIT2` rows |\n| **(d)** | `M_PBITCOND` | 31/32 | **`THUMB_PUSH_COLD_DATA_P2` alone -- the minimum** |\n| de-escalation | `M_ENTRY` | 27/32 | **all five writeback rows, nothing else** |\n\n**Every mutant dies on exactly the cells written for it, and `M_PBITCOND` on precisely one.**  *The class closure holds under a correct instrument, which is the thing table 4 could not establish.*\n\n*A small one of mine, recorded because I spent today cataloguing this class: my own shell check for a clean BASE tested `[ -z \"$B\" ]`, but the probe prints `FAILING: none` when clean -- a NON-EMPTY string -- so the check took its else branch and printed 'BASE still red on: none'.  The score `32/32` is what settled it.  A check that reports the opposite of the truth on a green run is the same family as the ones I filed against other people's scripts today.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PROMOTED TO regress/ AND WIRED INTO gate_arm.sh -- 32/32 AGAINST THE GATE'S OWN BINARY. ***\n\n**This discharges the obligation the round could not previously claim.**  Fable's adjudication found that *neither of today's probes is under `regress/` proper, so 'shipped a detector with the fix' is not yet true of either round.*  For `#524` it is true now.\n\n=== THE PROMOTION IS A COPY, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE ===\n`regress/artifacts/armbdt/agy_armbdt_witness_20260911.py` **stays in place as the AS-RUN record of tables 1-5**; `regress/arm_bdt_probe.py` is the copy that evolves and that the gate executes.  *Editing the artifact copy would falsify what those tables actually measured* -- the same reason the repaired kill-table driver was written as a new file rather than over the old one.\n\n**The promoted copy differs only by two PRINT-ONLY lines (`ARMBDT_CONTROL=`, `ARMBDT_RESULT=`, matching the gate idiom the other ARM probes use) and a header note.  Verified: the `record()` count is unchanged, so no row was added or lost in the copy.**  Its header also carries the `read_mem32` alignment warning, so the next probe copied from it does not inherit [[probereadalign]].\n\n=== MEASURED AGAINST THE GATE'S OWN BINARY, NOT THE SCRATCH CLONE ===\n`$PMAX` is `build/gxemul`.  After the propagate+rebuild, the promoted probe run directly against it returns **`SCORE 32/32`, `ARMBDT_CONTROL=OK`, `ARMBDT_RESULT=32/32`, `FAILING: none`**.  *A pre-check worth the thirty seconds: wiring the probe in BEFORE the rebuild would have run it against a pre-fix binary and produced a red gate that meant nothing about the fix.*\n\n=== THE GATE SECTION, AND WHY THE DENOMINATOR IS A LITERAL ===\nFollows `arm_writeback_probe.py`'s shape exactly: run -> `gate_skip` if no result line -> print the ok/FAIL rows -> check the control -> **`check \"bdt rows run\" \"$bwant\" 32`** -> check correct == run.\n\n> **The denominator is pinned to a LITERAL deliberately.  The probe grew 25 -> 29 -> 32 rows across three kill tables as clause-(d) mutants were closed, and a gate that trusts the probe's own denominator cannot tell a probe that LOST rows from one that never had them.**\n\nThe section also records all five measured-dead mutants with their scores and the row family that kills each (`M_ENTRY` 27/32 writeback rows, `M_MASK1` 29/32 the `+2` rows, `M_MASK7` 30/32 the bit-2 rows, `M_PBITCOND` 31/32 Thumb PUSH `+2`, `M_NULL_MEDIUM` 8/32), **so a future reader meets the evidence at the gate rather than having to find the artifacts.**\n\n*Targeted gate run in flight.  Nothing committed; the owner's hold stands until it is green.*",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:13486:     "ev": "fable5 adjudication 2026-08-28 (flagship, main-loop adjudicator hat); liveness token FABLE_PRETABLE_ALIVE_20260828 plus a verbatim first line of c_M0_delete.raw returned, so prompt delivery and file access were proven.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:13488:     "note": "ANSWERED by the pre-table mandate ruling: the four-check PRE-TABLE BLOCK IS MANDATORY for any round shipping or EXTENDING a kill-table-backed detector -- the same population the vacuity floor already binds, NOT every round.  The doctrinal trigger was met twice in four days: this round (#485) and #489 both went design->table without running it, and the structure-first rule says a rule earning its second instance while sitting in the carrier was mis-filed as prose.  At THIS round the value was specific: the GUARD-FIELD check enumerates D1..D5 mechanically, because the default: arm fatal() arguments ARE the guarded body field reads.  FORM: recorded OUTPUT (PRETABLE.out in the round artifacts dir, written BEFORE table 1 so its mtime proves the order), never assertion -- the two-part-clearance precedent, run it do not assert it.  Any check may discharge N/A WITH ITS REASON RECORDED; an unrun check and an inapplicable one must not render the same.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:22396:     "note": "Ran its own two-mutant table in an isolated tree and MEASURED both escapes rather than arguing them.  `M_cmdcount` -- deleting `d->cmd_count = 0;` from the accepted 0xa0 arm -- leaves ALL TWENTY ROWS BYTE-IDENTICAL while restoring a guest-reachable host kill, because that line is the third leg of another site's unreachability warrant; attack probe 5/5 exit0 on the fix tree, 3/5 exit1 on the mutant, attribution control green on both.  `M2_hicond` -- `if (idata >= 0x80) d->cond = idata;` -- sweeps all twenty, and it diagnosed WHY arithmetically: the three declined bytes union to 0x5d, so bits 1, 5 and 7 appeared in no probed value.  Its repair costs ONE CONSTANT.  It also caught that PRETABLE claims its own mtime as proof of order and had been edited twice before first commit, and that the previous commit message still carried the REFUTED contention diagnosis -- the failure was deterministic across four runs, and determinism is the discriminator against contention.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:22429:     "note": "Ruled F1-F9 UPHOLD, F10 REFINE, F11 UPHOLD-with-residual, and gave the sharpest form of the D1 refinement: the FILE ruling stands but its discharge does not exist, and PRETABLE's present-tense 'the cost is recorded' is itself an L4.  On Q2 it declined to name a wrong disposition and said why -- none is wrong on its stated logic, two are incomplete, and ALL TEN are unvalidated pending the clean run.  That is the honest answer to a question designed to catch complacency.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:22462:     "note": "Adjudicated from source rather than from the author's dispositions, opening the probe, the COND arms and PRETABLE before ruling.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:23689:     "note": "VERDICT (from the three-question adjudication): **this row FOLDS INTO `probetimebased`, as\nits own nine-seat panel already ruled on 2026-08-24 -- NOT the other way round, and\n`proberowsubset` does not fold into it.**\n\nThe spec for the row-filter fix MOVES to `proberowsubset`.  Leaving it here would lose it\nin the ruled merge -- which is the `#490` PRETABLE lesson (a specification living on a\ndifferent artifact from the row that discharges it) one level up.\n\nThis row closes by EXECUTING its ruled fold, citing the 08-24 '2181s acceptable'\nmeasurement as its closure evidence.  **Nothing about the filter appears in its closure.**\nCross-links stand until the fold executes."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:26107:     "ev": "fable5 adjudication 2026-08-28 (flagship, main-loop adjudicator hat); liveness token FABLE_PRETABLE_ALIVE_20260828 plus a verbatim first line of c_M0_delete.raw returned, so prompt delivery and file access were proven.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:26313:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/wdcoddstep/overtaken_20260827/CONTROL_clamp_removed.out (clamp-removed control; the queue figures atapi_len=-1 received=4 of 3, and the two pinning rows red) and HEAD_closed.out (green at HEAD), plus author_odd.py. *** REPOINTED 2026-08-28: this field cited _scratchpad/odd/out and _scratchpad/oddm/out, and BOTH ARE NOW DELETED -- verified by ls.  The durable copies existed the whole time.  Found by fable5 adjudication 2026-08-28 (flagship, main-loop adjudicator hat); liveness token FABLE_PRETABLE_ALIVE_20260828 plus a verbatim first line of c_M0_delete.raw returned, so prompt delivery and file access were proven.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:26324:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/wdcoddstep/overtaken_20260827/ (all three conditions verified against it by the adjudicating seat) -- fable5 adjudication 2026-08-28 (flagship, main-loop adjudicator hat); liveness token FABLE_PRETABLE_ALIVE_20260828 plus a verbatim first line of c_M0_delete.raw returned, so prompt delivery and file access were proven.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:27006:     "note": "VERDICT: **(1) OPTION C WAS NOT WRONGLY DISMISSED, AND MY QUESTION WAS MIS-POSED.\n(2) OPTION F STAYS FILED, with two named promotion triggers.  (3) NO rung-3 ask was\nmade or recorded -- a one-line records debt.**  Evidence basis: 7 of 9 mandated seats;\nopus5 still owed, so this ruling is PROVISIONAL on its face.\n\n*** FOUR ERRORS FOUND IN MY OWN FRAMING, and the third is a live records defect. ***\n\n**(i) THE (1) PREMISE IS FALSE.**  I wrote that #477's read-arm repair 'did a phase-scoped\nversion of exactly' option C.  It did the OPPOSITE.  C truncates the flag's LIFETIME; the\nrepair phase-scopes ONE READER and deliberately leaves the stale state in place.  Different\nstate object (the len/received pair, not the flag), opposite mechanism.  The shipped code's\nown comment says so: *\"that leaves this window untouched (the flag is legitimately 1 here)\"*.\nC is orthogonal to the window this row reproduces -- the defect fires MID-DATAIN, before any\ncompletion event, so no clear-at-completion rule can reach it.  C also breaks the ATAPI\nhandshake at four verified register-read sites.  **The tree converged on the ANTI-C, twice.**\n\n**(ii) 'THREE GUARDS' IS AN UNDERCOUNT -- IT IS FIVE, AND THE TWO I MISSED ARE THE STRONGEST\nEVIDENCE FOR MY OWN ARGUMENT.**  Four dispatch conjuncts plus the read-arm scope.  Four of the\nfive exist to compensate for shared-ring aliasing, and two have measured catastrophic\nweakenings (M_phaseonly swept 52 rows and destroyed a sector; M7_ge12 is a heap overflow\ninvisible to 44 rows).  So the structural argument has measured teeth -- but a GUARD CENSUS IS\nNOT A MEASURED DELTA, which is why F is round-eligible and still correctly ranked below live\ndefects.\n\n**(iii) 'A SEAT MEASURED F's MIGRATION COST' IS AN UNARTIFACTED OVERCLAIM, AND IT WAS\nECHOING.**  No build, no table, no log anywhere.  It was a READ.  Worse, it originated in\nOUTSTANDING_BUGS, I inlined it into two briefs, and codex quoted the diff back -- so five\nartifacts now carry the word 'measured' and a future grep reads independent confirmation.\n**CORRECTED IN PLACE the same hour** (OUTSTANDING_BUGS, the `wdccdbaccum` residual), including\nthe content: the accept side ports nearly as-is, and the reject rows go TARGET-DEAD owing\ncase-2 absence records rather than 'stopping working'.  Migration and target-death are\ndifferent dispositions.  The real cost is a re-cut plus a full re-table -- a STRONGER argument\nfor filing than the wrong one was.\n\n**(iv) THIS ROW HAS A SECOND HOLD AND I DID NOT SEE IT.**  Beyond the research stage, a\nregress-phase hold carries its own unanswered rule question -- *which panel discharges a row:\nits own stage, or the fixing round's?* -- and that was NOT among today's three adjudications.\n**Even at 9/9 research this row stays held until that is ruled.**\n\nTHE TWO PROMOTION TRIGGERS FOR F, both mechanical so no future round re-argues this:\n  * **(a) THE SIXTH-GUARD TRIGGER** -- the first time a MEASURED LIVE defect's fix would add\n    another dispatch predicate or another phase-scoped consumer guard, ship F instead.  At that\n    point the accumulated deltas ARE the measured warrant.\n  * **(b) THE DATAOUT TRIGGER** -- when DATAOUT payload support lands, F ships as its enabling\n    structure.  Implementing DATAOUT into the shared ring would create the aliasing a THIRD time.\n\nF's contract, if it ever ships: three classes become unrepresentable -- the `==12` occupancy\ncollision, the `>= 12` heap overflow, and wdcringnoflush's CDB-drop consequence.  An F round\nowes target-dead absence records, a full re-table with its own (d)-mutants, re-verification of\nthe four handshake readers, and a PRETABLE.out."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:28190:     "note": "ADJUDICATION: D3 ACCEPT -- literal `+ 0x2c', appended LAST, keyed on machine_type alone.  Three independent routes to 44: both BSD drivers' WDC_OBIO_AUXREG_OFFSET, codex's disassembly of the 7.6 ramdisk (`add #44,r5' before the map; `mov.b r7,@r6' at the census PC), and the census.  `0x16' is macppc's WDC_AUXREG_OFFSET factorisation (0x16<<4 = 0x160), a different board.  READY TO IMPLEMENT after: (1) PRETABLE.out written BEFORE table 1; (2) CHANGELOG cites file/version/define and the saved copies.  Witness valid: probe:regress/artifacts/landiskaux/rung3_opus_20260918/. Rows: `aux 2c reset' (DRQ purge), `aux 2c read' (= status), `aux 206 gone' (read AFTER fill = 0x00 -- the only row killing register-twice), `noctl RAM' (liveness).  Disclose, don't row: initializer 0x206->0x207 (invisible to a landisk detector; arc coverage NOT established), IQ80321 break deletion (no rig), machine_type-only keying.  The fix DELIVERS WDCTL_IDS writes, it does NOT implement nIEN -- say so.  The 080056 partial codex adds nothing.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29231:     "note": "*** THE HIGHEST-YIELD SEAT OF THE PASS: it went clause by clause through the vacuity floor and found exactly ONE undischarged -- (e), the CONTRACT -- then DRAFTED both. ***  Added to both probes in-round under L1, naming what each catches and, more importantly, what each is blind to by construction.  **Recovered the round's headline artifact from /tmp before it evaporated**: the 36/36 eighteen-row run existed ONLY at `/tmp/gx494/run/RESULTS.txt`.  Nine logs are now durable under `killtable_20260830/`, each sentinel-checked before copying.  *** FOUND THE COUNTER DEFECT NO ONE ELSE COULD SEE: `grep -c ' FAIL '` MATCHES THE PROBE'S OWN VERDICT LINE *** (*SOMETHING DID NOT HOLD -- read the FAIL line*), so every nonzero count was inflated by one.  True reds are m1=2, m2=4, m3=0, m4=2, cross-checked against the independent `N of 36` figure.  **The only count that was RIGHT was the zero** -- with nothing failing there is no verdict line to miscount -- so the anomalous-looking row was the correct one, which is why it hid.  TABLE.txt corrected IN PLACE rather than re-run, because silently restating history is the laundering shape.  Confirmed **both PRETABLE-prescribed rows survived the row-list rewrite -- no #490 recurrence** -- and verified m3's mechanism in the tree rather than accepting it (0x14/0xeb is keyed on `cur_command == COMMAND_RESET && diskimage_is_a_cdrom()`, and cur_command is assigned by the caller).  Caught a LABEL COLLISION: PRETABLE's C5 uses `m4` for the width-1 experiment while TABLE.txt's `m4` is the value guard -- same label, two mutants, one round.  Required the round record disclose that **both detectors are dated-exempt from gate wiring until 2026-09-20, so the battery defends none of this today**, and that the CHANGELOG carry a WIDTH QUALIFIER so offset decode is never read as width coverage.  Disclosed its own limit unprompted: it did not rebuild or `cmp` the /tmp binaries."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29241:     "note": "*** THE ONLY SEAT TO REFUSE RATIFICATION, AND IT BUILT THE REPAIR FOR BOTH THINGS IT FOUND.  Badge A on the mutants and the proposed probe; it authored NONE of the material under review. ***  **L2 FLOOR BREACH, MEASURED.**  `relative_addr != 0 && !(idata & WDCTL_4BIT)` **escaped BOTH detectors** -- 36/36 on the main, declined on the signature.  The reason is structural rather than bad luck: the detectors' only three offset-1 values are `0x06`, `0x02` and `0x04`, **all with bit 3 clear**, so every offset-1 row sat in ONE `WDCTL_4BIT` equivalence class.  The round defended the value axis against EQUALITY guards (`m4`) and never against MASK guards.  And it is not an exotic bit: `dev_wdc.c`'s own `#481` comment says `WDCTL_4BIT` is what libata holds as its STEADY-STATE `ap->ctl`, and asserts SRST with `0x0e`.  **A mask guard re-opens the defect at exactly the values the commonest driver writes.**  Measured at `0x0e`: base 50/ff, fix 58/71, mutant 50/ff -- defect fully restored.  **It shipped a positive control with the finding** (`n5`, a nulled decode, reds at 32/36), which is what makes the three escapes mean something rather than proving the harness inert.  **THE REPAIR, BUILT AND MEASURED: a P5/P6 phase pair at `0x0e`**, 47 of 49 predicted cells -- and the two 'misses' ARE the predicted P6 red under an over-reaching decline, **so the accept row is discharged by a measured kill rather than by existing.**  Shipped in-round as a third detector rather than as extra phases, because adding rows to the main probe perturbs every mutant's score and forces a full re-table -- the cost `#462` paid.  **L4 WRONG RECORD, and the correction is a STRONGER warrant for the same decision.**  My comment said `return 0` would route to unassigned-address handling and warn per access.  **False on the path AND on the symptom.**  A REGISTERED device returning 0 never reaches that path: `memory_rw` sets `res = -1`, raises `EXCEPTION_DBE`, returns MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED, and `memory_warn_about_unimplemented_addr` has exactly one caller, AFTER the device loop.  Measured under `-v`: 19,793 `device 'wdc_altstatus' ... failed` and **ZERO** `non-existant`.  At default verbosity it is **SILENT** while the guest loops forever re-executing the faulting store -- worse than a flood, not better.  It said plainly why the false reason had to go rather than just be dropped: *a maintainer who reads 'only noisy warnings' may well return 0*.  It also refuted **`PRETABLE.out`'s own GUARD-FIELD `N/A`**: the block claimed the predicate reads no field the body writes, and `n3` is precisely a predicate reading `d->atapi_cmd_in_progress`, a field the block's own body list names.  Two of its six mutants live in the space that `N/A` declared empty.  **And a coverage gap NEITHER block names: every alt-region access in all three probes is a STORE.  The handler's READ path -- the register's primary function -- is pinned by zero rows.**  Corrected one flat claim of mine: offset 1 is the Drive Address register only at `addr_mult == 1`; on landisk (2) and macppc (0x10) it is a byte lane, because the alt region is hardcoded two bytes wide and never divides by `addr_mult` the way `DEVICE_ACCESS(wdc)` does.  The behaviour is still correct there; the claim was not.  Ruled out a `&& len == 1` mutant BY READING rather than by building: `memory_rw` truncates a len-2 access at offset 1 to len 1, so it would be equivalent.  **Disclosed its own limits unprompted**: its `n2` result rests on a table whose run its OWN guard aborted when the main loop edited `dev_wdc.c` mid-table (preserved as `wave1_TABLE_P2.txt`, ending *clone is NOT the named baseline*); the landisk/macppc defect claim is a READING with no rig; and it had frozen the instruments but not the baseline -- **the same lesson one level up** -- which it fixed from wave 2 onward."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29272:   "note": "*** COMMITTED 2026-09-12 as 599a7a8 (local only, no push).  gate 10 PASS 384 checks at the 22 pin (regress/artifacts/wdcaltdigin/killtable_20260912_closure/gate_sh_rounding_22rows.log); pass-2 receipt in regress/pass2_ledger.tsv.  Still IN WORK: the closure records (closure.by) are owed to the records round that follows #524. ***\n\n*** PASS-3 CLASS CLOSURE MEASURED -- EVERY PREDICTION HELD, INCLUDING THE ONE BUILT TO FAIL IF THE\nDERIVATION WAS WRONG.  2026-09-12. ***  Cited: regress/artifacts/wdcaltdigin/killtable_20260912_closure/SCORES.txt (+ PRETABLE.out, written first)\n\n    BASE        22/22  CONTROL=OK                         predicted 22/22\n    HEAD        17/22  all five CORE rows red             clause (a) met\n    M_DRDY      20/22  PWR_SLAVE, EMPTYSLAVE              predicted t6, k0\n    M_DRDYDRQ   21/22  PWR_SLAVE only                     <- the ninth seat's 19/19 escape, DEAD\n    M_DRQERR    21/22  SRST only                          <- the second cell, DEAD\n    E_EQUIV49   22/22  FAILING none                       predicted to SURVIVE as equivalent\n    RESTORE     cmp-vs-SEC CLEAN, binary FRESH, SENTINEL CLEARED; all six logs end in their sentinel\n\nTHE ENUMERATION, derived before the table: every `if (odata & X)` over the 15 nonzero subsets of the\nfour settable status bits, against the five MEASURED CORE statuses {0x51 0x58 0x08 0x01 0x50}.\n**12 die; 3 survive, and all 3 are EQUIVALENT** -- they differ from the fix only at 0x00 (leak ==\ndecline) or at DSC-without-DRDY, which status_byte() cannot produce.  `E_EQUIV49` was built to make\nthat falsifiable: had it died, the reachable-status set would have been wrong.\n\n*** TWO ROWS ADDED: the 0x01 cell (absent slave AT POWER-ON -- ERR alone, since d->error = 1 at\nDEVINIT and no command has run) and the 0x50 cell (post-SRST, nearly free).  22 rows, 22 REGS, exact\nbijection -- checked structurally, not assumed.  `None` now renders as `--`, never `0x00`. ***\n\nOWED: gate_sh_rounding.sh at the new 22 pin; the closure diff's own pass (the enumeration is what\nit verifies); then commit.  SOLE CARRIERS DISCLOSED: k0 is the only killer of 3 masks, t6 of 3, k1 of\n1 -- inherent to one status per cell.\n\n---\n\n*** PASS-2 L1 DISCHARGED BY A MEASURED KILL, 2026-09-11 23:33. ***  Cited: regress/artifacts/wdcaltdigin/killtable_20260911_drdy/SCORES.txt\n\n    LEG BASE      19/19  CONTROL=OK  FAILING: none\n    LEG M_DRDY    18/19  CONTROL=OK  FAILING: CORE_READ_OFF1_EMPTYSLAVE\n                         (t6 want 0x00 got 0x08 -- the leak, exactly as predicted)\n    RESTORE       cmp-vs-SEC=CLEAN  binary=FRESH  SENTINEL CLEARED (both parts)\n\nThe mutant is `odata = 0;` -> `if (odata & WDCS_DRDY) odata = 0;`, which two seats independently\npredicted would sweep the 16-row instrument 16/16.  **It now dies on exactly ONE row -- the row\nwritten for it -- and disturbs nothing else.**  A row is not discharged by existing; it is\ndischarged by reddening under its mutant, and this is that measurement.\n\nHOW THE ROW WAS BUILT, and the design took THREE attempts because the first two were vacuous:\nthe mutant only shows where DRDY is CLEAR, and every pre-existing row drives DRDY SET\n(ST_POWERON 0x51, ST_ARMED 0x58).  A no-image session gives status 0x00, where a correctly\nDECLINED 0x00 cannot be told from a LEAKED 0x00.  `WDCC_DIAGNOSE` is unreachable without an image\n(`wdc_command()` clears d->error, then detects the missing image and RETURNS BEFORE the switch).\n**What works is the ABSENT SLAVE plus an armed write: DRQ is INDEPENDENT OF IMAGE PRESENCE**, so\n`0xb0 -> wd_sdh` between t8 and t9 gives status 0x08 -- non-zero with DRDY clear.  Three rows\n(CONTROL / ACCEPT / CORE), probe 16 -> 19, gate pin moved 16 -> 19 by hand with its reason.\n\n*** A MEASUREMENT ERROR OF MINE IS RECORDED IN THE PROBE ITSELF, because it nearly shipped as two\nfalse 'MEASURED' claims. ***  `REGS` drives the register readback; a row whose register is ABSENT\nfrom it is never read and scores 0x00 -- indistinguishable from a device answering 0x00.  t4/t5/t6\nwere missing, so two placements reported 'status 0x00' and I wrote each up as a refutation of the\nDEVICE STATE.  Adding the registers took the same probe to 19/19.  **Those placements are UNTESTED,\nnot refuted**, and the probe comment says so.\n\nREMAINING BEFORE COMMIT: re-run `gate_sh_rounding.sh` against the 19-row pin.\n\n---\n\nSplit from `wdcaltoffset` (#494) DELIBERATELY, and the split is the record that matters: #494 fixed the STORE half only, because its witness holds the read path byte-identical so the aliased store is the only variable.  Shipping a read fix under that number would have claimed coverage the witness was designed not to provide.  *** THE DEFECT IS WITNESSED AND CHEAPLY SO -- WHICH REFUTES THE RULING THAT IT HAS NO ORACLE. ***  Two adjudications held the read half unfixable for want of a bit layout (bit 7 high-impedance, write-gate representation, active-low drive/head select).  The opus5 seat refuted that by measuring, and the reason is that **the test is an INVARIANCE, not a value**: `status_byte()` never reads `d->head`, and a Drive Address register is by definition a function of head select.  On the committed build, reading 0x3f7 gives 51 at head=0 AND 51 at head=15 -- invariant -- while the SDH readback of 0xaf proves the head write landed and 0x3f6 is unchanged by it, so the invariance is not `nothing moved`.  That refutes every candidate model of 0x3f7 without naming a single expected bit.  *** WHAT IS STILL MISSING IS THE FIX'S ORACLE, NOT THE DEFECT'S. ***  An invariance proves today's value WRONG without saying what is RIGHT, and none of the fifteen PDFs under `_scratchpad/` is an ATA or ISA document -- `ls _scratchpad/*.pdf` is the authority, not this note.  Pinning digin's bits from model memory is exactly the #357/#362/#365 failure this project already paid for.  AND THE OWNERSHIP QUESTION MAY DISSOLVE IT: on a PC the byte belongs to the FLOPPY controller (see `wdcaltownership`), in which case the right answer is not a wdc value at all.  Settle ownership first.  SCOPE NOTE: the model does hold the raw material -- `case wd_sdh` latches `d->sectorsize`, `d->lba`, `d->drive` and `d->head` from the stored byte -- so ACCEPT is structurally available here in a way it was NOT for the store half, where there is no discarded right answer.  ARTIFACT, added on the fable5 seat's requirement that these numbers name their log: `regress/artifacts/wdcaltoffset/mutants_20260830/TABLE_B.txt` (sentinel `TABLE_B_DONE`).  It also records a SECOND refutation the summary above omits -- a read of 0x3f7 returns 58 with DRQ raised, and **a real Drive Address register never carries DRQ**, so the read is tracking the status register on a channel independent of the head-invariance argument.\n\n*** REVIEWED 2026-09-11 (wdcaltdigin/codex_digin_20260911.txt, GPT-6 Astra at max, ZERO cyber flags).  THE LAYOUT IS NOT DERIVABLE FROM THE TREE -- which is the honest negative the brief asked for -- AND ONE OF MY FOUR OPTIONS TURNS OUT NOT TO BE AN OPTION. ***\n\n*** MY OPTION (d), 'decline the read as the write is declined', IS LITERALLY IDENTICAL TO OPTION (a), 'change nothing'. ***  The handler computes `odata = status_byte(d, cpu)' at `:617', BEFORE it looks at `relative_addr', and the common tail at `:746-747' does `if (writeflag == MEM_READ) data[0] = odata;'.  **So an ignored offset-1 read still returns the already-computed status: a handled no-op does not release the address.**  Genuine delegation to the floppy controller would need routing and registration work outside this branch.\n\n*That is the same shape as the sketch rule this project already carries -- a proposed edit is not a fix until it has been run against the enclosing function's real control flow -- reached from the other end: my option was written from the SITE and the behaviour is decided by the TAIL.*\n\n**WHAT THE TWO PERMITTED FILES DO AND DO NOT ESTABLISH:**\n  * `wdcreg.h` carries a DISABLED register-OFFSET definition and **no Drive Address BIT definitions at all** -- so the disabled block is a placeholder, not a usable layout.\n  * the bit layout, polarity and the high-impedance bit are **not derivable from this tree**.  The seat declined to supply a remembered layout and said so, which is what was asked of it.\n  * every WDC instance this file initialises registers the region TWO BYTES wide; the machine-by-machine floppy-attachment census is outside the permitted sources.\n\n**AND OPTION (b) -- return a constant `0x80` -- IS WEAKER THAN IT LOOKS:** *\"'Undefined' does not mean 'must be one'\"*, and on the active-low premise I supplied, **zero select bits ASSERT signals**, so an otherwise-zero byte is not a neutral default.  It would also still supply no head-dependent readback, which is the very thing the witness measures.\n\n**OPTION (c) IS THE ONLY ONE THAT COULD BE RIGHT, AND IT NEEDS A SPECIFICATION FIRST.**  Encouragingly, `d->head` is **not** a variable nothing trusts -- it is used for actual disk addressing and holds the last programmed head nibble -- so a drive/head readback is honest as far as it goes.  What is unknown is whether drive and head suffice for EVERY defined bit.\n\n*** DISPOSITION: HOLD, PENDING AN ATA SPECIFICATION. ***  Preserving the current behaviour while the evidence is obtained is defensible; **declaring it hardware-correct is not**, and neither is inventing a byte.  This project fetched PowerPC Book I yesterday on exactly this pattern -- a review naming the document that would settle a question, the owner approving, and the load-bearing claims verified LOCALLY afterwards -- and it converted a dispute into a decided defect in twenty minutes.  The same move is available here.\n\n**A NOTE FOR WHOEVER RESUMES THIS: if the disposition becomes (c), the existing witness INVERTS.**  It currently passes by showing INVARIANCE across head select (51 at head=0 and head=15).  Under (c) invariance becomes the FAILURE condition, so the round owes a positive oracle asserting the specified encoding -- and a row that merely shows the value CHANGED would be satisfied by any head-dependent garbage.\n\n*** SETTLED 2026-09-11 BY FETCHING ATA-2, AND IT REFUTES ALL FOUR OF MY OPTIONS INCLUDING THE ONE THE REVIEW CALLED THE ONLY DEFENSIBLE ONE. ***\n\n`_scratchpad/ata2_x3t10_948d.pdf` / `.txt` -- **Working Draft X3T10/0948D, \"Information Technology -- AT Attachment Interface with Extensions (ATA-2)\"**, 409 KB -> 314 KB of text, 5,909 lines.  Fetched with the owner's approval on the Book I / vmesbcp2 / 21066 / ddi0406 precedent and verified locally.  *(`file` says \"6 page(s)\" and is wrong again -- third manual in a row.)*\n\n**A GREP FOR \"Drive Address\" RETURNS ZERO, AND THAT IS NOT AN ABSENCE OF EVIDENCE.**  The register is in the Control Block map by its ADDRESS, not by that name (`:1483-1495`):\n\n        CS0-  CS1-   DA2 DA1 DA0        READ (DIOR-)          WRITE (DIOW-)\n        N     A       1   1   0         Alternate status      Device control\n        N     A       1   1   1         (Note 1)              Not Used\n\n*Having been bitten yesterday by a grep-only negative, I looked for the register by ADDRESS after the name returned nothing -- which is what found it.*\n\n*** NOTE 1, `:1529-1531`, IS THE WHOLE ANSWER: ***\n\n    \"This register is obsolete.  It is recommended that a device not respond to a read of this\n     address.  If a device does respond, it shall be sure not to drive the DD7 signal to prevent\n     possible conflict with floppy disk implementations.\"\n\n**SO THE DISPOSITION IS NOT TO ANSWER AT ALL**, and every option I put to the review is wrong:\n  * **(a) keep the status alias** and **(d) decline-in-place** -- identical to each other, as the review found, and both RESPOND where the standard recommends not responding;\n  * **(b) return a constant** -- responds, and invents a value the standard declines to specify;\n  * *** (c) MODEL DRIVE AND HEAD FAITHFULLY -- which the review called the only disposition that could be right -- IS REFUTED OUTRIGHT. ***  It would implement, carefully, a register the standard marks OBSOLETE and tells devices not to answer.  **The review could not have known this: it was confined to two files that do not contain the layout, and it said so.**\n\n*** AND THE MODEL SATISFIES THE DD7 CONSTRAINT ONLY BY ACCIDENT, WHICH IS A LATENT HAZARD WORTH ITS OWN SENTENCE. ***  `WDCS_BSY` is `0x80` -- exactly DD7 -- and `status_byte()` never sets it, assembling only DRDY, DSC, DRQ and ERR.  So today's alias happens to leave bit 7 clear and happens not to conflict with a floppy controller.  **Nothing enforces that.**  An edit that made `status_byte()` report BSY -- an entirely reasonable thing for a future round to do -- would start driving DD7 at an address the standard reserves against floppy conflict, and no row anywhere would notice.\n\n**WHAT THE ROUND NOW OWES, and it is a design question rather than a documentation one:** what \"not respond\" MEANS in this emulator.  Returning 0 from a device access function is not silence -- `memory_rw.c` converts it into an architectural exception on MIPS and M88K -- so the round must choose between an unanswered access, an open-bus value, and leaving the region registered one byte narrower so the address is never claimed.  **The narrowing option is the one that matches the standard's language most closely and is the only one that also fixes the write half by construction.**\n\n**THE WITNESS SURVIVES AND ITS ORACLE INVERTS THE OTHER WAY FROM WHAT I EXPECTED.**  I noted above that under (c) the invariance test would become a failure condition.  Under the real disposition the invariance is still not the point: the row must assert that the address is NOT ANSWERED with a status byte, and a new row should pin **bit 7 clear** so the accidental compliance above becomes an enforced one.\n\n*** THE IMPLEMENTATION IS DESIGNED AND IT IS ONE NUMBER: register `wdc_altstatus` with length **1 INSTEAD OF 2**.  2026-09-11 (wdcaltdigin/codex_impl_RETRY_20260911.txt). ***\n\n**THE OTHER THREE CANDIDATES ALL FAIL, and for different reasons worth keeping:**\n  * **(b) keep two bytes, return `0` at offset 1** -- *\"It implements a FAILED MAPPED ACCESS.  It cannot be identified with ATA nonresponse without a justified bus translation.\"*  WDC still claims the address; the guest gets an architectural exception on MIPS/M88K, which asserts something about the hardware that nothing establishes.\n  * **(c) keep two bytes, answer with a constant leaving DD7 clear** -- *\"No.  WDC still supplies a SUCCESSFUL response.\"*  It removes the status alias and preserves the thing Note 1 actually recommends against: answering at all.\n  * **(d) move the `status_byte()` call inside the offset-0 branch** -- leaves offset 1 answering the initialised zero, which is still a response.\n\n**EVERY ATTACHING MACHINE REGISTERS TWO BYTES TODAY** -- `MACHINE_MACPPC` at `addr + 0x160`, `MACHINE_HPCMIPS` at `0x14000386` and via its default formula, `MACHINE_IQ80321` at `addr + 0x402` -- and **no alternate-register functionality needs the second byte**: it supplies only the read alias and the guarded write, and the header already compiles `wd_digin` out.\n\n*** THE HONEST LIMIT, STATED BY THE SEAT AND KEPT HERE: narrowing removes WDC's CLAIM to the address, but what the COMPLETE MACHINE then does at an unclaimed address is not established by the permitted files. ***  That is a measurement, not a reading -- and it may differ by architecture, since `memory_rw.c` delivers a failed-device exception on MIPS and M88K only.  **The round owes that measurement on each attaching machine before it can claim 'does not respond' rather than merely 'WDC no longer answers'.**\n\n*Note the pleasing consequence if it holds: narrowing fixes the WRITE half by construction too, since an unclaimed address cannot carry a guarded write -- so the earlier write-side fix becomes redundant rather than wrong, and the round should say which it is.*\n\n*** OWNER RULING 2026-09-11: NARROW THE REGISTRATION. ***  Put to the owner as three options (narrow / open-bus constant / hold-plus-DD7-guard); the owner chose NARROW.  The site is `dev_wdc.c:2028-2029`, a literal `2` -- **and a harvest seat verified it is a literal `2 -> 1`, NOT `1 * addr_mult`**: the alt callback never divides its offset by `addr_mult`, and all five machine cases at `:2003-2018` change the ADDRESS only, never the length.\n\n*** MY OWN PREMISE IN THE OPTION SET WAS WRONG, AND I CORRECT IT HERE BEFORE IT IS ACTED ON. ***  I told the owner narrowing gives `MIPS/M88K: architectural bus fault`.  **It does not.**  I carried the carrier's TRUE statement about the REGISTERED-device `res <= 0` path (`memory_rw.c:603-611`, which does raise EXCEPTION_DBE) into the UNMAPPED path, which does not share it.  Read at `memory_rw.c:676-712`:\n  * an unmapped READ does `memset(data, 0, len)` and returns ZEROES;\n  * an unmapped WRITE falls straight to `do_return_ok`, silently ignored -- `:709-710` literally asks *\"Shouldn't there be a DBE exception for invalid writes as well?  TODO\"*;\n  * the MIPS `EXCEPTION_DBE` block at `:698-704` is inside `#if 0` -- **disabled**;\n  * `memory_warn_about_unimplemented_addr` (`core/memory.c:598-606`) returns false outright for `paddr < physical_max + 0x40000`, so there is no warning flood from an I/O-range address.\n\n**So narrowing is SAFER than I represented it, not riskier** -- no fault, no infinite loop, no flood. This is the fifth instance of the scope-transfer class in two days, and the first where the wrong address was carried into a question put to the OWNER.\n\n**THE `#494` MEASUREMENT DOES NOT TRANSFER EITHER, AND A SEAT REFUSED TO LET IT.**  The comment at `dev_wdc.c:655-665` records 19,793 measured `device 'wdc_altstatus' ... failed` lines and a guest looping forever in silence.  That is a REGISTERED device returning 0 -- the `res <= 0` path -- not the unassigned path narrowing produces.  The seat declined to fill the gap with a guessed per-architecture matrix and said the permitted files do not establish narrowing is safe on **every** attaching machine (MACPPC, IQ80321, HPCMIPS unverified).  *That refusal is the correct form and the residual is real: the generic path is now verified by reading, the per-machine claim is not.*\n\n*** TWO OBLIGATIONS THE NARROWING CREATES, BOTH FOUND BY THE SEAT AND NEITHER IN MY OPTION SET: ***\n  1. **A SPANNING ACCESS IS UNRESOLVED.**  The callback assigns only `data[0]`.  Nothing in the permitted files establishes whether a transaction starting at `A` and spanning the new one-byte boundary is SPLIT, ACCEPTED or REJECTED.  A 16-bit access at the ALTSTATUS address is the obvious guest shape, so **the byte-access argument for narrowing is not proof about those transactions** and this must be measured before the edit ships.\n  2. **THE DD7 ROW I PROPOSED WOULD BE VACUOUS AFTER NARROWING, AND ITS PREMISE IS WRONG ANYWAY.**  *Driving DD7 low is still driving DD7* -- bit-7-clear is not the electrical equivalent of leaving it undriven.  Once the device never answers, a conditional *\"if WDC answers, bit 7 is clear\"* row can never fire, and an UNCONDITIONAL assertion about the guest's value is simply wrong: another responder or the unassigned value may legitimately set it.  **The replacement oracle is ZERO WDC CONTRIBUTION, PROVEN BY ROUTING** -- address, direction and width observed at dispatch.\n\nKEEP the existing write-side guard as defence against a future wider registration, and correct its comment: the ATA-2 draft's write entry for this address is **\"Not Used\"**, not \"read-only\".\n\n*** 2026-09-11 -- SPANNING ACCESS: SAFE.  THE BLOCKER ON THE OWNER-RULED NARROWING IS CLEARED, AND THE EDIT ADDITIONALLY RETIRES A FILED DEFECT BY CONSTRUCTION. ***\n\nThe dispatcher ACCEPTS a wide access whole against the shorter region and CLAMPS it: `memory_rw.c:495-524`.  The `#95` pre-zero at `:491-492` runs first with the caller's FULL `len`, so the guest-visible buffer is byte-for-byte `{status, 0...}` before and after the change.  **No out-of-bounds read or write in either direction.**\n\n*** AND THE SEAT CORRECTED ITS OWN FIRST ANSWER, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. ***  It had called a wide access THEORETICAL.  It is reachable in-tree: `dev_ps2_spd.c:73-77`, `case 0x5c` (aux control), relays the guest's **own `len`, unmodified**, to `d->wdcaddr + 0x206` -- and the chain is live, not `#if 0`'d (`:44` `DEV_PS2_SPD_LENGTH 0x800`; `:103` `d->wdcaddr = devinit->addr + DEV_PS2_SPD_LENGTH`; `:111-113` auto-adds the wdc; `machine_playstation2.c:106` adds `ps2_spd addr=0x14000000`).  `MACHINE_PLAYSTATION2` is absent from `dev_wdc.c:2006-2018`'s switch, so the default arm puts `alt_status_addr` at exactly the address `case 0x5c` targets.\n\n**That STRENGTHENS the verdict rather than weakening it.**  The safety never depended on nobody issuing a wide access -- it follows from the clamp plus the handler touching only `data[0]`.  *A reachable path that is behaviourally identical is a better answer than an unreachable one.*  And it is likely rather than merely possible: the sibling arm at `:61-71` maps the command block at **2-byte spacing**, the signature of a 16-bit-only ATA host.\n\n*** THE NARROWING CLOSES `wdcaltwidth` BY ELIMINATION. ***  With `length == 1` the found-test forces `relative_addr == 0`, and the clamp then forces `len = 1` for every `len >= 2`, so **the handler can never again be entered with `len != 1`.**  `wdcaltwidth`'s named mutant (`idata = data[0]` -> `data[len - 1]`, which *\"escapes all 103 rows\"*) becomes EQUIVALENT once `len == 1`: `data[len-1]` IS `data[0]`.  **The class is dead, not merely uncovered.**  Two standing self-declared blind spots go with it -- `wdc_altoffset_probe.py:114-115` and `CHANGELOG.md:8617-8620` both describe a `len == 2` store at offset 0 that the narrowing makes unreachable.\n\n**PER THE ABSENCE-RECORD RULE, THE WARRANT IS NAMED: *the clamp at `memory_rw.c:523` forces `len == 1` for a one-byte region.*  Any future edit restoring the region's width MUST re-run the death measurement.**  This is closure by ELIMINATION, not by coverage, so the three overtaken-closure conditions do not apply -- but it does supersede the `ps2_spd` exposure, which is the one live consumer of the width the edit removes.\n\nTWO REFINEMENTS TO MY OWN CORRECTION, both accepted:\n  * the unmapped path is silent about DATA but **not about LOGGING** -- `memory_rw.c:676-679` calls `memory_warn_about_unimplemented_addr()`, which prints at `VERBOSITY_WARNING` while the default is `VERBOSITY_INFO`.  *(The suppression I cited, `core/memory.c:605`, applies below `physical_max + 0x40000`; whether this address qualifies is machine-dependent and is NOT settled here.)*  The `EXCEPTION_DBE` at `:701-703` is confirmed dead (`#if 0` at `:685`).\n  * **the safety is CONDITIONAL ON THE HANDLER'S SHAPE.**  It holds because `wdc_altstatus` touches only `data[0]` (`dev_wdc.c:614`, `:746-747`).  The sibling `DEVICE_ACCESS(wdc)` DOES inspect width and latches warnings at `:1093` and `:1968` -- **if anyone brings `wdc_altstatus` into line with that idiom, re-derive this.**\n\nNO SHIPPED DETECTOR ROW CHANGES COLOUR: `killtable_20260830/PRETABLE.out:49-54` already measured width-1 *\"green on every row\"*.  No oracle re-pin owed -- and equally, **no coverage for the change**, which is what the round still owes.\n\n**STATUS: the edit is a literal `2` -> `1` at `dev_wdc.c:2029`, owner-ruled, blocker cleared.  It owes a panel pass 1 on the actual diff and a detector row before it lands** -- the disposition being ruled does not discharge the lifecycle.\n\n*** `wdcaltdigin` PASS 1, 2026-09-11 (codex GPT-6 Astra at max, 15,433 b, complete).  **APPROVED** as a withdrawal of the WDC auxiliary decode at offset 1 -- and two of my premises are refuted, one of them the round's planned oracle. ***\n\n*** THE WITNESS DOES NOT INVERT -- INVARIANCE PASSES BOTH VERSIONS. ***  I had recorded that the existing head-invariance witness INVERTS under the fix.  It does not:\n\n    observable                     two-byte (now)   one-byte (fixed)\n    A+1 read, head 0                    0x51             0x00\n    A+1 read, head 15                   0x51             0x00\n    SDH readback after head 15          0xaf             0xaf\n    EQUALITY ACROSS HEADS               True             True\n\n> **Equality across heads still passes BOTH versions, so invariance alone cannot detect restoration of the two-byte registration.**  An exact `0x51` alias witness would simply stop observing the alias.  **SDH readback establishes that the head write landed in the main bank; it does NOT establish who answered `A+1`.**\n\n**So the acceptance criterion must be replaced with ROUTING EXCLUSION**, and head selection kept only as historical context or a control.  *With another responder present, even post-fix head invariance is not an appropriate requirement at all.*\n\n*** AND I ATTRIBUTED THAT WITNESS TO THE WRONG FILE. ***  `regress/wdc_altoffset_probe.py:113` states explicitly that **no row reads either alternate-region offset**; it contains offset-1 WRITE tests.  The seat declined to authenticate the head-select measurements from it and **refused to infer another filename from the defect id** -- which is precisely the discipline my own `dev_crime.c` error broke four hours earlier, applied back to me.\n\n**THE DD7 ORACLE IS VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION, with the reason made exact:** `status_byte()` adds only DRDY, DSC, DRQ and ERR, **so its aliased result ALREADY has bit 7 clear.  A bit-7-clear detector would accept the current behaviour.**\n\n**AND THE ANSWER TO \"IS RETURNING ZERO A RESPONSE?\" IS THE RIGHT FRAMING:** *\"The emulator completes a read with a numeric result; that does not mean WDC supplied that result.  Removing WDC's address claim is an honest implementation of device NONPARTICIPATION at this emulator's decoding abstraction.  **It does not simulate electrical high impedance.**\"*  The round may claim adoption of a draft RECOMMENDATION with the abstraction stated -- no more.\n\n=== SEVEN DETECTOR ROWS PROPOSED, each with its named mutant and the observable that differs ===\n`A1_READ_WDC_UNCLAIMED` and `A1_WRITE_WDC_UNCLAIMED` against `M_REWIDEN` (callback count 0 -> 1; **WDC state alone would miss the write mutant, since offset-1 write handling is already a no-op**); `A0_READ_ALTERNATE_STATUS` against suppression and against a constant `0x51`; `A0_WRITE_SRST_SIGNATURE` against `M_OMIT_COMMAND_RESET`; two existing P1 accept rows reused.\n\n**THE ROW I HAD NOT CONSIDERED, and it is the sharpest: `A0_READ_PRESERVES_PENDING_IRQ`.**  ATA-2 `:1535` distinguishes Alternate Status from Status precisely in that it does NOT clear a pending interrupt -- and the normal Status handler **does** clear it at `dev_wdc.c:1938`.  So a mutant adding that clearing action to the Alternate Status path is invisible to any status-byte-only oracle: **the returned bytes need not differ at all.**\n\n*** TWO DETECTOR-PACKAGING DEFECTS IN THE SHIPPED PROBE, AND THE SECOND IS A CHECK THAT CANNOT FAIL: ***\n  1. **The existing probe's \"committed\" expectations describe the OLDER offset-1 write-reset defect**, which today's callback already rejects at `dev_wdc.c:622`.  **Reusing its unchanged baseline for this new before/after comparison would MIS-SCORE THE BASELINE.**\n  2. **`ALTOFF_DET_DONE` is emitted UNCONDITIONALLY, and failed comparisons produce no failing exit status** (`wdc_altoffset_probe.py:682`).  **So neither that sentinel nor ordinary process completion is evidence of detector acceptance.**  *This project's own rule is that a preserved log is verified by its completion sentinel -- and here is a sentinel that says DONE whether or not the rows passed.  Filed as its own finding.*\n\n**THE WIDTH RETIREMENT STANDS WITH A SHARPER WARRANT:** `memory_rw.c:492` zeroes the incoming read length **before** the clamp at `:523`, the callback uses the first byte, so a wide read leaves `{status, 0...}` and a wide write retains its first-byte effect.  After narrowing, every positive-length access reaches the callback at offset zero with `len == 1`, so `data[0]` and `data[len - 1]` are equivalent there.  **`:523` is the correct warrant** -- *and it does NOT establish that wide guest accesses disappeared, nor that all width behaviour is hardware-accurate.*\n\n*Premise correction: the initializer carries **three** machine-specific case labels, not the five I implied.  No all-machine clearance is establishable from the permitted files.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 -- **I NEARLY FILED FIFTEEN PROBES AS DEFECTIVE AND THE DESIGN IS SOUND**.  A NEAR-MISS, RECORDED BECAUSE THE CENSUS LOOKED AUTHORITATIVE AND WAS MEASURING THE WRONG PROPERTY. ***\n\nA pass-1 seat observed, correctly, that `wdc_altoffset_probe.py` emits `ALTOFF_DET_DONE` **unconditionally** and that *\"neither that token nor ordinary process completion is sufficient evidence of detector acceptance.\"*  I verified both halves: the sentinel is outside any conditional, and **there is no `sys.exit` anywhere in the file**, so the process always exits 0 however many rows failed.\n\nI then censused all 93 probes in `regress/`: **15 emit a completion sentinel and never call `sys.exit`.**  That reads like a fifteen-instance defect class.\n\n*** IT IS NOT A DEFECT.  I CHECKED WHAT ACTUALLY CONSUMES THEM BEFORE FILING, AND THE GATE DISCARDS `rc` ON PURPOSE. ***  `gate_arm.sh` runs the probe as `... > \"$SGILOG\" 2>&1 || true`, then:\n  1. **SENTINEL FIRST**, with the reason in its own comment -- *\"a preserved log is verified by its completion sentinel, never by its existence: a killed run loses every row and looks exactly like a run that produced none\"*;\n  2. **CONTROLS BY NAME, not by count** -- *\"a green-row total lets a control go red while the sum stays acceptable, and a run whose controls died is an APPARATUS failure, which must not be read as a wall of product failures\"*;\n  3. **PER-ROW PASS, checked individually** rather than by a total, explicitly against the padded-column class;\n  4. **THE DENOMINATOR PINNED TO A LITERAL**, so a detector that loses rows cannot pass by shrinking.\n\n> **So the sentinel's contract is COMPLETION, not ACCEPTANCE, and it discharges that correctly.  Pass/fail comes from per-row content checks the gate performs itself.  The seat's sentence is literally true and describes the DESIGN rather than a defect.**\n\n**MY CENSUS KEYED ON A CORRELATE -- the absence of `sys.exit` -- INSTEAD OF ON THE CLASS: whether pass/fail is OBSERVABLE TO THE CONSUMER.**  It is, everywhere it matters, through a channel my pattern could not see.  *That is the deriver lesson for the third time today: **before writing a deriver, ask what makes a member a member** -- and if the answer is a property the regex does not test, the error is invisible because the output is still a plausible number.*\n\n*Recorded as a near-miss rather than a finding because the cost of the other outcome was real: fifteen phantom defects filed against working probes, each of which a future round would have had to disprove.  What stopped it was the two-minute check this project already mandates -- **open the consumer and ask what it does when the thing is FALSE** -- and the answer was that the consumer never asked the question I was auditing.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 ASSESS (main loop, read from source at HEAD).  **#494 CLOSED THE WRITE CELL AND LEFT THE READ CELL OPEN IN THE SAME `if` -- so this is not an independent defect, it is the SYMMETRIC CELL of a repair this project already shipped, which is the `#499` shape the carrier names by its own number.** ***\n\n**THE HANDLER, `DEVICE_ACCESS(wdc_altstatus)` at `dev_wdc.c:609-675`, in the order it executes:**\n  * `:614`  `idata = data[0];`\n  * `:617`  `odata = status_byte(d, cpu);`  -- **UNCONDITIONAL.  The offset is not consulted.**\n  * `:619`  `if (writeflag == MEM_READ)` -> `debug(\"[ wdc: read from ALTSTATUS ... \")` and nothing else; the common tail then assigns `odata` into the caller's buffer.\n  * `:622`  `else if (relative_addr != 0)` -> the **`#494`** arm: a WRITE to offset 1 is declined silently and the whole action is refused.\n  * `:673`  `else` -> the genuine Alt Ctrl write path (`#481`'s `WDCTL_RST`).\n\n> *** THE READ PATH NEVER DECODES ITS OFFSET AT ALL.  `relative_addr` is tested in exactly ONE place, and that place is inside the `else` of `writeflag == MEM_READ`. ***\n\n**So a READ of offset 1 -- the Drive Address register, `0x3f7` on the PC layout -- returns the ALT-STATUS byte**, which is this row's filed claim, now confirmed from the source rather than asserted.\n\n=== THE CELL SPACE, AND IT IS THE WHOLE FINDING ===\nThe function's own inputs give a two-by-two: **{READ, WRITE} x {offset 0, offset 1}.**\n\n| cell | disposition at HEAD |\n|---|---|\n| WRITE, offset 0 | Alt Ctrl, correct (`#481`) |\n| WRITE, offset 1 | **CLOSED by `#494`** -- declined silently, whole action refused |\n| READ,  offset 0 | alt-status, correct |\n| READ,  offset 1 | *** OPEN -- returns the alt-status byte *** |\n\n**`#494` repaired the cell its mutant used and left the symmetric one.**  The carrier states this class in its own words -- *'the original breach, reproduced inside its own repair, by someone who had just spent an hour understanding it'* -- and prescribes the remedy: **when a class recurs, enumerate the full space from the function's own inputs and close every cell in ONE diff, then write into the contract which cells are covered.**  Four cells; three closed; one diff owed.\n\n*** AND `#494`'S OWN COMMENT CARRIES A MEASUREMENT THAT BEARS DIRECTLY ON THE REPAIR CHOICE, which is why it must be read before this round picks a disposition. ***  At `:655-665` it records, correcting an earlier wrong reason in place:\n\n> *'THE ACCESS STAYS HANDLED (`return 1`) ... this device is REGISTERED, so a 0 never reaches [unassigned-address] handling.  `memory_rw` sets `res = -1`, takes its `res <= 0` branch, and raises `EXCEPTION_DBE` -- measured, **19793 `device 'wdc_altstatus' ... failed` lines and ZERO `non-existant` ones** under `-v`.  The guest then loops forever re-executing the faulting store, and at DEFAULT verbosity it does so in SILENCE.'*\n\n**So `return 0` FROM THIS REGISTERED DEVICE IS MEASURED TO HANG THE GUEST SILENTLY.**  That is a fact about a *registered* device returning 0 -- it is NOT the same thing as narrowing the registration, which makes the address unmapped and takes an entirely different path.  *The two dispositions are easy to conflate and the tree has a measurement distinguishing them; it would be a wrong record to cite that 19793 against a narrowing.*\n\n=== THE OPTION SET FOR THE OPEN CELL, with what each actually does here ===\n  * **NARROW THE REGISTRATION** (`dev_wdc.c:2028-2029`, the literal `2` -> `1`) -- closes BOTH offset-1 cells structurally, and the handler's `relative_addr != 0` arm becomes dead code that must then be dispositioned rather than left as a comment describing a case that can no longer arrive.  **Changes 0x3f7 from HANDLED-AND-DECLINED to UNMAPPED**, so the `#494` measurement above does not transfer and the unmapped path must be measured on its own.\n  * **DECODE THE READ** -- mirror `#494` on the read side inside the handler, leaving the registration alone.  Smallest diff; keeps the address owned by wdc; makes the two-by-two symmetric.\n  * **BACKED ACCEPT / IMPLEMENT** -- model the Drive Address register.  **Refused by the tree's own evidence**: `wdcreg.h` compiles `wd_digin` out under an `#if 0` whose note reads *NOT MAPPED; fd uses this register on PCs*, and `dev_fdc.c`'s `DEV_FDC_LENGTH` carries *TODO 8, but collision with wdc*.  **The tree documents the collision from BOTH sides and resolves it from neither** -- which is [[wdcaltownership]], and it is a different question from this one.\n\n*** THE ORDERING CONSTRAINT, so a round does not ship the two in the wrong sequence: NARROWING SUBSUMES DECODING, and decoding first then narrowing leaves dead code behind. ***  [[wdcaltwidth]] is coupled to the same literal -- the region is registered two bytes wide while every existing row uses `len == 1`, so a narrowing also settles that row's unpinned width, and any detector written here should pin the width deliberately rather than inherit it.\n\n**STATUS: specified, NOT implemented.**  No edit made.  A build and a measurement are owed and could not be taken in this window -- a 24-leg regression tail holds the machine and this project's gates carry load-sensitive wall-clock oracles, so a measurement taken now would be worth nothing.  *The disposition question is put to the owner rather than assumed, because 'narrow the registration' and 'FAULT -- return 0' are different actions here and the tree has a measurement showing the second one hangs the guest.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 DESIGN (codex GPT-6 Astra at max, 23,793 b, 0 cyber flags) + **OWNER RULING: DECODE THE READ, registration untouched.** ***\n\n*VERDICT: The four-cell table is correct.  The registered-device fault is live, but its measurement does not establish narrowing behavior.*\n\n**MY CELL TABLE SURVIVED THE SOURCE CHECK** -- the read path does not decode its offset, and the open cell is (READ, offset 1).  **And the transferability question I raised was answered the way I suspected: `#494`'s measured 19793 failed-access lines describe a REGISTERED device returning 0, and do NOT establish what a NARROWED registration would do.**  *Citing that measurement against a narrowing would have been a wrong record, which is why it was asked rather than assumed.*\n\n*** AND IT CAUGHT MY THIRD INVENTED PATH OF THE DAY. ***  The brief listed `src/include/wdcreg.h`; the seat reported *'The system cannot find the file specified'*, **did not guess a replacement** (as the brief instructed), and scoped its answer to the three files it could open.  The real path is **`src/include/thirdparty/wdcreg.h`**.  *Three invented paths in one session -- `dev_crime.c`, `regress/sh_tlb_probe.py`, and now this -- each caught by a seat that refused to substitute a neighbour.  The brief rule that produced the catch is the one that says VERIFY EVERY PERMITTED PATH EXISTS, and I verified four of five.*\n\n=== THE REPAIR, AS THE SEAT WROTE IT ===\n\nDecline the offset-1 read, return byte **`0x00`**, retain handler return value **1**, keep the unconditional status calculation and the common tail:\n\n    if (writeflag == MEM_READ) {\n        if (relative_addr != 0) {\n            odata = 0;\n        } else {\n            debug(\"[ wdc: read from ALTSTATUS: 0x%02x ]\" ..., (int)odata);\n        }\n    } else if (relative_addr != 0) {\n        /*  Existing #494 write-decline arm.  */\n\nThe Alt Ctrl arm follows unchanged.\n\n**ZERO'S WARRANT IS NAMED AND ITS LIMIT IS NAMED WITH IT** -- the emulator's deterministic declined-read convention: `#95` pre-zeroes registered-device read buffers and the ordinary unimplemented-address path also chooses zero.  *** 'That is an emulator policy, not proof that a real Drive Address or floppy register must read zero.' ***  *An honest warrant with its scope stated, rather than a hardware claim the files cannot support -- which is the distinction [[wdcaltownership]] turns on.*\n\n=== *** THE ORACLE TRAP, AND IT IS THE SAME SHAPE AS TONIGHT'S n6 FINDING *** ===\n\nThe clause-(d) mutant is **B-null-effect**: keep `if (relative_addr != 0)` and its branch, but remove `odata = 0`, so offset-1 reads return the status byte S again.\n\n> *** A DECLINED READ MUST USE A FIXTURE WITH **S != 0** TO KILL THAT MUTANT.  IF S IS ZERO, THE FIXED AND THE BROKEN IMPLEMENTATIONS RETURN THE SAME BYTE. ***\n\n**That is a check that cannot fail, identified before any row is written** -- the second one caught at design time tonight, after `n6`'s `r7 == 0` row would have been vacuous against a mask-returning variant for exactly the same reason (*the mask would also be zero*).  *Two independent rounds, same failure shape: an oracle asserting ZERO is worthless unless the defective path is shown to produce NON-zero.*\n\n**AND THE CONTROL IS ESTABLISHABLE, which is why this is a constraint rather than a blocker:** **disklessness does NOT imply zero status** -- initialisation sets `d->error = 1` at `dev_wdc.c:1994`, and `status_byte()` includes `WDCS_ERR` when `d->error` is set at `:594-595`.  *So a diskless fixture is a legitimate null medium AND can carry a nonzero S; the round must establish that explicitly rather than assume it.*\n\n**THE SECOND NULL-MEDIUM MEANING NEEDS ITS OWN REJECTION:** a null test vehicle that returns zero for every read without exercising wdc at all **is accepted by an offset-1-zero assertion alone**.  The detector must reject it through a **legal offset-0 read expected to return nonzero S**.\n\n*** A NOTE ON THE ROAD NOT TAKEN, recorded because the reasoning is reusable: for option A (narrowing) the seat observed that `2 -> 1` HAS NO GUARD-AND-EFFECT PAIR, so 'changing 1 back to 2' is REVERSION, not a clause-(d) mutation. ***  Its structural analogue would be **A-null-exclusion** -- keep the one-byte registration but add a SECOND one-byte registration of the released address back to this handler, defeating the exclusion while the original line still reads `1`.  **Checking only that the registration still says `1` would miss it.**  *Filed as reasoning for whoever eventually takes [[wdcaltwidth]]; not this round's problem.*\n\n**STATUS: specified and RULED.  Implementation, witness and kill table are the next unit of work.**  The `#519` regression tail has now cleared the machine, so measurement is available.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 WITNESS + KILL TABLE (agy 3.8, measuring seat).  **WITNESS BUILT AND TABLE RUN: HEAD 14/16, FIX 16/16, AND S WAS MEASURED NONZERO (0x51 / 0x58) -- so the oracle trap the design pass predicted was real and is discharged by measurement rather than by assertion.** ***\n\n**THE FIXTURE ESTABLISHES NONZERO S EXPLICITLY, which is the whole reason this round needed care.**  The clause-(d) mutant `M_NULL_EFFECT` keeps `if (relative_addr != 0)` and deletes `odata = 0`, so offset-1 reads return the status byte again -- **and if S were 0 the fixed and broken builds would return the identical byte and the row would be vacuous.**  Measured:\n  * at devinit `dev_wdc_init()` sets `d->error = 1`, so with a disk attached `status_byte()` gives `WDCS_DRDY | WDCS_DSC | WDCS_ERR` = `0x40 | 0x10 | 0x01` = **`S = 0x51`**;\n  * armed by `WDCC_WRITE` (clearing error, setting `write_in_progress`): `WDCS_DRDY | WDCS_DSC | WDCS_DRQ` = **`S_armed = 0x58`**.\n**Both values are printed in the log rather than asserted** -- so the discrimination is visible in the artifact, not just claimed in a report.\n\n=== SCORES -- AND THE SCORES ALONE ARE MISLEADING, WHICH IS THE FINDING ===\n*(`regress/artifacts/wdcaltdigin/killtable_20260911/`, `KILLTABLE_DONE` present; each score read from its own `probe_<leg>.raw`)*\n\n| leg | score | WHICH rows failed |\n|---|---|---|\n| `BASE` (fix) | **16/16** | none |\n| `HEAD` (pre-fix) | 14/16 | `CORE_READ_OFF1_POWERON` (want 0x00, got **0x51**), `CORE_READ_OFF1_ARMED` (want 0x00, got **0x58**) |\n| `M_NULL_EFFECT` (clause d) | 14/16 | the SAME two core rows -- **killed by the nonzero S** |\n| `M_REINSTATE` (clause b) | 14/16 | the same two core rows |\n| `M_NULL_MEDIUM` (clause c) | 14/16 | *** A DIFFERENT PAIR: `ACCEPT_READ_OFF0_POWERON` (want 0x51, got 0x00) and `ACCEPT_READ_OFF0_ARMED` (want 0x58, got 0x00) *** |\n| `RESTORED` | 14/16 | = HEAD -- the clean-restore proof |\n\n> *** ALL FOUR MUTANTS SCORE 14/16, AND `SCORES.txt` ALONE THEREFORE SAYS NOTHING DISTINGUISHES THEM.  OPENING THE RAW LOGS SHOWS THE NULL MEDIUM FAILS AN ENTIRELY DIFFERENT PAIR OF ROWS. ***\n\n*That is this project's own 'a count check cannot see the list landing in the wrong section' class, arriving in a kill table: **the numerator is identical and the discrimination lives entirely in the ROW IDENTITY.**  A record citing `14/16` four times would be true, useless, and would read as a detector that cannot tell a null medium from the shipped defect.*  **So the row identity, not the score, is what this record cites** -- and a future reader checking this round should read `probe_M_NULL_MEDIUM.raw`, not `SCORES.txt`.\n\n**THE DUAL REJECTION WORKS AS DESIGNED.**  A null vehicle returning 0 for every read without exercising wdc at all would pass an offset-1-zero assertion by itself; it fails BOTH accept rows (`ACCEPT_READ_OFF0_*` want 0x51 and 0x58), which is exactly the second null-medium meaning the design pass said needed its own rejection.\n\n**WRITE CELLS UNREGRESSED**, which the brief made mandatory: `#481`'s (WRITE, offset 0) SRST path and `#494`'s (WRITE, offset 1) decline both pass on every leg.  *A repair that fixed the read cell and perturbed a write cell would be worse than the defect.*\n\n**ORDER PROVEN BY MTIME:** `PRETABLE.out` 09:44:27, first table run `probe_BASE.raw` 09:45:18 -- 51 s earlier, so the pre-table block cannot have been fitted to the answers.  Two-part restoration verified in the scratch clone `/tmp/gxaltdigin_clone`.\n\n*** DISCLOSED UNDER CLAUSE (e): `M_NULL_EFFECT` AND `M_REINSTATE` ARE INDISTINGUISHABLE TO THIS INSTRUMENT *** -- same score, same rows, same observed bytes.  That is expected (both restore the pre-fix behaviour at the read cell) and it is stated rather than left for a later pass to discover.\n\n**STATUS: the fix is MEASURED BUT NOT APPLIED TO SEC.**  Owed: the edit itself, propagation into the build trees, a pass 2 on the real diff, and the targeted gate.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 IMPLEMENTED.  **FIX APPLIED TO SEC AS `#525` -- THE FOURTH CELL IS CLOSED.** ***\n\n**Round `#525`** -- `#523` is staked by the `wdcerrfamily` design artifact and `#524` was taken by [[armbdt]] an hour earlier, both checked before claiming.\n\n**THE TWO-BY-TWO IS NOW COMPLETE:**\n\n| cell | disposition |\n|---|---|\n| WRITE, offset 0 | Alt Ctrl / `WDCTL_RST` (`#481`) |\n| WRITE, offset 1 | declined silently (`#494`) |\n| READ,  offset 0 | alt-status, unchanged |\n| READ,  offset 1 | **`odata = 0`, declined silently (`#525`)** |\n\n**STRUCTURE VERIFIED AFTER THE EDIT, not assumed:** the change converts an `if` / `else if` chain into `if { if / else } else if`, which is the shape most likely to go wrong silently.  Braces balance 6/6 across the function, and a comment-stripped read-through confirms all four arms reach their intended bodies.\n\n*** AND THE COMMON TAIL STILL RUNS FOR THE DECLINED READ, which is the half a naive decline gets wrong: *** `if (writeflag == MEM_READ) data[0] = odata;` executes, so the guest receives an explicit 0 rather than an untouched buffer.  *The design pass warned that leaving the caller's output buffer untouched would be an INCOMPLETE decline, and the source itself carries that warning for TimerClear.*\n\n**ZERO'S WARRANT IS RECORDED AT THE SITE AND ITS LIMIT WITH IT** -- the emulator's deterministic declined-read convention (`#95` pre-zeroes registered-device read buffers), **explicitly not a claim about what real hardware returns.**  Whose register `0x3f7` actually is remains open as [[wdcaltownership]]; the comment cites both sides of the tree's own collision evidence (`wdcreg.h`'s `#if 0` *'NOT MAPPED; fd uses this register on PCs'*, and `dev_fdc.c`'s *'TODO 8, but collision with wdc'*) without resolving it.\n\n**THE NARROWING IS DECLINED IN THE COMMENT, WITH ITS REASON**, so a future round meets the argument rather than re-deriving it: a `2 -> 1` at `memory_device_register()` closes both offset-1 cells structurally but moves `0x3f7` from HANDLED-AND-DECLINED to UNMAPPED -- an unmeasured path -- and leaves `#494`'s arm as dead code carrying a measured comment.\n\n**PROPAGATION:** `est/` DONE and verified byte-identical, after confirming it equalled SEC@HEAD first.  **Build trees NOT propagated** -- same deliberate hold as `#524`, for the same reason.  **Three files now owe propagate+rebuild together:** `cpu_arm_coproc.c`, `cpu_arm_instr.c`, `dev_wdc.c`.\n\n**STILL OWED: the rebuild, a pass 2 on the real diff, and the targeted gate.**  Witness and kill table are done (HEAD 14/16, fix 16/16, S measured nonzero at 0x51 / 0x58).\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PASS 2 on the real `#525` diff (codex GPT-6 Astra at max).  **PASS 2: NOT RATIFIED -- BRANCHING CORRECT, COMMENT OVERCLAIMED, DETECTOR COVERAGE UNVERIFIED.** ***\n\n*VERDICT: NOT RATIFIED -- branch binding is correct; full call purity and detector coverage remain unverified, and the comment overstates its hardware evidence.*\n\n**ALL FOUR CELLS VERIFIED AGAINST THE EDITED SOURCE, and the brace count independently reproduced (six and six):**\n\n| dir | ofs | body | common tail |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| READ | 0 | inner `else`: ALTSTATUS debug, status preserved in `odata` | writes status to `data[0]`, `return 1` |\n| READ | 1 | inner `if`: `odata = 0`, no debug | **writes ZERO to `data[0]`**, `return 1` |\n| WRITE | 0 | outer `else`: ALT. CTRL; `WDCTL_RST` -> `COMMAND_RESET` + reset helper | no buffer assignment |\n| WRITE | 1 | outer `else if`: DRIVE ADDRESS, ignored | no buffer assignment |\n\n**The unbraced `else` binds to the INNER `if`**, as intended.  *'A READ cannot enter either WRITE body.  A WRITE at offset 1 cannot reach either reset operation, regardless of its data byte.'*\n\n=== *** THE PURITY QUESTION I FLAGGED AS MY LEAST-CERTAIN: ANSWERED, AND BETTER THAN I HOPED *** ===\n`status_byte()` *'has no direct device-state mutation'* -- it builds a local integer and READS image existence, ring occupancy, `write_in_progress`, `error` and ATAPI command/phase, **with no assignment through `d` or `cpu`, no ring consumption, no interrupt operation.**\n\n> *** AND THE INTERRUPT ACKNOWLEDGEMENT IS OUTSIDE THE HELPER: *** the `INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(d->irq); d->int_assert = 0;` pair lives in the MAIN register handler beside its own `status_byte()` call, **and is absent from `wdc_altstatus` entirely.**  *So calling the shared helper does not drag the normal-STATUS acknowledgement along with it, and a declined read acknowledges nothing.  The decline is COMPLETE.*\n\n*Honest limit kept: the seat verified the helper's body, not its complete call chain.*\n\n=== L4 APPLIED: THE COMMENT OVERCLAIMED TWICE, AND BOTH ARE NARROWED IN PLACE ===\n  * *'matches no hardware on any layout'* -> **'the value of a DIFFERENT register, from an offset this handler never decoded'**.  The header establishes auxiliary offset 0 and a disabled `wd_digin` with its PC-floppy note; *'It does not prove \"matches no hardware on any layout\"'*.\n  * *'a correct guest reads here expecting a FLOPPY effect'* -> narrowed to what the tree actually shows: **on the PC layout this byte is CONTESTED and documented from both sides**, which is reason enough to stay silent without the universal claim.\n\n*** AND I VERIFIED BOTH QUOTES MYSELF, because the seat could not: *** it did not have `dev_fdc.c` in its permitted set and said so rather than accepting my quotation.  Checked here: `dev_fdc.c:49` is `#define DEV_FDC_LENGTH 6 /* TODO 8, but collision with wdc */` and `wdcreg.h:71-73` is `#if 0 /* NOT MAPPED; fd uses this register on PCs */` around `wd_digin`.  **Both verbatim.**\n\n> *** A REFINEMENT NEITHER OF US HAD: `DEV_FDC_LENGTH` IS **6**, SO dev_fdc.c DOES NOT REGISTER 0x3f7 TODAY.  THE COLLISION IS PROSPECTIVE -- the \"TODO 8\" is what WOULD collide. ***  Added to the comment, because 'contested today' and 'contested if someone finishes the TODO' are different claims and the round was making the stronger one.\n\n**`M_NULL_EFFECT` CONFIRMED CANONICAL** -- keeping the offset guard and deleting `odata = 0` leaves the earlier status value intact, and *'the common tail then overwrites the dispatcher's pre-zeroed byte with that status'*, which is exactly why the nonzero CORE states discriminate it.  It is also *more isolated than a full reinstatement* because it preserves the new suppression of the offset-1 diagnostic.\n\n**STILL OWED: the selector audit** (the seat calls `M_NULL_EFFECT` *'not a sufficient selector audit'*) **and detector-coverage verification.**  *The `sgirandomcolour` round found six mutants conditioning a repair's effect on a selector no row varies; the same question is now open here and is carried forward rather than assumed answered.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PANEL CONVENED on the combined `#524`/`#525` packet, and **SEAT LEDGER FOR THE #524/#525 PANEL -- THREE SEATS DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED, BY NAME** ***\n\n**FIRED: the eight scriptable transports** via `bash GXEMUL-SEC/tools/panel.sh <absolute brief>` -- codex GPT-6 Astra (max), grok 4.6 (xhigh), kimi 3, and the Ollama cloud seats glm-5.3, deepseek-v4-pro, minimax-m3 (+ the kimi-k3 substitute if configured).  **All four Ollama transports were confirmed REACHABLE by `api/tags` beforehand**, so a non-answer from any of them is a seat failure rather than a dead daemon.\n\n=== *** DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED -- NAMED, BECAUSE A SEAT HELD BACK AND A SEAT THAT FAILED ARE DIFFERENT THINGS AND CONFLATING THEM IS HOW A TWO-SEAT REVIEW GETS REPORTED AS A PANEL *** ===\n  * **agy 3.8** -- RESERVED FOR MEASUREMENT by standing owner ruling, and it is the reason both of these rounds have witnesses at all: it built the `armbdt` witness + kill table (25/25) and the `wdcaltdigin` witness + kill table (16/16) earlier today.  *Spending its bounded budget on a reading seven other seats can do is spending the roster's only independent-transport measuring capability on a fungible job.*\n  * **Opus 5** -- HELD.  It answers through the Agent tool and consumes the same Anthropic quota the owner wants preserved for the adjudication.  *Not a failure, not agreement: a budget decision.*\n  * **Fable 5** -- HELD BY EXPLICIT OWNER SEQUENCING: *'don't run fable until all other panel seats have had a say.'*  **This panel is the 'all other seats' step.**  Its availability is to be checked by a plain subagent rather than by Opus when the time comes.\n\n**WHY THE PACKET IS SHAPED AS IT IS:** every fact is INLINED -- both diffs, both full enclosing contexts, both measured kill tables, and **both first-pass NOT-RATIFIED findings** -- because the Ollama transports cannot read files and a brief containing *'check the source'* is a documented way to turn a live seat into a stall that looks exactly like a quota wall.  *The packet also asks the seats to attack the FIRST PASS'S FINDINGS, not only the code, so the corrections get adversarial scrutiny too.*\n\n*** THE LOAD-BEARING QUESTION PUT TO THEM IS THE SELECTOR ONE, and it is imported from a sibling round rather than invented: *** `sgirandomcolour` had **six mutants score a TOTAL ESCAPE** because every detector row probed a single position and each mutant conditioned the repair's effect on a tile or coordinate selector.  **`#524` has 25 rows and `#525` has 16, and neither has had a selector audit.**  The panel is asked to name a conditioning that a plausible edit could produce and the rows could not see.\n\n*Landing remains HELD by owner ruling until the full panel reports -- nothing is committed and no gate has run.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PANEL (`_scratchpad/panel_20260911_103402`).  **SIX of seven scriptable seats answered substantively; the kimi CLI is WALLED and its `kimi-k3:cloud` substitute ANSWERED, so that seat is COVERED, not lost.** ***\n\n**Seat sizes and tails verified individually rather than by size alone:** codex 300,614 b (streams its reasoning; the tail is substantive), grok 11,840 b, `ollama_minimax` 12,053 b, `ollama_glm` 9,695 b, `ollama_kimi` 7,601 b, `ollama_deepseek` 4,219 b.  **`kimi.txt` is 350 b ending `See log: ...kimi-code.log` -- the CLI failure signature, NOT a considered non-answer.**\n\n**CONSENSUS ON THE PRODUCT CODE: NEITHER FIX IS WRONG.**  glm -- *'#524 is architecturally correct and its scope is what was overstated'*; minimax -- *'Both are correct, minimal, and correctly scoped'*; `kimi-k3` -- *'`addr & 0xfffffffc` per-access is provably equivalent'*; deepseek -- *'Neither fix is wrong as a local correction, but #524 is incomplete (combined handlers)'*, **independently reproducing the codex finding about `netbsd_memset`/`netbsd_memcpy`.**\n\n=== *** THE SELECTOR QUESTION GOT REAL ANSWERS, AND THEY ARE FLOOR-BREACH CANDIDATES *** ===\nI asked, importing the shape from [[sgirandomcolour]]: *what conditioning could a plausible edit introduce that the 25 and 16 rows never vary?*\n\n| round | proposed conditioning | seat | predicted |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `#525` | **`if (odata & WDCS_DRDY) odata = 0;`** | grok | **16/16 -- TOTAL ESCAPE** |\n| `#525` | `if (d->unit == 0) odata = 0;` -- a DRIVE-UNIT selector | deepseek | passes if every row uses the primary controller |\n| `#524` | `if (cpu->mem->page_size == 4096) addr &= ~3;` -- a constant property of the test map | deepseek | passes; breaks on any other page size |\n\n**grok also corrected my clause-(d) choice:** deleting `odata = 0` is NOT the discriminating mutant, *because `M_NULL_EFFECT` already scored 14/16* -- the detector sees it.  **The one that escapes keeps the assignment and gates it.**  *That is the difference between a mutant the rows catch and a mutant the rows are blind to, and I had picked the first and called it the floor test.*\n\n**PRESCRIPTION (grok):** *'Product diffs can stand.  The detectors cannot ship as the round's \"we pinned the repair\" claim until `#524` has a `+2` and a `+3` (or an anchored `& 3` mutant) and `#525` has one CORE observation with `WDCS_DRDY` clear.'*  **That is an L3 -- an undischarged obligation, where the row is the sole carrier of something the round claims.**\n\n*** AND A MEASURED-VS-PREDICTED DISCREPANCY IN MY OWN ARTIFACT, caught by grok and verified here: `PRETABLE.out:54` predicts `M_NULL_MEDIUM 4/25`; `SCORES.txt` measures **6/25**. ***  *I cited 6/25 -- the measured one -- so no record is wrong, but the PREDICTION sits in a durable artifact where a future reader can cite it as a measurement.  That is the frozen-number class with a two-row error already in the drawer.*  grok's reading of the remainder: *'PATH/ATTRIB-style controls surviving an early `return`'*.\n\n**Seats DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED, by name: agy** (reserved for measurement; it built both witnesses today), **Opus 5** (held -- same Anthropic quota the adjudication needs), **Fable 5** (held by owner sequencing until every other seat has spoken -- **this panel is that step**).\n\n*** 2026-09-11 FABLE 5 ADJUDICATION (`FABLE_ADJ_20260911`).  **SEAT ALIVE -- token AND a verbatim `CHECKPOINT.md` HEAD quote returned, and it re-ran `git log --oneline -1` itself to confirm the checkpoint is not stale.**  Fired ONLY after all seven other scriptable seats had spoken, per owner sequencing, and via a subagent rather than Opus. ***\n\n=== RULING: **SHIP, after in-round RECORDS work.  Grok's L3 does NOT block it.** ===\n\n*** AND THE REASON IS A DOCTRINAL POINT I HAD WRONG: THE CLAUSE-(d) ENUMERATION HERE IS **EMPTY**, AND AN EMPTY ENUMERATION MUST BE RECORDED AS **N/A-WITH-REASON, NOT AS PASSED**. ***\n\nThe guarded body is `odata = 0;` -- **it reads nothing and tests nothing.**  The dominating predicates are `writeflag == MEM_READ` and `relative_addr != 0`, and **over a TWO-BYTE region `!= 0` admits no non-equivalent weakening.**  So DEPENDS-ON enumerates the empty set.  *This project's oldest rule about blanks applies exactly here: an unrun check and an inapplicable one must not render the same.*\n\n**Floor otherwise discharged from the artifacts, each cited:** (a) HEAD 14/16 red; (b) `M_REINSTATE` = HEAD; (c) `M_NULL_MEDIUM` fails a DIFFERENT pair -- all in `SCORES.txt` and the `.raw` files.\n\n=== *** TWO OF THE THREE PANEL SELECTOR MUTANTS DO NOT COMPILE *** ===\n  * `if (d->unit == 0)` -- **`struct wdc_data` HAS NO `unit` FIELD.**  Verified here: `unit` has **0 occurrences** in `dev_wdc.c`; the fields are `drive` (`:134`) and `base_drive` (`:57`).  *Not a mutant.*  Restated as `d->drive == 0` it is clause-(e) debt for the same reason as the DRDY one.\n  * `if (cpu->mem->page_size == 4096)` (this was `#524`'s) -- **`page_size` appears nowhere in `src/include/` except `thirdparty/alpha_rpb.h`**, an Alpha boot struct.  *Not a mutant.*  **DROPPED, with the field-name correction recorded so nobody rebuilds them.**\n  * `if (odata & WDCS_DRDY) odata = 0;` -- **clause-(e) DEBT, not floor**, on the identical test that governed [[sgirandomcolour]]'s `tile_nr == 0` today: *the body never reads or tests `odata`, it only writes it.*\n\n*** AND GROK'S OWN ROW SPEC WOULD HAVE BEEN VACUOUS, which is the sharpest catch in the adjudication: *** *'a DRDY-clear row with status 0x00 reads 0 under HEAD, fix and mutant alike.'*  **The working recipe, derived from `status_byte()` at `:584-600`:** DRDY is set iff `diskimage_exist(d->drive + d->base_drive)` and ERR iff `d->error`; a command to an IMAGELESS drive sets `d->error |= WDCE_ABRT` (`:913`).  **So: SDH 0xb0, any command, read `0x3f7` -> HEAD `0x01`, fix `0x00`, DRDY-conjunct `0x01`.**  Two rows.  *`d->error` is one SHARED field (`:126`), so these rows must go LAST in their session* -- an ordering constraint that would have silently contaminated neighbours if missed.\n\n**RECORDS WORK OWED BEFORE IT SHIPS:** the L4 sibling fix (below, applied); one line citing ATA-2 X3T10/0948D Note 1, which the comment currently never mentions though `030e961` records it as governing and two seats raised it; a CHANGELOG `#525` block recording the `030e961` DD7 bit-7 row as **DISCHARGED BY SUBSUMPTION** (`CORE_READ_OFF1_*` want `0x00`) and naming the (e) disclosures.\n\n> *** AND A FINDING THAT APPLIES TO BOTH ROUNDS: NEITHER OF TODAY'S PROBES IS UNDER `regress/` PROPER, so *'shipped a detector with the fix'* IS NOT YET TRUE OF EITHER. ***  Promote per the `#494` precedent (`wdc_altoffset_probe.py`, wired into `gate_hygiene.sh:378`'s list).\n\n*** 2026-09-11 STEP 2 OF THE ADJUDICATED ORDER DONE: RECORDS WORK, INCLUDING THE ATA-2 WARRANT THE ROUND NEVER CITED. ***\n\n**(a) THE L4 SIBLING FIX -- APPLIED.**  `#494`'s write arm carried the identical claim this round's read-side comment retracts 34 lines above; the handler contradicted itself in one function.  Narrowed in place, with the provenance (*found by grepping for the sibling of a correction already called finished* -- mechanical rule 2, which I had skipped).\n\n**(b) THE ATA-2 CITATION -- APPLIED, and it is a STRENGTHENING rather than a tidy-up.**  Verified locally before writing, not taken from the adjudication: `_scratchpad/ata2_x3t10_948d.txt:1529-1531`, Note 1 -- *'This register is obsolete.  It is recommended that a device NOT RESPOND to a read of this address.  If a device does respond, it shall be sure NOT TO DRIVE THE DD7 SIGNAL to prevent possible conflict with floppy disk implementations.'*  **`dev_wdc.c` cited ATA-2 ZERO times before this** (measured), though `030e961` records the Note as governing and two seats raised it.\n\n> *** THREE CONSEQUENCES, AND THEY GIVE THE FIX A HARDWARE WARRANT WHERE IT HAD ONLY AN EMULATOR-POLICY ONE: *** **declining is the RECOMMENDED behaviour**, not merely a choice; **DD7 is bit 7 and `odata = 0` leaves it clear**, so the returned value satisfies the one constraint the standard places on a device that does answer; and **the floppy conflict the tree documents from both sides IS the reason the standard gives.**  *Zero stays an emulator policy WHERE THE STANDARD IS SILENT -- it fixes which non-DD7 value is returned -- but it is not arbitrary, and the comment now says exactly that much and no more.*\n\n**(c) THE CHANGELOG `#525` BLOCK -- WRITTEN** (106 lines, prepended; newest-first order verified, `#522` intact below).  It records the four-cell table, the measured legs, **clause (d) as N/A-WITH-REASON rather than passed**, the ATA-2 warrant, the **`030e961` DD7 row as DISCHARGED BY SUBSUMPTION** (both `CORE_READ_OFF1_*` rows want `0x00`, pinning every bit including DD7), the three clause-(e) disclosures including the two panel mutants that **do not compile**, and the seat ledger with the three held-back seats named.\n\n*** (d) STILL OWED, AND RECORDED RATHER THAN CLAIMED: THE PROBE IS UNDER `regress/artifacts/`, NOT `regress/` PROPER -- so *'shipped a detector with the fix'* IS NOT YET TRUE OF THIS ROUND. ***  The `#494` precedent is confirmed to exist (`regress/wdc_altoffset_probe.py`, referenced in `gate_hygiene.sh`).  **Deliberately NOT half-done:** a probe promoted into `regress/` that no gate executes is precisely [[probewiring]]'s defect, so the copy and the wiring land together, verified by a gate run, or neither lands.\n\n*Step 1 (the [[killtablebaseline]] repair) is complete and negative-controlled.  Step 3 -- the `M_MASK1` build for [[armbdt]] -- is RUNNING.*",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29290:     "note": "11,755 b, 98% novel, NO verdict line (format miss, NOT a seat failure -- the body is substantive and its findings are the sharpest in the panel). Predicts the smallest escaping edits: #524 `0xfffffffc'->`0xfffffffe' at both memory_rw sites, ONE CHARACTER, 25/25; #525 keep the offset guard and write `if (odata & WDCS_DRDY) odata = 0;', 16/16 -- and notes deleting `odata = 0' outright is NOT that edit since M_NULL_EFFECT already scored 14/16. *** ITS #525 PREDICTION WAS REACHED INDEPENDENTLY BY THE FLAGSHIP ADJUDICATION HOURS LATER and is now a pre-ship L1 obligation. *** Also corrected a frozen number -- PRETABLE predicted M_NULL_MEDIUM 4/25, SCORES.txt measured 6/25, *\"do not cite 4/25\"* -- and found that `mutate_armbdt.py' applies `addr & ~3' while the working-tree file carries `0xfffffffc' plus a 20-line comment, so a re-run would fail to find POP_RW_OLD: *\"freeze the mutator against the text you intend to commit.\"*"
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29371:     "note": "COMPLETE for the declared mask family: 15 classified, 12 killed, 3 equivalent, 0/12 non-equivalent escapes -- a zero WITH its denominator, which is what closes the repair chain. Answered the premise attack from the JOIN RULE: the guarded body `odata = 0` has an empty read set, so underlying state adds zero joined axes. Drew the boundary honestly: positive disjunctions over odata are closed; arbitrary underlying-state predicates (`!A`, `P || W || E`) are not and are not owed -- clause (e). One PRETABLE wording correction (per-mask equivalence cells), applied. Flagged the memory_rw.c clamp as unverifiable from its files; verified at source by the main loop (:495, :523)."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29413:     "note": "*** RAN THE WHOLE TABLE ON REAL BINARIES: nine mutants, scratch clones only, base-leg apparatus check on all fourteen runs. ***  Confirmed ALL EIGHT of the brief's predictions, M7 in its grok-corrected form, with red-counts matching the PRETABLE's arithmetic at every mutant.  *** THEN FOUND E4 -- `&& len != 4` -- SWEEPING 30/30 WHILE RESTORING THE DEFECT BYTE-FOR-BYTE. ***  Rows G/H/I return the injected a5/5a/00, identical to the PRE-FIX build, against the fix's real disk content c2/c3/c4: the guest is handed its own bytes AS DISK DATA and the detector scores clean.  **It DISSENTED on disposition and the dissent is correct**: `if (X)` -> `if (X && Y)` on the predicate #495 ADDED, which DOMINATES the edited lines, is clause (d)'s canonical form, and #488's adjudication names that exact shape a floor breach.  Filed as coverage debt by an earlier seat; RE-DISPOSITIONED to L2 and repaired in-round.  It derived the CLOSURE CONDITION rather than guessing: `&& P(len)` escapes iff P is true on every INJECTED width and false on some IMPLEMENTED width, so only {1,2,4} closes the family.  Repair is ONE instruction and NO new row -- a 4-byte `sw` -- and it kills E4, M3 and M6 together.  *** AND THE PROBE HAD NO 4-BYTE EMITTER AT ALL: the device's `case 4:` arm and its whole `consumed` accounting had never executed on any leg of any run this detector ever made. ***  Not an uncovered mutant -- an uncovered ARM.  E5 (`cur_command == ATAPI_PKT_CMD`) also sweeps 30/30 and the N arm does NOT defeat it: it REPLACES the flag operand rather than exempting an opcode, so both vehicles leave it green.  Filed as `wdcflagswap2`, ranked below E4 because its harm is over-refusal and transient.  It also narrowed what the arm buys: rows B and M differ in ONE readable dimension, so E2 is their only UNIQUE kill and *'the accept side is pinned' is measurably false*.  Confirmed the `WORDS_OK` dead check independently, caught a wrong record in kill495_M7.sh, and flagged this loop's own killswap.sh appearing while its table built."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29423:     "note": "Went clause by clause and ruled the arm's channel VALID, `harness:` the right witness term, and the design sound -- then named what was still owed.  **Its headline is an evidence hole the round was about to build on: the E2 escape figure's cited artifact (`opus495/wave2/run_q3.out`) NO LONGER EXISTS**, and the row disagrees with itself -- witness field `20/20`, note `all 22 rows`.  A number that disagrees with itself and whose evidence is gone is a transcription, not a measurement.  **The table now re-measures it as LEG 0** (22/22 ESCAPED on the committed probe), so the escape is a result of this round.  It also caught: clause (a) CANNOT govern an accept row (green on both legs by definition) -- the governing rule is ACCEPT-ROW FAILABILITY, discharged by a MEASURED kill, and claiming clause (a) would have been an unfalsifiable L2 claim; the probe's clause-(e) CONTRACT did not name this family; the header sentence *'THE PROGRAM AND THE ROWS BELOW ARE UNTOUCHED ... measured 20 of 20'* was ALREADY FALSE when committed, because #495 added the J/K/L arm in the same round; the wrong post-packet narrative had an uncorrected SIBLING in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md whose REMEDY line prescribed the VACUOUS form of this very row; `kill495.sh`'s `WORDS_OK` is computed and referenced nowhere while the script claims the encodings agree; and PRETABLE item (ii) as first worded LICENSED EDITING #495's true records.  It derived two escapes (`wdcwidth4`, `wdcringresetblind`) and named a per-row kill the block had missed: an empty-ring gate passes the FIRST accept row and fails only the SECOND, so the two are not redundant.  It advised splitting the unrelated gate_hygiene edit into its own commit -- done, `e3cf79f`."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29513:     "note": "Went clause by clause and ruled the measurement discipline *the best this queue has produced* -- then found **clause (e) BREACHED as shipped**, with five tells of one cause: the probe's contract was written at the 13-row stage and the round grew past it.  `rows M` in a file with M AND N arms; a revision history stopping at 13 rows/26; the BLIND-TO block still calling a repaired escape live; a dangling *'two further accept-side mutants -- see BLIND-TO'* pointer whose referents were one repaired and one absent; and **E5 disclosed nowhere in the instrument**.  All corrected.  *** ITS SHARPEST FINDING WAS THE ONE UNMEASURED CLAIM: the empty-ring mutant was never built. ***  The claim that each pair's SECOND row is non-redundant rested on a prediction -- and unlike E2/E5 that mutant sits INSIDE clause (d)'s enumeration.  Built as E6: **26/30, and exactly the predicted asymmetry** -- first row green, second red, in both arms.  It also ruled that **clause (a) cannot govern an accept row** (green on both product legs by definition) and that ACCEPT-ROW FAILABILITY is the governing rule, so the record must say *discharged by a measured kill*, never *clause (a) satisfied* -- as a floor claim the latter would be unfalsifiable and L2-enforceable.  PRETABLE audit against the `#490` precedent: four of six carry-forward items shipped, one was moot, and **the anti-`#490` clause itself says 'diffed against these four' over a list of SIX**.  It ruled the file must NOT be edited to fix that -- its mtime is the round's order proof -- so the erratum lives in the round block."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29523:     "note": "Read the tree and the artifacts rather than the packet, and **refuted the round's central evidence table by opening `kill_E4.out`** -- independently of opus5 and before it.  Also caught a census error: the brief and PRETABLE say FIVE nonzero-phase write sites; **there are SEVEN**.  My grep pattern required a non-`=` character AFTER the `=`, so it silently skipped the two assignments SPLIT ACROSS LINES.  It verified the invariant is untouched -- both missed sites sit behind the flag -- so only the count was wrong.  On the width family it dissented usefully: `&& len != 8`, `&& len != 3` and `&& len <= 4` all leave every row green, so the closure sentence as written was too strong.  **Both it and glm were partly right** -- those survivors are behaviourally EQUIVALENT mutants, because 3 and 8 push nothing.  The sentence conflated *escapes the detector* with *escapes and re-opens the defect*, and now carries two clauses.  It ratified the N arm as worth its rows and ruled a third vehicle the forbidden growth.  It also found a second CALLER of the reset helper (`WDCTL_RST` via alt-ctrl), which matters for E5 and not for the vehicles."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:29593:     "note": "*** PASS: the regression material is fit to live in the harness permanently.  Six findings, ALL non-blocking, TWO of them defects nobody had written down. ***  **Q1 maintainability -- YES.**  It called the probe the best-documented instrument in the tree and enumerated why: a clause-(e) CONTRACT with its measured figures kept as DATED 10-row-era numbers, a BLIND-TO list whose every entry names its filed ledger id, a 10->11->15 revision history with per-round attribution, per-row provenance (row L's want is honestly *'RECORDED FROM THE FIRST RUN, not predicted'*), and the flag-implies-phase invariant carried as the M rows' non-vacuity warrant.  Both PRETABLE obligations aimed at the probe are discharged IN the file.  **Q2 artifacts -- YES, with the one real mechanism defect.**  Everything resolves: 13 logs sentinelled, PRETABLE's mtime (13:19) precedes the table (13:46), the CHANGELOG table matches TABLE.txt leg-for-leg, and **E2's four reds are exactly the M+N rows with B GREEN -- so the 'only UNIQUE kill' claim is in the log rather than only in the prose**.  *** FINDING 2, AND IT IS THE ROUND'S OWN LESSON APPLIED TO HALF ITS OWN MECHANISM: `killswap.sh` tagged the TABLE but not the KILL LOGS. ***  So the E6 follow-up overwrote the twelve-leg table's `kill_L0_committed_probe.out` -- and by the time this was acted on, the X4 run had overwritten it AGAIN.  **The seat's own report went stale about it within the hour**, which is the sharpest possible evidence that the mechanism was actively destructive rather than a one-off.  The original survived only because it had been COMMITTED and was recovered from the git blob -- the second such recovery in this one round, after `shipped.diff`.  Fixed: logs and LEG 0 are tagged, backward-compatibly.  **FINDING 1:** the probe's LEG-1 banner asserted *'source-identical to GXEMUL-SEC at HEAD c125450'* -- a frozen claim about an INPUT the probe cannot see, and FALSE in all thirteen of this round's own logs, whose base leg was pinned elsewhere.  Immaterial behaviourally (comment-only delta) and it is this file's own header lesson one screen below the paragraph that teaches it.  Now prints the binary it was handed.  **FINDINGS 3-4:** the CHANGELOG's M6 bullet said *'checked, and M6 is correct'* **while the same commit edited M6.sh by thirty lines** -- true of the mutant line, false of the rationale paragraph, and contradicting its own diff.  And the errata section covered two of PRETABLE's errors but not a third (block 8 repeats the over-broad M6 phrasing).  Both corrected; PRETABLE left mtime-frozen as required.  **Q3 gate-wiring exemption -- still right, and it named planning nobody had written down: FOUR wdc probes share the single-leg-mode debt and the SAME 2026-09-20 expiry.**  That is ONE design round covering four instruments, not four rounds, and it should be scheduled before the cluster expires together.  **Q4 did the harness get worse -- NO, better, with controls.**  It verified the fourth-tree check skips rather than fails on a sleeping VM, that the hookctl runner is fail-capable in both directions, that the hook shims make divergence IMPOSSIBLE rather than policed (it `cmp`'d both pairs), and that the M6/M7 corrections are proper in-place L4s naming their finders.  **Q5 owed and unwritten:** the untagged copy-back (fixed), the stale banner (fixed), the M6 qualifier and the missing PRETABLE erratum (fixed), eight untracked files inside the repo (relocated -- it checked programmatically that zero ledger rows cite them), and the four-probe design round (filed above)."
