GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:33963:     "note": "PASS 5 (file-reading; checked every table against the driver logs row for row, including the rb t1ctl artifact subtraction; the Opus pass-4 sweeps' mutation proofs and mtime stamps; part4/frozen_probe.py md5 == the shipped probe; the 72759b4f copy; the three-file PRETABLE split; the eight/seven/five counts; the product diff = comments + typedef). One false sentence (the TEST == disclosure). Cosmetic: the IPL/MASK arm comments say 'now per-instance' above bodies whose #119 statics remain process-global -- true of the width latch, misleading about the body's. ACCEPTED: the comments now say which latch."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:33974:     "note": "PASS 5 READER. BUILT and MEASURED on the shipped 101-row instrument (md5 06594f0e, own clone, BASE 101/101, negative control guard-deleted 92/101): the guard QUALIFIED BY ITS OWN LATCH BIT -- `&& !(d->warned & (1u << bit))', the canonical clause-(d) conjunct addition -- 101/101 TOTAL SWEEP at GENCTL alone AND at all twelve. Non-equivalence on the saved binaries: two wide writes then read back -> GENCTL 0xbb vs 0x82, MASK 0xbb vs 0x12; one wide write reads identically on both, which is every shipped row's shape. Cause: no row observes the device with its latch already set (latch same counts messages only; every rb row reads after ONE write). This round's own PRETABLE Section 1 listed d->warned as a body-read field and then dropped it from the disagreeing-state enumeration. Also: the announce's len argument unpinned (cosmetic, disclose); the typedef bound tests a literal not the field (gdrom's sizeof idiom is in the tree); the PRETABLE.out restore re-stamped its mtime again and PRETABLE_amendments.out is no witness for 5-6; the wide-READ fall-through to each arm's second predicate (delete it and a read stores 0) -- outside the (d) closure, flagged; the generic-DECL count is over the whole transcript (three other devices emit it, none on mvme187). Reachability re-derived from the offsets: exactly six 2-aligned blocks, one 4-aligned (SCCERR), none 8-aligned. ACCEPTED IN FULL: rb2 * at nine blocks + pscale/ipl noig2 (the class closed at every block), hrd * rb x4 + hrd ipl noro, the typedef in gdrom's form, the disclosures, the header; measured in part 5 with M_QUAL_GENCTL, M_QUAL_ALL, M_NOWRITECHK_GENCTL."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:34033:     "note": "PASS 6. Re-derived the guard census, enum cardinality, arm arithmetic, state initialization and writers, the SCCERR equivalence, the reassert count, reachability and the helper-consumer claims from the live fork; no new behavioural escape. Record defects: PRETABLE_pass5 predicted `rb2 genctl' = 0xbb where the artifact reads 0xb8 (the row's input is 0xaab8) -- measurement-refuted prediction text; 'six mutants' counts BASE; 'every write arm uses idata' is false (SCCERR stores 0, IPL stores nothing); the brief still said 'this diff adds five rows' and '96 rows'; PRETABLE_pass4.out's mtime is now AFTER part 4 -- my 'all nine' wording correction re-stamped it; numstat drift post-packet; the packet lacked the pass-5/6 roster. Coverage recommendations: repeat the latch-set check at widths 4 and 8; measure the cross-bit mutant on the shipped instrument; bound the mutation grammar against pending_timer*, cur_int_vec[] and unrelated registers or add active-state rows. ACCEPTED: all record items corrected; the coverage items disclosed in the contract (other device state sits at its init value in every session; the pass-5 reader walked those operands) -- the widths-4/8 latch-set check FILED as coverage debt."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:34077:     "note": "PASS 6, MEASURING seat.  Saved FINDINGS.md (F1-F9, every line re-derived from source with a VERIFIED mark) and launched a frozen 3-mutant table in its own clone /tmp/gxopus_p6 BEFORE the Agent transport hit the five-hour session limit (HTTP 429, reset 22:20 CDT); the orphaned driver ran to TABLE_DONE at 22:26 with RESTORE cmp-clean and the sentinel cleared -- the seat ANSWERED; the rate limit ended the agent, not the measurement.  BASE 118/118.  M1_W4FALL_ALL `break;' -> `if (len < 4) break;' at all twelve: 118/118 ESCAPE -- announced AND landed for len 4/8; `latch all w'/`latch all d' count only the announce, no readback exists at len 4 or 8, so the contract sentence 'pin the REFUSAL at every block for each non-byte width' is FALSE (L4) and the repair is nulled at two widths (L2).  M2_PENDT_TXICR `&& d->pending_timer1_interrupts == 0': 118/118 ESCAPE (F1; the fable5 seat measured the timer-2 twin independently).  M3_DELGUARD_GENCTL: 108/118, ten rows red -- the negative control.  F3 INT-bit twin, F4 IPL-register qualifier (message-only), F5 cur_int_vec out of closure (N/A), F6 announce-once unpinned at len 4/8, F7 `latch all fail' rests on memory_rw's debug() (stepping-gated), F8 PRETABLE_pass4 re-stamp 20:23 unrecorded in PRETABLE.out, F9 Section-1 deriver keyed on the NAME `warned'.  One-token sweep with denominator: no escape.  Its FINDINGS.md was written before either escape was measured, so the predictions (118/118 both) preceded the table."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:34110:     "note": "PASS 7, FILE-READING (walked all twelve write bodies in the fork; line numbers match).  VERDICT NO-GO on closing: the three pass-6 cells are closed, the operand-space sentence is not -- the IPL write body reads a function-static `w' (`if (!w) { fatal; w = 1; }') and no row holds it SET at a wide write, so `&& !w' on the IPL width guard is equivalent on all 159 rows; the distinguishing session is one st.b to IPL then a wide write, DECL 1 vs 0.  Same rule as pass 5's `warned' and pass 6's pending: L2, one row.  Also: PRETABLE_pass6b Section 13 lists SCCTX's landed lane as 0x44 -- SCCTX is `& 0x1f' like SCCICR, so 0x04 (kill holds; grouped with SCCRX by mistake); premise 7 'written ONLY by pcc_timer_tick' is FALSE (the TxICR body clears and decrements; the tick is the only producer of > 0); the MASK arm comment claims a `static int w' the MASK body does not have (copy-paste from IPL, earlier pass, wrong record); the CHANGELOG's part-6b RED line is truncated at `rb8' (a `cut -c1-200' in the fill); rd(8.0) is a wall-clock drain, not a prompt wait -- under load `print r4' is sent to a running guest, `pend* arm' prints `died' falsely and decl/rb fail for a reason other than the one under test -> it should say FAILED FOR SOME OTHER REASON.  Verified right: the 0x31 liveness+signature claim (bb0 0x34 no-delay-slot, d16 -1 = previous instruction; INT only via reassert), the landed-lane tables at both widths, the transfer of the four part-6 scores, the L2 ruling on the pending qualifier (agrees), the out-of-closure claims for reg[IPL] and cur_int_vec[].  Denominator stated.  A READING with source verification."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:34121:     "note": "PASS 7, FILE-READING (pointer-fed; 440 KB stream, answer at the tail).  VERDICT NO-GO.  (1) TxCTL's body is an RMW that PRESERVES the upper-nibble overflow state (PCC2_TCTL_OVF, set only by pcc_timer_tick on a count overflow); every TxCTL preset has that nibble zero, so `&& !(d->pcctwo_reg[relative_addr] & PCC2_TCTL_OVF)' at the TxCTL guard is predicted 159/159 -- the byte was counted covered because its LOW bits are non-initial, inconsistent with enumerating TxICR's INT bit separately.  (2) No rb4/rb8 rows at T1CTL/T1ICR: `if (len < 4 || relative_addr == PCCTWO_T2CTL) break;' announces and lands len 4/8 at T1CTL while every T1 halfword row and every T2 wide row stays green -- the T1/T2 split the contract recognised at len 2 owes its width cross-product.  (3) rd(8.0) drains the pty for eight seconds without recognising a prompt; under starvation later commands reach a running guest and the final waitpid() can HANG the detector; `pend* arm' prints `died' while the child is alive -- distinguish timeout/no-breakpoint, no-r4, died:<status>, with bounded cleanup.  Also: IPL's static `w' (agrees with grok); the reg[IPL] out-of-closure claim is true only under the stated 'lexically direct reads, excluding callees' definition (reassert reads IPL) -- the contract must SAY the definition; 'nine of nine answered' is impossible with agy not fired (eight answering of nine); the CHANGELOG part-6b RED line truncated; PRETABLE_pass6b's mtime equals the part6/driver.log COPY's second, so it witnesses precedence over 6b, not strict order after 6; the transfer of the four scores is defensible; the landed-lane tables agree with grok's (SCCTX 0x04 at len 8).  Three of its findings are one class: device state that presets cannot reach (OVF nibble, static w) -- the enumeration must be by body-read LVALUE with its full bit-domain."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:34363:     "note": "PASS 10, FILE-READING (pointer-fed).  VERDICT NO-GO.  Rule: the pair belongs in (d) ('single-operand only' appears nowhere in the rule); the bound needs SOURCE-DERIVED EQUIVALENCE CLASSES (each count {zero, nonzero} because that is how the body tests it), pairwise coverage of reachable class products -- and the 194-row detector does NOT close that: (relative_addr == T1ICR, pending_timer2 > 0) is held by no row, so `&& !(T1ICR && p2)' is predicted 194/194 (= the Opus seat's M_P2T1).  Rows sound; `pendboth arm1' sound; the four rows are scheduling-sensitive ('load-STABLE' too strong: value-stable after the callback, starvation-sensitive).  Masked ovfhi: every intended kill preserved, only the 8-vs-9..15 distinction lost; the control is right and its b27 reds are real.  Records: PRETABLE prediction refuted (acknowledged); the pass-9 heading's arithmetic ('nine seats fired, eight answering plus the measuring seat' -- seven reading + opus = eight, agy not fired); the contract's {..0x81..} set overclaims under the mask; 'count at 8' notes; the brief's enclosing C was EMPTY (case labels only); no denominator for the pairwise closure.  Swept the 2x2 pending cells, the four relative_addr x p2 cells, both labels, AND/OR/label shapes, the three measurements.  ADOPTED: the equivalence-class bound is the same rule the fable5 seat wrote; the open cell is closed by the p11 repair (the full cross-product)."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:34429:     "note": "PASS 10, FILE-READING + MEASURING (own clone /tmp/gxopus_p10 of build/, cmp-clean, baseline rebuilt, frozen probe md5 43471ae2, each mutant diff-proven vs SEC, restored + rebuilt + cmp-clean, sentinel cleared; git status verified unchanged).  VERDICT DO NOT CLOSE, L2 in-round.  MEASURED: BASE 194/194; M_P2T1 = `&& !(pending_timer2 && relative_addr == T1ICR)' 194/194 TOTAL ESCAPE; M_T1JOINT = the T1-label joint form 194/194 TOTAL ESCAPE -- both predicted before its first build (PREDICTIONS.md 13:19, binary 13:24); BASE run first on an idle host so the zeros are not starvation.  RULE: joint states ARE in the closure -- rule (2) bounds the ALPHABET, not the ARITY; its own pass-9 dissent fails four ways (the guarded body itself is a four-term conjunction over exactly these lvalues, so 'nobody writes that' is false of this function; the exponential argument attacks a bound nobody proposed; per-enumerated-mutant is about the denominator; the dissent's remedy is inconsistent with the rows pass 9 shipped).  The main loop's four-point disposition fails too: it cited class closure and made a cell-by-cell repair (TxICR serves TWO labels, the space was 2x2x2); the OpenBSD warrant is UNSETTLEABLE in-tree (no source, no datasheet) -- the in-tree substitute is the model's own joint handling (one callback services both timers; reassert reads both counts); 'load-stable' conflates value with arrival; the asymmetry argument alone licenses any row.  ITS BOUND: the cross-product of the value-classes the rows already establish per body-read lvalue, with the arm's DISPATCH axes (relative_addr) as full axes; demand INCREMENTAL (a repair that adds a class owes it at every dispatch value in the same diff); everything else disclosed by name.  ADJUDICATED: the pass-10 ruling (fable5's body-tested partitions) and this bound agree on the cells that matter here and differ on whether the classes come from the ROWS or the BODY'S TESTS -- carried to pass 11 as part of H1 (the pendn 'thousands' class is a cell under Opus's form and disclosed under fable5's).  Records: `pendboth arm1' observes a STICKY INT bit -- the proof is the mutant (folded); the ovfhi mask's kill channel is CEN alone, bit 7 is arming, and the driven value must keep bit 0 clear (folded); the brief's enclosing C was EMPTY and both T1 labels absent (generator fixed, asserts a body); the pass-9 denominator swept arity 1 only; PRETABLE_pass9.out carried NONE of the four mandated pre-table checks (TRUE -- PRETABLE_pass10 now carries them, with the sibling-parity clause 'at a shared block the siblings are the LABELS'); its `pendboth decl' becomes-2 derivation is refuted by the hand-verification (the announce is latched once per block: 1).  Repair shipped by p11: the full {label} x {p1} x {p2} x {IEN} product, 203 rows."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:34495:     "note": "PASS 11, PACKET-FED via the kimi-k3:cloud SUBSTITUTE (CLI weekly-walled, 350 b).  Names accepted #12 wrong (a sticky INT observed after the event does not establish 'non-zero now') -- ADJUDICATED AGAINST: INT being set does not consume the count; the joint fact is established by the MUTANT reddening the readbacks (and the note now says so; grok's nit that it should say rb/rbi, not decl, is taken).  R1-R3 right.  H1: NO as 112 rows -- but disclosure by count UNDER-discloses 0xe0; BOUND: row the partitions that decide whether the write proceeds plus any partition whose effect feeds back into that decision (IEN, by zeroing the count); the rest get ONE value-witness each.  Its witness census was partly wrong (0x0013 carries no ICLR bit; the idle 0xaab8 rows DO carry ICLR and 0xe0 at the (0,0) cell).  Also caught the PRETABLE predictions' legibility defect (it read 'decl 1' as a red; the lists match their totals)."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:34662:   "state_note": "*** PASS 2 FOUND SIX CLEAN SWEEPS of the shipped 60-row detector (b78bb0f), the smallest ONE CHARACTER; the REPAIR is on disk, uncommitted: 82 rows (+8 IPL/MASK, +7 per-arm channels, +2 latch cardinality, +1 operand-swap, +4 odd-arm fall-through). Measured: part 1 nine mutants all killed at 78 (part1/driver.log); the builder's pass 3 killed all six sweeps at 78 and its 3b showed four fall-through TOTAL SWEEPS at 78 that its 3c kills 81/82 each at 82 (opus_pass3_20260903/); part 2 (M_IDATA_GENCTL vs 82) pending at the time of this note. PRETABLE.out written before table 2 (Section 0 records that table 1 had none). OWED before commit: part 2's sentinel, propagate dev_pcc2.c to build/ build-asan/ gxsec-build + two-part clearance, gate 14, CHANGELOG block from the logs, then PASS 3 (the eight other seats; the builder's pass 3 is one seat of it).  *** Superseded text, kept: FIXED (uncommitted at the time of writing; commit follows). Twelve width-guard blocks -> direction split: reads ACCEPT (the memcpy above the switch already served them; the tree's own header says wide access is LEGAL), writes DECLINE + per-instance 12-bit latch. DELETE is measurably wrong (big-endian compose: the low byte is the neighbour's lane) -- M_DELETE killed. The seven odd arms are OPERATOR-reachable (debugger put h, MEASURED) -- all twelve converted, M_OPKILL killed through that path. Detector 60 rows, gate 14 626/0, PREFIX 14/60, BASE 60/60, seven mutants zero sweeps. Design pass on 6 of 9 + one measurement (Opus x2 529, grok x2 CLI). Filed: pcc2c040kill (HIGH), pcc2counter8 (MED), pcc2widecompose. Owes pass 2.  2026-09-04 FIX REVIEW (owner directive): GPT-6 Astra (max) APPROVES the fix, DOES NOT CLOSE; Fable 5.1 GO-WITH-CHANGES.  Seven Astra findings confirmed from the tree; the main loop graded two as open floor cells -- the five HUH?-clear arms were then MEASURED HELD by announce-count rows (part 13 M_HUHV 249/251; the rbq rows strengthen the channel, L4 + coverage, not L2), the INT-set/count-0 cell awaits M_ZINT -> PASS 13 owed: 9 rows (242 -> 251), 7 mutants, gate, panel.  Astra dissents on the floor bound (Opus strength-t); the adjudicator adopts Fable's floor + the generating set.  PASS 13 PANEL COMPLETE 9/9 (2026-09-05 02:17-03:46).  RULING: PASS 14 OWED -- the class closure (three file-reading seats derived the same two gaps: the generating set one-sided at the two new INT cells; the INT axis not crossed with the other count), graded L1 (uncommitted detector material).  The pass-13 state is COMMITTED with the obligation OPEN (thirteen uncommitted passes were the larger exposure); pass 14 is drafted (fix_pcc2_p15.py: +18 rows -> 269, 8 mutants, PRETABLE_pass14, plus a pre-repair breach witness part14pre) and runs after the pcc2defaultkill fix ships (owner priority: critical first).\n\n*** THE TITLE'S \"TEN LIVE WIDTH KILLS\" IS STALE.  `b78bb0f` REMOVED ALL TEN.  Verified by me at\nHEAD, not taken from the seat. ***\n\n    grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);' src/devices/dev_pcc2.c   ->  2\n      :276  reassert_interrupts()          <- the C040 bit; that is [[pcc2c040kill]], a separate row\n      :380  pcctwo_interrupt_common()\n\n**Both are OUTSIDE the width guards.**  codex's count across the history is\n**13 before `b78bb0f`, 3 immediately after, 2 at HEAD**, and it read the guards directly: the commit\nconverted **all twelve**, so reads accept and wide writes decline.\n\n> **THE ROW STAYS OPEN AND ITS REASON CHANGES.**  `7076134` explicitly leaves pass 14 owed -- the new\n> timer interrupt-state cells lack the full generating set and the cross-product.  *Keeping it open\n> for that detector obligation is justified; describing ten width kills as LIVE is not,* and this is\n> a `high` row, so the overstatement is the kind that mis-ranks the whole queue.\n\n*This is the third row today whose headline count outlived the fix that removed it -- after\n`gdromkeyindex`'s \"five mutants score 32/32\" and `sh4iprunpinned`'s \"only four of ~20\".  A count in a\nTITLE is the least likely part of a row to be re-derived, because the title is what a reader uses to\ndecide whether to open the row at all.*\n\n*** THE CENSUS MOVED: `dev_pcc2.c` IS DOWN TO **ONE** `exit(1)`.  Re-derived 2026-09-08 with this\nrow's own deriver, after `#512` landed. ***\n\n    grep -cE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);' src/devices/dev_pcc2.c   ->  1\n    grep -nE '^[[:space:]]+exit\\(1\\);' src/devices/dev_pcc2.c   ->  :414\n\nThis row's note above enumerates TWO at HEAD -- `:276` in `reassert_interrupts()` (the C040 bit) and\n`:380` in `pcctwo_interrupt_common()`.  **`#512` removed the first**, and the second has moved to\n`:414` because that fix added lines above it.  *Both facts illustrate this row's own lesson: the\nline numbers decay and the DERIVER does not, which is why the deriver is recorded beside them.*\n\n**WHAT THE SURVIVOR IS, checked while verifying `#512`'s interrupt path rather than as a new hunt:**\n`pcctwo_interrupt_common()`'s `default:` arm kills on an interrupt line the switch does not case --\nit handles `PCC2V_SCSI`, `PCC2V_SCC_TX` and `PCC2V_SCC_RX`, and **`PCC2V_TIMER1` (0x09) and\n`PCC2V_TIMER2` (0x08) have no case**, although `reassert_interrupts()` names both when filling\n`cur_int_vec[]`.\n\n> **IT IS NOT GUEST-DRIVABLE TODAY, and the reason is construction rather than a guard.**\n> `interrupt->line` is fixed when a device is wired: `machine_mvme88k.c` connects only `clmpcc` and\n> `osiop` to pcc2, and the timers are serviced internally through `pending_timerN_interrupts`, never\n> through this callback.  **So the arm is a latent trap for a future device or machine that wires a\n> new line, not a live defect** -- which is why it is recorded here rather than filed as its own\n> round.\n\n*Checked against the ledger before writing: `pcctwo_interrupt_common` is already named in this row,\nso this is an update to an existing record and NOT a new row.  (Earlier this session I filed a\nduplicate that passed the gate and had to be withdrawn; the check is cheap and it worked here.)*",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:34673:   "note": "*** COMMITTED 2026-09-12 as a616d20 (local only).  Pass-2 receipt in regress/pass2_ledger.tsv.  L4 on the commit message: it says \"both build trees rebuilt\" -- it was all THREE (build/, build-asan/, /tmp/gxsec-build), cleared CLEAN/FRESH after the commit.  State stays `in work`: closure records owed. ***\n\n*** 2026-09-12: TABLE 5 CLOSES THE CLASS AND THE ROUND LANDS.  Kill table 5 (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/killtable5_20260912/SCORES.txt): 9 legs, EVERY ONE ON PREDICTION including the readback pairs -- BASE 75/75, HEAD 15/75, and M43/M44/M47 (the three table-4 L2 escapes), M_RULE2, M_STORE01, M_CONF_B4, M_TBENA_B8 each 74/75 on ONE named row.  The two new mutants red their D12 row ALONE -- D12 measured necessary.  Codex closing pass: 12/12 cells (six arms x widen/narrow) rowed, WITH the denominator.  Probe lands as CLASS_DETECTOR + dated EXEMPT (no gate runs Alpha; #520 precedent).  Five comment-level wrong records corrected in-round (D11 x2, header above-33, two dev_lca.c comments). ***\n\n*** 2026-09-12, codex farm (read-only, not a panel stage): table-5 DESIGN (regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_table5design_20260912.txt) found D11's complement closure covered 4 of the 6 filtered arms -- CONF and TB_ENA had no reserved-bit complement.  Verified by reading the probe; D12 added (`conf filter` 0x3/0x0, `tbena filter` 0x80/0x0), 73 -> 75 rows, UNMEASURED, L1 (table 5 not yet run).  A codex REVIEW of D12 (codex_d12review_20260912.txt) predicted 75/75, confirmed pair() scores its own session, and found a WRONG D11 comment (the BASE seed lacks WEN) -- corrected in the probe.  OWED: table 5 with R7/R8 in its PRETABLE. ***\n\n*** THE #443 BADVAL CLASS AT SCALE -- THE SAME SHAPE AS `gdromcondlies`, WHICH WAS RATED HIGH AND HAS SINCE BEEN FIXED, AND AS `sgideprimop`, FILED HIGH TONIGHT. ***  **Four registers accept exactly ONE 64-bit value and end the host process on every other**, and two more accept only a single-bit mask.  The author documented the decision rather than hiding it: each `fatal()` reads *\"from the only implemented value\"*.  That is an honest TODO expressed as a host kill.  **A guest here is firmware** -- SRM or NetBSD bringing up the chipset -- so the reaching value is whatever that firmware writes, not an attacker's choice.  A model that implements one value and dies on the rest converts every unmodelled firmware revision into a host death.  *** SCOPE, STATED SO IT IS NOT OVER-CLAIMED: the two RANGE guards (`dev >= 21`, `ofs > len`) are a DIFFERENT question from the four VALUE guards and are not analysed here. ***  A bounds guard that kills may be protecting a real array, in which case the disposition is decline-not-delete, and deciding that needs the array geometry -- which is exactly the check that withdrew `sgideoob` as a false OOB.  Named, not judged.  **SINGLE CONSUMER: `machine_alpha.c:61`, `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`.**  There is no alpha rig in this tree, which is why the file has escaped attention -- the same reason `dev_pcc2.c` did.  *No rig means no coverage rather than no bug.*  **COMPANION TO `pcc2widthkills`, AND DELIBERATELY EXAMINED SEPARATELY.**  Both files surfaced from one question -- is `dev_sgi_re.c` uniquely bad or uniquely looked at? -- but they are different classes: pcc2 is WIDTH kills (12 sites, all `len != 1`), this is VALUE kills.  Filing them as one row would have merged two fix designs, and the reopening rule already says a round takes one site unless the SAME fix design covers both.  *A census that finds two files at once is exactly where over-claiming starts.*  *** THIS ROW HAS NO RUNG-3 ATTEMPT RECORDED AT ALL, AND THERE IS A PLAUSIBLE TARGET IT NEVER NAMES.  Found 2026-08-31 by grepping the row for the attempt the 2026-08-27 lifecycle ruling requires. ***  The row reasons from *\"No rig means no coverage rather than no bug\"*, which is true about COVERAGE and is not the same claim as *no machine instantiates this device*.  **`src/machines/machine_alpha.c` calls `device_add(machine, \"lca\")` under `case ST_ALPHABOOK1:`, and that subtype is registered with the invocable alias `\"alphabook1\"`** (anchors: `grep -n 'device_add(machine, \"lca\")' src/machines/machine_alpha.c` and `grep -n 'alphabook1' src/machines/machine_alpha.c`).  So the prerequisite a rung-3 probe needs -- a real, committed, UNMODIFIED in-tree machine description that maps the device -- **exists**, and the invocation is `-E alpha -e alphabook1`.  *What is NOT established, and must not be assumed: whether that machine constructs far enough to accept a cold-debugger probe, and whether the four registers are reachable through its decode.  Those are exactly what the ATTEMPT is for.*  **The ruling's point is that the ask costs one invocation and nobody had made it** -- `witness: none` must mean *tried and could not*, never *did not think to*, and those two states must not render the same.  A seat that reports it cannot build one has given a real answer and the row proceeds as documented-only, with the attempt recorded.  *Filed as the next action on this row rather than done here: firing a probe contends with a measuring seat that was running when this was found, and a probe measured under load is the load-sensitive-oracle failure this project has already paid for once.*  *** THE STATED BLOCKER -- \"there is no rig\" -- IS NOT A REASON A RUNG-3 WITNESS IS IMPOSSIBLE, AND THIS PROJECT'S OWN MOST RECENT WITNESS REFUTES IT.  Measured 2026-08-31. ***  `#498`'s rung-3 witness for `sgidewidth` execs `gxemul -V -x -E sgi -e ip32 -M 64 0x80001000:<DUMMY>` and **`DUMMY` falls back to `/dev/null`** when `regress/dummy_kernel.bin` is absent (anchor: `grep -n 'DUMMY = ' regress/sgi_de_width_probe.py`).  **No disk image, no kernel, no boot -- a cold-debugger probe needs a machine that CONSTRUCTS and an empty load file, and that is all.**  Verified by running all four candidates under WSL with `<addr>:/dev/null`: `-E sgi -e ip32`, `-E alpha -e alphabook1`, `-E mvme88k -e mvme187` and `-e mvme188` **all reach `machine:` and print a cpu line**.  *A rig is what a rung-4 BOOT needs.  Conflating the two is what left both of these rows ranked as witness-blocked when neither had been tried.*  **WHAT IS STILL NOT ESTABLISHED, and the attempt is exactly for this:** whether the debugger reaches these registers through the machine's real decode, and whether the observable differs pre- and post-fix.  Constructing is necessary, not sufficient.  *My first run of this test reported all three machines failing -- because I ran the WSL-built ELF from git-bash and got `cannot execute binary file`, while my own grep for `GXemul|machine:` matched the word `gxemul` INSIDE that error and scored it a healthy banner.  A broken apparatus plus a check that matched the error text nearly filed a confident \"these machines do not construct\".*  For this row the target is **`-E alpha -e alphabook1`**, which is the only subtype whose arm calls `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`.  *** PASS-1 PACKET-FED PANEL, 2026-08-31 (`_scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346`).  THIS ROW'S OWN TITLE WAS WRONG, MY BRIEF CONTRADICTED ITSELF, AND BOTH ARE NOW SETTLED FROM THE SOURCE. ***  **(1) THE CENSUS.  The title said each register *accepts exactly ONE value*; that is true of ONE arm out of four.**  Derived, with the last constant looked up after glm named it as the settling fact:  `W_MASK0` accepts exactly `{0x700000}` -- one.  `W_BASE0` accepts `{0, 0x300800000}` -- **two**.  `IOC_CONF` kills iff `idata & ~1`, so it accepts `{0, 1}` -- **two**, and the predicate is *bit 0 only*, not a constant.  `TB_ENA` kills iff `idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN`, and `alpha_lcareg.h` defines `IOC_TB_ENA_TEN 0x0000000000000080ULL` -- a SINGLE bit, so it accepts `{0, 0x80}` -- **two**.  Title corrected.  *This matters beyond tidiness: the de-escalation rule owes an accept row per predicate, and a detector built from `exactly one value` would pin the wrong accept side on three of the four arms.*  **glm's sharpest point is about WHICH value to pin:** for `W_BASE0` the accepted `0` is the *maximally ordinary* write -- zeroing a window -- so an accept row pinned to the exotic magic constant *misfires on the most legal write rather than an unusual one*.  **(2) MY BRIEF CONTRADICTED ITSELF AND THE SOURCE SETTLES IT AGAINST ME.**  Q2 asserted these fields *feed address-window decoding*; Q3's note said the emulator *does not otherwise model them*.  glm caught that both cannot be true and asked which.  Measured (`grep -n 'window_base_0|window_mask_0|tlb_enable|ioc_conf' src/devices/dev_lca.c`): `window_base_0`, `window_mask_0` and `tlb_enable` are **written and read back and nothing else** -- each appears exactly twice, as `odata = d->X` and `d->X = idata`.  **Only `ioc_conf` is CONSUMED**, at `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1`.  *** SO THE FIX DESIGN SPLITS, AND IT WOULD NOT HAVE WITHOUT THIS CHECK. ***  Three of the four arms are pure BACKED ACCEPT storage -- a decline that keeps the store harms nothing, because no emulator code reads the field.  **The fourth is different**: `ioc_conf`'s low bit selects the bus, and its guard is `idata & ~1`, so a rejected value's bit 0 still reaches the consumer.  glm's *\"a decline that keeps the store is an accept with a complaint, so validate BEFORE storing and leave the previous value\"* is right **for that arm specifically**, and is over-general for the other three.  **(3) `idata` IS `uint64_t`** (`memory_readmax64`), so `0x300800000` is representable and glm's width worry is answered -- the comparison is reachable, not dead code.  **(4) Reachability stays a READING, correctly.**  All three seats declined to say whether these are the only values REAL HARDWARE accepts or merely the only ones this emulator was developed against, and read the code's own wording (`TODO`, *\"differs from the expected values\"*) as supporting the second.  There is no LCA datasheet in `_scratchpad/`.  *That is the honest form and it is flagged as a reading rather than a fact.*\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP MEASUREMENT 2026-08-31 (not a seat answer; recorded here rather than in `entries` so it cannot be counted toward the panel). ***  evidence: dev_lca.c :203 :222 :237 :252 :276 :291 (six guards); :129 inside a comment block\n\n*** THREE WRONG RECORDS ON THIS ROW, found by the rung-3 seat and RE-VERIFIED BY READING dev_lca.c before being written here. ***\n\n(1) SCOPE: there are SIX value-guarded arms, not four.  Verified at dev_lca.c :203 (idata & ~1, IOC_CONF), :222 (idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN, TB_ENA), :237 (W_BASE0), :252 (W_MASK0), :276 (W_BASE1), :291 (W_MASK1).  OUTSTANDING_BUGS scoped four-plus-two-masks (= the correct six); the LEDGER's pass-1 census picked a DIFFERENT four and silently dropped W_BASE1 and W_MASK1.  A detector built from the ledger's four ships NO ACCEPT ROW for those two, which the de-escalation clause requires per predicate.\n\n(2) THE EARLIER TITLE CORRECTION IS ITSELF WRONG.  It says the one-value claim is `true of ONE arm out of four'.  Against all six, THREE arms accept exactly one value: W_MASK0 {0x700000}, W_BASE1 {0x240000000}, W_MASK1 {0x3ff00000}.  W_BASE0 accepts two, IOC_CONF {0,1}, TB_ENA {0,0x80}.  **The original title was closer to right than the correction that overturned it** -- this project's own rule that a correction carries more authority than the error and therefore earns a SECOND reading.\n\n(3) hold_question says TWELVE live exit(1).  It is ELEVEN: `grep -c` returns 12, but dev_lca.c:129 is inside a /* ... */ block.  The row's own `witness' field already said eleven, so the row contradicted itself.\n\nCONSUMPTION, confirmed and stronger than filed: five of the six guarded arms are PURE STORAGE (each field appears exactly twice -- odata = d->X and d->X = idata).  `ioc_conf' is the sole consumer, at dev_lca.c:121 `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1'.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PREDICTION AUDIT (codex GPT-6 Astra at max, 17,859 b, complete, sentinel cleared). **NO WRONG `want` ESTABLISHED** -- 52 handler-level expectations AGREE, 21 are not independently derivable from the permitted files, and **both D11 complements are correct at 64 bits.** ***\n\nThat is the reassuring half, and it is worth stating plainly because every `want` in this probe was DERIVED BY READING `dev_lca.c` and not one was measured.  *The audit does not convert them into measurements -- the seat says so itself: \"these results remain source predictions, never measurements.\"*\n\n**MY ROW COUNT WAS WRONG.  The file emits 73 rows** -- 20 witness/control and 53 detector -- from **48 `row()` call sites, five of which expand six times.**  I had been carrying 25.  *A count taken from call sites rather than from emissions is a correlate, not the class: the expanding sites are invisible to a `grep -c '^row('`.*\n\n*** SIX ROWS ARE VACUOUS AS REPAIR DETECTORS, AND THAT WAS KNOWABLE BY READING BEFORE ANY TABLE RAN. ***\n\nAll six `<arm> attrib` rows test `alive or hit`.  **Correct survival gives `yes` -- and the ORIGINAL KILLING BEHAVIOUR, with its expected diagnostic, also gives `yes`.**  The oracle cannot vary under the mutant it is written for, which is the CHANNEL line's vacuity-by-construction in its exact form: *a row whose named channel cannot vary under its named mutant is vacuous, and that is knowable at writing time.*  Six of seventy-three.\n\n**AND TWO ROWS ARE ONE OBSERVATION WEARING TWO NAMES:** `wmask1 indep` and `wmask1 once` are the same transcript, the same needle, the same count and the same `want`.  Neither is individually vacuous against latch removal, but **they do not supply two independent observations**, and a reader counting rows reads two where there is one.\n\n**CLAUSE (e) IS NOW FACTUALLY WRONG AND MUST BE CORRECTED IN PLACE:** the contract says reserved BASE bits above bit 33 are NOT rowed.  **They ARE exercised** -- by D6 at BASE0 and by D11 at both BASE registers.  *Leaving the disclosure as written would misstate the probe in the direction that understates its coverage, which is the rarer and more confusing direction.*\n\nTwo of the D11 block's own coverage explanations were also found wrong or overbroad.\n\n**DISPOSITION: L1 -- this is pre-ship construction, not a reopening.**  The probe is an uncommitted `#518` deliverable, so six vacuous rows and a false contract sentence are unfinished work rather than coverage debt.  They are repaired before it lands, alongside the three `OWED` markers in `changelog_518_pass2.md` and the unrun `manifest_518.py`.\n\n*The seat also corrected a premise of my brief: the `dev_lca.c` it was given carries the REPAIRED IOC arms, so the killing arms quoted in the probe's opening comments are historical descriptions rather than current code -- and it said so rather than auditing the wants against code that is no longer there.*",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:34995:     "note": "grok-4.6 xhigh, file-reading (--prompt-file).  Re-derived the six store masks from the HRM: they match.  L4 IN THE DIFF: the CONF comment and announce called bit 1 the type-1 select; `type1 = d->ioc_conf & 1' (#515) says bit 0 -- bit 1 is CFG AD<1> (encodings 10/11, not a configuration-cycle type).  Reworded.  THREE PREDICTED 40/40 ONE-TOKEN EDITS: the CONF announce operand `0x2' -> `0x1' (D3 wrote 0x1 first); `window_mask = idata' on success; the WEN announce deleted at ONE label (D4 enables BASE0 first).  All three rowed (D7's leading 0x2; `wmask0 widen'/`hif'; `wen base0'/`wen base1').  Q4: PRETABLE §4 named M_nolatch_defw, M_base0_nonzero, M_mask_field, M_wen_perreg and §5's list dropped them -- the prescribed-rows-lost-in-a-rewrite shape, restored in §7.  Q5: `conf live' and `tbena live' are the two accept rows whose planned mutants leave them green (M34/M35 added).  NEW ROW: the default arm also swallows DOCUMENTED registers -- STAT0 +0x040, STAT1 +0x060, SFT_RST +0x0c0, PAR_DIS +0x0e0 -- and S6.4.14 items 1-2 require SROM to write SFT_RST and STAT0; filed as lcaiocstat0 (member 7), and the default-arm comment no longer calls them `unlisted'.  Also: the registers are WO on hardware, readback UNPREDICTABLE (:980-988) -- the read-back oracles test the model's echo POLICY, written into the contract; HEAD_74daefd.out's banner stamps 54a2eb4."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:35025:     "note": "PASS 2 on the real diff.  kimi-k3:cloud SUBSTITUTE (the CLI is on its weekly 403 wall), packet-fed.  FOUND THE LIVE DEFECT: `def read0' read nth(vals, 0), the STALE t3 printed after the store-only phase, so M20_def_echo -- in the author's own table -- passed all 40; fixed to index 1.  Named four more cells: DEF_R latch removed, SG drop, CONF mask widened (bits 2-31), cross-arm latch sharing (never co-observed) -- all rowed (D5 two reads, D6, D7) -- and the TB_ENA no-store (the accepted 0x80 equals the normalized legal value) -- rowed as `tbena clear'.  Q6: `-q' and `bit 0 is what lca_pci_conf reads' are claims without rows -- sentenced in PRETABLE §6.  Walked all seven old-accepted writes for regressions: none."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:35055:     "note": "GPT-6 Astra @ max, a RECORDS AUDIT of the CHANGELOG DRAFT against the artifacts BEFORE commit (AUDIT_brief_20260910.md; 4 content-filter markers inside the 1.3 MB stream, answer complete, NO-GO, 27 numbered items).  It opened every cited manual line, every log, both frozen instruments, the seat answers and the ruling.  MEASURED WRONG IN THE DRAFT: the 63/64 score had NO preserved log (run3 was overwritten by run4) -> re-measured from the backed-up instrument as FIX518_detector_run3_D9unsplit.out; `every log carries its sentinel' must exclude the quarantined first table-1 attempt (pre_repair_aborted/); `added rows can only add reds' is not the argument because the 64-row instrument CHANGED a session (D6's last phase) -- the per-row argument is in PRETABLE §9; `four vacuous rows' conflated six non-equivalent sweepers, four coverage gaps, and absent-vs-vacuous rows; S6.4's quadword line is :10180-10181 (:10175 is the heading); IOC_CONF's two-bit field does not make four valid PCI cycle types; `eight transports' was six of seven answering; the seats' verdicts were flattened; `member 3 throughout' contradicts the 09-09 ruling (four arms, CONF member 4, TB_ENA out of scope -- the six-arm implementation is a SCOPE REVISION the witness forced, now recorded); `no state change' at the default arm is `no IOC register-value change' (d->warned moves); the accepted-first prose overstated its guarantee; the 40-row mask-row family was five rows; PRETABLE's mtime proves nothing (the brief's verbatim copy does); `untestable' per-instance latching is `untested'; the -q clause IS this diff's claim (source-supported, unmeasured); `def width' matched the echoed put-d command -- a check that cannot fail -> re-keyed on the announce form; §4's M_wen_perreg was implemented by neither mutator -> M48 for table 4; M20's sweep cannot measure the index defect (M29 does); D9 is one silence + one announcing session; lcaiocstat0 was not yet in the ledger (it is in this commit's batch, with codex's REVISE); the Opus wall has no on-disk artifact (the two task notifications are quoted in the seat entry); propagation was already DONE at 00:24 while the draft said OWED; the two HEAD witness logs differ in timestamp and HEAD (dev_lca.c identical).  TWO ABSENT ROWS it named from its own saved answer: legal ZERO at W_MASK and SG at BASE1 -> `wmask0 zero', `wbase1 sg' (round 4), plus `def state'.  Every item is disposed in PRETABLE §9 and the block was rewritten from the artifacts."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:35073:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/lcabadval/killtable_20260910/PRETABLE.out",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:35085:     "note": "DISPOSITION OF TABLE 4's THREE ESCAPES, farmed codex, read-only: 'VERDICT: FLOOR BREACH (L2) -- M43: L2; M44: L2; M47: L2. None is equivalent.  Keep the round held.'  It CORRECTED my premise for all three: I had them as acceptance-guard weakenings; M43 and M47 weaken STORED-FIELD FILTERING (the guard is kept and the repair's mask is partly nulled -- clause (d)'s repair-mask case) and M44 substitutes the VALIDATION OPERAND so a legal normalised field can be DECLINED.  M43's non-effect on acceptance was established by enumerating all 4096 fields against 256 low-bit combinations, and the 13 accepted fields were enumerated and matched against HRM Table 6-6.  All three are guest-observable through the handler's own readback, so none is disposed of as equivalent.  It named the two rows that kill all three (W_MASK1 seed 0x700000 then 0x1001f0000; W_BASE1 seed 0x240000000 then 0x680000000) AND the arithmetic trap that 0x1fff00000 would NOT distinguish M44 because its shifted field 0x1fff has bit 13 clear.  *** AND IT PREDICTS THE TWO-ROW REPAIR IS ITSELF THE #499 TRAP ***: `(f & (f+1)) <= 2' at MASK1 (admitting exactly f = 2) and a 0xfff01000 store mask at MASK0 are predicted to sweep the resulting 69-row instrument, the third generation of this round's own window-0/window-1 asymmetry -- so what is owed is a CLASS CLOSURE matrix over {window} x {repair class} in one diff, not two rows.  Two L4s of mine: the warn-bit count is six IOC bits (2-7) plus two IRQ bits, not eight IOC bits; and the contract's 'BASE reserved bits above 33 unrowed' disclosure is stale because `wbase0 widen' already drives bit 34.  All recorded in PRETABLE section 10."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:35210:   "note": "*** THIS CLASS HAS THREE INSTANCES IN THREE DAYS AND THE STRUCTURAL FIX IS ALREADY BLESSED IN THIS PROJECT FOR THE SAME SHAPE. ***  **Instance 1, MEASURED:** `#498` added `SGIRE_W_DE_LEN = 1 << 6`; `#499`'s witness generator independently inserted `SGIRE_W_UNMAP_WR = 1 << 6`.  Legal C, silent, and because `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_de)` casts the same `struct sgi_re_data *`, `d->warned` is shared -- **14/14 total escape**, with a transcript showing one guest `sb` to `sgi_de` permanently silencing `sgi_re`'s catch-all diagnostic.  **Instance 2:** the codex seat independently proposed `1 << 7` -> `1 << 6` as the canonical clause-(d) mutant for the `#500` fix -- i.e. an adversary reaches for this collision unprompted.  **Instance 3, PREVENTED:** `#499` and `#500` were both designed for `1 << 7` while in flight together.  Caught by listing the enum before writing either fix; `#500` moves to `1 << 9`.  *Two of three instances were found by measurement AFTER the fact; the third by one `grep` BEFORE it, which is the cheap end of this.*  **THE FIX, and it is the idiom the carrier already records for `SH4_VAL_*`:** that enum was converted to a C99 enum ending in `_NCLASS` precisely so *duplicates are impossible by construction*, and the carrier marks that work DISCHARGED.  The same shape applies here with one extra step, because these are MASKS rather than ids -- enumerate the BIT POSITIONS and derive the masks:  `enum { SGIRE_WB_TLBA, SGIRE_WB_TLBB, ..., SGIRE_WB_NBITS };` then `#define SGIRE_W_TLBA_LEN (1 << SGIRE_WB_TLBA)`.  **The enumerator auto-increments, so two bits cannot collide however many rounds add one**, and `SGIRE_WB_NBITS` gives a compile-time check that the set still fits `uint32_t warned`.  **NOT a rider on either round in flight.**  It touches every latch site in the file (seven today, nine after `#499` and `#500`), it is a pure refactor with no behavioural delta, and under the ADDITION LICENCE a no-delta change needs a warrant -- which it has, in a MEASURED instance rather than in \"cannot hurt\".  It ships as its own round with the obvious control: the pre- and post-refactor binaries must be byte-identical, or the refactor was not one.\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP MEASUREMENT 2026-09-01 (not a seat answer; recorded here rather than in `entries` so it cannot be counted toward the panel). ***\n\n*** THE HAZARD STOPPED BEING HYPOTHETICAL TODAY: TWO ROUNDS IN ONE DAY WERE BOTH DESIGNED FOR `1 << 7', AND THE COLLISION WAS CAUGHT ONLY BY A HUMAN LISTING THE ENUM. ***\n\n`#499` took 1 << 7 and 1 << 8; `#500` took 1 << 9.  Both were originally written against 1 << 7.  `#500`'s own PRETABLE.out records it: *\"Both rounds were designed for 1<<7 and the collision was caught by listing the enum before writing either fix.\"*\n\n**WHAT A COLLISION WOULD HAVE COST, measured rather than argued:** `#498` measured a reused bit at 23/25, and `#500`'s own kill table measured `M_BITALIAS` (SGIRE_W_PRIM_OP aliased onto `#498`'s 1 << 6) at 18/21 -- killed by ONE row, `xlatch decl`, which exists only because a seat pointed out every other row ran in a fresh session.  **Without that row the collision would have been INVISIBLE**, and a duplicate enumerator is legal C with no diagnostic.\n\n**And it crosses DEVICES, not just windows:** the sgi_re / sgi_de / sgi_mte / sgi_de_status handlers all cast ONE malloc'd struct, so `d->warned` is shared.  A bit reused between two of them is silenced by the other's ordinary traffic.\n\n**THE FIX IS STRUCTURAL AND COSTS NOTHING AT RUNTIME**, and the tree already has the precedent: `dev_sh4.c` converted `SH4_VAL_*` to a C99 enum ending in `_NCLASS`, where duplicates are impossible by construction.  For BIT FLAGS the analogue is an index enum plus a shift --\n    enum { SGIRE_B_TLBA_LEN = 0, SGIRE_B_TLBB_LEN, ..., SGIRE_W_NBITS };\n    #define SGIRE_W_TLBA_LEN (1 << SGIRE_B_TLBA_LEN)\n-- because auto-incrementing enumerators cannot collide, whereas explicit `1 << N` values can and did nearly do so twice today.\n\n**NOT DONE THIS SESSION, and the reason is the addition licence rather than effort:** it touches product code in a file two rounds have just edited, it owes its own pass 1, and `#500`'s repair chain is still open.  Filed with the near-miss recorded so the next round does not have to rediscover that the hazard is live.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:36074:     "note": "MEASURING seat (Agent).  In its own scratch clone (instrument pair md5-verified equal to the live one; BASE reproduced 23/23) it BUILT two mutants and MEASURED both at 23/23: M_O1 the read announce's value -> 0 (one token: `named rd srvd' counts the phrase, never the value) and M_O2 `} else if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE) pcctwo_reg[ra] = idata;' -- member 6 exempting the FIRST write per offset (both rdawr rows store once to a fresh offset in a fresh process).  Diagnosis: the (writeflag x latch) 2x2 was held on the announce-COUNT channel and the latched-path DISPOSITION had no row -- the pass-1 PRETABLE's guard/body line reached the cell and the row was written against the wrong observable.  Its extra rows (latch sameoff val, srvd val, srvd val text, wr word declined) red both; adopted (as sameoff val / srvd val / srvd val text / wr declined).  Fix right: one return 1, zero exit(, fold bounds the shift, separate latch words zeroed at :731, memcpy untouched; 'the arm can only add output where the old code called exit(1), so no boot that works today can regress' (adopted as the safety sentence).  Four L4s (kill-table header md5; three->four exact; M_EXIT's 16-row set was right and only the numeral wrong; `## Records' describing unmodified files).  Also: every non-control row carries a measured kill and the seven that do not are exactly the seven declared controls; NO mvme88k boot rig exists (queue fact); the announce omits len; the witness script reads its pty helper from the live path.  26 KB + logs."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:36107:     "note": "FILE-READING (grok-4.6 xhigh, --prompt-file; 14 KB; verified against HEAD 7076134 + the working set, line numbers match the fork).  Fix right and should ship; the detector pins member 3 vs 6 and kills the six tabled mutants but does not close (d).  TWO one-line mutants predicted 23/23 by construction of the row list: A -- the latch SET conditioned on writes (READ-after-READ is the empty cell: the bit-set state was only ever reached through a write); B -- `if (len != 1) exit(1);' (every default-path row is st.b/ld.bu while the pass-1 PRETABLE's own SIBLING PARITY sentence claimed byte-and-wide).  Both ADOPTED (latch rd rd, latch rd wr; wide wr/rd) and both measured exactly as predicted in table 3.  Per-row channel table for the 23 rows; table-vs-prediction table (M_EXIT 'fifteen' vs a 16-name list).  Four L4s: the c76e053a header; the alias row vs the docstring (same file, both present tense); the Records paragraph; 'four arms up' (ten case blocks).  Also: NAMED18 includes SPEED (0x38) whose interior 0x39 is bucketed unnamed; `unnamed wr' describes an overrun M_BACKED's end-span guard prevents; the ledger state_note's 'no manual on disk' sentence reads as a live contradiction beside Table 6-1 (chronology marked at records).  Weaker escapes filed as probe-shape limits: 'declined' -> 'ignored' (now `wr declined'), debug() for fatal() (the probe steps, both print)."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:36118:     "note": "FILE-READING (kimi CLI, kimi-code/k3).  FIRST FIRING STALLED on the 54 KB POINTER form: 4.5 KB of paging monologue ('the Read tool keeps returning the whole file with a 50000-char cap') and exit with no answer -- kept as kimi.stall_pointerform.txt; RE-FIRED ONCE with the 19 KB packet inline (4.5 KB answer).  PASS: the built fix matches the packet verbatim; every checkable premise held against the tree; PRETABLE_501.out predates driver.log; M_EXIT's set was right with the arithmetic off by one; M_RDKILL's two extra reds explained by the LD_BU in each rdawr session; disposition pinned by value with the sccerr control.  A READING that did not build the edits the three dissenting seats named -- overtaken by measurement, recorded by name, not a seat failure."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:36195:     "note": "FILE-READING (GPT-6 Astra, max; 405 KB stream with a mid-run reconnect storm, 9 KB final).  NO-GO.  Read all 928 brief lines, the tree, the frozen hashes, the predictions and the individual .out files: all twelve table-3 mutants die through their claimed channels; all six .old23 at 23/23.  FOUND: (1) the tick writes back T1COUNT/T2COUNT ONLY -- six interiors change asynchronously, the six CMP interiors never do, and the new nonzero fixture (T1CMP) sits OUTSIDE the changing subset: F2 #7's 'twelve interiors' rationale corrected; (2) PRETABLE predicted `latch sameoff 2' under M_UNLATCH and the measurement is 3 (the appended read also announces unlatched) -- a safe-direction VALUE miss the record had not recorded: recorded; (3) DERIVED M_LATCH_RD_ZERO -- `else if (writeflag == MEM_READ) data[0] = 0;' -- 36/36 predicted: every rowed read after a preceding access expected 0; `srvd val' read its nonzero byte with the latch clear -- the mirror of M_BACKED2 on the READ response channel -> rows `srvd rd latch' / `srvd wr latch' (nonzero re-reads after a read-set and a write-set bit); (4) three one-token escapes: the reload (disclosed), `len' -> `4' on either arm (no byte row required `(len 1)' -> `srvd val text' reworded to `0x5 (len 1) -- served 0x34', `wr val text' carries `(len 1)'), the write value -> 0 (rowed); (5) RECORDS: the pass-2 section corrected four pass-1 sentences only in a later paragraph while the originals stood -- 'the array's default' (:29), 'no guest reaches' (:34), 'would have shipped green' (:97), 'listed fifteen' -- all corrected IN PLACE now; 857 not 875 (the 18 nested PIT-probe ok lines); F2 #10's safety sentence has a narrow warrant (recorded); SEATS.txt's 'kimi MISSING' predates the answer (reconciled in the record).  Closure denominator: 0 survivors of the 12 shapes; excludes the derived escapes above, len-2, end-span, instance scope."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:36316:     "note": "FILE-READING (kimi CLI, kimi-code/k3; the 108 KB pointer form paged fine; 326 KB of transcript ending in a formatted review and a verdict line -- unlike pass 3).  NO-GO.  It BUILT in its own scratch clone (/tmp/p4_seat_501) and MEASURED M_WRLEN2SILENT -- the write announce suppressed at len == 2 with the latch still set -- at 46/46, twice, a differential proof of the same cell every other seat named: the len-2 WRITE announce is never observed because the probe's one st.h is latch-silenced.  It grades the cell clause (d) and NO-GO; adjudicated clause (e) (len is printed, never tested by the arm -- the joint-cells rule enumerates only body-tested partitions; the Opus, grok and Fable seats read it the same way) -> DISCLOSED with its one-instruction repair spec, filed pcc2anngrid.  Q1: encoders, register plan, alignment, anchors and `srvd wr latch' 0x78 verified.  Q2: the 22 kills causal, the five safe misses understood as double-duty rows, not accidents.  Q3 mirrors: nine of ten rowed or disclosed; the tenth is the cell above; len-4 byte selection indistinguishable by construction.  RECORDS: the 44/44 (corrected), the PANEL501P4-OWED placeholder (defanged here; this pass's own marker, filled by this record), the 'both ways' denominator wording (narrowed to the widths actually announced).  Something else: the literal `(once)' marker is pinned by no row -- dropping it passes 46 (cosmetic; the latch behaviour is pinned by the count rows) -> disclosed.  Independently recomputed the 43-offset partition, all md5s, the PRETABLE/driver.log order, the gate arithmetic 857 + 10 = 867."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:36425:   "state_note": "\n\n=== MAIN-LOOP ROUND RECORDS, 2026-09-06 -- moved out of the per-seat `entries' column ===\nThese are the round's OWN records -- table results, repair descriptions, stage summaries -- not any seat's\nanswer.  They were written into `entries' with seat=`main', which is not one of the nine declared seats; the\ndashboard generator refused the file and a census confirmed the label is this session's invention rather than\nan established convention (every other entry in the ledger uses a declared seat).  The seat column is the\nper-seat record that check_boilerplate, check_seats_read and the scorecard all read, so a round note living\nthere is a round note wearing a seat's clothes -- the stamped-summary shape one level up.  Moved here verbatim;\nthe same material is in the CHANGELOG round block.\n\n  [2026-09-05 / assess] Witness built (rung 3) before any edit: HEAD 0/9 (every trap-like class stopped the machine), fix 9/9 (16:16 build).  Pre-table check PRETABLE_m88kexc.out written before table 1.\n\n  [2026-09-05 / research] [MAIN-LOOP STAGE SUMMARY, not a seat's own answer -- relabelled from an undeclared `panel' seat on 2026-09-06 when the dashboard generator refused it.  The per-seat cells for this pass are the entries that name their own seat; this one is the round's summary of the stage and is labelled as such, because a summary sitting in a seat column is the stamped-cell failure the boilerplate check exists to refuse.]  PASS 1 panel_20260905_143211: Opus (MEASURED: the one-site form halts at the tail default; supervisor tb1 naming 7 halts HEAD -- BOUNDS reachable), kimi, grok, deepseek, minimax answered; codex GPT-6 Astra REFUSED twice (content filter); glm BURNED (0-byte response, 117 KB thinking; mini packet answered); Fable WALLED (session limit) and re-fired at pass 2; agy DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED.  Preserved under regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/pass1/.\n\n  [2026-09-05 / review] [MAIN-LOOP STAGE SUMMARY, not a seat's own answer -- relabelled from an undeclared `panel' seat on 2026-09-06 when the dashboard generator refused it.  The per-seat cells for this pass are the entries that name their own seat; this one is the round's summary of the stage and is labelled as such, because a summary sitting in a seat column is the stamped-cell failure the boilerplate check exists to refuse.]  PASS 2 panel_20260905_212630: codex, kimi, grok, deepseek, minimax, Opus (measuring: four 10/10 escapes), Fable (review: delay-slot arm wrong; level observable) answered; glm BURNED then answered the mini packet ollama_glm_mini.txt; agy DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED.  Every seat: the DIFF is right; the DETECTOR needed repairs -- made (v3, 22 rows) and re-measured (table 3, 14 mutants: 13 killed, M_TRAPONLY equivalent).  Preserved under regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/pass2_panel_20260905_212630/, opus_p2.md, fable_p2.md.\n\n  [2026-09-05 / review] [MAIN-LOOP STAGE SUMMARY, not a seat's own answer -- relabelled from an undeclared `panel' seat on 2026-09-06 when the dashboard generator refused it.  The per-seat cells for this pass are the entries that name their own seat; this one is the round's summary of the stage and is labelled as such, because a summary sitting in a seat column is the stamped-cell failure the boilerplate check exists to refuse.]  ADJUDICATION PASS (F1..F24, D1..D3; brief_m88kexc_adj.md) fired ~23:10 to all nine (agy not fired): results land in the closure entry.\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] [MAIN-LOOP STAGE SUMMARY, not a seat's own answer -- relabelled from an undeclared `panel' seat on 2026-09-06 when the dashboard generator refused it.  The per-seat cells for this pass are the entries that name their own seat; this one is the round's summary of the stage and is labelled as such, because a summary sitting in a seat column is the stamped-cell failure the boilerplate check exists to refuse.]  ADJUDICATION PASS landed (pass3_panel_20260905_220902): codex, kimi, grok, deepseek, minimax answered; glm BURNED again; Opus and Fable Agent seats DIED on an Anthropic session limit (429) and are re-fired on pass 4; agy not fired.  grok/kimi CLOSE; codex/deepseek/minimax ONE MORE REPAIR -> v4: row indclr (IND never started clear -- the carry shape again), HALT classifier requires the stopped state + the named vector (+ pc == 0x40 for sfu1h), LEVEL contract narrowed to the ERROR/WARNING regression, sfu1h's channel restated (nested SFU1 halts at the fallback; with it off, re-routed to ERROR); mutants M_NOIND, M_NOSTOP; table 4 re-measures the carried-forward table-1 shapes.  Records: census (11 bypass / 10 delivered / vector 0 RESET-stop / 117 fallback), table-1 tally 11+2, M_TAILOFF equivalent, gate 2/22->3/22, PRETABLE correction appended, three phantom rows filed (m88kexctaildefault, m88ktbnd, m88kmxmkill).\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-5 REPAIR (probe v5, 29 fault rows, md5 96ab47a0 shipped / c83b1f38 frozen -- docstring-only diff): rows umisalign, uovf, errovf, errdiv0, errpriv, errunimpl; classifier keeps one `stopped` observation.  TABLE 5 (killtable5/, PRETABLE5 written 08:47 before it): BASE 29/29; M_OVFSUPONLY 28/29 (uovf), M_MISSUPONLY 28/29 (umisalign), M_SFRZMIS 25/29 (the four err* rows), M_SFRZNOOVF/NODIV0/NOPRIV/NOUNIMPL 28/29 each (one row each, as predicted), M_NOSFD1 1/29 (27 bad=psr + sfu1h STOP; resid50 the survivor).  HEAD 2/29 (probe_v5_head.out: usertb DELIVER, resid50 HALT); fix 29/29 (probe_v5_fix_final.out).  Comment corrections (Table 6-3 attribution; the tb1-naming-10 frozen case) propagated to all trees, rebuilt, two-part clearance CLEAN/FRESH x3 (rebuild_fix2.log).\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-6 REPAIR (one diff, two classes found by pass 5): probe v6 adds resid127 (the fallback window's FAR bound; table 6 = killtable6/: M_FALLBACK127 29/29 on v5 -> 29/30 on v6; M_FALLBACK51 28/29 on v5 (sfu1h saw it) -> 28/30; M_FALLBACKLE 28/29 -> 29/30 control); probe v7 adds trapfrozen4/5/6/8/9 with a harness field `handler_at` (trapfrozen8 plants its handler at the OVF entry so the frozen trap naming DIV0 is observable), class 10 disclosed as observationally identical; table 7 = killtable7/: M_SFRZTRAP4/5/6/8/9 30/30 on v6 -> 34/35 each (own row), M_SFRZTRAP 29/35.  HEAD with v7: 3/35 (usertb DELIVER, resid50 and resid127 HALT); fix 35/35.  PRETABLE6/7 written before their tables; both PRETABLE corrections were APPENDED to the same file after the table and refreshed its mtime -- stated in the record; corrections go to separate files from now on.  Gate pins 35/35 + resid127 HALT + six trapfrozen DELIVER.\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-6 REPAIR part 3 (probe v8, 36 rows, md5 e550e3fe): row usfu1 for kimi's measured M_FBSUPONLY (fallback conditioned on MODE: 35/35 on v7 -> 35/36 on v8); M_SFRZTRAP4 re-measured 35/36.  HEAD with v8: 4/36; fix 36/36.  Gate pins 36/36 + usfu1 HALT.  Pass 6 fired ~10:30 to the eight scriptable transports (Opus/Fable walled).\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-7 REPAIR (probe v9+v10, 41 rows, md5 ee2c78c2 = shipped): rows trapfrozen10, resid11, resid50ind, resid50c, trapfrozen128 and the refatal channel (the re-route's diagnostic pinned present on err* rows, absent on trapfrozen* rows).  TABLE 10 (killtable10/, PRETABLE10 before it, untouched; table 9 folded): M_ERRORFRZOFF, M_SFRZTRAP10, M_FB_LO, M_FBIND, M_FBNOC, M_SFRZTRAPUSER each 36/36 on the frozen v8 (the escapes measured) and 40/41 on v10, killed by exactly the predicted row (M_SFRZTRAP10 via bad=refatal on trapfrozen10).  BASE 41/41; HEAD 7/41 (usertb DELIVER; six HALT rows); fix 41/41.  Also repaired this pass: three gate check lines with a literal backslash-n between arguments (codex, pass 6) -- would have failed on correct output.  Gate pins 41/41 + the seven HALT rows + eight frozen-trap rows + refatal absent.\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-7 REPAIR (probe v10 -> v14, 46 fault rows; shipped v15 md5 eb11ab7c, identical to frozen v14 outside the module docstring, AST-compared).  Rows added: trapfrozen450 / trapfrozen496 (codex: switch 1's last two deliverable labels), trapfrozen511 (kimi: the exemption's far edge, tb1's 9-bit field), errind / errc (grok: the re-route x {IND, C} -- the re-route predicate reads PSR, so a PSR-bit conjunct there is clause (d), unlike the switch-1 arms).  TABLE 11 (killtable11/, PRETABLE11 11:49 + addendum 2 11:55 + addendum 3 12:16, all before the 12:17 start; frozen v14 md5 99eb9c00; each mutant ALSO scored on the frozen v10): M_SFRZTRAP450 41/41 -> 45/46; M_SFRZTRAP496 41/41 -> 45/46; M_SFRZTRAPHI 41/41 -> 43/46; M_ERRORFRZON 40/41 -> 45/46 (kimi's sweep CLAIM refuted by measurement, as the existing errortb/bounds rows predicted); M_MISNOC 41/41 -> 45/46 (errc HALT: the disclosed {MISALIGNED} x {C} representative is killed as a side effect of the re-route rows -- disclosure narrowed in v15's docstring); M_RRIND 41/41 -> 45/46 (errind); M_RRNOC 41/41 -> 45/46 (errc).  Six measured sweeps of v10, each killed by the row the PRETABLE named.  BASE 46/46 before and after; clone cmp-clean, sentinel clear.  A MIS-CUT ROW was caught by the driver's own BASE gate (killtable11_attempt1_v13/): v13's errc wanted PSR 0x8000000b and exception entry leaves C alone, so BASE read 45/46 bad=psr before any mutant and the driver aborted -- PSR_WANT repaired in the instrument only (v14), no product code, no mutant re-aimed.  HEAD 7/46 (probe_v14_head.out, re-run on the HEAD binary with the final instrument); fix 46/46 (probe_v14_fix_final.out).  Gate pins 46/46, err(...|ind|c) 6, trapfrozen(...|511)? 11.  Pass 8 packet drafted (brief_m88kexc_p8.DRAFT.md).\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS 8 FIRED (_scratchpad/panel_20260906_150800_m88k_p8/, brief = the FROZEN artifact copy regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/brief_m88kexc_p8.md, per the new template section on citing frozen copies).  Seat state MEASURED at launch, not assumed: opus5 ALIVE and fired first (it is the only measuring-seat review this round gets before it commits, since fable5 is walled); codex, kimi and grok fired and running -- the brief is 31,166 b, above the 30,000 argv cap, so codex and kimi got the POINTER form and grok is immune (--prompt-file).  *** THE THREE OLLAMA CLOUD SEATS ARE DOWN ON AN ACCOUNT-LEVEL QUOTA, NOT A TRANSIENT RATE LIMIT, AND THE SEAT FILES COULD NOT SAY SO. ***  Two firings forty minutes apart returned `OLLAMA_SEAT_ERROR: <HTTPError 429: 'Too Many Requests'>` and nothing else, which is indistinguishable from a five-minute wait; a direct POST to the same endpoint carried the reason in the response BODY -- a WEEKLY account usage limit.  Owner states it resets in about four hours from ~15:10 (so ~19:10).  panel_ollama.py now reads the HTTPError's body and appends it, CONTROLLED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS on the live wall: the fixed form prints the weekly-limit text, the pre-fix form from HEAD prints the bare status.  Pass 8's Ollama third fires when the quota resets; until then the stage is 6/9 and the three are recorded DOWN by name, never as agreement.  agy remains DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (owner ruling, reserved for measurement).\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] **TABLE 12 -- THE DISCRIMINATING TABLE.  THE PASS-8 PANEL SPLIT IS SETTLED BY MEASUREMENT, AND codex AND grok ARE RIGHT.**  (regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/killtable12/ with its driver log; PRETABLE12 written 15:58:33 and the first BASE at 16:00:48, so the order proof is the file's own mtime; the frozen instrument is the SHIPPED probe v15, md5 eb11ab7c -- which also closes the records gap kimi named, since the gate runs the shipped file and earlier tables measured a frozen copy of an older revision.)  | mutant | on frozen v10 | on shipped v15 | red rows |  M_RRTRAPIND `if (!is_trap || !(PSR & IND))` | 41/41 | **46/46 SWEEP** | none |  M_RRTRAPC `if (!is_trap || (PSR & C))` | 41/41 | **46/46 SWEEP** | none |  CTL_RRTRAPINV `if (!is_trap || (PSR & IND))` -- THE POLARITY TWIN | 33/41 | **35/46** | ELEVEN: trapfrozen, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 450, 496, 511, 128 all STOP, and trapfrozen10 BADREGS bad=refatal |  **THE CONTROL IS WHAT MAKES THE SWEEPS MEAN SOMETHING.**  It is the same edit with one token changed -- the sense of the bit test -- and it reddens exactly the eleven frozen-trap rows, on both channels the PRETABLE named (the entry pc moves to 0x50, so the breakpoint is never reached; and trapfrozen10 additionally shows the re-route's diagnostic where REFATAL_EXPECT says it must not appear).  So the find-text landed, the probe CAN see this site, and the two 46/46 results are genuine blindness rather than a mis-built mutant -- the three explanations a non-dying mutant has, reduced to one.  BASE 46/46 before and 46/46 after; HEAD 7/46 with the same instrument; the rebuilt build/ 46/46; clone cmp-clean against SEC with no sentinel.  All five PRETABLE12 predictions held, including the control's.  **WHAT IT SETTLES.**  kimi's `killable-and-undisclosed survivors: ZERO' is refuted at the RE-ROUTE as it already was at the switch-1 arms -- and the reason is visible in kimi's own enumeration, which wrote the re-route family as `{label trap x 11}': the eleven rows cover eleven LABELS in ONE status state, and every one of them arrives with PSR 0x8000000b, so no row varies the trap side's status at all.  Counting labels is not covering the axis.  **WHAT IS NOW OWED.**  Three consecutive passes have found this class at three sites -- the fallback predicate (pass 6), the re-route's non-trap side (pass 7), and now the re-route's TRAP side plus the switch-1 arms (pass 8, measured here and by opus5) -- so the class-closure amendment applies and the repair must close the enumerated space in ONE diff rather than buy another cell.  opus5's five rows close the arms; these two cells close the re-route's trap side.  L2, in-round.\n\n*** THE PASS-5 PANEL WAS CITED AT A DURABLE PATH THAT DID NOT EXIST; THE EVIDENCE WAS SITTING IN\n`_scratchpad/`, WHICH IS NEVER COMMITTED.  Found and fixed 2026-09-08. ***\n\nFive seat entries cite `regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/pass5_panel_20260906_090810/<seat>.txt`.\n**That directory did not exist.**  The panel was at `_scratchpad/panel_20260906_090810/` -- the\nvolatile path -- so the citations were unverifiable and the answers were one cleanup from gone.\n\n**COPIED to the cited path**, and all five now resolve:\n\n    codex.txt          1,299,047 b      grok.txt              5,213 b\n    ollama_glm.txt         3,704 b      ollama_minimax.txt    7,559 b\n    ollama_deepseek.txt    1,297 b      SEATS.txt               829 b\n\n*** AND A SIXTH SEAT ANSWERED THAT THE LEDGER NEVER CITED: `kimi.txt`, 312,574 BYTES. ***  It is\nrecorded in no entry on this row.  **That is an UNREAD REVIEW -- not a seat failure and not\nagreement -- and this project has found real defects in one four times.**  Preserved with the rest;\n**reading it is an outstanding obligation on this row**, not discharged here.\n\n**A SIZE NOTE, recorded rather than acted on unilaterally: `codex.txt` is 1.3 MB.**  The carrier\nrecords that a codex research run here has exceeded 1 MB, and `adjudicate.sh` takes only the TAIL\nfor that reason.  **I did NOT truncate it**: the ledger cites this file as a seat's evidence, and\ntruncating cited evidence to save space converts a verifiable citation into a modified artifact --\nwhich is worse than a large file.  If the repository size matters more than the citation, that is\nan owner decision and this note is where it should start.\n\n> **THE GENERAL FINDING, which is why this was worth an audit rather than a fix: the volatile-path\n> rule was OBEYED for the round's own logs and MISSED for its panel.**  A round that carefully\n> copies its kill-table output to `regress/artifacts/` can still leave the panel that reviewed it in\n> `_scratchpad/`, because the panel feels like process rather than evidence.  **It is evidence: the\n> ledger cites it by path.**\n\n*Found by an EXISTENCE audit over every artifact path in the ledger's witness and `ev` fields --\n71 witness citations, all resolving, and 181 `ev` paths of which these five were the only misses.\nAn existence check cannot false-positive the way a count or a proximity grep can, which is why it is\nworth running and the stale-count audit was declined.*\n\n*** THE AUDIT'S FIRST RUN REPORTED EIGHT ADDITIONAL MISSES AND EVERY ONE WAS ITS OWN BUG. ***  Bare\nfilenames (`check_witness.py`, `gate_arm.sh`, `precommit_check.sh` ...) were joined to the SEC root\nwhen they live in `tools/pipeline/` and `regress/`, and one citation carried a `GXEMUL-SEC/` prefix\nthat the join then doubled.  **With the resolver fixed: 71 of 71.**  *Recorded because reporting\nthose eight as ledger defects would have been exactly the wrong-apparatus failure this project keeps\ncatching -- and the tell was that they were all HARNESS files, which is a suspicious population.*\n\n=== 2026-10-16 -- the close adjudication, repairs 13 and 14 (probe v22, v23) ===\nThe four-seat close adjudication split 3-1 (Astra, Grok, GLM CLOSE; Fable DO NOT CLOSE) and the split was settled by MEASUREMENT: `implemented = !cpu->delay_slot' swept the 59-row probe at four arms (delayslot_20261016/).  Repair 13 (v22, four dslot* rows) went to pass 14, which found the same axis open at more cells (Fable: the {7 labels} x {SFRZ} x {delay_slot} grid, 28 cells, 19 rowed; Astra: the re-route sides, measured in table 17).  Two consecutive passes, one class: repair 14 (v23, 73 rows) closes the grid 28/28 plus the fallback row, in one diff.  Tables 16, 16b, 17, 18, 19 and 19b in m88kexcdeliver/repair13_20261016/, every score as predicted in a PRETABLE written first.  The pass-13 F5 sentence, adopted: only PSR & SFRZ is TESTED by the delivery path, so the PSR-bit cells other than SFRZ at all four sites are clause (e), closed voluntarily; their recorded L2 grades (v16/v17/v19) were given under a reads-PSR criterion that came from clause (d)(2), which enumerates MUTANTS TO BUILD, not CELLS TO ROW.  The criterion this round uses, named: a state value the delivery path TESTS -- which is why delay_slot is floor and MODE/SFD1/IND/C/SER are not.\nPass 15 (repair13_20261016/pass15/), the closing pass on v23, split 2-2: Astra and Grok CLOSE, Fable and GLM DO NOT CLOSE.  Ruled by the argument: Fable's -- is_trap is the fourth input the delivery path tests, an input bit at every floor cell under the carrier's generating-set clause, and this chain repaired the same shape in-round on passes 5 and 7 -- so the five measured 73/73 escapes of table 19 are floor breaches (L2).  The chain stays OPEN: repair 15 (probe v24, ten is_trap cells, the grid {7 labels} x {SFRZ} x {slot} x {is_trap} = 48 reachable cells, after which the function has no untested input) is in flight, measured in table 20; its own pass follows.  GLM's user-trap labels x {slot} are disclosed (outside the fix's seven labels).\n\n=== 2026-10-16 -- repair 15 (probe v24) and pass 16 ===\nRepair 15 = probe v24 (73 -> 84 fault rows): the is_trap axis at the five instruction labels -- dslot<L>tb (unfrozen) and trapfrozen<L>dslot (frozen) -- plus sfu1hdslot (the fallback's frozen slot cell, clause (e), rowed on its measured escape).  Table 20 (39 runs, PRETABLE_20.out first) as predicted except trapfrozen9dslot's +8 handler geometry (its target was its own OVERFLOW breakpoint): an L1 construction defect, fixed to +16 and re-measured in table 20b (PRETABLE_20b.out first), all as predicted.  Pass 16 (four seats) CLOSE, records applied, two clause-(e) rows filed (m88kutrapdslot, m88kresidtbdslot).  CLOSED.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:36751:     "note": "PASS 6: ONE MORE REPAIR -- (i) switch 1's ERROR arm is a predicate of its own: a frozen tb1 naming 10 enters switch 1 AS ERROR (no v8 row did), so `implemented = !(PSR & SFRZ)` there (M_ERRORFRZOFF) is predicted 36/36 -> v9 row trapfrozen10 (the class-10 disclosure was true only for the re-route mutant); (ii) A GATE REGRESSION: the three check lines added this morning carried a LITERAL backslash-n between arguments, so check() compared the wrong pair and all three would fail on correct output -- repaired before any gate ran; (iii) the PRETABLE chronology is an unverified historical premise (agreed: stated as such).",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:36869:     "note": "PASS 8: **ONE MORE REPAIR -- and it SETTLED THE PANEL'S DISAGREEMENT BY MEASUREMENT, which is the only way this project resolves one.**  codex and grok predicted a floor gap; kimi enumerated four families and reported ZERO survivors.  Opus BUILT the mutants: **THREE CLEAN SWEEPS AT 46/46 ON THE SHIPPED COMPOSITE**, row-for-row identical to BASE -- `case ILLEGAL_INTEGER_DIVIDE: implemented = (PSR & IND) != 0`, `case ERROR: implemented = !(PSR & C)`, and `case BOUNDS_CHECK_VIOLATION: implemented = (PSR & IND) != 0`.  So the class is at the SWITCH-1 ARMS, not only at the re-route where pass 7 repaired it.  Its polarity twins prove the mutants are not inert: CTL_DIV0INDINV 23/46, CTL_OVFNOC 45/46 (ovfc), CTL_DIV0SFD1 45/46 (trapfrozen8).  Eight predictions, eight hits; PRETABLE 15:13:15, first build 15:14; BASE 46/46 before and after; clone cmp-clean, no sentinel.  Artifacts preserved under regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/killtable_opus_p8/ plus CENSUS_TABLE_p8.md, KILLTABLE_OPUS_P8_RESULTS.md and RECOMMENDED_REPAIR_p8.txt.  **WHAT M_DIV0IND COSTS, and it is why this is blindness rather than an inert edit: a guest divide-by-zero taken with INTERRUPTS ENABLED stops every emulated CPU.**  IND-clear is the NORMAL state of running kernel and user code, and every DIV0 row in the probe enters either from reset (PSR 0x80000002 = MODE|IND) or from inside a frozen handler -- so the instrument's entire population sits in the abnormal state for that bit.  One token, 46/46.  **ITS DENOMINATOR IS MEASURED, NOT LISTED**: a `fatal()` census at function entry harvested from the probe's own transcripts, 43 distinct (vector, PSR, is_trap) triples, with the census build itself scoring 46/46 so it is non-perturbing; derived by reading first, then measured, and identical.  **7 arms x 5 stcr-settable 88100 PSR bits = 35 cells; 2 UNREACHABLE with a warrant (BOUNDS/ERROR x MODE -- a user tb1 naming 7 or 10 raises PRIVILEGE, and no instruction raises 7 or 10); 33 reachable; 23 closed; TEN OPEN: {UNIMPL, PRIV, BOUNDS, DIV0, ERROR} x {C, IND}.**  Only three rows in the whole probe ever present C = 1 and only three ever present IND = 0.  **ANSWERING THE ROUND'S OWN CUT: `the arm reads no status bit' is the WRONG TEST**, on four grounds, and the round need not lose the floor-versus-debt argument for the repair to be owed.  (i) It is not the carrier's test, which speaks of the device-STATE lvalues the fix's own REPAIR PATH reads -- path, not statement -- and that path reads PSR twice.  (ii) The round does not follow it at two of its three sites: the FALLBACK predicate reads no PSR bit either, yet pass 6 shipped resid50ind and resid50c for exactly these conjuncts and the contract lists them as CLOSURE.  (iii) Clause (d) asks what a plausible future edit does, and M_DIV0IND is the same SHAPE as M_RRIND two hundred lines earlier in the same function, restoring the same kill.  (iv) The contract's only measured evidence for its debt is M_MISNOC, which landed on a CLOSED cell.  Cut right -> L4 wrong record plus two undisclosed arms; cut wrong -> L2 floor breach; either way in-round.  **THE DISCLOSURE IS MEASURABLY WRONG THREE WAYS**: it names OVERFLOW (measured CLOSED by ovfc), claims {any arm} x {SFD1} (measured closed at all seven -- at DIV0 architecturally, since X(divu) tests SFD1 BEFORE the divisor so only the tb1 route reaches that arm with SFD1 set), and OMITS BOUNDS and ERROR, which are open.  Its provenance contradicts it: kimi's pass-7 answer said `sweeps on every arm except OVF', which is exactly the census; the transcription added OVERFLOW and dropped BOUNDS/ERROR.  **THE REPAIR, BOUNDED: five rows, one diff, 46/46 -> 51/51.**  One PSR value closes BOTH open cells at an arm because the open cells are always (C never set) and (IND never clear): PSR = 0x90000000 (MODE|C, IND clear).  Rows unimpltbc, privtbc, boundstbc, div0tbc, errortbc, each `[STCR_R5_CR1, TB1_0_R3_n]` with fault_pc 0x10008 and PSR_WANT 0x9000000b -- the exact shape of resid50c.  MISALIGNED and OVERFLOW need nothing.  SMALLER RECORDS ITEMS, all checked: the gate carries a check label saying `43 fault rows' while pinning 46/46, and its header comment says the same -- stale by three; this row's ledger `witness' field still reads 3/22 and fix 22/22, eight instrument revisions behind; the contract's `{SFRZ set, is_trap} x EVERY deliverable switch-1 label' is literally false, since 0..3 are deliverable and unrowed (disclosed elsewhere as m88kexchwtraps, but EVERY overclaims); and the product comment says the pre-fix path stopped EVERY guest CPU while the cited rows run on a one-CPU testm88k, so no row observes the multi-CPU half.  It also confirms kimi's finding independently: the stale REFATAL_EXPECT trailer was DUPLICATED, not removed -- frozen v10 has one occurrence, shipped v15 has two.  APPARATUS, disclosed rather than hidden: its first table run aborted on the carrier's own Bash-to-heredoc backslash trap while building the census, was fixed with chr(92), dry-run and relaunched, and the `[ binary -nt source ]` gate is what caught it before a stale binary could be scored.  It ran concurrently with the #503 table at -j2 and reports no unexpected STOP, so nothing needed a solo re-run.  Wrote only to /tmp/gxopus502p8 and its own scratchpad; cpu_m88k.c and the probe md5s unchanged; every project tree untouched.  [PASS 8; panel dir _scratchpad/panel_20260906_150800_m88k_p8]"
