GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:37188:   "note": "DEFECT, HIGH -- MEASURED (rung 3).  dev_luna88k.c: reassert_interrupts() (anchor `static void reassert_interrupts') walks `for (cpu = 0; cpu < MAX_CPUS; cpu++)' and calls INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->cpu_irq[cpu]) -- interrupt.h expands that to a direct call through `.interrupt_assert', no NULL check -- while DEVINIT connects cpu_irq[1..3] only under `if (devinit->machine->ncpus >= 2/3/4)' and memset()s the device to zero.  The INT_ST_MASK0-3 store arm (anchor `case INT_ST_MASK0:') does `d->interrupt_enable[cpunr] = idata; reassert_interrupts(d);' with NO ncpus check, and the SOFT_INT0-3 arm sets a per-slot pending bit and calls reassert synchronously.  On the default ONE-cpu luna-88k two ordinary guest word stores -- 0xfc000000 -> 0x65000004 (INT_ST_MASK1), then 1 -> 0x69000004 (SOFT_INT1) -- call a NULL function pointer: the emulator process dies with SIGSEGV, no diagnostic.  WITNESS: _scratchpad/lunaabs/luna_absentcpu_probe.py on the HEAD binary /tmp/gxopus501p4 (dev_luna88k.c cmp-equal to git HEAD 83df95c), committed unmodified `-e luna-88k', cold debugger: C1 RAM liveness OK, C2 device signature OK (MASK0 >>8), A1 the SAME two stores on the CONNECTED slot 0 SURVIVE (attribution), A2 the MASK1 store alone SURVIVES (readback 0x00fc0000: stored as-is), W1 SOFT_INT1 store: HOST DIED signal 11.  LUNA_ABSENTCPU_WITNESS=PRE_FIX_SYMPTOM, 1.4 s; log regress/artifacts/lunaabsentcpu/witness_head_20260905_2130.out, probe as run beside it.  Pre-existing upstream (the loop and the connect guards are 0.7.0 code); #438 removed the exit(1) on low bits in the SAME arm; its probe's D4 row stores 0x84000100 to MASK1 (bits 31, 26, 8 -- bit 26 SET, slot 1 ARMED for the rest of that session) and could not see this only because a cold debugger has nothing pending and the probe never stores SOFT_INT1 (an earlier wording here said 'bit 26 CLEAR'; corrected by the batch-7 Fable adjudication, which also notes that ANY device interrupt after the arming store kills the host on a running guest).  OPTION SET -- batch-7 Fable adjudication RULING (2026-09-05 ~22:40): (a) ALONE -- bound the reassert loop to `d->ncpus' captured at DEVINIT (house idiom dev_mp.c:134/:191 and this file's own RESET_CPU_ALL arm `i < cpu->machine->ncpus'); (b) declining stores to absent slots is REJECTED for this round (an unsourced board-model claim, changes guest-visible readback, reddens two shipped es438 rows D4/E2) and FILED as the question row lunaabsentmaskdecl; (c) a no-op connection fabricates plumbing with no in-tree idiom; (e) a NULL check guards the symptom.  Detector: W1 kill row + the connected-slot control + a `-n 2' ACCEPT row whose channel is cpu 1 ASSERTING (irq_asserted / vector entry), not survival; PRETABLE mutants `cpu <= d->ncpus', ncpus never assigned (slot 0 never asserted: the boot gate reds), `ncpus = 1' hardcoded.  Wire luna_absentcpu_probe.py into gate_m88k_rounding.sh.  Also owed in that round: es438cells (MASK2/MASK3 readback rows).  Ranked HIGH: guest-reachable process death by an ordinary instruction on the machine the project boots (precedent: pcc2defaultkill, m88kexcdeliver ranked on reachability, not on a known driver doing it).  Provenance: retrospective batch 7 -- GPT-6 Astra (codex, max effort, read-only) predicted it from the source ('interrupt delivery can still reach an unconnected CPU callback'); the main loop built and ran the witness the same evening; Fable 5.1 adjudication of batch 7 in flight.  The fix round runs the full nine-seat panel.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:37189:   "state_note": "[2026-10-09, queue 7n-2, Fable 5.1 + GPT-6 Astra] CLAUSE (c): by construction via the gate's pins, leg NOT run -- regress/gate_m88k_rounding.sh's luna section reds a missing LUNAABS_DONE (the guarded idiom) and pins the result line, the live/devsig RESULT=OK pair and the HALT/NOSTART rows by EQUALITY, so an absent or non-emulator binary reds it (Astra: every startup fails, the session branches give 0/14 and FOUR gate checks red -- not the ruling's three), and a device-absent build reds devsig and the device rows.  The probe header's 'identity (floor c)' label is a TRUE by-construction claim, not an L4.  ||  \n\n=== MAIN-LOOP ROUND RECORDS, 2026-09-06 -- moved out of the per-seat `entries' column ===\nThese are the round's OWN records -- table results, repair descriptions, stage summaries -- not any seat's\nanswer.  They were written into `entries' with seat=`main', which is not one of the nine declared seats; the\ndashboard generator refused the file and a census confirmed the label is this session's invention rather than\nan established convention (every other entry in the ledger uses a declared seat).  The seat column is the\nper-seat record that check_boilerplate, check_seats_read and the scorecard all read, so a round note living\nthere is a round note wearing a seat's clothes -- the stamped-summary shape one level up.  Moved here verbatim;\nthe same material is in the CHANGELOG round block.\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-2 REPAIR (one diff): fix capture moved below the `ncpus > 4` exit; probe v3 adds sessions B2 (sio5 deliver), C2 (both deliver) and E (-n 4: four cpus / cpu3 deliver / others idle); docstring corrections; mutants M_MASK30, M_SWONLY, M_IRQACTIVE0, M_AND1, M_MIN2 added to the eleven; PRETABLE2 written before table 2 (corrections to go to a separate file).  Table 1 (killtable/, v2, 22 rows): BASE 22/22 PASS; M_REVERT/M_OFFBY1/M_LATCH 15/17, M_NULLA/M_GUARDASSERT 16/17, M_NOASSIGN 17/19, M_HARD1/M_SKIP1 19/20, M_NULLAB 22/22 (equivalent), M_NOREASSERT 22/22 (debt), M_CALLGUARD 16/18 -- the pass-1 F4 alternative measured NOT a fix (sio5, siofirst HALT: the SIO line reaches the walk through the device-line callers).  HEAD 15/17 PRE_FIX_SYMPTOM (soft1, siofirst; the denominator drops from 22 when the host dies mid-session).\n\n  [2026-09-05 / assess] WITNESS built before any edit (rung 3, unmodified -e luna-88k): two supervisor stores (INT_ST_MASK1 <- 0xfc000000, SOFT_INT1 <- 1) on the default one-cpu machine end the emulator with SIGSEGV -- witness_head_20260905_2130.out.  Filed from retrospective batch 7 (Fable adjudication of the m8820x rounds) after the #438 probe missed it: that probe stored 0x84000100 (bit 26 SET) but never stored SOFT_INT1, so nothing was pending when the walk ran.\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-3 REPAIR (probe v4 -> v5, 34 rows, frozen v5 md5 3fd0198f; fix v2b unchanged): v4 added sessions B3 (redeliver, reenter, noreenter) and C3 (noreenter1) for the deassert side (deepseek, minimax) and pended slots 2 AND 3 in E (grok, kimi: M_SKIP2); v5 (kimi, landed after v4 was cut) pends ALL FOUR slots in E (cpu0/cpu1 deliver4, others-idle removed) and adds C4 (sio5 deliver1: a device line to slot 1).  TABLE 3 (killtable3/) and TABLE 4 (killtable4/, PRETABLE4 written 11:00 before the 11:31 start, untouched): 22 mutants, BASE 34/34 before and after, clone cmp-clean; every mutant killed except M_NULLAB 34/34 -- PREDICTED EQUIVALENT (a both-sided NULL guard is the same predicate as the bound on every reachable configuration; DEVINIT connects exactly slots 0..ncpus-1 and the ncpus > 4 exit bounds ncpus), recorded as an alternative fix with its warrant, not a breach.  M_IRQACTIVEP1 32/34 (cpu2/cpu3 deliver: the parity alias), M_NOREASSERT 31/33 (killed by the noreenter rows; the v2/v3 disclosure withdrawn), M_CALLGUARD 28/30 (pass 2 had predicted it equivalent; it is not).  HEAD 83df95c with v5: 27/29 PRE_FIX_SYMPTOM (soft1, siofirst).  PRETABLE4's one mis-prediction was in the safe direction (it said M_NOREASSERT would stay green on the NEW rows; it stays killed by the v4 rows).  Pass 4 (closing) fired to the scriptable seats at ~12:28 (brief_lunaabs_p4.md); Opus 5 / Fable 5 owed when the wall lifts.\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] PASS-4 REPAIR, graded L2 (a clause-(d) floor breach: an operand swap drawn from a field the guarded body READS, on the predicate dominating the walk's body) -- repaired IN-ROUND, not filed.  Because passes 3 and 4 both found breaches of the {slot} x {per-cpu array} alias class, the repair closes the CLASS in one diff rather than the cell: probe v6 (md5 3be01e9d) RESTORES v4's session E beside v5's all-four E as session E2 (slots 2 and 3 pending, 0 and 1 idle; rows cpu3 deliver23, cpu2 deliver23, others idle23 -- 37 rows), in which every per-cpu array the walk touches has a disagreeing alias target.  Mutants added: M_ENP1, M_SWP1 (the two predicted sweeps, each ALSO scored on the frozen v5 so the escape is measured), M_EN0, M_SW0, and -- instead of disclosing the other alias forms as debt -- M_ENX1 (`cpu ^ 1`, derived to be killed by sio5 deliver1) and M_ENSH1 (`cpu >> 1`, killed by E2).  A first draft of PRETABLE5 had disclosed `cpu ^ 1` as an escape; that was wrong about C4 and was corrected BEFORE the table ran.  Table 5 (24 mutants) launched 12:5x.\n\n  [2026-09-06 / review] **PASS 5 -- TWO OF MY OWN RECORDS ARE WRONG, both found by codex AND grok independently, and I VERIFIED BOTH BY OPENING THE FILES.**  (1) **THE NARROWED M_NULLAB EXPIRY IS FALSE, and it came from the opus5 pass-4 answer, which I propagated without checking.**  That clause said an EMPTY interrupt name installs non-NULL `no_interrupt_assert' / `no_interrupt_deassert' stubs, so `has a callback' and `slot < ncpus' differ in MECHANISM.  Read `src/core/interrupt.c' (anchor `if (name[0] ==' the empty-string test): the arm assigns the stubs and **DOES NOT RETURN** -- the handler scan runs immediately after it, and an unregistered name falls through to `printf(... failed)' and `abort()`.  So the stub path is DEAD unless the empty string is itself a registered handler name, and `INTERRUPT_CONNECT(\"\")` appears nowhere.  The two predicates coincide **because the stub path is dead**, not because slots 1..3 happen to use non-empty names.  grok adds that pass 1 already had this.  The M_NULLAB equivalence is UNAFFECTED and the fix is not reopened; only the expiry sentence is corrected.  (2) **THE PASS-5 PACKET NAMES THE WRONG RED ROWS FOR M_IRQACTIVEP1.**  Its table says `cpu2 deliver23, cpu3 deliver23' (session E2).  killtable5/driver.log at the `=== M_IRQACTIVEP1' anchor says **`cpu3 deliver' and `cpu2 deliver' -- session E**, and E2 passes.  Verified.  The mutant aliases a WRITE, so the first arrival of each pair consumes the latch whichever session pends them; E2 exists for the READ aliases and correctly does not red here.  A transcription error in a packet that had the log open beside it.  **WHAT THE TWO SEATS RULED ON THE ROUND'S ONE QUESTION: the closure bound HOLDS.**  grok: `function of cpu alone' is the right bound for THIS class, with the derivation -- the one-cpu session forces f(0)=0, session C forces f(1)=1, session E2 forces f({2,3}) inside {2,3}, so every single-token map except the identity dies; cells outside f(cpu) are body-field indices (`enable[irqActive[cpu]]' is [0] on the first walk and dies on C); a joint same-f on enable and software_interrupt_status still dies on E2 (opus5 measured M_ENP1 and M_ENP1E both 35/37); and expanding past single-token f(cpu) is the unbounded chase.  No clean-sweep sits in the remainder.  **ONE REAL PROBE DEFECT, ship-time rather than a new pass (grok): session E2 never sets cpu 0's VBR/PSR, though session E does.**  So `others idle23' can see a steal onto cpu 1 (M_AND1 reads BADREGS with pcs[1] at the vector) and CANNOT see one onto cpu 0 -- a CHANNEL filter on an existing row, not a new cell.  Fix: copy E's two sends.  Both seats also note the product-comment L4 is still present in killtable5/baseline_dev_luna88k.c -- correct, and it stays: that file is the frozen record of what the table ran.  The SHIPPING script (fix_lunaabs.py) was corrected before either answer landed.\n\n=== TABLE 6, 2026-09-06 ===\nTABLE 6 (regress/artifacts/lunaabsentcpu/killtable6_codexpred/), run against codex's two predictions on the shipped 37-row instrument.  BASE 37/37 opening and closing; scratch clone clean against SEC; no sentinel left; frozen probe md5 7170ae7a.  **M_LOWBIT `cpu < (d->ncpus & -d->ncpus)`: 37/37, TOTAL ESCAPE.  M_SOFTAND1 `software_interrupt_status[cpu]&1`: 37/37, TOTAL ESCAPE.**  *** BOTH ARE MEMBERS OF FAMILIES THIS PROBE HAD ALREADY DISCLOSED, WHICH IS CLAUSE (e) WORKING RATHER THAN A FLOOR BREACH. ***  M_SOFTAND1 is the `== 1` cell opus5 disclosed on pass 4 (the body tests nonzero-ness and never partitions the nonzero class; every SOFT value any session writes is 1, so any predicate true of 1 preserves every observation) -- two spellings, one cell.  M_LOWBIT is the ncpus-dependent fold kimi NAMED on pass 5 before a member existed, writing that it folds only at `-n 3' which no session boots.  Contract upgraded from NAMED to MEASURED in probe v8 (docstring-only; proved by comparing executable structure with docstrings stripped against the frozen v7 the table ran).  Closure filed as lunancpus3.\n\n\n*** CLOSED 2026-09-07 AS A RECORDS CORRECTION.  THE FIX SHIPPED IN `79cd12e` ON 2026-09-06 AND THE\nROW WAS NEVER UPDATED. ***\n\n`dev_luna88k.c` carries the repair BY NAME: *\"#503 (lunaabsentcpu): bound `reassert_interrupts()` to\nthe slots connected above.  It walked all MAX_CPUS and called the NULL interrupt_assert of an\nunpopulated slot ... measured, `regress/luna_absentcpu_probe.py`.\"*  The loop bound changed from\n`cpu < MAX_CPUS` to `cpu < d->ncpus`, the `ncpus` snapshot field was added with its own comment, and\nthe 48 kB probe was committed in the same commit.  Kill tables ran; two total escapes were measured\nand both disposed under clause (e) as members of families the probe had already disclosed; the\nresidual is filed as `lunancpus3`.\n\n**So the work was complete and only the row was wrong** -- state `open`, `commits` empty, severity\nHIGH.  It inflated the open-HIGH count and would have misled anyone picking the next round: I\nreported ten open HIGH rows earlier today and the true figure was nine.\n\n*** THE SWEEP, because one instance is not a population. ***  Two derivers were run and the first was\nwrong in the way this project keeps re-learning:\n  * **keyed on a CORRELATE** -- open rows citing a recent commit SHA anywhere in their note -- gave\n    EIGHT, and seven were false: they cite a commit as CONTEXT (a blocker, the round they audit, a\n    records commit), not as their own fix;\n  * **keyed on what DEFINES the class** -- row ids named inside a `#NNN (rowid)` fix tag in `src/` --\n    gave THREE, of which `pcc2defaultkill` is correctly closed and `wdcflood` is `in work` WITH\n    commits, a legitimate intermediate state.\n\n**Population: ONE.**  No checker catches this shape -- `check_row_states` verifies that no open row\nNAMES a committed detector, and this row named nothing; `check_bugfile_sync` and `check_witness` both\npass on it.  *A row can be complete in the tree and open in the ledger with every gate green.*",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:37370:     "note": "PASS 3: ONE MORE REPAIR -- session E pended only slot 3, so `if (cpu == 2) continue;` (M_SKIP2) is predicted 27/27: the interior cell of the slot axis, the #499 empty-cell shape (PRETABLE2's 'slot 2 is not owed' was the CHANNEL miss) -> v4 session E pends slots 2 and 3.  Records: es438 is already CLOSED (cf4b083) -- mask2/mask3 ANNOTATE it, they do not close it; the fix comment's '<= MAX_CPUS by construction' overclaimed (the exit guarantees it; deleting the exit would walk cpu_irq[4]) -- reworded; `cpu3 deliver` read BADREGS not STOP under M_AND1 (cpu 1 took the interrupt) -- the packet said STOP.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:37502:     "note": "PASS 4: ONE MORE REPAIR -- and it MEASURED, in two tables of its own (artifacts opus_p4_table/ and opus_p4_table_v5/; PRETABLE 14:32:55 before the 14:34:54 BASE; fresh /tmp clones from a frozen driver, baseline cmp-identical to table 4's, sentinel per mutant, both ended clean). (1) CONFIRMED kimi/grok by BUILDING it: M_ENP1 is 34/34 PASS on the frozen v5 the brief names and 35/37 on v6, the two reds being session E2's new rows; M_ENP1E (enable alone) the same. (2) CLOSES the (d) enumeration with a DERIVED denominator rather than a list: session C (slot 0 idle, slot 1 pending) reds unless f(0)=0 and f(1)=1, killing [0], [cpu^1], [cpu>>1], [cpu|1], [cpu&2], [cpu|2] and [3-cpu]; session E2 reds unless f({2,3}) lies inside {2,3}, killing [cpu&1]; session E constrains f NOT AT ALL, which is exactly why v5 was blind.  Residue: a slot-SPECIFIC fold of 2 and 3, not a single-token edit -- DISCLOSE, do not row. (3) Two mutants that escape and are NOT breaches, both measured: M_EXIT8 (`ncpus > 4' -> `> 8' at the capture's dominating predicate) sweeps 37/37 but is EQUIVALENT -- machine_luna88k.c does device_add at :54 and carries its OWN `ncpus > 4' exit at :103, so relaxing the device's exit lets DEVINIT capture 5 and the MACHINE layer exits before any guest instruction (measured both ways at -n 5: base prints the device's message, mutant the machine's, both rc=1, neither walks); M_SWEQ1 (`software_interrupt_status[cpu] == 1') escapes 37/37 and is clause-(e) debt, since the body tests nonzero-ness and never partitions the nonzero class -- ONE DISCLOSURE LINE OWED. (4) L4 IN PRODUCT CODE, in the pending fix: the capture comment's `(delete that exit and a 5-cpu machine would walk cpu_irq[4])' is MEASURED FALSE for the reason above.  Worse, the CHANGELOG records kimi's and grok's pass-3 refutation of the pass-2 wording as ACCEPTED, and that refutation is itself false -- both seats read only the device file -- so the wording it displaced (`<= MAX_CPUS by construction, not by that exit') was closer to right. (5) The `E all four' disposition IS wrong but NOT for the reason the seat first derived: it measured M_IRQACTIVEP1 on v4 at 32/32 PASS, refuting its own claim that v4's E would have killed it (2&1 == 0, so slots 2 and 3 are in DIFFERENT parity classes).  The true picture is a COMPLEMENTARY TRADE with neither configuration dominating -- v4's E kills M_ENP1 30/32 and misses M_IRQACTIVEP1; v5's E does the reverse -- so the disposition is wrong because the rewrite REPLACED a session that was killing something, and the one row it deleted (`others idle') was never investigated.  v6 restoring both is the right shape. (6) Premise 5 needs a clause: interrupt_handler_lookup aborts on an UNKNOWN name but an EMPTY name installs non-NULL no_interrupt_assert/no_interrupt_deassert stubs (src/core/interrupt.c, anchor `if (name[0] ==' plus the empty-string test), so `has a callback' and `slot < ncpus' differ in MECHANISM and coincide here only because slots 1..3 use literal non-empty names. (7) A PROCESS FINDING against the main loop, and it is right: the brief describes a 34-row v5 while the working file it cites had been edited to the 37-row v6 at 14:31, three minutes before the seat started -- a seat pointed at a WORKING file silently answers a question the packet did not ask.  VERDICT: pass 4 is NOT a closing pass.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:37874:   "note": "Detector-tier (harness) row from the retrospective batch 9 adjudication (Fable 5.1 on GPT-6 Astra's batch; Grok's 14/14 prediction re-enumerated row by row against the probe as it stands).  Under `+2' with the shipped rows: live/devsig not this device; asic ok round-trips mask_13[2] both ways (green); safe 8 / safe 4 are survive-only (green); the kill rows land on default: (green); sweep 256 makes four OOB reads and no store (green); alias/crossarr/alias2 all read back +0x10, which +2 does not move (green); mask9 rt round-trips mask_9[0] (green) -- the tick anchor never fires because no row STORES at an offset that +2 sends to mask_9[3].  The de-escalation membership test the probe's own mask9 rt comment states (apply the translation to the PRE-FIX code: does the old exit(1) fire on the first legal access?) is met by +2 exactly as by +1 (+0x04/+0x14/+0x24/+0x34 are all documented registers), so an accept-side row is OWED, not debt.  Repair: an anchored row `mask9b rt' -- store 0xb2 at +0x34 and read it back (under +2 the store lands on mask_9[3] = asserted_13 by layout, which the tick zeroes) -- optionally `mask9a rt' at +0x30; the contract then claims the whole mod-4 translation group.  Witness owed: harness: HEAD 15/15, +2 14/15 with mask9b rt alone red, the 13 existing mutants re-measured, PRETABLE first, artifacts under regress/artifacts/asic463/table6_15rows/.  Measuring seat: Opus (agy fallback).  Records this row corrects (L4, batch-9 records round): CHANGELOG #463 close-out `+2 is the measured anchor'; ledger asicindexcase state_note x3 and asicdecline witness/note; CLAUDE.md + tools/CLAUDE.md (corrected 2026-09-06, section J).  RANK: top of the harness tier, below live product defects.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:37910:   "state_note": "[2026-10-02] COMMITS FIELD FILLED by #557 from the census of closed rows with no commit (regress/artifacts/closeassess_20261002/grok/grok_closedcensus.txt; Astra's git log, regress/artifacts/closeassess_20261002/aconf_census/codex_aconf_census.txt): 79cd12e8 -- names #502; touches regress/m88k_exc_probe.py.  FILED as clause-(e) DEBT on 2026-09-06 and ADJUDICATED TO FLOOR the same evening by the pass-11 panel; REPAIRED IN-ROUND as L2 (probe v19, 57 -> 59 rows).  The original filing text is preserved below the rule, because a filing that was overturned is part of the record and not an embarrassment to be tidied away.\n\n*** WHY IT IS FLOOR AND NOT DEBT -- TWO INDEPENDENT PRECEDENTS, BOTH FROM THIS ROUND'S OWN RECORDS, BOTH VERIFIED BY OPENING THE ARTIFACT BEFORE ACTING. ***\n  * grok: PRETABLE12 graded this EXACT inner `if' as L2 -- its disposition line reads \"L2, in-round\" -- when     `if (!is_trap || !(PSR & IND))' swept 46/46.  IND, C and SER at this site were then repaired in-round as     floor (trapfrozenind/c/ser).  Grading the remaining two bits at the SAME SITE as debt would be     inconsistent, and would retroactively un-grade three rows this campaign shipped as floor.\n  * kimi: ITS VERDICT LINE SAID DEBT AND ITS BODY SAID FLOOR, reaching grok's conclusion by a different     route -- the pass-5 and pass-7 mutants `!is_trap || vector == <class>' condition on `vector', which the     guarded body does not test either, and those were repaired in-round (trapfrozen4/5/6/9, then     trapfrozen450/496).  So the PRACTISED rule already covers fields a mutant can condition on at this guard.\n  * minimax voted DEBT and is outvoted BY ARGUMENT, not by count: neither precedent was contested by anyone,     and both are checkable in one grep.\n\nMy own reading -- the generating-set letter, `the partitions the guarded body itself tests' -- loses on its own terms, which kimi showed: it would ALSO have made the vector rows debt, and the round shipped those as floor.  The letter was written for idata bits of a device write; here PSR is the GUARD's operand and the value the body writes is `vector = ERROR'.\n\n*** AND MY REACHABILITY ARGUMENT WAS WRONG IN ITS DETAIL, which grok corrected. ***  I claimed the cell is reachable by composing `errpriv' (a frozen handler dropping to user mode) with `trapfrozen128' (a frozen user trap).  `trapfrozen128' is a SUPERVISOR trap NAMING vector 128, MODE=1 -- not a user-mode trap -- and `errpriv''s handler does stcr then ldcr, which raises PRIVILEGE with is_trap == 0 and never reaches a trap at all.  The cell IS reachable, by a different construction: `[STCR_R5_CR1, TB1_0_R3_128]' with r5 = PSR_FRZUSER and **r3 = 0x11**, because bit 0 must be SET or the tb1 does not trap and the row is silently inert.\n\nTHE REPAIR: two rows in trapfrozenind's shape, so they inherit its frozen-shadow expectations and its no-refatal channel with no new machinery.  `trapfrozenuser' puts MODE clear at the trap side (a frozen handler drops to user mode, then traps at vector 128 -- >= USER_TRAPS_START so the fallback does not claim it, and X(tb1) checks privilege only for vector < 128).  `trapfrozensfd1' puts SFD1 clear (PSR_FRZSUP, then the same tb1 naming 7 as trapfrozenind).  CHANNEL: under the mutant a trap that WOULD be exempted is re-routed, so each row moves from DELIVER at its own vector to STOP at 0x50 AND the re-route diagnostic appears where REFATAL_EXPECT says it must not -- two observables, both already read.\n\n*** THIS IS THE FOURTH TIME THIS CHAIN HAS CLOSED AN AXIS AT SOME CELLS AND CALLED THE REST SOMETHING ELSE, AND I DID IT INSIDE THE CENSUS WRITTEN TO END THAT PATTERN. ***  Passes 6, 7, 8 and 9 each found a repair that reproduced the defect it repaired; the cell census was built to make the whole space visible at once, and the filing written from it closed three cells at a site and filed the other two under a different heading.\n\n--- THE ORIGINAL FILING, preserved ---\nFILED 2026-09-06 from the #502 pass-10 cell census (regress/artifacts/m88kexcdeliver/derive_cells.py, output CELLCENSUS.out).  DERIVED, NOT MEASURED, and said so.\n\nAt the SFRZ re-route's TRAP side every one of the fourteen trap rows carries MODE=1 and SFD1=1.  A widened inner guard -- `if (!is_trap || !(PSR & M88K_PSR_MODE))' -- would therefore be invisible to the shipped instrument.  THE CELL IS REACHABLE, and both halves already exist in the probe separately: a frozen handler can drop to user mode (errpriv/errunimpl do exactly that, via PSR_FRZUSER) and a user trap at a vector >= 128 is a genuine trap (trapfrozen128).  No row combines them.  The SFD1 cell is the same shape via PSR_FRZSUP, which errdiv0 already builds.\n\nDEBT, NOT A FLOOR BREACH, and the distinction is the carrier's own bound rather than a convenience: the floor asks for the partitions THE GUARDED BODY ITSELF TESTS.  That body tests `is_trap' and the outer `PSR & SFRZ', and both polarities of both are rowed.  MODE at the trap side is a single-axis marginal against an lvalue the body does not test there, which clause (e) discloses.\n\n*** A FALSE L4 WAS NEARLY FILED BESIDE THIS AND WAS CHECKED FIRST. ***  The contract's sentence `every stcr-settable 88100 bit now varies there' reads like a per-branch claim and is measurably FALSE of the trap side alone.  It governs the OUTER guard, which sees both branches -- and over their union every bit does vary (MODE by errpriv, SER by errser/trapfrozenser, C by errc/trapfrozenc, SFD1 by errdiv0, IND by errind/trapfrozenind).  The claim is correct as written.  Recorded because the near-miss is the instructive part: a census that reports per-branch will make a correct union claim look wrong.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:37916:     "note": "PASS 11.  VERDICT: REACHABLE-FLOOR -- overturned my DEBT filing and was RIGHT, on a precedent I could check in one grep: PRETABLE12 graded this exact inner `if' L2 for IND/C.  Also CORRECTED MY REACHABILITY COMPOSITION (trapfrozen128 is a supervisor trap naming a user vector, not a user-mode trap; errpriv's handler never reaches a trap) and caught that r3 must be 0x11 or the tb1 is a nop and the row is silently inert.  Gave the exact mutant text and the exact row shapes, and named what it could NOT run (shell denied) rather than implying measurement.  Also noted correctly that no EXISTING row reddens once the cell is closed -- the repair needs NEW rows, which is the vacuity-by-construction check applied before the table.  Panel dir _scratchpad/panel_20260906_202957",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:38221:     "note": "PASS 2.  Answered ALL FIVE dispositions with **NONE IS WRONG** -- and named the question's own frame as the unsupported assumption: *'the \"which is wrong?\" frame is the unsupported assumption; every item is the round-scope / addition-licence / stopping-rule answer.'*  **That is the second seat tonight to make that correction, independently, and neither knew of the other** -- the template row briefforcedwrong now has two instances.  *** AND IT FOUND THE ROUND'S ONE L4, WHICH I CREATED. ***  `SGIRE_W_PRIM_OP`'s comment still said *'the next free bit is 1 << 10; the enum ends HERE on purpose'* -- both false once SGIRE_W_BADMODE was added AT 1 << 10 below it.  **That is precisely the duplicate-enumerator collision this file already paid for and warns about four lines above**: I read that warning while placing my bit, obeyed it, and left the signpost pointing at the bit I had just taken.  Fixed in-round by DEMOTING the superseded sentence inside its own correction, leaving exactly one live next-free claim on the last enumerator.  It also confirmed my PRETABLE prediction miss was the PREDICTION and not the mutant -- clause (b) restores BOTH halves the fix changed, so M_RANDOM at 6/8 is correct.  Its denominator names cells, shapes swept, AND what is NOT swept: junk modes 3/6/7, latch-share, the dest-side LIN_B PUT, MTE's `&bg' GET, and the colour-mode default.  Panel dir _scratchpad/panel_20260906_234311 (preserved: regress/artifacts/sgirandompixel/pass2_panel/)",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:38490:     "note": "verbatim: 'VERDICT: REVISE the detector and coverage claims. The submitted store guard is correct, but \"all 14 rows pass\" does not establish the claimed floor.'  Confirmed both of grok's one-token escapes; found PRETABLE §4 assigning M_declineall to `live silent' (it reds `live'); §1's 'skipped rather than returned early' claim false (after the switch the guarded store is the only statement); the diff is THREE hunks, 48 additions and 13 deletions = 61 changed lines (the brief said 2 hunks / 58: the non-blank count); 'four HOST-PRNG bytes' needs its width qualifier (one at 8 bpp, four at 32, four plus an unassigned tail at depth 8); the contract's 'PUT arms assign four bytes' excludes CI; 'the arm does not read the nibble' is literally false (the diagnostic prints it).  Said plainly it cannot authenticate which binaries produced the saved logs.  All disposed in PRETABLE §6.  [PASS 2 on the real diff (brief PASS2_brief_20260910.md, diff + detector block + PRETABLE §1-5 inlined; panel dir copied verbatim from the project-root _scratchpad).]"
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:38500:     "note": "verbatim: 'VERDICT: NOT RATIFY the detector as complete; the product fix is sound.'  Read the tree, not the packet.  Q2: TWO ONE-TOKEN EDITS keep all 14 rows green -- `if (ok)' -> `if (ok || x)' (every PUT sat at (0,0)) and SGIRE_W_BADCOLOR -> SGIRE_W_TEX_RD (independence rowed only against BADMODE).  Q4.3: `indep paint' cannot observe the LIN_B half (setup_put re-paints).  Q4.4: skip vs early return unobservable.  Two accept rows had only M8 as their growth mutant (dropcase rgb/rgba missing from the plan).  Cosmetic: the announce closed its bracket twice.  Confirmed by reading: the GET default is still an unlatched per-pixel fatal (sgirebufuninit, out of scope); random() is gone from executable code in the file.  Named the two measurements that settle Q2 -- built as M13/M15 (table 2).  [PASS 2 on the real diff (brief PASS2_brief_20260910.md, diff + detector block + PRETABLE §1-5 inlined; panel dir copied verbatim from the project-root _scratchpad).]"
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:38510:     "note": "packet-fed.  verbatim: 'the fix as displayed is semantically sound and every detector expectation I could walk checks out -- but two of the brief's own meta-premises fail packet-internal audit, and the latch-independence property is defended for only ONE of the twelve other latch sites.'  Caught §C0's '2 hunks' against three hunk headers in the same packet; noted that at bufdepth 8 the SUPPORTED arms store an unassigned tail (sgirebufuninit's question, filed).  Honest about what it took on trust (the run results, the witness half).  [PASS 2 on the real diff (brief PASS2_brief_20260910.md, diff + detector block + PRETABLE §1-5 inlined; panel dir copied verbatim from the project-root _scratchpad).]"
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:38520:     "note": "packet-fed.  verbatim: 'No self-contradiction found in the packet; the diff as inlined is internally consistent. Q4: none/36. Q2: changing SGIRE_W_BADCOLOR to any other existing warn bit not exercised by the rows (e.g., SGIRE_W_TEX_RD) passes all 14 rows; latch indep stays green.'  Denominator given (36 cells).  ONE WRONG CLAIM, recorded: its Q5 says M8_declineall makes `live silent' read 1 -- it does not; the announce sits in the default arm only, so M8 reds `live' and leaves `live silent' at 0 (PRETABLE §6.a).  [PASS 2 on the real diff (brief PASS2_brief_20260910.md, diff + detector block + PRETABLE §1-5 inlined; panel dir copied verbatim from the project-root _scratchpad).]"
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:38530:     "note": "packet-fed.  verbatim: 'The brief's derivations are mostly sound. Two real defects. Several premises not checkable from the packet.'  Defect 1: `two declined' does not pin skip-vs-early-return (adopted: contract reworded).  Named M_dropcase_rgb as missing from the plan (built as M16, table 2b) and the double closing bracket (fixed, one token).  Its packet-internal complaint -- the five witness rows were referenced, not inlined -- is a BRIEF defect (§C2) and is recorded as such.  [PASS 2 on the real diff (brief PASS2_brief_20260910.md, diff + detector block + PRETABLE §1-5 inlined; panel dir copied verbatim from the project-root _scratchpad).]"
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:38540:     "note": "PACKET-FED SUBSTITUTE kimi-k3:cloud -- the kimi CLI is on its weekly 403 wall (pass2_20260910/kimi.txt is the 350-byte error); the seat is COVERED, not lost.  verbatim: 'The derivation follows from the inlined diff and I reproduce its two enumerations (12-of-16 nibbles; 6/14 on HEAD) without contradiction; the guarded-decline logic itself is sound. Three premises are NOT settleable from this packet.'  Asked for the warrant of 'the struct is shared by four handlers' and 'the two demotions above it' -- both VERIFIED by the loop (d->warned is used in exactly four functions; both demoted LAST-ENUMERATOR sentences exist) and recorded in PRETABLE §6.h.  Reconstructed the 6/14 HEAD total exactly from the row list.  [PASS 2 on the real diff (brief PASS2_brief_20260910.md, diff + detector block + PRETABLE §1-5 inlined; panel dir copied verbatim from the project-root _scratchpad).]"
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:38550:     "note": "Agent-tool MEASURING seat, re-tested LIVE after the 03:00 reset: TABLE 1 on the FROZEN 14-row pre-repair instrument (sgi_re_colourdefault_probe.FROZEN.py) against the FROZEN baseline BASELINE_FIX519.c in the scratch clone /tmp/gxopus519 with its own sentinel and mutator (mut519.py): M1_reinstate (b) .. M12, the null medium M11 being the pre-fix file wholesale [L4 2026-10-09, 7n-1: M11 is the whole-file PRE-FIX leg -- clause (a)'s medium, never clause (c)'s; clause (c) is unmeasured at that table].  Every score read from a .raw ending SGICOLOUR_DONE.  Its baseline differs from the SHIPPED source by ONE TOKEN in a string literal (the announce's second bracket, edited after the table started; no row or mutant reads it -- PRETABLE §6.h).  TABLE 1 (Opus seat, FROZEN 14-row instrument + frozen baseline, /tmp/gxopus519): BASE 14/14; all twelve planned mutants killed -- M1_reinstate 6/14 (b), M2_noguard 9/14, M3_zerofill 10/14, M4_okinlatch 13/14, M5_nolatch 13/14, M6_sharedbit 13/14, M7_silent 10/14, M8_declineall 10/14 (reds live/ci8/rgb/abgr, confirming PRETABLE 6.a), M9_dropcase_ci 13/14, M10_dropcase_abgr 13/14, M11_null 6/14 (c), M12_prefix_reworded 13/14. *** THE SEAT'S OWN TWO MUTANTS ARE TOTAL ESCAPES: M13_widthdecline and M14_lineardecline, 14/14 on the shipped instrument AND 19/19 on the repaired one. *** TABLE 2 (16-row, t2/SCORES2.txt): BASE 16/16; M13_okorx 15/16 and M14_okory 15/16, each killed by ycrcb x4y1 alone; M15_texrd 16/16, the PREDICTED escape that motivated the class closure. TABLE 2b (19-row): BASE_final 19/19; M16_dropcase_rgb 18/19; M17_dropcase_rgba 17/19. TABLE 2c (19-row): BASE_19 19/19; M15_texrd_19 18/19 and M18_sharedbit_tlba 18/19, both killed by allbits announce -- the latch-collision class closure is MEASURED. Round HELD on the two width/tiling escapes."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:38560:     "note": "RECORDS AUDIT of PRETABLE section 6 (the pass-2 repair record), fired by the loop as farmed codex: 'VERDICT: NO-GO (6 items)'.  Item 1 is the finding that changed the instrument: the 6.f disclosure of the eleven non-BADMODE latch bits rested on a FALSE COST PREMISE -- one session primes all twelve other bits, with the exact access sequence given and the in-tree precedent named (sgi_re_width_probe.py `tex xbit', CLASS CLOSURE ON THE LATCH-COLLISION AXIS).  Adopted as the `allbits' rows.  Items 2-6 are wrong records in section 6, every one verified and corrected in 6.i: nine sessions not fourteen; the overlapping byte is word 1's MOST significant byte and an overlap is a possible change, not a guaranteed red; the header's guessed timestamp and the mtime-is-the-proof clause (withdrawn: the verbatim copy in the pass-2 brief, 04:00:19, is the order proof); the partition justification for the (4,1) row (withdrawn); the two demotions above the TEX_RD comment are PRIM_OP's and BADMODE's.  It also re-derived 6.b's counts and 6.a's red set against 1ce8296 and confirmed both."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:38570:     "note": "READ-ONLY CHECK of the class-closure rows before their table (farmed codex): 'VERDICT: NO-GO (1 item)' -- the priming sequence and collision oracle are SOUND on this tree: each of the twelve accesses named to its arm by grep anchor, every PRIME_LIT entry an exact substring, nothing dies, the priming does not obstruct the YCRCB PUT, and the warn mask reaches 0xfff before the default arm runs, so aliasing BADCOLOR to any bit 0-11 reads count 0.  The one item is a LOAD SENSITIVITY of session(): 197 fixed 0.22 s windows and no prompt wait, so a delayed emulator can leave the final print unread (`absent') -- a loud false red on the fix, never a false green; recorded in PRETABLE 6.j and the block comment, no instrument change."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:38589:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/killtable_20260910/PRETABLE.out",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:38590:     "note": "ADJUDICATED BY THE MAIN LOOP RUNNING AS FABLE 5.1 (owner directive), on 6 of 7 scriptable transports (kimi via the substitute) + the Opus table; agy not fired.  THE FIX STOOD: no seat found a defect in the product change (grok: 'the product fix is sound'; codex: 'the submitted store guard is correct').  EVERY DETECTOR FINDING WAS PRE-SHIP (L1) OR A WRONG RECORD (L4) AND ALL WERE REPAIRED IN ROUND, in three cuts: (1) the (4,1)-vertex row `ycrcb x4y1' + `x4y1 announce' for grok's `if (ok || x)' -- the first cut used (1,0) and claimed ofs = 1 * bufdepth, WRONG for the LINEAR path (`ofs = x & 4095', a byte offset, y ignored), corrected before any table ran on it; the second-word paint is emitted only for a non-origin vertex so the fourteen pre-existing sessions' command streams are byte-unchanged and table 1's per-row kills carry over; (2) the contract's three wrong sentences (skip-vs-return, 'PUT arms assign four bytes', 'does not read the nibble') and the `indep paint' note; (3) the announce's stray second bracket (one product token, mirroring the BADMODE sibling's shape) and the two drop-case growth mutants M16/M17 for `rgb put' and `live'; then (4) the shared-latch-bit CLASS CLOSED rather than disclosed, at codex's records audit of PRETABLE section 6: the first cut disclosed the eleven non-BADMODE bits on the premise that rowing them costs one session per bit, and codex refuted the premise from the tree -- sgi_re_width_probe.py's `tex xbit' row already primes every other latch in ONE session -- so `allbits primed' / `allbits announce' / `allbits paint' close the class and M15_texrd goes from expected-escape to expected-kill (table 2c, with M18_sharedbit_tlba).  The same audit corrected four wrong sentences of section 6 (sessions vs rows, the overlapping byte, the chronology -- the order proof is the verbatim copy in the pass-2 brief, not the mtime -- and the two demotions) and withdrew my partition justification for the (4,1) row in favour of the contract's own sentence.  COUNT THE ARGUMENTS: the two one-token escapes came from ONE seat reading the tree and were confirmed by a second; the packet-fed seats found the brief's own defects (hunk count, uninlined witness rows) and one packet-fed claim was wrong (deepseek's `live silent' under M8) -- corrected in §6.a.  The repair chain owes ITS OWN PASS on the repair diff (instrument vs the frozen 14-row copy + the one-token product edit) with the table-2/2b scores as its denominator; that pass is the closing pass if it finds zero breaches.  TABLE 1 (Opus seat, FROZEN 14-row instrument + frozen baseline, /tmp/gxopus519): BASE 14/14; all twelve planned mutants killed -- M1_reinstate 6/14 (b), M2_noguard 9/14, M3_zerofill 10/14, M4_okinlatch 13/14, M5_nolatch 13/14, M6_sharedbit 13/14, M7_silent 10/14, M8_declineall 10/14 (reds live/ci8/rgb/abgr, confirming PRETABLE 6.a), M9_dropcase_ci 13/14, M10_dropcase_abgr 13/14, M11_null 6/14 (c), M12_prefix_reworded 13/14. *** THE SEAT'S OWN TWO MUTANTS ARE TOTAL ESCAPES: M13_widthdecline and M14_lineardecline, 14/14 on the shipped instrument AND 19/19 on the repaired one. *** TABLE 2 (16-row, t2/SCORES2.txt): BASE 16/16; M13_okorx 15/16 and M14_okory 15/16, each killed by ycrcb x4y1 alone; M15_texrd 16/16, the PREDICTED escape that motivated the class closure. TABLE 2b (19-row): BASE_final 19/19; M16_dropcase_rgb 18/19; M17_dropcase_rgba 17/19. TABLE 2c (19-row): BASE_19 19/19; M15_texrd_19 18/19 and M18_sharedbit_tlba 18/19, both killed by allbits announce -- the latch-collision class closure is MEASURED. Round HELD on the two width/tiling escapes."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:38689:   "note": "[2026-11-02, records: SUPERSEDED. The closure-records commit (50805312) landed 2026-10-18 (closure.note) and the row is held for fable5.] *** COMMITTED 2026-09-12 as fd15065 (local only).  Pass-2 receipt in regress/pass2_ledger.tsv.  State stays `in work`: closure records owed. ***\n\n[2026-11-02, records] The title is historical: YCRCB appeared 0 times in the pre-#519 file; at 987cb596 it is named once, in #519's comment (dev_sgi_re.c:367).  So is its 'FOUR random() bytes': #519 (fd15065) made that arm (dev_sgi_re.c:363 at 987cb596) a latched decline that stores nothing, and random() now appears in dev_sgi_re.c only in comments (:289, :312, :363, :371, :375); 'four' held only at bufdepth 4, as this row's state_note records.\n\n*** 2026-09-12: THE CLASS CLOSURE IS MEASURED AND THE ROUND LANDS.  Kill table 5 (regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/killtable5_20260912/SCORES.txt): 15 legs, EVERY ONE ON PREDICTION -- BASE 89/89, HEAD 28/89, eleven single-cell zero-store controls 88/89 each on its own row, NIB_OTHER 78/89, TAIL8 81/89.  Codex closing pass (codex_passE_20260912.txt): 0/32 cells unoccupied, 11 measured + 21 predicted isolated kills -- a zero WITH its denominator.  Probe wired into gate_arm.sh (pin 89): gate 14 PASS 914 checks (gate_arm_89rows.log).  Three wrong records corrected in PRETABLE.out's appended section (GUARD/BODY, CLAIM->ROW, and the mtime claim the append itself destroyed).  Disclosed, not closed: bare-predicate mutants with an uninitialised buf; the exact historical M28/M28r edit. ***\n\n*** 2026-09-12, codex farm: refused once by the cybersecurity filter, answered when re-worded (regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_closurediff_20260912b.txt).  Occupancy DERIVED from the sessions: 21 of 32 projection cells, 11 empty.  Its seven replacements APPLIED to regress/sgi_re_colourdefault_probe.py (every OLD unique): the controlling CONTRACT, full depth-8 readbacks (the four existing depth-8 rows were blind to tail-only corruption), and 12 rows closing the 11 cells plus an M28-family row.  89 assertions / 29 sessions PREDICTED; 13 named `_ZERO` controls specified.  UNMEASURED.  OWED: PRETABLE + table.  Honest limit carried from the answer: the exact historical M28 edit is not in the records, so the M28 row is a family control, not an identification. ***\n\n*** PASS 2, 2026-09-11: THE `M28' DISPOSITION IS UPHELD, MY REASON FOR IT IS REFUTED, AND THE ESCAPE TURNS OUT TO COST **ONE ROW** RATHER THAN THE TWELVE-CELL EXPANSION I FEARED. ***  Panel `_scratchpad/panel_20260910_202650/`.\n\nI filed `M28_permit_othernib' (59/59) as clause-(e) debt on the ground that *\"`color_mode` is the switch SELECTOR and flows into no lvalue the guarded body reads\"*.  **The seat that READ THE SOURCE calls that sentence poetry, and it is right:** it is true of the EDITED arm -- the `default:` does not write `buf` -- and **FALSE of the accept arms**, where `buf[0..3]` are exactly what the colour-selected code writes.  A reason that is true only of the arm you edited is not a reason.\n\n**THE CORRECT WARRANT IS THE BODY-TESTED PARTITION, which is the joint-cells clause this project already carries:**\n  * the PUT switch distinguishes `{CI, RGB, RGBA, ABGR, default}`;\n  * **all twelve reaching nibbles are ONE `default` cell** -- the body never tests `0xf0` against `0x50` against `0x40`;\n  * a value the body never tests AS A CASE is clause (e), by the pass-11 ruling;\n  * so `M28` is an **intra-bucket carve, not a new axis**.\n\n*** AND MY COST ESTIMATE WAS A WRONG SCARE. ***  I told the panel that flipping the call would make the closure `4 x 4 x 12`.  It would not: **it would owe ONE extra `default` nibble -- say `0x40` -- at a cell that is ALREADY ROWED, and that single row kills M28.**  The 192-cell explosion existed only in my brief.\n\n*The seat also noted that `if (color_mode == 0xf0) ok = 0;` ALREADY DIES on the existing `reserved put` row, which drives `0x50`.  `M28` survives only because it is the JOINT of the two driven default values, letting the other ten store -- which is why one more driven nibble closes it.*\n\n**ACTION, and it is the cheapest thing in this round: ADD ONE ROW driving a third `default` nibble at an already-rowed cell.**  That converts a disclosed clause-(e) escape into a killed mutant for the price of one readback, with no new session and no new load sensitivity.  It needs a re-table like any added row, so it waits on a measuring seat -- **but it is a one-row change, not a twelve-cell one, and the round should not close without it now that the cost is known.**\n\n*** PASS 2 PRODUCED TWO CONCRETE ROW CHANGES AND THEY GO IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS -- THE NET IS FEWER ROWS AND STRICTLY BETTER DISCRIMINATION. ***\n\n**(1) ADD ONE ROW.**  A third `default` nibble (e.g. `0x40`) driven at a cell that is already rowed **kills `M28`**, the round's one disclosed escape.  One readback, no new session, no new load sensitivity.\n\n**(2) DROP OR DEMOTE FOUR ROWS.  The `ann cum` rows earn NO kill.**  Checked against `SCORES3.txt`'s own red lists rather than argued: `M5` reds `once`, three `ann1` and the four `ann cum`; `M7` the same.  **Delete all four `ann cum` rows and both mutants still die** -- `ann1` and `once` already carry them, because the mid-block has four declines so the count cannot be 1.  *They are the part of the 40-row expansion that is score noise: no mutant reds an `ann cum` row and not also `ann1` or `once`.*\n\n`silent` and `ann1` DO earn their place and the reasons are specific: `tA/tB/tC silent` pins an accept-side announce with the packing still landing, where the pre-existing `live silent` covers LIN_A RGBA only; `tA/tB/tC ann1` catches `M5`, `M7`, drop-case latch consumption and an announce conditioned on `tlb_mode`, where the pre-existing `once` is a LIN_A process.\n\n**NET: 59 -> 56 rows, one escape closed, no kill lost.**  Both changes need a re-table like any row change, so they wait on a measuring seat.\n\n*** AND NO NEW ROW IS VACUOUS UNDER ITS NAMED MUTANT -- the CHANNEL check was run against the PRETABLE map and the SCORES3 red lists, not asserted. ***  Two subtleties worth keeping: `tA ycrcb d4` does NOT vary under `M13` and that is a CONTROL rather than a vacuity (it dies under `M14` and `M23`); and `tA ycrcb d1` does not die under `M4` while `rpt` does, so CHANNEL is right to pin `M4` on `rpt`.  The `silent`/`ann1` rows FAIL CLOSED if the marker is absent -- *\"no marker\"* is neither 0 nor 1 -- and the explorer's `\"0x00519519\"` miss is **not** in the shipped split, which uses `\"0x519519\"`.\n\n*One understatement found in my own PRETABLE: its CHANNEL line for `ann1` misses that the row also reds the drop-case mutants, because CI/RGB/etc. consume the latch before the marker and the mid-block goes silent.  That understates a kill; it does not make the row vacuous.*\n\n*** TWO SEATS INDEPENDENTLY KILLED THE SAME SENTENCE OF MINE, ONE FILE-READING AND ONE PACKET-FED, WHICH IS THE STRONGEST FORM THIS PANEL PRODUCES. ***  Both identified that *\"`color_mode` flows into no lvalue the guarded body reads\"* is **true only of the edited `default:` arm**: the store reads `buf`, and `buf` is what the colour switch FILLS.  The disposition survives on the body-tested-partition warrant recorded above; **the sentence does not, and it is the sentence that was in the contract.**\n\n*The packet-fed seat additionally marked the claim NOT CHECKABLE from the packet and said so rather than ratifying it -- which is the honest form, and is what the PACKET_PREAMBLE exists to elicit.*\n\n**A THIRD ROW ACTION, from the packet-fed seat, and the two seats SPLIT on its disposition:** every new cell sits at `(0,0)`, so the tiled address arithmetic is pinned by nothing and `ofs`-arithmetic mutants are uncaught.  **One row at `(1,0)` closes the only address-arithmetic hole in the new material.**\n  * the FILE-READING seat calls it *out of the 16-cell floor, a real hole, disclose rather than row in-round*;\n  * the packet-fed seat says one row closes it.\n**Both agree it is a real hole; they differ only on in-round versus disclosed.**  Since the round is ALREADY re-tabling for the `M28` row and the `ann cum` removal, **the marginal cost of one more readback is a readback** -- so it ships with them, and the disclosure stands if a measurement says otherwise.\n\n**RUNNING TOTAL FOR THE RE-TABLE: +1 (`M28` nibble), +1 (`(1,0)` address), -4 (`ann cum`) = 57 rows**, one escape closed, one address hole closed, no kill lost.\n\n*A premise imprecision of mine, also caught: the brief used \"type nibble\" and `color_mode` interchangeably without nailing down the selector's bit position.  They coincide here, but the brief did not say so and a reader could not check it.*\n\n*** MY SETTLED PREMISE WAS WRONG: THE GUARDED BODY READS **THREE** MODE-CONTROLLED LVALUES, NOT TWO -- AND ALL THREE SEATS THAT ENGAGED WITH IT FOUND THE SAME THIRD ONE. ***\n\nI told the panel, as a SETTLED PREMISE, that the body reads exactly `tileptr` and `bufdepth`.  **It also reads `buf`, which the `switch (color_mode)` arms FILL.**  Three seats found it independently -- the file-reading one, and two packet-fed ones -- which is as close to unanimity on a repo fact as this panel gets.  *A wrong settled premise is the defect this project has measured costing a whole panel before; here it cost nothing only because every seat caught it.*\n\n*** AND THE SEATS SPLIT ON WHICH CLAUSE GOVERNS, WHICH IS A GENUINE RULE QUESTION AND THE FIRST ONE THIS ROUND HAS PRODUCED THAT IS WORTH THE FLAGSHIP SEAT'S TIME. ***\n\n  * **FLOOR BREACH (deepseek, kimi-substitute):** clause (d)'s operand-swap form draws swaps from *fields the guarded BODY reads*.  `buf` is body-read and `color_mode` selects what fills it.  Therefore `color_mode` is in the DEPENDS-ON set and `M28` is an L2 breach.\n  * **CLAUSE (e) (grok, which READ THE SOURCE):** the JOINT-CELLS clause bounds enumeration to the partition **the body's own tests induce**.  The PUT switch distinguishes `{CI, RGB, RGBA, ABGR, default}` and **never tests `0xf0` against `0x50` against `0x40`** -- so all twelve reaching nibbles are ONE `default` cell, and the pass-11 ruling says a value the body never tests as a case is clause (e).  `M28` is an intra-bucket carve.\n\n**MY READING, OFFERED AS THE MAIN LOOP'S AND NOT AS A RULING:** grok applies the clause AS AMENDED and deepseek applies it as ORIGINALLY STATED.  The pass-11 amendment exists precisely to stop the `2^k` explosion that the raw body-read reading produces, and it is the governing text.  **So the disposition stands -- but the fact that two seats reached the opposite answer from the same correct observation means the CLAUSE IS AMBIGUOUS AS WRITTEN**, and that is the flagship seat's question rather than mine.\n\n*** THE QUESTION FOR THE FLAGSHIP SEAT, stated so it need not be reconstructed: ***\n\n    Clause (d)'s operand-swap form says swaps are drawn from fields the guarded BODY READS.\n    The joint-cells clause says enumeration is bounded to the partition the BODY'S OWN TESTS\n    induce, and pass 11 says a value the body never tests as a case is clause (e).\n\n    When a selector fills a body-read buffer but the body never tests that selector's VALUES\n    against each other, is the selector IN the DEPENDS-ON set or OUT of it?\n\n    `M28` is the instance: 59/59, twelve nibbles in one `default` bucket, and closing it costs\n    ONE ROW either way -- so the ruling decides the RULE, not this round's cost.\n\n**That last clause is why this is worth asking and safe to ask:** the round ships the closing row regardless, so a walled seat blocks nothing, and the answer governs every future round with a selector-filled buffer.\n\n*** SUPERSEDING THE ONE-ROW PLAN ABOVE: A THIRD SEAT MADE THE ARGUMENT THAT ACTUALLY DECIDES THIS, AND THE ANSWER IS THE CLASS CLOSURE -- ELEVEN ROWS, NOT ONE. ***\n\n**THE SYMMETRY ARGUMENT, and it is fatal to my filing as written:**\n\n    M13 conditions the decline on `bufdepth`.   M14 conditions it on `linear`.\n    M28 conditions it on `color_mode`.\n\n**All three are the SAME guard-weakening shape on three different fields**, and all three permit reachable stores of uninitialised `buf` -- *the exact defect class `#519` exists to close.*  M13 and M14 were treated as floor kills and justified a forty-row expansion.  **The only thing separating M28 from them is which rows happen to exist to see it** -- and in the seat's words, *\"an instrument-relative escape is not a contract-relative exemption.\"*\n\n*** AND THIS PROJECT'S OWN CLASS-CLOSURE AMENDMENT FIRES ON EXACTLY THIS PATTERN. ***  [CORRECTED 2026-10-18 by the 7n-5 ruling (regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_35.md; GPT-6 Astra confirming): it does not.  The amendment's trigger is two consecutive passes finding BREACHES of one class (its own words, quoted next), and M28 is not a breach: it slices WITHIN the one `default:` label that twelve nibbles share, which the ruling's table grades clause (e) ('already so ruled') and this note's own partition table, further down, says too.  The colour-mode closure that followed was L1 strengthening.]  It says: *when two consecutive passes find breaches of ONE CLASS, the next repair owes the CLASS CLOSURE -- enumerate the full space and close every cell in ONE diff, rather than buying cells one at a time.*  Pass 1 found M13/M14; pass 2 found M28; **same class, consecutive passes.**  The amendment exists precisely so a round stops repairing the cell it was shown and closes the space -- which is what the measuring seat did for `{tlb_mode} x {bufdepth}` and what I was about to NOT do for `color_mode`.\n\n**THE COST IS ELEVEN ROWS, NOT ONE AND NOT 192.**  Single-axis predicates over all three fields close with the sixteen `(tlb, depth)` cells already shipped at nibble `0xf0`, PLUS the twelve reaching nibbles at ONE cell -- of which one is already rowed.  **Eleven new readbacks at an existing cell.**  My brief's \"4 x 4 x 12\" was a wrong scare in one direction and grok's \"one row\" is a cell repair rather than a closure in the other; *neither number was the closure, and the closure is the thing the rule asks for.*\n\n**REVISED RE-TABLE: +11 (`color_mode` closure), +1 (`(1,0)` address), -4 (`ann cum`) = 67 rows.**\n\n*** MY DISPOSITION CHANGES: `M28` IS NOT CLAUSE-(e) DEBT.  It is the second pass of a recurring class, and the round owes the closure before it ships. ***  [CORRECTED 2026-10-18 by the 7n-5 ruling (regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_35.md; GPT-6 Astra confirming): M28 IS clause-(e) debt: `default:` is one label for twelve nibbles (the ruling's table: '(e) -- already so ruled'), so no closure was owed, and the ten-nibble session the round added is L1 strengthening.]  The rule question for the flagship seat stands and is now sharper -- it is no longer *is M28 in or out*, but **does the body-tested- partition bound survive the symmetry argument at all**, given that `bufdepth` and `color_mode` weaken the same guard in the same way.\n\n*Recorded against my own earlier entry in this row, which said one row and clause (e).  Three seats gave three different answers -- clause (e), floor breach on the operand-swap route, and floor breach on the symmetry route -- and the third is the one that cites this project's own governing amendment.  Count the arguments, not the seats.*\n\n*** PASS 2, MEASURED: **FLOOR BREACH.**  SIX MUTANTS SCORE 59/59 ON THE SHIPPED INSTRUMENT AND EVERY ONE WRITES UNINITIALISED HOST STACK INTO GUEST MEMORY.  `#519` DOES NOT CLOSE ON THIS DETECTOR. ***  [CORRECTED 2026-10-18 by the 7n-5 ruling (regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_35.md; GPT-6 Astra confirming): the six escapes are measured and real, and FLOOR BREACH was wrong AS A GRADE.  The body tests none of them as a case: tile_nr only at >= 256 on the tiled branch and >= 32 on LIN_A (M30, M31; M32's tile_nr_x is an untested component of it), x and y only against their bounds, < 0 and >= 2048, before `y &= 127` (M33), *color not at all (M37), and the twelve nibbles that reach `default:` only as that one cell (M28r).  Under D1 (2026-09-25: 'tests' means masks or compares as a case) each is clause (e).  The 59 -> 77 -> 89 repairs were L1 strengthening on a severity warrant (M30 re-opened the fix over 98.75% of the screen), legitimately done before fd15065 landed; only the recorded grade was wrong.  And the 59-row instrument this heading calls SHIPPED never shipped: fd15065 landed on 2026-09-12 with 89 rows.]  13 builds, 11 instrument runs, 13 controls; `regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/pass2_20260911/`.\n\n    M30_tilenrdecline   ok=0 -> if (tile_nr == 0) ok = 0;        59/59   tile 1, 0x70630000\n    M31_tilenrguard     if (ok) -> if (ok || tile_nr)            59/59   tile 1, 0x6368696e\n    M32_tilenrxdecline  ok=0 -> if (tile_nr_x == 0) ok = 0;      59/59   tile 1, 0x706300fe\n    M33_tiledorigin     ok=0 -> if (linear || y == 0) ok = 0;    59/59   tiled (0,1)\n    M28r_permit_othernib   my disclosed escape, replicated       59/59   nibble 0x60\n    M37_fgvalue         ok=0 -> if (*color == 0xaabbccdd) ok=0;  59/59   fg 0x12345678\n\n*** `0x6368696e` IS ASCII **\"chin\"** -- host stack TEXT reaching guest-visible memory. ***\n\n**THE SMALLEST IS ELEVEN CHARACTERS: `if (ok)` -> `if (ok || tile_nr)`.**\n\n**THE NEGATIVE CONTROLS PROVE THE FINDING IS SPECIFIC, NOT 'the probe ignores geometry':** `if (ok || ofs)`, `|| x` and `|| y` each score **58/59**, killed by exactly one row -- `ycrcb x4y1`.  **That row works.**  It simply cannot reach the tile INDEX: its cell is LINEAR at (4,1) where `x >> 12 == 0`, and a tiled (4,1) would not have helped either since `x / tilewidth == 0` at every bufdepth.  Each mutant moved exactly one control cell and all four liveness/attribution rows were invariant across all 11 legs, so every write is attributable.\n\n*** SEVERITY, AND IT INVERTS THE USUAL PICTURE: at 32bpp a tile is 128x128, so `tile_nr == 0` is `x < 128 && y < 128`.  On the default 1280x1024 framebuffer M30 KEEPS the fix over **1.25% of the screen and RE-OPENS IT OVER THE OTHER 98.75%** -- and all 59 rows sit inside the 1.25%. ***\n\n**AND THE WRONG WARRANT I RECORDED THIS MORNING WAS ALREADY REUSED, WHICH IS EXACTLY WHY THIS PROJECT CORRECTS REASONS AND NOT ONLY CONCLUSIONS.**  The flow test was applied CORRECTLY to `tlb_mode` (floor axis, via `tileptr`) and then **stopped at the ARRAY and never reached the INDEX**: `tileptr = d->re_tlb_a[tile_nr] << 16`, so `tile_nr` flows into the guarded body's ADDRESS operand at least as directly as `tlb_mode` does -- and `#100` in this same function already calls `tile_nr` guest-derived and bounds it.  [CORRECTED 2026-10-18 by the 7n-5 ruling (regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_35.md; GPT-6 Astra confirming): flow is not the test.  This is the 2026-09-09 label clause with its first half dropped: `tlb_mode` is a label the body tests as a case AND flows into `tileptr`; `tile_nr` flows and is tested only at >= 256 (tiled) and >= 32 (LIN_A), never for zero, so the tile-index family is clause (e).  Flow alone is the transitive chasing the 2026-08-24 DEPENDS-ON bound forbids.  This note's own partition table, further down, reached the same grade.]  `ofs`'s own formula `512*y + (x % tilewidth) * bufdepth` contains x and y, while my PRETABLE named only `tlb_mode` and `bufdepth` as its dependencies.\n\n**QUESTION 2's SENTENCE IS TRUE AND THE WORD DOING ALL THE WORK IS 'ALONE'.**  Of the four body-read lvalues, `bufdepth` is closed over its domain and `buf` over its label partition -- **`tileptr`'s INDEX and `ofs` are each held at a SINGLE VALUE by all 59 rows.**\n\n*** THIS IS THE THIRD CONSECUTIVE PASS FINDING THE SAME SHAPE (bufdepth/linear, then color_mode, now tile index/y/fg), SO THE CLASS-CLOSURE RULE BITES HARD: the next repair owes the closure in ONE DIFF, not another cell. ***  [CORRECTED 2026-10-18 by the 7n-5 ruling (regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_35.md; GPT-6 Astra confirming): no closure was owed: the cells were clause (e), so the closure that followed was L1 strengthening chosen on a severity warrant, not one the class-closure rule mandated; and the 'same reasoning' the next sentence extends to the tile index is the flow warrant corrected above.]  The 16-cell closure was principled and **incomplete on its own principle** -- the same reasoning that justified closing `tlb_mode x bufdepth` justifies closing the tile index.  *Reachability is a `sw` to `DE_MODE_DST`, not a driver design choice, so the \"guests do not traverse this space\" objection fails too.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PASS 2 / CLASS CLOSURE (Opus 5, `/tmp/gxop519p2`).  **THE CLASS CLOSURE LANDED IN ONE DIFF: 59 -> 77 ROWS, BASE 77/77, AND ALL SIX 59/59 ESCAPES DIE.** ***\n\n    M30 74/77   M31 74/77   M32 75/77   M33 75/77   M37 67/77   M28 67/77\n\n**The enumeration is BOUNDED and that is why this is not `#392`:** the guarded store reads `tileptr`, `ofs`, `buf`, `bufdepth`, and **`tile_nr`, `tile_nr_x`, `tile_nr_y`, `xofs` and `ofs` are not independent axes** -- each is a pure function of `(x, y, bufdepth, tlb_mode)`, the latter two already closed by table 3.  So *close every derived coordinate* IS *close the pair (x, y)*: **two free variables, read off the function, unable to grow by argument.**  [CORRECTED 2026-10-18 by the 7n-5 ruling (regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_35.md; GPT-6 Astra confirming): this is the 2026-09-04 finiteness bound under a new name, and it bounds nothing: `(x, y)` ranges over 2048 x 128 after `y &= 127`, and the four coordinate cells SAMPLE it.  The derived coordinates are clause (e) because the body does not test them as a case, not because two free variables close them; this closure was L1 strengthening on a severity warrant.]\n\n**MY ELEVEN-ROW COSTING WAS ONE TOO MANY.**  Twelve nibbles reach `default:` and **two were already driven** (`0xf0` and `0x50`, at the same cell), so ten.  The `*color` axis costs **zero** extra rows because the nibbles are drawn in a second foreground colour.\n\nFour coordinate cells, each in its own tile so every accept is a clause-(f) anchor at a distinct physical address: C1 (132,0) C2 (0,1) C3 (0,128) **C4 (132,129) -- THE JOINT CELL**, where the pairwise disjunctions are true everywhere else.  `x = 132` not 128 because at 128 `x % tilewidth` is 0 and `xofs` stays pinned; `y = 129` not 128 because `y &= 127` runs before the colour switch.  **11 of 12 predictions exact, including `M41` reddening ONLY C4** -- the joint cell's entire warrant, measured.\n\n**`silent` IS JUSTIFIED BY MEASUREMENT, NOT JUDGEMENT:** `M38_acceptannounce` reds the four `silent` rows and **zero** `ann1` rows.  The four `ann cum` rows are deleted -- their kill sets were strict subsets.\n\n*** AND A READ-ONLY AUDIT OF THE SEAT'S OWN BLOCK FOUND REAL DEFECTS IN IT, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING: ***\n* **The word \"complete\" did not belong in the enumeration.**  The TLB array's *contents* were named as an input to `tileptr` and then dropped from the conclusion.  `tileptr` is the store's address operand, so by the same flow test that makes `tlb_mode` a floor axis, **the array VALUE is a genuine floor-shaped gap** -- `if (ok || (tileptr & 0xfff00000))` is predicted 77/77.  **Filed as OWED, not fixed, because a repair owes its own pass.**  [CORRECTED 2026-10-18 by the 7n-5 ruling (regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_35.md; GPT-6 Astra confirming): clause (e), not owed.  Bit 31 is the only tested bit of `tileptr` and it is constant-cleared before the store, so `if (ok || (tileptr & 0xfff00000))` is equivalent to the fix on every driven row, by inspection; the array-contents slice is coverage debt, one of the four readbacks filed as `sgirecontentslice`.]\n* `mode & 1`, `x & 1`, `y & 2` also sweep -- **within-class value slices, not closable by any finite row set.**  [CORRECTED 2026-10-18 by the 7n-5 ruling (regress/artifacts/fablequeue7n_20261009/fable5_rule7n_35.md; GPT-6 Astra confirming): each slice is predicted one-row closable (derived, never run: an uninitialised-buffer kill needs a run, since an identical-byte write is invisible) and only the FAMILY is not, so 'closable by a finite row set' discriminates nothing (the 2026-09-04 finiteness bound under a new name); they are clause (e) because the body does not test them as a case.]  The contract is rewritten to separate **EXHAUSTED** axes (`tlb_mode`, `bufdepth`, `color_mode`) from **SAMPLED** ones, rather than calling all of them closed.\n* **L4, corrected in place:** a probe comment claimed every row held `xofs` at zero; it is 16 at `ycrcb x4y1`.\n* One audit claim was CHECKED AND REJECTED -- table 3's latch mutants are carried forward, in the regression tail.\n\nSTILL OWED: the 25-leg regression tail is live for score comparability; the clone holds its sentinel until its two-part clearance.  **Gate wiring is OWED, not exempt** -- `gate_arm.sh:1283` already runs the sibling probe on the same machine -- **and it must pin 77, not 59.**\n\nTWO RESIDUALS, both READINGS not measurements (`killtable4_20260911/RESIDUALS.md`): the sibling `tlb_mode` arm has **the same blind spot** (`if (tile_nr) *color = random();` predicted to sweep its 8-row detector), and **the GET-side `default:` has no detector at all** plus an unlatched guest-drivable `fatal()` filed nowhere.\n\n*A method note from the seat, and it is this session's recurring shape: its own \"sentinel absent\" line was a FALSE NEGATIVE -- it used git-bash `test -f /tmp/...`, and **git-bash `/tmp` and WSL `/tmp` are different filesystems.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11, `M1_reinstate` (clause (b), verbatim reinstatement) -- **THE DELETION WARRANT IS CONFIRMED EMPIRICALLY, NOT BY KILL-SET ANALYSIS ALONE**. ***\n\n    37 - 4 + 16 = 49,  measured 49.\n\n**The clause-(b) mutant lost PRECISELY its four `ann cum` reds and nothing else.**  Those four rows were deleted from the detector on the grounds that their kill sets were strict subsets of other rows' -- an argument from analysis.  *The arithmetic now closes on the measurement*: had the deletion cost any mutant its only kill, the reconciliation would not have balanced.\n\nThe 16 gained are exactly the designed set -- the ten `nib` declines, the four `xy dec` cells, and both new announce rows.  *(`M1` restores the old UNLATCHED `fatal()` with different text, so the `ANN` count drops to zero, which is why both announce rows red.)*\n\n**And the five accept anchors plus `xy anchor t0` correctly stayed GREEN**, because `M1` touches only the `default:` arm -- a negative control on the deletion, in the same leg.\n\n*This is the shape the vacuity floor asks for and rarely gets in one number: a row-count change, a predicted kill-set delta, and a measured total that reconciles without slack.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 -- **CLAUSES (a) AND (b) DISCHARGED ON THE NEW 77-ROW INSTRUMENT**, by a matched pair rather than by assertion. ***\n\n    M11_null      28/77     (whole-file PRE-FIX dev_sgi_re.c)\n    M1_reinstate  28/77     (verbatim reinstatement of the random() arm)\n\n**The two score IDENTICALLY -- as they did on the 59-row instrument, where both scored 22/59.**  That pairing discharges two clauses at once: **(a)** the detector fails on the pre-fix build, and **(b)** it fails on a verbatim reinstatement of the removed defect.\n\n**And both remain well short of clean, so adding 22 rows did not WEAKEN either clause** -- which is the thing a closure that grows an instrument has to show and usually does not.  *A larger detector can dilute a clause without anyone noticing: the same absolute kill count against a bigger denominator reads as a higher score.  Here the pre-fix and reinstatement legs are still 49 rows short of BASE.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 CLASS-CLOSURE REVIEW (codex GPT-6 Astra at max).  **THE CLASS CLOSURE I DEMANDED IS NOT ESTABLISHED, AND FOUR OF MY PREMISES WERE WRONG.** ***\n\n*VERDICT: The declined PUT repair is correct as written; the claimed complete class closure is not established.  A separate buffer-initialization defect remains in the worktree.*\n\n**THE REPAIR ITSELF STANDS.**  What is refused is the framing I put around it.\n\n=== FOUR PREMISE CORRECTIONS, each checkable ===\n  1. **The probe is already past the 59-row instrument I quoted.**  It carries the pass-4 nibble and coordinate additions; statically expanding its loops gives **77 assertions in 17 sessions** -- four earlier assertions removed, 22 added.  *I was reasoning about a detector two passes stale.*\n  2. *** THE SIX ESCAPES ARE NOT SIX POSITION-SELECTOR ESCAPES, and my own table had FIVE entries. ***  The probe names them **M28, M30, M31, M32, M33 and M37** -- and describes **M28 as a COLOUR-NIBBLE mutant and M37 as a FOREGROUND-VALUE mutant.**  *So the 'one class, six instances' story that triggered my class-closure demand is itself wrong: at most four are position selectors.*\n  3. **There is no shared executable tail after this function's PUT/GET branches** -- the function ends immediately after them.  *The common-tail restriction I cited belongs to a DIFFERENT function, `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re)`.*\n  4. **`63 68 69 6e` spells \"chin\" and that does not establish provenance.**  *'The security-relevant evidence is the transfer of uninitialized automatic storage across the guest boundary.  Printable content is neither necessary nor sufficient to prove that provenance.'*  **I had used the recognisable string as if it were the proof.**\n\n=== *** THE DOCTRINAL KEY IS IN THE PARTITION TABLE, AND IT DISSOLVES MY FLOOR CLAIM *** ===\nThe seat enumerated what the body ACTUALLY TESTS, distinguishing *a value being tested* from *a value contributing to an operand*:\n\n| lvalue | partition the BODY induces |\n|---|---|\n| `put` | zero / nonzero |\n| `tlb_mode` | switch labels 0,1,2,4,default; other tests split default's 3 from 5-7 |\n| `linear` | derived from `tlb_mode > 3` -- **NOT an independent axis** |\n| incoming `x`, `y` | `< 0` / in-range / `>= 2048` where evaluated.  **There is no `x == 0` or `y == 0` test**, and a later `y &= 127` changes the value a mutant sees |\n| tiled `tile_nr` | `< 256` / `>= 256`.  *** NO ZERO/NONZERO TEST EXISTS *** |\n| linear `tile_nr` | `< 32` / `>= 32` **after reassignment `tile_nr = x >> 12` -- a DIFFERENT value from the tiled calculation** |\n| `color_mode` | CI / RGB / RGBA / ABGR / default, where **default is twelve NIBBLE VALUES, not twelve switch labels** |\n\n> *** BECAUSE THE BODY NEVER TESTS `tile_nr` FOR ZERO, `M30`'s `if (tile_nr == 0) ok = 0;` IS NOT A FLOOR AXIS UNDER THE BODY-TESTED-PARTITION RULE -- IT IS CLAUSE-(e) DEBT. ***  *My brief demanded closure over selectors the rule does not put inside the floor, which is the `#392` shape wearing the class-closure rule's clothes.  The seat's closing sentence is the one to keep: these additions **'must not be represented as full closure over arbitrary predicates on all function inputs.'***\n\n=== THE BOUNDED, REVIEWABLE IMPROVEMENT -- what is actually owed ===\n  * **eleven missing mode/depth/latch cells**;\n  * **one reverse-latch assertion** -- one aggregate `colour/others` assertion appended to an EXISTING colour-first session: after the colour announcement, drive the twelve other sites and require each expected announcement.  **Control family `SET_OTHER(j)`: the colour warning update also sets another site's bit**, observable as the missing announcement from site `j`.  *The current other-first sequence stays green under that error because the other bit was already set.*  **One assertion, no new process.**\n  * **strengthened four depth-eight assertions**;\n  * an **unconditional default-arm `return`**;\n  * a **contract that names its ACTUAL partitions**.\n\n*** A CAUTION ON EXTENDING COORDINATES, which would have bitten the obvious implementation: *** *'Reusing C1's fixed vertex at another depth can select another tile; changing only the mode can turn a proposed decline test into an earlier invalid-pointer return.'*  **Each added address/depth setup needs an ACCEPTING control proving that mapping reaches its intended destination** -- otherwise a row that looks like a decline test is really an early-return test.\n\n=== `buf[8] = {0}` IS ACCEPTED, BUT NOT AS BELT-AND-BRACES ===\n**Accepted as a candidate companion repair on a TRANSFERRED WARRANT**: the same file's `#509` comment records the same buffer-to-guest failure mechanism and explicitly leaves other widths outstanding.  **Not a measured delta.**  And it is not free -- *'I would not describe it as merely belt-and-braces'*:\n  * **it changes six accepted PUT format/depth combinations**;\n  * at the shared declaration it **also changes GET behaviour when the read supplies fewer bytes than packing consumes**;\n  * it establishes deterministic bytes, **not hardware-correct semantics** for every unsupported combination;\n  * its generated-code and performance cost are unmeasured.\n\n*'The permitted files do not establish that zero padding is the intended hardware behavior for all those combinations.  They do establish why copying uninitialized storage is unacceptable.'*\n\n**AND THE TWO ROWS STAY SEPARATE, with the invariants named:** [[sgirandomcolour]] = *an unsupported PUT performs NO STORE*; [[sgirebufuninit]] = *every byte consumed by an actual transfer or colour reconstruction is INITIALIZED*.  **Different invariants, so the distinction survives** -- I had asked whether the six escapes collapse them, and they do not.\n\n**STATUS: `#519` still must not commit as-is** (the escapes are real and the default arm should return unconditionally), **but the round owes a BOUNDED list, not the unbounded closure I specified.**  Landing held by owner ruling.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:38974:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  **FOUND BY THE CODEX SEAT** on an unrelated brief, and VERIFIED at HEAD before filing.\n\n    int depth = 8 << ((mode & MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK) >> MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT);\n\n`MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK' is `0x00000300' and `MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT' is 8, so the extracted field is TWO BITS, 0..3,\nand `depth' takes exactly the values **8, 16, 32, 64**.  The function then tests `if (depth == 4)', which is\ntherefore **never true** -- the guarded block is dead code.\n\ncodex's reading of what is lost: the dead block is *\"its intended RGBA construction\"*, so *\"its helper calls\nretain CI\"* -- i.e. the transfer runs in colour-index mode where the author meant RGBA.  **That second half\nis NOT verified here** and is the part a round would have to establish; what IS verified is that the branch\ncannot execute.\n\n*** IT ALSO REFUTES A PREMISE OF THE `sgirebufuninit' BRIEF, which is why it was filed rather than noted. ***\nThat brief asks whether callers constrain the (colour type, depth) pair.  Here is a caller that TRIES to and\nFAILS SILENTLY -- so the model's own producer does not supply the pair its author intended.  Any round\nreasoning about what callers guarantee must account for this one.\n\nLOW because a dead branch cannot itself corrupt anything; the SEVERITY lives in whatever the missing RGBA\nconstruction was supposed to do, which is unestablished.\n\n*** RAISED LOW -> HIGH.  I FILED THIS SAYING `a dead branch cannot itself corrupt anything' AND THAT IS\nWRONG: THE DEAD BRANCH IS WHAT MAKES THE MTE PATH LEAK.  Found in the kimi seat's pass-1 answer\n(regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/pass1_panel/kimi.txt) -- which I had already opened and under-read -- and VERIFIED here. ***\n\n    src_mode = (src_tlb << 10) + (((mode & MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK) >> MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT) << 8);\n    dst_mode = (dst_tlb << 10) + (((mode & MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK) >> MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT) << 8);\n    if (depth == 4) {                     <- DEAD: depth = 8 << field, so 8/16/32/64, never 4\n            src_mode |= DE_MODE_TYPE_RGBA;\n            dst_mode |= DE_MODE_TYPE_RGBA;\n    }\n\n**The constructed modes carry NO colour bits, so the colour type is 0x00 = CI.**  The only thing that would\nmake them RGBA is the dead branch.  So **the MTE path always runs CI** -- and CI's PUT arm fills ONLY\n`buf[0]' while the transfer is `bufdepth' bytes.\n\n    MTE_DEPTH_8  = 0  -> bufdepth 1   matched, no leak\n    MTE_DEPTH_16 = 1  -> bufdepth 2   fills buf[0], writes 2  -> 1 uninitialised byte per pixel\n    MTE_DEPTH_32 = 2  -> bufdepth 4   fills buf[0], writes 4  -> 3 uninitialised bytes per pixel\n\n**All three are DOCUMENTED constants in `crmfbreg.h'.**  kimi: *\"every MTE_TLB_A/B/C fill with MTE_DEPTH_16/32\nleaks buf[1..3] per pixel (documented MTE_DEPTH values!).  This is the strongest reachability evidence:\nPROM/NetBSD crmfb_fill_rect uses this path.\"*\n\n**SO THE FIX ORDER MATTERS AND IS NOT WHAT I ASSUMED.**  Repairing `depth == 4' ALONE would switch the MTE\npath to RGBA, which fills `buf[0..3]' -- accidentally masking the leak at depth 32 while leaving depth 16 (2\nbytes, RGBA fills 4) and every DE-path case untouched.  **A round that fixes only this would look like it\nfixed [[sgirebufuninit]] and would not have.**  That is the shape the vacuity floor exists to catch, arriving\nbefore either round starts.\n\n*** WHOLE-FUNCTION AUDIT, codex 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/sgimtedepth4dead/codex_audit_20260907.txt).  MY Q4 CONCLUSION IS PARTLY\nWRONG AND THE SCOPE RULING IS FINER THAN `SEQUENCE, DO NOT SHARE'. ***\n\nVerdict: *\"Q1 is correct for the tiled transfer path.  Q4 is partly wrong: correcting the condition for\n32-bit transfers leaves depth 16 in CI.  Additional defects exist; the current `zerobuf' chunk sizing is\nbounded.\"*\n\n**WHAT I GOT RIGHT AND WHAT I MISSED.**  I argued that repairing `depth == 4' alone would switch the path to\nRGBA and MASK the leak at depth 32.  True for depth 32 -- and I stopped there:\n\n    depth 32   -> RGBA both ways; four source bytes preserved, all four transmitted bytes initialised\n    depth 16   -> STAYS CI: two bytes transmitted, ONE initialised          <- I missed this\n    depth 64   -> STAYS CI: eight bytes transmitted, ONE initialised        <- I missed this entirely\n\n**So the repair masks ONE of three leaking depths and leaves two.**  My sentence said it leaves \"depth 16 and\nthe whole DE path\"; depth 64 was not in it at all.\n\n**And forcing RGBA at depth 16 would not help either:** *\"its PUT would initialize four local bytes but\ntransmit only two.  Its GET would still decode four bytes after reading only two, exposing another helper\ninitialization problem.\"*  The colour type and the transfer width are independent, so no choice of colour\nfixes a width mismatch.\n\n**THE SCOPE RULING IS FINER THAN MINE.**  I said the two defects must be SEQUENCED rather than shared.\ncodex: *\"The defects CAN share a change round, but successful 32-bit MTE behavior would then provide no\nindependent evidence that the shared helper defect was fixed.  For separate rounds, handling the helper\ndefect first avoids that masking.\"*  **So sharing is permitted and costs the independent evidence** -- which\nis a vacuity-floor concern, not a reopening-rule one.  If separated, the HELPER (buf initialisation) goes\nFIRST.  That inverts nothing I had planned but states the cost precisely, which my version did not.\n\n**CONFIRMED SAFE:** the `zerobuf' chunk sizing is bounded -- so the earlier reading of that path stands.\n\n**RE-DERIVED, NOT NEW:** it independently found the RGB GET/PUT layout mismatch ([[sgirergbroundtrip]] --\nnow THREE independent derivations) and the MTE access handler's unreachable width branch ([[sgimtewidth]],\nalready filed, whose title already anticipates that the obvious fix would CREATE a width kill).  **No\nduplicate rows filed** -- the C0 check catching its second duplicate of the session.\n\n*** AND IT SELF-REPORTED A SCOPE VIOLATION, WHICH IS WORTH RECORDING BECAUSE NOTHING WOULD HAVE CAUGHT IT:\n*\"One faulty search exclusion returned forbidden artifact matches, crossing your scope; no finding above\nrelies on them.\"*  The brief forbade reading `regress/artifacts/'; a bad exclusion pattern let some through,\nand the seat said so unprompted and stated the consequence.  That is the honest form, and it is the same\ndiscipline this project asks of its own records. ***\n\n**INDEPENDENTLY CONFIRMED 2026-09-07 by the codex seat**, unprompted, on a panel about a different\ndefect in the same file (`#505`, `_scratchpad/panel_20260907_051055`).  Its verdict line ends:\n*\"The cited MTE RGBA branch is unreachable.\"*\n\nThat is a second seat, on a separate brief, reaching this row's central claim from the source rather\nthan from this row -- which is the distinction the seat ledger draws between a reading that RATIFIES a\nbrief-supplied premise and one that RE-DERIVES a fact the brief did not supply.  **The `#505` brief\nmentioned the branch only in a question about whether RGB mode is guest-reachable; it did not state\nthat the branch is dead.**\n\n*And the glm seat, packet-fed, raised a DIFFERENT hazard on the same two lines and was honest about not\nbeing able to settle it: it warned that `src_mode |= DE_MODE_TYPE_RGBA` yields ABGR (0x30) rather than\nRGBA IF the type field already holds RGB (0x10), and named the settling fact as \"the full mode\nconstruction around that line\".  Checked: `src_mode = (src_tlb << 10) + (((mode & 0x300) >> 8) << 8)`\nsets bits >= 10 and bits 8-9 only, so the type nibble is always 0 and `|=` gives exactly RGBA.  **The\nconditional is sound and its antecedent is false.**  It remains a real latent note -- if that\nconstruction ever gained a type field, `|=` would corrupt rather than replace it -- but it is moot while\nthe enclosing `if (depth == 4)` never executes, which is this row.*\n\n*** PASS-1 DESIGN AUDIT, 2026-09-07 (codex,\n`regress/artifacts/sgimtedepth4dead/codex_pass1_20260907.txt`).  THE DISPOSITION IS **DEFER**, AND\nIT RE-ORDERS THE QUEUE. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"Defer `depth == 32` activation.  Correct the comment now and fix buffer\ninitialization under `sgirebufuninit`.  The one-token repair changes defined fill behaviour as well\nas copy behaviour, and exposes another existing arithmetic defect through MTE.\"*\n\n**So the obvious repair is the WRONG one, for the reason the brief asked about:** making the dead\nbranch live is not a bug fix, it is enabling a path that has never executed in this fork, and it\nchanges **fills as well as copies**.  The addition licence wants a measured delta or a transferred\nwarrant; neither exists, and activation additionally surfaces a further arithmetic defect.\n\n**WHAT TO DO INSTEAD, in the seat's order:**\n  1. **Correct the comment.**  It asserts *\"this makes sure that we copy 32 bits when doing 32-bit\n     transfers\"* -- a behaviour the code does not have.  That is a wrong record in product code and\n     is cheap to fix.\n  2. **Fix the buffer initialisation under [[sgirebufuninit]]**, which is where the defect actually\n     lives.  The dead branch is the MECHANISM of its reachability, not the defect itself.\n\n*** A PREMISE CORRECTION -- MY S2 WAS TOO BROAD, and this is the fourth of the session. ***  I wrote\nthat *every* MTE transfer runs through `horrible_getputpixel` with CI colour.  The seat scoped it:\n**TILED MTE transfers use this helper; LINEAR MTE fills use a separate path.**  So the exposure is\nreal but narrower than the row claimed, and any detector must select a tiled transfer deliberately\nrather than assume it.\n\n**Q1 CONFIRMED with its enumeration, which is worth keeping because it also bounds the selectors:**\nonly TLB selectors A, B and C reach this block -- the source check rejects larger ones -- and the\ndepth field maps as\n\n    field 0 -> depth  8 bits -> bufdepth 1 -> modes 0x000 / 0x400 / 0x800   (A / B / C)\n    field 1 -> depth 16      -> bufdepth 2 -> modes 0x100 / 0x500 / 0x900\n    field 2 -> depth 32      -> bufdepth 4 -> modes 0x200 / 0x600 / 0xa00\n    field 3 -> depth 64      -> bufdepth 8 -> modes 0x300 / 0x700 / 0xb00\n\n-- which confirms `depth` is never 4 and that the intended test was `depth == 32`.\n\n**QUEUE CONSEQUENCE:** `sgirebufuninit` is the round to run, not this one.  This row's own\ndisposition shrinks to a COMMENT CORRECTION plus a disclosure that the branch is dead, and it should\nbe taken as a records rider rather than as a behaviour round.  *That is the option-set discipline\nworking in the conservative direction for once: the answer was \"do less than you proposed\".*\n\n*** INDEPENDENTLY CONFIRMED BY THE CODEX SEAT, 2026-09-07, while answering an unrelated design\nbrief on `sgirandomcolour` -- it was not asked about this row. ***\n\n*\"MTE's RGBA-selection branch is dead.  `depth` evaluates to 8, 16, 32, or 64, but the branch tests\n`depth == 4`.  Consequently, these helper calls retain CI, whose PUT fills only one byte even when the\ntransfer is wider.\"*  (`dev_sgi_re.c` depth calculation `:1311`, dead branch `:1365`.)\n\n**And it draws the boundary against [[sgirebufuninit]] in one sentence:** *\"Buffer initialisation\nwould address undefined bytes without restoring the intended multi-byte copying behaviour.\"*  So\nzero-filling `buf` does NOT subsume this row -- the wide copy stays broken, it merely stops being\nundefined.  That is the same boundary the `sgirebufuninit` panel drew from the other side.\n\n*** THE DIAGNOSIS IS A UNITS MISMATCH, AND THE CODE SAYS SO FOUR LINES EARLIER.  2026-09-07. ***\n\n    :1311  int depth = 8 << ((mode & MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK) >> MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT);   /* BITS */\n    :1359  src_x1 /= (depth / 8);                          <- the author converting bits -> bytes\n    :1369  // ... this makes sure that we copy 32 bits when doing 32-bit transfers.\n    :1371  if (depth == 4) {                               <- compares BITS to a BYTE count\n\n**`depth` is in bits and takes 8/16/32/64; the test wants 4 BYTES.**  The comment states the intent\noutright -- *\"copy 32 bits when doing 32-bit transfers\"* -- so the intended predicate is\n`depth == 32`, and `:1359` shows the same function converting between the two units four lines above\nthe mistake.\n\n**AND THE CONSTANTS DEFINE ONLY THREE OF THE FOUR FIELD VALUES:** `crmfbreg.h:268-270` names\n`MTE_DEPTH_8 = 0`, `MTE_DEPTH_16 = 1`, `MTE_DEPTH_32 = 2`.  **Field value 3 -- depth 64 -- is named\nnowhere**, so the two-bit field is wider than the encodings that exist and a fix owes a decision\nabout it.\n\n> **MY ARITHMETIC FOR THE OBVIOUS FIX, offered to the seat as a question rather than a conclusion:**\n> `depth == 32` is field 2, giving `bufdepth = 1 << 2 = 4` bytes AND RGBA, which assigns `buf[0..3]`\n> -- matching the four-byte transfer exactly.  **But that repairs only the 32-bit case.**  Field 1\n> still moves 2 bytes with `buf[0]` assigned, and field 3 moves 8 with `buf[0]` assigned.  So the\n> one-token fix may be the same bug one layer up.\n\n*Design out to codex: `regress/artifacts/sgimtedepth4dead/DESIGN_brief.md`.*\n\n*** CODEX DESIGN PASS 2026-09-07 (`regress/artifacts/sgimtedepth4dead/codex_design_20260907.txt`).\nVERDICT: confirmed dead comparison; `depth == 32` is a justified NARROW correction. ***\n\n**TWO PREMISES OF MINE CORRECTED, both toward less confidence:**\n  * **S3 needed the word PUT.**  The CI *PUT* initialises only `buf[0]`; a successful *GET* reads\n    `bufdepth` bytes into its own buffer and then retains only `buf[0]` in `*color`.  Different\n    mechanisms, and I had written them as one.\n  * **S4 OVERSTATED CAUSATION.**  This branch explains the 32-bit MTE mismatch, but **DE\n    independently reaches the buffer defects** -- so the dead branch is *a* mechanism of\n    [[sgirebufuninit]], not *the* mechanism, and it does not prove every affected copy observably\n    leaks.\n\n*** THE STRUCTURAL LIMIT, and it settles Q1 against the broader repair: THE HELPER CANNOT CARRY\nEIGHT BYTES AT ALL. ***  `*color` is a `uint32_t`, so no implemented type represents a 64-bit\ntransfer regardless of which type is selected:\n\n    8 bits    CI carries the one transferred byte\n    16 bits   CI loses the second; RGBA would make GET consume TWO BYTES BEYOND the two read\n    32 bits   RGBA carries exactly four        <- what `depth == 32' buys\n    64 bits   NO implemented type carries eight; the intermediate uint32_t cannot represent them\n\n**So 'select a type per width' is not a complete solution with this helper.**  A broader repair needs\nexplicit transfer-width handling or refusal of unsupported combinations, and *the scoped source\ncannot establish the hardware's intended 16-bit packing or any 64-bit format*.  The separate\n`DE_MODE_PIXDEPTH_*` definitions do not settle it -- **the helper does not interpret those bits.**\n\n**FIELD VALUE 3 IS GUEST-STORABLE AND REACHES THE TILED PATH** (`:1522` retains the field; the tiled\ndestination cases at `:1341` have no depth validation), and the emulator then treats it as an\neight-byte transfer.  *The separate LINEAR destination path rejects non-8-bit depth by exiting.*\n**But its hardware meaning is unknown**: the missing constant proves only that this header names\nthree encodings, not that the fourth is reserved -- and the regression's `MODE_RGB64` is no evidence\neither, being a DE test value I constructed locally.\n\n> **So a complete MTE repair owes an explicit DISPOSITION for value 3.  Declining it as UNIMPLEMENTED\n> is defensible pending a hardware contract; calling it RESERVED is not established.  A\n> comparison-only correction must leave that obligation recorded** -- which is what this note is.\n\n**Q5 -- THE CLAUSE-(d) MUTANT IS ONE CHARACTER:** after the fix, change `32` to `33` and the branch\nis unreachable again.  `rgb tiled64` and the other nine rows of the RGB script are all expected to\nstay green, **because they exercise DE and never construct an MTE mode.**  *Static prediction; no\nmutant built.*\n\n**AND THE WITNESS HAS AN ORACLE PROBLEM WORTH RECORDING BEFORE ANYONE BUILDS ONE:** MTE is registered\nunconditionally beside DE and dispatches synchronously to guest physical memory, so it is reachable\n-- but *\"an affected copy can appear correct if the PUT's uninitialised tail happens to retain bytes\nfrom the preceding GET.  These are separate automatic objects; that preservation is neither\nguaranteed nor evidence of correctness.\"*  **So a simple copy-equality row cannot be trusted**, and\nfill needs separate validation from copy because fill bypasses the source conversion.  The sibling's\nDE programming also cannot be reused unchanged: its LIN_A destination is exactly what MTE's linear\npath exits on.\n\n*** GROK WITNESS DESIGN 2026-09-07: ACCEPT `depth == 32`, REFUSE MY ORACLE, and it is NOT a\n`detector:` round -- a FILL channel IS deterministic. ***\n\n**MY PROPOSED ORACLE WAS WRONG, and the reason is a fact about the write path:** CI PUT does not\nreplicate `buf[0]` across the span and does not leave a tail.  It writes `buf[0]` at the lowest\naddress and `buf[1..bufdepth-1]` as uninitialised stack, and `memory_rw` memcpys `bufdepth` bytes.\nSo a pre-painted destination is **overwritten with garbage**, not preserved:\n\n    belief                          what the code does\n    CI writes 1 byte, 3 stay paint  CI writes FOUR bytes at MTE_DEPTH_32; the tail is garbage\n    remaining paint IS the defect   on HEAD the tail is not paint -- the row reds because the path\n                                    RAN, which reads as \"the witness never reached MTE\"\n    tail != paint IS the defect     true on HEAD *and* after the fix.  Vacuous.\n    tail equals the previous GET    separate automatic buf[8] objects; neither guaranteed nor\n                                    evidence\n\n*** THE CHANNEL THAT WORKS: TILED **FILL** `dest[0]`.  FILL never GETs. ***  With `bg` chosen to\nhave four distinct bytes (`0x11223344`):\n\n    dest[0]   HEAD (CI)  0x44   -- buf[0] = (uint8_t)bg, the LOW byte\n    dest[0]   fix (RGBA) 0x11   -- RGBA lays [bg>>24, bg>>16, bg>>8, bg], the HIGH byte\n    dest[1..3]                  -- uninitialised on HEAD: **DO NOT ASSERT**\n\n**COPY CANNOT WITNESS THIS AT ALL.**  Source `aa bb cc dd`: CI GET takes `buf[0] == 0xaa` and CI PUT\nwrites `0xaa` at `dest[0]`; RGBA GET reconstructs `0xaabbccdd` and RGBA PUT also writes `0xaa` at\n`dest[0]`.  **The first byte of a copy is the one byte CI already gets right**, and the broken bytes\nare exactly the uninitialised ones that must not be read.  *\"If the witness is a blit, refuse it.\"*\n\n**NOT a `detector:` round.**  The path is reachable and the attempt has not been made; it only looked\nimpossible because the oracle was bad.  Four ways to produce a FALSE \"cannot witness\": MTE LIN_A/B at\ndepth 32 (LINEAR `exit(1)`s -- host death, not a red row); COPY; asserting `dest[1..3]` on HEAD; and\n**TLB_A without bit 15**, which returns silently and looks like \"MTE did nothing\" -- the trap\n`sgi_re_rgb_probe.py` already paid for twice.\n\n**LEAVE FIELD 3 ALONE in this diff.**  A tiled decline is a SECOND SITE -- new predicate, new\nannounce, new accept row -- and it is not the units bug.  The tiled/LINEAR disagreement is\npre-existing and `if (depth == 32)` does not worsen it.  File field 3 and the LINEAR depth kill as\ntheir own row.\n\n**THE REGISTER MAP FOR THE BUILD, derived today so the next step is mechanical rather than a\nre-derivation:**\n\n    CRIME RE base            0xb5000000   (RGB probe: CRIME_RE_TLB_A 0x1000 -> 0xb5001000)\n    CRIME_MTE_MODE  0x3000   MTE_MODE_COPY 0x800 (1=copy, 0=CLEAR/fill -- FILL needs it CLEAR)\n    CRIME_MTE_BG    0x3018   MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK 0x300, MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT 8, MTE_DEPTH_32 = 2\n    CRIME_MTE_DST0  0x3030   MTE_MODE_DST_BUF_MASK 0x1c, MTE_TLB_A = 0\n    CRIME_MTE_DST1  0x3038   -> MTE_MODE for a tiled 32-bit FILL is 0x200\n    CRIME_DE_START  0x0800   ORed into the register address; `if (startFlag) do_mte_transfer()'\n                             at the END of DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_mte), so any MTE write with 0x800 set\n                             fires the transfer -- write DST1|START last\n    TLB_A[0] = 0x8002        BIT 15 IS THE VALID BIT (same constant the RGB probe uses)\n\n*NetBSD's `crmfb_fill_rect` is exactly this path -- FILL, tiled `MTE_TLB_A`, `MTE_DEPTH_32`, X\nbyte-scaled -- so the driver path and the oracle are the same path.*\n\n*** THE PROBE'S MODE VERIFIED SAFE BY READING, 2026-09-07 -- I answered my own review question\nrather than waiting for the seat, per *anything checkable in two minutes gets CHECKED*. ***\n\n`MODE_TILED_FILL = 0x200` passes all three pre-checks in `do_mte_transfer` before the dst switch:\n\n    :1323  dst_y_step != 0 && != 1 && != -1   dst_y_step is 0 (register never written)   SAFE\n                                              -- and its exit(1) is commented out anyway\n    :1328  mode & MTE_MODE_STIPPLE            STIPPLE is **0x400**; 0x200 & 0x400 == 0     SAFE\n    :1336  src > MTE_TLB_C                    src = (0x200 & 0xe0) >> 5 = 0 = MTE_TLB_A    SAFE\n\n**And my worry about the bytemask kill does not apply: `:1413 if (bytemask != 0xffffffff)` is inside\nthe LINEAR arm** (its own debug line at `:1407` says `LINEAR TRANSFER`), which a tiled fill never\nreaches.  *A zero bytemask would kill a LINEAR transfer and is invisible to the tiled path* -- worth\nknowing for anyone who later writes a linear MTE probe.\n\n`MTE_MODE_COPY` is `0x800` and is CLEAR in `0x200`, so this is a FILL and no GET runs -- which is the\nwhole reason the oracle is deterministic.\n\n*** PASS 1 PANEL, 2026-09-07, panel dir `_scratchpad/panel_20260907_170713`.  SEVEN transports\nfired; SIX answered with a verdict.  Seat-by-seat, because a seat that did not answer is a SEAT\nFAILURE and never agreement: ***\n\n    codex GPT-6 Astra max   ANSWERED 127,698 b, full verdict\n    grok 4.6 xhigh          ANSWERED  12,341 b\n    ollama glm-5.3          ANSWERED   7,686 b        ollama deepseek  ANSWERED 4,381 b\n    ollama minimax          ANSWERED   8,735 b        ollama kimi-k3   ANSWERED 8,014 b\n    kimi CLI                *** WALLED -- 403 weekly (7-day) usage limit, 350 b ***\n                            the SEAT IS COVERED by the kimi-k3:cloud substitute above\n    agy 3.7                 DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED -- measurement reserve (owner ruling)\n    Opus 5 / Fable 5        DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED -- held for the 2026-09-09 adjudication\n\n**THE CENTRAL CLAIM SURVIVED ALL SIX**, each by enumerating the four field values rather than\nagreeing: `depth = 8 << (2-bit field)` gives {8,16,32,64}, so `depth == 4` is unreachable, both\n`|= DE_MODE_TYPE_RGBA` assignments are dead, every tiled transfer takes CI, and at 32 bits CI\nassigns `buf[0]` while `memory_rw` copies four.  **F1 (`depth == 32`) is unanimous.**\n\n*** THREE ERRORS IN MY OWN BRIEF, EACH FOUND BY A SEAT.  Recorded because the premise-audit\nopener is the one piece of prose in this project that demonstrably fires. ***\n\n**1. A WRONG NUMBER, caught INDEPENDENTLY BY TWO SEATS (kimi substitute and codex).**  I wrote\nthat F2 (`depth >= 32`) \"converts a four-byte problem into a four-byte problem at a different\noffset\".  At field value 3 the copy length is 8 and CI assigns ONE byte, so **SEVEN** are\nuninitialised; RGBA would assign four, leaving **FOUR**.  F2 is a REDUCTION, not a neutral\nswap.  *The conclusion (reject F2 for want of a semantic warrant) stands; the arithmetic under\nit did not, and a leak-count argument is exactly where that matters.*\n\n**2. A WRONG RECORD IN THE PROBE HEADER (codex).**  It said \"only the top byte of the\nbig-endian word is read\".  The probe executes `lw` and masks afterwards: it **READS four bytes\nand ASSERTS one**.  Corrected in place -- and the distinction is load-bearing, because the tail\nbecomes assertable the moment the fix lands, which is what `fill bot' now does.\n\n**3. AN IMPRECISE MECHANISM (kimi substitute).**  My `copy top' table said the dest-only cell\ngives 0x00 because \"the write puts the high byte of a low-byte colour first\".  It gives 0x00\nbecause the CI GET *assigns* `*color = buf[0]`, zero-extending; had the GET left `bg` untouched\nan RGBA PUT would have emitted `bg >> 24` = 0x11.  The row is right for a different reason, and\nthe reason is now in the row's own comment so a future seat does not \"correct\" the want to 0x11.\n\n*** TWO HOLES IN MY ROW SET, BOTH MEASURED-CLASS ESCAPES, BOTH CLOSED BEFORE TABLE 1. ***\n\n* **RGB-for-RGBA is invisible to dest[0] forever** (glm and grok, independently).  The RGB PUT\n  arm writes {bg>>24, bg>>16, bg>>8, **0x00**} -- IDENTICAL to RGBA at dest[0].  So a one-token\n  `|= DE_MODE_TYPE_RGB' substitution passed all twelve rows I had proposed.  Closed by\n  **`fill bot'** (dest[3]: 0x44 vs 0x00), and on the copy side by **`copy 4th'**.\n* **Over-reach to depth 16 is unpinned** (glm).  `depth >= 16' fires at 32 (so `fill top' stays\n  green) and is cold at 8 (so `depth8 top' stays green).  **Nothing reds.**  Closed by\n  **`depth16 top'** -- the ADJACENT DOCUMENTED cell, which is why 8 alone was never enough.\n\n**AND A THIRD, FOUND BY THE PRE-TABLE CHECK ITSELF rather than by a seat:** `M8`, a SOURCE-side\nRGB substitution, escaped all twelve rows including `copy top' and `copy 2nd', because RGB's GET\ndrops only the LOW byte.  `copy 4th' (dest[3] = 0x4d) closes it and closes two other cells with\nit.  *That is what the block is for, and it fired on its first mandated application.*\n\n*** A NEW LIVE DEFECT VOLUNTEERED UNDER THE \"if you found something else\" LICENCE (codex), which\nturned out to be ALREADY FILED -- so it is recorded as an independent re-derivation, not a new\nrow. ***  The RGB and RGBA GET arms compute `buf[0] << 24` on a promoted `uint8_t` (ABGR and the default arm shift `buf[3]`; CI shifts nothing -- \"every GET arm\" was FALSE and the pass-2 codex seat caught it), which promotes to signed `int`;\nfor a first byte >= 0x80 the shift exceeds INT_MAX and is UB.  That is [[sgirebufshiftub]].\n**It changed this round's design:** the copy rows use `SRC_WORD = 0x1a2b3c4d`, first byte\ndeliberately below 0x80, so this detector does not stand on undefined behaviour.\n\n**THE SCOPE ANSWER, converged across four seats:** ONE round for the 32-bit cell -- it is the\nonly cell with an intent oracle (the comment names 32) and the only one with a UB-free channel.\nThe 16-bit and encoding-3 cells share a DIFFERENT defect (CI never fills `bufdepth` bytes) which\nbelongs to [[sgirebufuninit]] and must close in ONE round across all widths, **not one round per\ndepth**.  This is explicitly not the `#499` same-class-three-times shape.\n\n> *** THE TRAP GROK NAMED, AND IT GOES IN THE CHANGELOG VERBATIM: F1 fills buf[0..3] at depth 32,\n> so it ACCIDENTALLY MASKS the 32-bit tiled cell of [[sgirebufuninit]].  A later round that rows\n> only depth 32 on this path will look fixed and will not be.  KEEP THAT SIBLING ALIVE ON DEPTH\n> 16.  Do not write prose claiming F1 closed the leak. ***\n\n**SIBLING PARITY -- the one place two seats disagreed, settled by reading rather than by vote.**\ncodex called MTE_TLB_B and _C \"uncovered\" and wanted rows; grok held that the repaired comparison\ndoes not read `dst_tlb`, so a per-label row would pin the helper's ARRAY SELECTION -- a different\nproperty -- and called it \"cheap, not required\".  **grok's reading is adopted and the cell is\nDISCLOSED rather than rowed**, with the escape stated in `PRETABLE.out`: a repair conditioned on\n`dst_tlb == MTE_TLB_A` escapes this detector.\n\n**MEASURED, same script both sides (witness clause (i)):**\n\n    6-row witness   HEAD  5/6    witness_prefix_HEAD.raw\n    13-row detector HEAD  9/12 at 12 rows (re-measured in the clone as M1; see the table)\n    13-row detector FIX  13/13   witness_12row_FIXED.raw + the table's BASE row\n\n\n*** SHIPPED as `2956c7e` on 2026-09-07.  STATE DELIBERATELY LEFT `in work': both stages stand\nat 7/9 awaiting opus5 and fable5, and a row may not CLOSE while a stage awaits a seat.  The work\nshipping and the row finishing are different facts.  Pass-2 receipt in regress/pass2_ledger.tsv;\nsix questions for the adjudication in fable_queue.md 7g. ***  *** FOUR UNREAD SEAT ANSWERS FROM _scratchpad/panel_20260907_051055 READ 2026-09-18 -- the FIRST of the 23 to be opened, chosen because this panel is cited by a LIVE HIGH row. ***  They contain real content, which is the fifth time this project has found something in an unread review.  (1) *** THREE SEATS INDEPENDENTLY DERIVED THAT `+' -> `|' IS AN UNCATCHABLE NULL MUTANT in the RGB pack: every term is a uint8_t shifted into DISJOINT bit ranges, so sum == OR always. ***  glm: 'No test can or should catch it.'  kimi: 'Uncatchable by construction.'  minimax: 'NOT caught by behavioral tests.'  That is an EQUIVALENT mutant and therefore a RESULT, not a coverage gap -- the same disposition #531 reached for its latch-macro renumbering.  Three independent derivations is the one kind of agreement that IS evidence.  (2) glm AND kimi independently named a CLEAN-SWEEP mutant: the MIRROR EDIT IN THE PUT ARM (make PUT write [0,R,G,B]).  It round-trips perfectly against the corrected GET, so every round-trip row passes while the on-the-wire byte order is inverted.  A clause-(d) concern for whatever detector shipped.  (3) deepseek challenged a MEASURED VALUE and the resolution is PATH-DEPENDENT, which is worth more than either claim: it derived that an RGB PUT->GET round-trip on 0xaabbccdd yields **0xbbcc0000**, not the 0xbbccdd00 the records carry.  BOTH ARE CORRECT FOR DIFFERENT PATHS, verified here against dev_sgi_re.c: a COPY reads its source as four REAL bytes from guest memory (buf = {aa,bb,cc,dd}) so GET-reads-buf[1..3] gives 0xbbccdd00; a PUT->GET round-trip writes an explicit PAD at buf[3]=0 (the PUT arm sets it) so the same GET gives 0xbbcc0000.  **The defect, if any, is a record that quotes one path's value while describing the other's** -- NOT verified here which way #505's brief put it, and deliberately NOT asserted.  Filed for checking.  (4) CORROBORATION OF TWO OPEN ROWS, from seats that had never been read: glm found 'no arm initializes buf[4..7] -- a bufdepth-8 PUT transmits four uninitialised bytes' (= `sgirebufuninit'), and kimi found 'all three 32-bit arms pack/unpack four bytes regardless of bufdepth, which can be 1, 2, 4 or 8' (= `sgirebufshiftub').  kimi also independently re-found the YCRCB default writing four bytes of host random() (= `sgirandomcolour', HIGH).",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:38990:     "note": "Gave the floor table this detector was built from: fill bot (dest[3] RGBA-vs-RGB), depth16 top (the adjacent DOCUMENTED cell), copy 2nd. NARROWED codex on sibling parity -- the comparison does not read dst_tlb, so a per-label row pins the helper's ARRAY SELECTION, \"cheap, not required\"; I adopted this and DISCLOSED the cell. NAMED THE MASKING TRAP now in the CHANGELOG and PRETABLE verbatim: F1 fills buf[0..3] at depth 32 and so hides the 32-bit tiled cell of [[sgirebufuninit]] -- keep that sibling alive on depth 16.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:39177:     "note": "7g-1 self-adjudication (brief_7g1_20260909.md), asked to argue against its own pass-1 position first.  VERDICT: B/C stay clause-(e) debt.  `switch (dst_tlb)' DOMINATES the edited line; the three LABELS do not each dominate it (a path through B reaches the edit without A), so the dominating predicate is GROUP MEMBERSHIP dst_tlb in {A,B,C}; the guarded body (two `|=') never TESTS the selector, so pass-10's EXACTLY-WHEN clause does not fire on the per-label split.  Its pass-1 REASON was incomplete (clause (2) only); its discriminator: labels are floor only when the body or the repair path RE-TESTS the selector after the join (TxICR).  CAUGHT TWO WRONG PREMISES IN MY BRIEF: `fill b top' NEVER SHIPPED (0 matches; 14 rows, 11 transfer rows all TLB_A -- five of the 72 input combinations), and `dispatch axes as full axes' is the measuring seat's competing bound carried as a residual, not adopted law.  It also noted, against its own verdict, that the shared switch block DOES read dst_tlb into dst_mode -- the fact the ruling turns on -- and set it aside because clause (1) forbids walking into the callee that consumes it.  OVERRULED on the verdict, ADOPTED on the premise corrections and on TxICR's distinction.  Read the handler, crmfbreg.h, the probe, dev_pcc2.c, PRETABLE.out, the CHANGELOG; ran nothing."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:39197:     "note": "Agent-tool MEASURING seat; liveness token and file quote returned.  Scratch clone /tmp/gxopus7g1 (cp -a of build/, dev_sgi_re.c cmp'd against SEC), sentinel dropped before every mutation, restored FROM SEC, part 1 cmp CLEAN, part 2 binary -nt source, rebuilt base md5 reproduced bit-for-bit, sentinel cleared; no project tree mutated (git status 0 modifications).  SCORES (killtable3_20260909/): BASE 14/14 (BASE.raw); M_tlbA `&& dst_tlb == MTE_TLB_A' 14/14 (M_tlbA.raw) -- diff against the base log empty but for the label; M_tlbAB `&& dst_tlb != MTE_TLB_C' 14/14 (M_tlbAB.raw).  PLUS A REACHABILITY CONTROL the floor keys on and no prior artifact held (tlb_reach_control.py, rung 3, guest-only, unmodified -E sgi -e ip32; reach_*.raw): BASE = A/B/C all RGBA; M_tlbA = A RGBA, B and C CI; M_tlbAB = A,B RGBA, C CI -- the control separates all three binaries, the detector separates none, and under M_tlbA dest[0] at B/C returns to 0x44, the CI PUT assigning buf[0] alone while memory_rw copies four: the defect #509 closed, LIVE in the exempted cells.  VERDICT: FLOOR, not (e); the fix is correct at all three labels and the detector is blind at two; closure costs two rows.  Also: PRETABLE.out predicted this escape before table 1 ran (the round is not accused of missing it -- what is new is the measurement and the measured reachability); the added comment `every tiled transfer took the CI arm' is a universal over three labels instantiated at one, and PRETABLE §1 disclosed B/C while §2 mapped the sentence to A-only rows without noting the quantifier gap.  Its report argues the DEBT side fairly (pass-10's `partition the body's own TESTS induce' wording supports it) and bounds the demand to 3 cells, not the 48-cell product.  Not attempted: whether NetBSD crmfb or the PROM ever programs B/C (bears on severity, not on the floor)."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:39718:     "note": "No verdict line.  (2,0) and (8,0) lane-0 cells unrowed on both arms; the PRETABLE's sibling-parity line was silent on LANES per width, which is how the hole passed.  Q3: the fix comment's split parenthetical; the dsession retry comment; the p_port10 disclosure ('every probed port is below 0x400' -- the split tails are at 0x400).",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:40305:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/lcaisalane/table7_20260916/PRETABLE.out (written FIRST -- its mtime precedes the driver, every build log and every probe log), check_pred.py (written BEFORE the run), kill530g.sh",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:40306:     "note": "TABLE 7 IS BUILT AND QUEUED, DELIBERATELY NOT LAUNCHED: #529's closing-pass-8 tables are running and both sides carry wall-clock oracles, so they are serialized. PRETABLE 7 discharges all four mandated checks with its OUTPUT rather than an assertion. SIBLING PARITY: pit0/pit1/pit2 are parity-matched at 37 rows; the two asymmetries found are DISPOSED OF with their reasons -- lane 3 has no pit session because port 0x43 advances no flip-flop (MEASURED, cdxZL3 150/150), and counter 0 takes the timer_add path where 1 and 2 emit the fatal() flood. CLAIM->ROW: six added consequence-claims each mapped to a row or to an explicit no-row sentence, including the fatal()-flood claim, which base 352/352 discharges by itself. GUARD FIELD vs BODY FIELD: N/A WITH ITS REASON RECORDED -- repair 6 edits the probe only and adds no predicate; an unrun check and an inapplicable one must not render the same. CHANNEL: every new row family names the mutant it kills and the observable that differs, and the family that could NOT -- a count row resting on a latch -- is exactly what repair 6 removed from the count role. 18 legs, names DERIVED by running each of the four frozen mutators' own list action and then RE-VERIFIED at run time by the driver itself (18/18 OK on a read-only dry run). The mutants span THREE source files (dev_lca.c 15, dev_pckbc.c 1 = nox, dev_8253.c 1 = noff) and the driver restores and clears all three together. THE /tmp BINARIES ARE NOT REUSED -- every leg is rebuilt from the frozen mutators, and each rebuilt edit is cross-checked hunk-for-hunk against the diff closing pass 5 preserved, because a re-derivation that silently differs would make the table a measurement of something else. Predictions are COMPONENT SUMS with the carried measurements separated from the fresh derivations and each carried component naming its log; the three components closing pass 5 never reached (W23 and Rdrop and Wdrop on the 8253 rows, and kbcmd against anything) are marked LOW CONFIDENCE in advance, so a miss there reports as a prediction error rather than as a finding. Expect 4-6 hours over two probe lanes.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:40317:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/lcaisalane/table7_20260916/kill530g.run-012437.sh (frozen copy); PRETABLE.out 22:18:40, check_pred.py 22:19:17, driver 22:20:17 -- the mandated order proven by mtime",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:40328:     "grader": "main-loop (Opus 5), against PRETABLE 7 whose mtime precedes the driver",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:40329:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/lcaisalane/table7_20260916/logs/probe_base.raw, regress/artifacts/lcaisalane/table7_20260916/logs/probe_RD2z.raw, regress/artifacts/lcaisalane/table7_20260916/logs/probe_RD2post.raw, regress/artifacts/lcaisalane/table7_20260916/logs/probe_cdxZL3.raw, all ending LCAISALANE_DONE; PRETABLE.out 22:18:40 against the driver at 22:20:17",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:40330:     "note": "*** THE FIRST FOUR LEGS OF KILL TABLE 7 MATCH THE PRE-WRITTEN PREDICTIONS EXACTLY, INCLUDING THE ONE EXACT-RED-SET PREDICTION. *** base 352/352 (predicted 352/352); RD2z 320/352 (predicted 320); RD2post 286/352 (predicted 286); cdxZL3 351/352 (predicted 351). *** AND cdxZL3 IS THE SHARP ONE: PRETABLE 7 predicted its red set as EXACTLY {stub3 z1}, one row, and the measured failing row is exactly that -- 'stub3 z1  0x10  0x0  (1,3) STORE ZERO at SP(0x63)'. *** That is three things at once, and none of them was assumed: (1) the predictions were written BEFORE the run with the order mtime-proven, so this is predict-then-measure rather than a number fitted to an answer; (2) repair 6's 'stub3 z1' row IS the sole carrier for the lane-3 x zero-data joint, exactly as it was designed to be -- cdxZL3 scored 185/185 and 150/150 against BOTH prototype instruments before repair 6 existed, and now dies on the one row added for it; (3) the CARRIED components transferred correctly from the two separate closing-pass-5 instruments onto the merged 352-row one, which was the assumption the whole component-sum prediction method rested on. A COUNTING NOTE, because it nearly produced a wrong reading: a bare 'grep -c FAIL' over these logs returns 33 and 67 rather than 32 and 66, because it also matches the summary token LCAISALANE_WITNESS=FAIL. The row counts are 32 and 66 and they match; the extra one is not a row. An unanchored grep over probe output matching a word in a non-row line is a defect class this project already records, and it appeared here within a minute of the first result.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:40440:     "note": "*** AN L4 WRONG RECORD IN REPAIR 6'S OWN ADDED COMMENT, FOUND BY THE OPUS SEAT AND CORRECTED IN PLACE. ***  The shipped kbcmd <w> post note stated the phantom-read relationship EXACTLY BACKWARDS.  Verified against probe_Wpure.raw before editing: 'a PHANTOM read pops early and drops pre to 0' is FALSE -- all four kbcmd <w> pre read 0x1 against want 0x1, green; and 'kbcq <w> dib cannot see either' is FALSE -- all four kbcq <w> dib go 0x1 -> 0x0, red, and kbcq is the ONLY 8042 family that reds under Wpure.  The roles are INVERTED: kbcq is on 0x60 (DATA, a read POPS) and sees the phantom read; kbcmd is on 0x64 (STATUS, a read is INERT) and cannot.  Under a DUPLICATE the sentence is right and kbcq is correctly blind.  *** AND PRETABLE's OWN CHANNEL BLOCK GOT IT RIGHT -- it claims only 'Kills the write arm's DUPLICATE at the direct widths', with no phantom-read claim.  The pre-table check produced the correct narrow statement and the SHIPPED material carried the broader false one.  That is the #490 prescribed-row amendment's shape in a new place. ***  GRADED L4, not a floor breach: kbcmd post is non-vacuous (a measured kill on WWall at all four widths) and the phantom-read shape IS covered, measured, by kbcq dib.  Only the prose overclaimed.  Corrected in place in 10 lines, quoting what it refutes.  AND THE kbcmd PREDICTION MISSES ARE NOW PROVABLE RATHER THAN ASSUMABLE: the command goes THROUGH the lca window but all three observations are FLAT reads that never enter the handler, so kbcmd is a WRITE-ARM-ONLY oracle BY CONSTRUCTION -- predicting 4 and 8 reds for the read-arm mutants RWall and RXW was arithmetically impossible from the probe source, not merely optimistic.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:40569:     "note": "[NOT A PANEL STAGE: NO PANEL CONVENED]  *** THE OWED PASS IS DONE AND #530 SHIPS WITH IT -- CLOSURE, WITH THE SHA, FOLLOWS IN THE NEXT RECORDS COMMIT. ***  CLOSE6_VERDICT.md pre-committed, before any leg ran: *escapes AND len > 8 is guest-reachable -> L2, add a len > 8 row in-round.*  Both halves established: `KILLLEN8` and `&& len <= 8` each sweep this probe's 352 rows (352/352), and `store_buf` (memory.c:837-857) via PROM GETENV (cpu_alpha_palcode.c:141-144) delivers len 16/64/256/1024 with a guest-controlled destination and length.  The row is `regress/lca_widewrite_probe.py`: base 3/3, both mutants 2/3, every value predicted in PRETABLE.out at 14:59:49 before any run, and the SHIPPED file re-run as shipped with the same scores.  Shipped as its own file so this probe's 352 measured rows do not move; its header now says where the width cell is covered.  **With this commit `dev_lca.c` carries NO live host kill** -- measured with a comment-stripping parser: HEAD has exactly one, the `exit(1)` this fix removes.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:40570:     "ev": "regress/lca_widewrite_probe.py; regress/artifacts/lcalenzerodrop/widewrite_20260918/{PRETABLE.out,logs/TABLE.out,logs/certify_shipped/TABLE.out,logs/run1_L1_no_return_address/WHY.txt}",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:41118:   "state_note": "[2026-09-21 16:2x] #541 IN WORK (e8a2bc).  KILL TABLE 5 (killtable5_20260921/, 34 legs, no rebuilds) CLOSED the three direction-keyed escapes pass 4 left open -- M_loadannounce 28/30, M_storedecl 29/30, M_storeredirect8 29/30.  *** PASS 5 SAYS THE CHAIN DOES NOT CLOSE, AND IT IS RIGHT. ***  The kimi CLI seat BUILT and MEASURED a clean-sweep mutant: `if (flags & FLAG_WRITEFLAG) return 0;' INSIDE the latch block drops only the FIRST store per cpu and swept v5 30/30, because session 1's store is not its first access and sessions 3/4 checked only the announcement and survival -- never WHERE the access went.  agy reached the same gap by reading.  L2 repair in-round: v6 gives sessions 3 and 4 the EFFECT anchor session 1 had (32 rows); KILL TABLE 6 is RUNNING with the mutant measured BOTH ways -- it must escape the frozen v5 and die on v6, both predicted before either ran.  The codex seat REFUSED this brief and was RE-ROUTED in plain engineering words: it answered (rc 0, zero refusal lines) and found that my repair is INCOMPLETE -- `fe past ram4' measures the DISASSEMBLY read rather than the execution read, and the RELOAD is a second unanchored access (a +8 mapping shift for loads once the latch is set keeps every row green; predicted, not measured).  Pass 6 material.  Two records defects of mine corrected: PRETABLE5 said a declined store ends a session (measured FALSE -- all four alive rows stay green) and `fe past ram' is a transcript-wide presence check, not a per-step count.  In five passes NO seat has challenged the fix's C.  [16:5x] CLASS CLOSED AND MEASURED: v7 (34 rows) anchors EVERY VMKILL-family access the harness makes -- the +8 load and the RELOAD were the two unanchored ones.  The reload cell was MEASURED escaping v6 first (M_reloadshift8, 32/32, exactly as the re-routed codex job predicted from source), so the repair rests on a measurement.  KILL TABLE 7: 36 legs, no builds, base 34/34, M_reloadshift8 KILLED 33/34 on `rl past ram', M_pagedecl KILLED 32/34 on `anchor +8' AND `+8 past ram' -- neither new row is vacuous.  TWO legs differed, both with an EXTRA red: a STORE redirect moves the RELOAD too, because the store installs the translation the following load is served from -- the mechanism was already in table 4's record and not in my rule.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:41914:     "note": "THE STRONGEST ANSWER OF THE DAY, and it was asked to be harder on this work than on a stranger's because a DIFFERENT Opus instance had built the instrument.  verbatim: 'SHIP THE ONE-LINE FIX. DO NOT SHIP THE COMMENT OR THE ROUND RECORD AS WRITTEN -- three wrong records, one of them in product code.'  IT VERIFIED RATHER THAN TRUSTED: all 17 scores re-read from the raw logs instead of RESULTS.txt, all 17 sentinel-checked by tail, and the byte-identical-to-BASE property for M_D and M_E reproduced by diff and md5 -- which it notes is STRONGER evidence than a score match.  THE THREE WRONG RECORDS: (1) *** L4 IN PRODUCT CODE ***, the comment cited regress/alpha_tbi_probe.py for '5/7 ... 7/7' and that file had GROWN from 7 rows to 13, so the named artifact now scores 10/13 and CONTRADICTS the number beside it -- the reused-log-name decay (#462's shape) inside a source comment; re-pointed at the frozen witness and its preserved logs.  (2) The brief's 'neither [held round] touches Alpha' is FALSE -- dev_lca.c IS the Alpha PCI bus and machine_alpha.c's ST_ALPHABOOK1 arm does device_add(machine, 'lca'), and the probe uses alphabook1, so ALL 17 legs were built over #518's uncommitted file; the DIFFERENTIAL stands (every leg shares it, the mutator edits one text in one other file) but 'on the committed build' does not.  (3) RESULTS.txt claims 'EVERY ROW HAS A MEASURED KILL' in capitals and names a counterexample four lines later: the true figure is EIGHT of thirteen, with four declared CONTROL rows and the pre-existing positive control unkilled.  IT ALSO: confirmed the ASM direction against the manual's own sections rather than against the seat that corrected it; confirmed vaddr_mask defaults to identity BY CENSUS and then showed the round had MISSTATED THE CONSEQUENCE and had ALREADY MEASURED THE PREMISE (a degenerate mask would turn four rows RED, not vacuous, so BASE = 13/13 is itself impossible under one -- 'the measurement subsumes the premise; say that instead of reasoning about it'); found that `bnd lo' silently PINS THE ITB/DTB CONFLATION, so implementing the split would red it on a CORRECT implementation; named a better authority for the selector set that was already in the tree and uncited (alpha_cpu.h's ALPHA_TBIA/TBIAP/TBISI/TBISD/TBIS definitions -- 'a header table you are simultaneously editing is not an authority over the edit'); censused that `(once)' appears 40 times under src/devices and ZERO under src/cpus, so the round's appeal to 'the house latched-announce idiom' was to the wrong layer (conclusion strengthened, appeal corrected); and named the in-file #514 precedent as the transferred warrant the option-set rule actually wanted.  ON COVERAGE it reached deepseek's axis independently and went further: the unrowed axis is OPERAND MAGNITUDE, a narrowed cast is predicted 13/13 byte-identical to BASE, and it named two more edits on the same axis (`<= 3' -> `!= 4'; `&& a1 < 0x8000').  Its disposition -- clause-(e) debt IF the contract genuinely discloses it, and it does not, because the disclosure's warrant is the same cell-collapse reasoning the round's own PRETABLE demolished for a0 = 0 -- is ADOPTED.  It also established the closure costs ONE row and NO new instruction encoders (bound_session already loads a0 from memory, so one bsetup slot plus one LDQ variant does it), correcting its own first answer that an SLL encoder would be needed.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:41947:    "note": "FIXED AND CLOSED 2026-09-10 as #520. PAL_OSF1_tbi took its selector in a uint64_t a0, so `if (a0 >= 1)` was an UNSIGNED compare and both documented negative selectors (-1 = invalidate everything with ASM=0, sparing ASM=1; -2 = invalidate everything) compared GREATER than 1 and took the single-VADDR branch -- a guest TBIA/TBIAP flushed ONE page and left every other cached translation live, the direction that hands a guest stale translations silently. THE FIX IS ONE EXECUTABLE LINE: a two-sided window whose else-arm is the conservative INVALIDATE_ALL. THE ROUND'S SUBSTANCE IS THE CLASS CLOSURE: three consecutive passes had each bought ONE axis of the same guard (the window's four sides, then a1, then operand magnitude), which is the class-closure amendment's trigger, so the detector enumerates all NINE axes in its own header and closes them in ONE diff -- 15 rows, 11 legs, all twelve logs ending ALPHATBI_DONE, BASE 15/15 and null medium 11/15. Both of PRETABLE.out's predicted clean sweeps (M_D `>=0`, M_E `&& a1 != 0`) are dead at 14/15, and the magnitude row kills two mutants no other row could see -- the (int32_t) narrowing and `<=3` -> `!=4` -- both invisible at all seven selector values the other rows drive, smallest discriminator a0 = 0x100000001. THREE L4 CORRECTIONS, each settled by enumeration rather than by vote: the signed casts are BEHAVIOURALLY REDUNDANT (300,037 values, zero disagreements, because a negative selector fails the UPPER bound unsigned exactly where it fails the LOWER one signed) so the comment no longer calls them the repair; the witness citation is re-pointed at the FROZEN 7-row artifact because the live probe grew to 15 rows and came to CONTRADICT the number beside it; and the ASM/ASN spared set was stated backwards. CLAUSE (e): no row can separate -1 from -2 -- this model's translation cache has no ASM concept and such a row would be vacuous by construction. SEATS: fable5 WALLED on a per-model 429 with NO substitute transport, re-fired once and still walled; agy DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED (measurement reserve); kimi CLI walled and COVERED by kimi-k3:cloud. CLEARANCE run not asserted: all four trees part1=CLEAN, the three with a binary part2=FRESH, est/ part 2 N/A as a source tree, verified by GREPPING THE PREDICATE rather than by reading timestamps."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:42719:     "outcome": "VERDICT: RATIFY THE FIX, DO NOT SHIP THE DETECTOR.  *** THE ROUND'S HIGHEST-VALUE FINDING: M4_condwrite in the MIRROR POLARITY (`&& writeflag == MEM_WRITE') SWEEPS THE ELEVEN-ROW PROBE CLEAN, and my own PRETABLE CHANNEL line claimed `t1 alias' killed it. ***  Root cause is the ADDRESS not the polarity: every alias row used dev 0, an EMPTY slot, where forwarding returns the same all-ones the refusal synthesises -- blind by construction.  Verified all six premises against the fork tree and CORRECTED P4: the 0x00000003 high dword is not from PCIINIT(i82378zb) (which never writes offset 4) but from bus_pci_add's default IO|MEM enable.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:42829:     "outcome": "ASK-AGAIN, on the ANALYSIS-shaped re-fire only (the edit-shaped form was content-filtered, as predicted): the `reg != 0` branch of the refusal expression is wholly untested, and it ranked this above len and func because it is a BRANCH rather than an unvaried value.  Rows `t1 reg0` / `t1 reg4` added.  This round had already disclosed the gap in its own PRETABLE and deferred it.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:42861:     "note": "ADJUDICATED BY THE MAIN LOOP RUNNING AS FABLE 5.1 (owner directive 2026-09-09: \"proceed with fable for any adjudication\").  This is the adjudicator hat, not an independent seat review; it is recorded as fable5 because that is the seat the hold names.  [A] RULING: NEITHER (a) NOR (b) -- the round found (c), and shipped it as #515 (b4d4177).  The premise 'no row can separate IMPLEMENT from an all-ones DECLINE' was true of the VALUE channel only.  The DIAGNOSTIC channel discriminates: bus_pci_data_access prints the decoded tuple ('bus 1 func 2 device 3 ]', terminated so it cannot match device 30/31) on a FORWARDED access to an empty slot and prints nothing on a REFUSAL.  `t1 anchor' keys on that tuple and killed M5_typeswap/M6_busshift/M7_devshift/M8_busmask; `t1 alias' keys on the ABSENCE of 'bus 0 func 0 device 0 ]' and killed M3_aliasoff/M4_condwrite.  18/18, fourteen mutants, zero escapes, PRETABLE.out written before table 1.  So the synthetic bus-1 device in (b) was never needed -- which is the addition-licence answer arriving from the CHANNEL check: asking 'which observable differs?' found a channel that already existed.  Design hold DISCHARGED.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:42864:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/lcaconfbus1kill/pass2_20260908/PRETABLE.out"
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:42877:   "state_note": "[2026-09-19] SHIPPED as #515 (b4d4177, 2026-09-08) -- the fix commit was missing from this row's commits (only the witness 41923ac was listed), so the dashboard showed it open.  Awaiting its regress/closure stage (Fable-only by standing directive).  FILED 2026-09-07.  Surfaced while verifying a claim in the grok seat's `lcabadval` pass-1 BODY (its\nverdict line did not carry it) -- and **verified to be sharper than the seat stated it**, by opening\nboth functions.\n\n**THE DEFECT: an ACCEPTED write to one register arms a host kill in a DIFFERENT function.**\n\n`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc)`, `case LCA_IOC_CONF`:\n\n    d->ioc_conf = idata;\n    /*  Only bit 0 is implemented so far, the PCI bus 0 vs\n        bus non-0 selection bit.  */\n    if (idata & ~1) {\n            fatal(\"TODO: Write to unimplemented bit of IOC_CONF: 0x%\" PRIx64 \"\\n\", ...);\n            exit(1);\n    }\n\n**`0x1` PASSES that guard.**  `idata & ~1` is 0, so the write is accepted, stored, and the arm's own\ncomment describes bit 0 as the implemented selector.\n\n`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)` then consumes it:\n\n    bus = d->ioc_conf & 1;\n    if (bus == 0) {\n            for (dev=0; dev<21; dev++)\n                    if (tag & (0x800 << dev))\n                            break;\n            if (dev >= 21) {\n                    /*  fatal(\"[ LCA: No bus 0 device? TODO ]\\n\");\n                    exit(1);  */\n                    dev = 0;\n            }\n    } else {\n            fatal(\"TODO. Non-zero bus.\\n\");\n            exit(1);\n    }\n\nSo: **guest writes 1 to IOC_CONF (accepted), then performs any PCI configuration-space access, and\nthe host process exits.**  Two guest accesses, both to registers the model implements, neither of\nthem rejected.\n\n*** WHY IT IS ITS OWN ROW AND NOT PART OF [[lcabadval]]. ***  Every kill `lcabadval` covers is a\nguard REFUSING a value it does not recognise.  **This one fires on a value the guard ACCEPTS**, in a\ndifferent function, on a later access.  The acceptance and the kill are separated in both code and\ntime, which is why a per-arm audit of `lca_ioc` walks straight past it -- as mine did, twice.\n\n*** AND THE SEAT'S FRAMING NEEDED CORRECTING, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH RECORDING. ***  grok wrote\nthat this exit becomes *\"newly reachable from a CONF extra-bit write that also sets bit 0; today\nthat write dies in the CONF guard first.\"*  That is true of an EXTRA-BIT write (e.g. `0x3`), and it\nmade the kill sound like a CONSEQUENCE of adopting option A.  **It is not: `0x1` alone reaches it\ntoday, with no fix applied.**  A widens which CONF values arrive here; it does not open the door.\nThe door is open now.  *Checked by reading both arms rather than by accepting the seat's sentence --\nwhich is this project's own rule, and the seat was right about the site while being wrong about the\ntiming.*\n\n**THE IRONY, and it is a design signal rather than a joke:** the `bus == 0` branch is the file's own\ngraceful-degradation PRECEDENT -- a commented-out `fatal()`/`exit(1)` with `dev = 0;` substituted --\nand [[lcabadval]] cites that very loop as a transferable in-tree warrant.  **The `else` five lines\nbelow it is a bare kill.**  Same function, opposite dispositions, no comment explaining why.\n\n**REACHABILITY, stated honestly rather than assumed.**  Bit 0 is a bus SELECTOR, so a guest reaches\nthis by selecting a bus the model does not implement and then doing config access -- plausible for\nfirmware or an OS that probes beyond bus 0, and trivially reachable by a probe.  **Whether any\nshipped guest on `alphabook1` actually does it is NOT established here**, and the row does not claim\nit.  What is established is that no guard stands between an accepted write and the kill.\n\n**WITNESS: a rung-3 probe is available and most of it already exists.**  `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`\nis `ST_ALPHABOOK1` ONLY (`machine_alpha.c`), and `regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py` ALREADY drives a\nguest `stq`/`ldq` at `LCA_IOC_CONF` (`0xfffffc0180000020`, K0SEG | physical) on unmodified\n`alphabook1`.  This row needs one more session: `stq 0x1` to CONF, then a PCI config-space access.\n**Three traps that probe already paid for:** debugger `dump` skips `DM_DEFAULT` handlers; a debugger\n`put d` of a killing value IS a host kill but is NOT a guest instruction, so it does not witness; and\nHAE at window offset 0 silently succeeds, so a miss control must not use `LCA_IOC_BASE+0`.\n\n**DISPOSITION, not yet adjudicated:** it is the same de-fatalisation family, but the option set\ndiffers because the kill is not a value refusal -- the honest members here are DECLINE THE CONFIG\nACCESS (return without dispatching, with a latched announce) or IMPLEMENT bus != 0.  **Note the\narchitecturally correct error path is blocked for the same reason grok gave on the sibling row:\n`LCA_IOC_STAT0`/`STAT1` have no case at all and hit the `default:` arm's `exit(1)`, so LCA cannot\nreport a device error through the register the header designates for it.**\n\nsev HIGH: a guest-drivable host kill, reached by two ordinary accesses with no rejected value\nanywhere on the path.\n\n*** SIBLING CHECK DISCHARGED WITH A DENOMINATOR, 2026-09-07 -- the class has EXACTLY ONE member in\nthis file. ***  The rule is grep for the siblings before calling a finding whole; here the question\nis which lca_ioc fields have a consumer at all, since a field nothing reads cannot arm a delayed\nkill.  Counting references that are neither the readback (`odata = d->X`) nor the store (`d->X = `):\n\n    ioc_conf          1 consumer   <- `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` in lca_pci_conf.  THIS ROW.\n    tlb_enable        0\n    window_base_0     0            window_base_1     0\n    window_mask_0     0            window_mask_1     0\n    window_t_base_0   0            window_t_base_1   0\n\n**So the accepted-write-arms-a-later-kill shape cannot recur elsewhere in dev_lca.c**: the other\nseven fields are write-and-read-back with nothing downstream.  That is also why [[lcabadval]]s five\npure-storage arms are a different problem from this one, and why a fix here does not generalise to\nthem.\n\n*Not claimed beyond this file.  The general class -- an ACCEPTED store that a later access consumes\ninto a terminating path -- is a plausible cross-device census and is NOT run here.*\n\n*** THE WITNESS-DESIGN CHECK WAS REFUSED BY codex ON A CONTENT FILTER, 2026-09-07 -- recorded as a\nSEAT FAILURE, never as agreement. ***  Zero-byte `.txt`, exit 0, the error only in the `.err`:\n*\"This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk.\"*  Full analysis and the two same-day\nnegative controls are on [[codexsubjectrefusal]].  **Re-fired on grok**, which reads the tree, takes\nits brief from a file and has no such filter -- so the question is covered, not lost.  The brief\nitself is preserved at `regress/artifacts/lcaconfbus1kill/WITNESS_brief.md` so the trigger can be\nstudied rather than guessed at.\n\n*** THE WITNESS DESIGN IS SOUND -- grok, 2026-09-07, ON THE BRIEF codex REFUSED.  The\nalternate-transport rule paid off completely: the recovery is BETTER than the refused seat would\nhave given, not a degraded substitute. ***\n\n**DENOMINATOR, and it is the largest this row has seen:** all of `dev_lca.c` (390 lines -- both\nhandlers, `DEVINIT`, ISA, interrupt stubs); `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)` and\n`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc)` end to end; `bus_pci_data_access`/`bus_pci_setaddr` in `bus_pci.c`;\n`alpha_translate_v2p` K0SEG in `memory_alpha.c`; the device and unmapped paths in `memory_rw.c`;\n`LS_GENERIC_N`/`LS_N` in `cpu_alpha_instr_loadstore.c`; `fatal()` in `debugmsg.c`; `ST_ALPHABOOK1`\nin `machine_alpha.c`; and all of `regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py`.\n\n**CONFIRMED:** S2 reaches `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` for ANY access in the window -- no `switch` on\n`relative_addr`, no `len` test, no `writeflag` test that can skip it, no early `return`; the only\n`writeflag` use before it is filling `idata`, which is not a guard.  `len` is first used AFTER the\nkill.  And `ioc_conf` has **exactly two readers**, so nothing else can be armed by S1.\n\n*** FOUR CORRECTIONS THAT CHANGE THE CONSTRUCTION: ***\n\n1. **MY LIVENESS CONTROL IS MISLABELLED.**  `ioc rt` proves the WRITER (`lca_ioc`'s CONF arm)\n   round-trips `0x1`.  **It says nothing about S2's window.**  PCI_CONF liveness is the armed\n   session's unique message (the handler ran) PLUS the control surviving (the same access is not an\n   unconditional kill).  A positive signature is available if wanted: an empty-slot read returns\n   `0xffffffffffffffff`, where unmapped returns 0.\n2. **S2 MUST BE A READ, NOT A WRITE.**  Both reach the kill, but an empty-slot WRITE makes\n   `bus_pci_data_access` print `[ ... write to non-existant device ... ]` -- so the CONTROL would\n   carry a second `fatal()` on stderr.  An empty-slot READ of reg 0 is the architectural ID probe\n   and is silent.\n3. **A TIGHTER VARIANT: ONE PROCESS, not two sessions.**  control `ldq` (survives), then S1, then\n   the armed `ldq` (dies) -- **only `ioc_conf` changes between the two loads.**  Separate sessions\n   are still adequate, but this removes the residual that two processes could differ in something\n   that ran before the debugger prompt.\n4. **KEEP IT ON `step`.**  `continue` would make the `lca_interrupt_assert`/`_deassert` stubs a\n   confounder -- and `cpu_dyntrans.c` skips interrupt delivery while single-stepping anyway.\n\n*** THE CONTRACT LIMIT, AND IT IS THE MOST IMPORTANT LINE FOR THE FIX ROUND: THIS IS A WITNESS OF\nTHE `exit(1)`, NOT A DETECTOR OF THE REPAIR. ***  grok: *\"'Host survives, session completes' greens\nfor a silent `return 1` that skips `bus_pci_*` and `memory_writemax64`, for a latched announce, AND\nfor falling through into existing `bus_pci` with `cur_bus=1` (empty type-1 also survives).\"*  So the\npost-fix oracle cannot discriminate between fix members, and a round that ships one must build a row\nthat can.  **Post-fix caution in the same breath: if someone later implements type-1 by falling\nthrough to `bus_pci_setaddr(bus=1, ...)`, empty bus 1 is a non-killing miss today, so the witness\ngoes green -- do NOT read that green as \"type-1 is modelled.\"**\n\n**WHY OFFSET 0 IS THE RIGHT CONTROL ADDRESS, which I had asserted without warrant:** it is not\nHAE-like (HAE is an exact-address `break` in `lca_ioc`; `lca_pci_conf` has no per-offset special\ncase).  On the ARMED session the offset is irrelevant -- `bus == 1` exits before `tag`/`dev` are\nused.  On the SURVIVING control, `tag == 0` matches no IDSEL bit, so the `dev >= 21` body runs --\n**and that body is already the commented-out kill with `dev = 0;`** -- then an empty-slot read\nreturns `(uint64_t)-1` without exiting.  `0x18` (the NetBSD `| 0x3 << 3` encoding) yields the same\n`tag`/`reg` and is equivalent.  **Do NOT use `relative_addr = 0x800000`**, which talks to the\nregistered `i82378zb` at device 7.\n\n*** AND grok FOUND THE DESIGN ARGUMENT SITTING FIVE LINES ABOVE THE DEFECT: the `dev >= 21` arm is\nALREADY DE-FATALISED, in the same function, and the surviving control USES that path. ***  *\"If that\n`exit(1)` were still live, the control would die and you would misread PCI_CONF as unconditionally\nfatal.  The file already contains the decline precedent the `else` lacks.\"*  That is the strongest\navailable warrant for declining here, and it is one function away from the kill.\n\nAlso confirmed: FAULT (member 5) is unavailable -- `memory_rw.c` delivers a failed device access as\nan exception only under `MEM_MIPS`/`MEM_M88K`, and this path always `return 1` on the live path, so\nthe kill is `exit(1)` and not `return 0`.  And `lca_isa`'s `ofs > len` kill is a DIFFERENT window\n(`LCA_PCI_SIO`), not reachable from `0x1e0000000`.\n\n*** CROSS-REFERENCE, AND A CORRECTION TO THIS ROW'S OWN NOVELTY CLAIM.  [[lcabadval]] ALREADY\nENUMERATED THIS KILL ON 2026-09-06. ***\n\nIts eleven-kill census for dev_lca.c lists **line 134, class BUS, condition `bus != 0`, i.e.\n`d->ioc_conf & 1`** -- so the site AND its dependence on ioc_conf were both already recorded, and I\nfiled this row saying only lcabadval mentioned lca_pci_conf without reading how.  **Sixth\nrediscovery this session; see [[refilingclass]], which predicts exactly this.**\n\n**WHAT THIS ROW GENUINELY ADDS, stated narrowly:** the census records the MECHANISM; it does not\nrecord that **`0x1` PASSES the CONF guard**.  That is what makes the kill reachable with no rejected\nvalue anywhere on the path -- an accepted write in one handler arming a terminating branch in\nanother -- and it is why the disposition differs from lcabadval's six value guards, whose kills all\nfire on refusal.  Plus the witness design, now reviewed SOUND by grok.\n\n**KEPT AS A SEPARATE ROW** because it has its own disposition (decline the config access, or\nimplement bus != 0) and its own witness, and because folding it into a row named for VALUE GUARDS\nwould hide it -- which is the census's own closing lesson: *a row named after one class hides the\nmembers of the others.*\n\n*** AND THE CENSUS NAMES SOMETHING PLAUSIBLY WORSE THAN THIS ROW, WHICH I SHOULD SAY OUT LOUD RATHER\nTHAN LEAVE IN A TABLE: the two INTERRUPT STUBS at :73 and :85. ***  `lca_interrupt_assert` and\n`lca_interrupt_deassert` are bare `fatal(\"...TODO\"); exit(1)` with **NO GUARD AT ALL**, so *any*\ndevice asserting an interrupt through the LCA ends the process regardless of what it writes.  In the\ncensus's words: the value-guard registers *\"need a firmware revision that writes something else;\nthese two need only that an interrupt is asserted.\"*  That is member 7 (IMPLEMENT) of the option\nset -- a whole path stubbed out, with no value being refused -- and it is a stronger candidate for\nthe next critical round than this one.\n\n*** THE RUNG-3 WITNESS EXISTS AND THE DEFECT IS MEASURED, 2026-09-07.  regress/lca_confbus_probe.py,\nartifact regress/artifacts/lcaconfbus1kill/witness_HEAD_20260907.raw, sentinel LCACONFBUS_DONE. ***\n\n    bus ctl      yes                    yes                    CONTROL: identical read at ioc_conf==0 survived   ok\n    bus ctlsig   0xffffffffffffffff     0xffffffffffffffff     DEVICE SIGNATURE: empty-slot ID probe             ok\n    bus kill     dead                   alive                  <- THE DEFECT                                     FAIL\n    bus attrib   yes                    yes                    death carried THIS arm@s diagnostic               ok\n      EVIDENCE: TODO. Non-zero bus.\n    LCACONFBUS_RESULT=3/4\n\n**One emulator process, unmodified `alphabook1`, real guest instructions through real address\ndecode.**  The sequence is: guest `ldq` from `LCA_PCI_CONF+0` with `ioc_conf == 0` (SURVIVES), then\nguest `stq 0x1` to `LCA_IOC_CONF` (ACCEPTED -- `idata & ~1` is 0), then the IDENTICAL `ldq` again\n(HOST DIES).  Only `ioc_conf` differs between the two loads, which is what attributes the death to\nthe VALUE rather than to the address, the width, or touching the window at all.\n\n*** grok@s PREDICTED DEVICE SIGNATURE CAME BACK EXACTLY. ***  It reasoned from `bus_pci.c` that an\nempty-slot read returns `0xffffffffffffffff` via `memory_writemax64` while UNMAPPED returns 0.  The\ncontrol row reads **0xffffffffffffffff**.  That is a prediction from source, confirmed by\nmeasurement -- so the signature control is established, not assumed.\n\n**ORACLE POLARITY is the ladder@s: the rows assert the CORRECT (post-fix) behaviour, so `bus kill`\nis RED on the committed build and goes GREEN when the defect is fixed, with the SAME script.**\nClause (i) is therefore HALF discharged -- the pre-fix symptom is shown; the post-fix green awaits a\nfix, which is correct for test-first.\n\n*** TWO APPARATUS DEFECTS CAUGHT IN THE FIRST RUN AND FIXED, both worth keeping. ***\n1. **The first artifact carried NO EVIDENCE for `bus attrib`.**  The row tested the PTY buffer and\n   printed nothing, so the preserved log showed a green row whose diagnostic a later reader could\n   not check.  The probe now ECHOES the matched line into its own output.  *A green row whose\n   evidence is not in the artifact is not verifiable, however sound the logic.*\n2. **I nearly filed a false correction to grok.**  It said the `TODO. Non-zero bus.` literal is\n   unique in `src/`; my grep returned TWO.  The second hit is `dev_lca.o`, the compiled object.  One\n   source site, `dev_lca.c:133`.  grok was right; my apparatus swept binaries.\n\n*** THE FIX DESIGN IS CLEARER THAN THIS ROW ASSUMED, AND IT DIVERGES FROM ITS SIBLING FOR A REASON\nWORTH STATING.  Derived 2026-09-07 from the codex fix design on [[lcairqstubkill]] plus this row's\nown witness. ***\n\n**THE TWO LCA ROWS LOOK LIKE ONE PROBLEM AND ARE NOT.**  Both are bare `fatal()+exit(1)` reached by\nordinary guest access, so it is tempting to give them one disposition.  The difference is what the\nguest is ASKING FOR:\n\n* [[lcairqstubkill]] -- an interrupt DELIVERY.  There is no correct answer that is not delivery: a\n  decline LOSES AN EVENT, silently or otherwise, and the guest can never learn of it.  codex's\n  disposition there is \"diagnose and stop the guest CPU\", because continuing is a lie.\n* **THIS ROW -- a PCI configuration cycle on bus 1.  There IS a correct answer, and hardware gives\n  it: a config read of an absent device returns ALL ONES.**  An unimplemented BUS is legitimately\n  EMPTY.  Declining is not a lost event; it is the architecturally right reply.\n\n> **So the dispositions properly differ, and the reason is not \"one is worse\".  For a DELIVERY path\n> an unimplemented target has no honest continuation; for an ADDRESSING path it has one, and the\n> tree already implements it.**\n\n*** AND THE TREE ALREADY DOES THE RIGHT THING ONE STEP DOWN, which the witness measured by accident.\n***  The `bus == 0` branch already reaches `bus_pci_setaddr` / `bus_pci_data_access`, and this row's\nown witness recorded the result: **a read of an empty slot returns `0xffffffffffffffff`** (the\n`bus ctlsig` row, MEASURED).  grok noted the same about the armed side in its design review --\n*\"falling through into existing bus_pci with cur_bus=1 (empty type-1 also survives)\"*.\n\n**SO THE LIKELY FIX IS NOT A DECLINE AT ALL BUT A FALL-THROUGH:** stop special-casing `bus != 0`,\nlet the existing `bus_pci` path handle it, and an empty bus 1 answers all-ones by itself.  That is\noption-set member 7 (IMPLEMENT) reached cheaply, because the machinery exists -- unlike the sibling\nrow, where member 7 is blocked at the dyntrans core.\n\n*** THE DISCRIMINATING ROW IS THEREFORE THE RETURNED VALUE, NOT THE RUNNING STATE. ***  codex's\noracle for the sibling row (\"stopped guest state, plus the reason\") is right THERE and wrong HERE,\nbecause a correct fix must leave the guest RUNNING.  The observable that separates the candidates\nhere is what the armed read returns:\n\n    fall through to bus_pci (empty bus 1)      0xffffffffffffffff    <- architecturally correct\n    silent return without servicing            0x0 or unchanged      <- looks like UNMAPPED\n    latched announce then return unserviced    0x0 or unchanged      <- same, plus a message\n    the existing exit(1)                       host gone\n\n**And this probe ALREADY HAS THAT ROW SHAPE**: `bus ctlsig` asserts exactly `0xffffffffffffffff` on\nthe bus-0 control, and distinguishes it from the `0x0` an unmapped read gives.  The fix round adds\nthe mirror of it on the ARMED side.  *No new capability is needed, which is not true of the sibling.*\n\n**NOT YET VERIFIED, and it is the one thing to check before building:** that `bus_pci` genuinely\ntolerates `cur_bus == 1` rather than asserting or indexing something sized for one bus.  grok's\nreview says empty type-1 survives; **that is a seat's reading and this row has not measured it.**\nThe cheap check is to build the fall-through and run the existing probe -- the armed row should turn\nfrom `dead` to `0xffffffffffffffff`.\n\n*** THE UNVERIFIED LINK IS CLOSED BY READING: `bus_pci' IS BUS-AGNOSTIC BY CONSTRUCTION, SO THE\nFALL-THROUGH FIX IS SOUND WITHOUT A NEW CAPABILITY. ***\n\nThe paragraph above flagged one thing to check before building -- whether `bus_pci' tolerates\n`cur_bus == 1' rather than asserting or indexing something sized for a single bus.  It does:\n\n    bus_pci.h:84   int  cur_bus, cur_device, cur_func, cur_reg;   <- a plain int, not bus-0-only\n    bus_pci.h:87   struct pci_device *first_device;               <- a LINKED LIST, not an array\n                                                                     indexed by bus number\n    bus_pci.c:102  if (dev->bus == pci_data->cur_bus && ...)      <- devices carry their OWN bus\n                                                                     field and are MATCHED against it\n    bus_pci.c:200  pci_data->cur_bus = bus;                       <- set from the caller, unchecked\n\n**So a lookup with `cur_bus = 1` walks the list, matches nothing (no device carries `bus == 1`), and\nfalls into the no-device path -- which this row's own witness has already MEASURED returning\n`0xffffffffffffffff` on bus 0.**  Nothing is sized for one bus and nothing asserts.\n\n*** THAT CONFIRMS grok's READING AND UPGRADES IT FROM A SEAT CLAIM TO A CHECKED ONE. ***  Its design\nreview said *\"falling through into existing bus_pci with cur_bus=1 (empty type-1 also survives)\"*,\nwhich this row correctly recorded as unmeasured.  It is now verified by source rather than accepted.\n\n**CONSEQUENCE: the fix is a DELETION, not an addition.**  Remove the `bus != 0` special case and let\nthe existing path serve the cycle; an absent device on an absent bus answers all-ones by itself.\nThat is the cheapest member-7 in the option set -- and the addition licence is satisfied trivially,\nbecause nothing is added.\n\n**WHAT STILL OWES A MEASUREMENT, and it is one run:** build the fall-through and re-run\n`regress/lca_confbus_probe.py`.  **PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTION: the `bus kill' row turns from `dead'\nto `alive', and a mirror of `bus ctlsig' on the armed side should read `0xffffffffffffffff`** -- the\nsame value the bus-0 control already returns, which is what distinguishes a serviced empty-bus cycle\nfrom a silent unserviced return (`0x0`, indistinguishable from unmapped).\n\n*** PASS-1 FIX PANEL, 2026-09-07 -- PROVISIONAL: recorded on THREE landed seats (deepseek, minimax,\nthe kimi-k3:cloud substitute) while codex, grok and glm were still answering.  Any ruling short of\nthe roster is provisional on its face. ***\n\n**Q1 IS SETTLED AND MY WORRY WAS UNFOUNDED -- AND THE PROOF IS INSIDE THE EXISTING CODE, WHICH I HAD\nNOT THOUGHT TO USE.**  I asked whether the file's tag comment describes the same `tag` this code\ncomputes (`(relative_addr >> 5) & ~0xff`) or a pre-shift value.  minimax:\n\n> *\"The existing IDSEL check proves the code's `tag` IS the comment's `tag`.  The `bus == 0` branch\n> checks `tag & (0x800U << dev)` for dev 0..20.  The mask `1 << (dev+11)` only matches real hardware\n> if `tag` holds IDSEL bits at positions 11..31 -- exactly the comment's type-0 layout.  That branch\n> is EXERCISED TODAY on real bus-0 traffic and works, so the interpretation is FORCED.\"*\n\n`0x800` is exactly `1 << 11`, and the kimi substitute reaches it independently (*\"func at bits 10..8\nin both rows\"*).  **So `dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f` is the faithful type-1 decode.**  *A working branch\nof the same function is a stronger oracle for a layout question than the comment is -- the comment\ncould be stale, the working code cannot be.*\n\n*** Q2 IS THE REAL DEFECT IN MY SKETCH, AND ALL THREE SEATS FLAG IT: THE FIX AS WRITTEN WOULD BE\nINCOHERENT. ***  deepseek: *\"the sketch is incomplete/incoherent because it keeps\n`bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` while the type-1 tag comment says bus is in tag bits 23..16.\"*  The kimi\nsubstitute sharpens it: **the model computes THREE bus/type sources from the address -- the AD[1:0]\ntype bits, the tag's bus field, and `ioc_conf & 1` -- and DISCARDS TWO.**  *\"The packet cannot say\nwhich one real hardware honours.\"*\n\n> **So decoding `dev` from the type-1 layout while taking `bus` from a register bit mixes two\n> addressing models.**  Either the tag is authoritative (and `bus` must come from bits 23..16) or the\n> register is (and the type-1 tag layout does not apply).  **The fix cannot be written until that is\n> chosen, and no seat could choose it from the packet.**\n\n**Q4: THE DISCRIMINATING ROW IS NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT -- unanimous among the three.**  My\nproposed armed-side all-ones row separates *serviced an empty bus* from *returned without servicing*\n(`0x0`, indistinguishable from unmapped).  **It cannot separate IMPLEMENT from an explicit all-ones\nreturn**, because an EMPTY bus 1 answers all-ones under both.  kimi: *\"only a populated bus-1 device\ncan\"* tell them apart -- and nothing in this tree places one.\n\n*That is a real limit on what this round can claim, and it is better to know it before building the\nrow than after.*\n\n**NOT ADOPTED PENDING THE ROSTER.**  The Q2 question is a hardware-behaviour question the packet\ncannot settle, and `_scratchpad/` holds no LCA/21066 datasheet (the `ls _scratchpad/*.pdf` list has\nDC21285 but no 21066), so it may not be settleable from this machine at all -- which would make it a\ncandidate for the 2026-09-09 adjudication rather than for a fix round.\n\n*** grok LANDED AND SETTLED Q2 -- THE ANSWER THE PACKET-FED SEATS COULD NOT REACH, AND IT DISSOLVES\nTHE INCOHERENCE RATHER THAN CHOOSING A SIDE. ***\n\n> **VERDICT: IMPLEMENT, but the sketch is one assignment short.**  `dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f` is the\n> right extract of *this* `tag` (Q1 is not a double-shift).  **Also assign\n> `bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff` -- `ioc_conf & 1` is the PCI cycle TYPE, not the bus number.**\n> Member 7; fall through into `bus_pci`.  The proposed all-ones row is necessary and not sufficient:\n> the witness address is not a type-1 tag, and empty-slot data cannot pin the extract.\n\n**WHY THIS RESOLVES THE CONFLICT INSTEAD OF PICKING A WINNER.**  deepseek and the kimi substitute\nwere right that taking `dev` from the tag while taking `bus` from a register bit mixes two addressing\nmodels.  grok's reading shows the register bit is not a bus selector at all: it chooses the CYCLE\nTYPE -- type 0 addresses the local bus, type 1 addresses a bus behind a bridge -- and in type 1 the\nbus number comes from the tag.  **The file's own comment, \"the PCI bus 0 vs bus non-0 selection bit\",\nis a loose description of exactly that**, so the code comment and grok agree once the names are\nstraightened.  *The variable is simply misnamed: `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1;` stores a cycle type in\nsomething called `bus`.*\n\n**THE FIX IS THEREFORE TWO ASSIGNMENTS, NOT ONE:**\n\n    } else {\n            bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff;    /*  type-1: bus is bits 23..16  */\n            dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f;    /*  type-1: device is bits 15..11  */\n    }\n\n*** AND grok BREAKS MY PROPOSED DISCRIMINATING ROW FOR A REASON I HAD NOT SEEN: \"the witness address\nis not a type-1 tag\". ***  The existing probe reads at `LCA_PCI_CONF + 0`, so `tag == 0` -- which\nunder the type-1 decode means bus 0, device 0.  **The row would therefore exercise the new code with\na degenerate tag and prove nothing about the extract**, and \"empty-slot data cannot pin the extract\"\nbecause an absent device answers all-ones whatever bus/dev were decoded.\n\n> **So the round needs a row that drives a NON-ZERO type-1 tag and observes something that depends\n> on the decoded bus/dev -- and with no device on bus 1, there is nothing to observe.**  That is the\n> same limit the three packet-fed seats reached from the other side (*\"only a populated bus-1 device\n> can\"* separate IMPLEMENT from a faked all-ones return).  Two independent routes to one conclusion.\n\n**HARDWARE CLAIM, FLAGGED AS UNVERIFIABLE FROM THIS MACHINE:** that `ioc_conf & 1` is the cycle type\nis a statement about the 21066/LCA, and **`_scratchpad/` holds no LCA datasheet** -- the PDF list has\nDC21285 but nothing for this chipset.  It is consistent with the file's comment and with standard PCI\ntype-0/type-1 addressing, but it is not CHECKED here, and this project's rule is to cite only what\nhas been read.  **That is a good candidate for the 2026-09-09 adjudication rather than for me.**\n\n*** THE PANEL IS COMPLETE (6 seats) AND UNANIMOUS ON THE DESIGN -- AND codex CITED A PRIMARY SOURCE\nSITTING ON THIS MACHINE THAT I HAD JUST DECLARED UNAVAILABLE. ***\n\nI wrote, one paragraph above, that the cycle-type claim is *\"a statement about the 21066 ... not\nCHECKED here\"* because `_scratchpad/` holds no LCA datasheet.  **codex pointed at\n`_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/alpha/pci/lca_pci.c` -- the REAL OpenBSD LCA driver, 4,925 bytes,\ndated 1997, on disk in this project.**  Verified and read:\n\n    :103   if (bp != NULL)  *bp = (tag >> 16) & 0xff;      /*  BUS      */\n    :106   if (dp != NULL)  *dp = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f;      /*  DEVICE   */\n    :108   if (fp != NULL)  *fp = (tag >>  8) & 0x7;       /*  FUNCTION */\n\n**That is grok's proposal, verbatim, from the driver the hardware was written for.**  `bus` is bits\n23..16 of the tag and `dev` is bits 15..11 -- not inference, not a datasheet paraphrase, the actual\ndecomposition routine.\n\n*** AND `lca_conf_read` SETTLES Q2 OUTRIGHT: ***\n\n    :125   /* secondary if bus # != 0 */\n    :126   pci_decompose_tag(&lcp->lc_pc, tag, &secondary, &device, 0);\n    :127   if (secondary) {\n    :130           REGVAL(LCA_IOC_CONF) = 0x01;\n    :132   } else {\n    :134           /*  on the LCA, must frob the tag used for devices on the primary bus,\n    :136              in the same ways as is used by type 1 configuration cycles on PCs.  */\n    :139           tag = (1 << (device + 11)) | (tag & 0x7ff);\n    :140   }\n\n**The driver derives the bus from the TAG and then writes `IOC_CONF = 0x01` to select the SECONDARY\nconfiguration FORMAT.**  So `ioc_conf & 1` is the cycle type, exactly as grok and codex said and as\nthe fork's own loosely-worded comment (\"bus 0 vs bus non-0 selection bit\") was groping at.  **The\nfork's variable is misnamed**, and its type-0 handling is correct -- `(1 << (device + 11))` is\nprecisely the IDSEL form the `0x800U << dev` loop scans for.\n\n*** THE ROW IS MASSIVELY STRENGTHENED, AND THIS IS THE PART THAT MATTERS. ***  My witness writes\n`0x1` to `LCA_IOC_CONF` and then performs a config access, and I described that as an ordinary but\nsynthetic pair of guest accesses.  **It is not synthetic: it is the OpenBSD driver's own\nsecondary-bus path, line for line.**  `REGVAL(LCA_IOC_CONF) = 0x01` followed by the config read is\nwhat `lca_conf_read` DOES whenever it is asked about a device behind a bridge.\n\n> **So the kill is not merely reachable in principle -- it is on the code path a real guest driver\n> takes the moment it probes a secondary bus.**  The row previously said \"NOT CLAIMED: that any\n> shipped guest issues this sequence.\"  That caution can now be withdrawn: a shipped guest driver\n> issues exactly this sequence, by construction, for secondary-bus config cycles.\n\n**THE FIX, now backed by the driver rather than by reading the fork's comment:**\n\n    } else {\n            bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff;    /*  type-1: bus is bits 23..16   */\n            dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f;    /*  type-1: device is bits 15..11 */\n    }\n\n**WHAT IS STILL NOT SETTLED, and every seat agrees:** the discriminating row.  grok -- *\"the witness\naddress is not a type-1 tag, and empty-slot data cannot pin the extract\"*; glm -- the row *\"conflates\nIMPLEMENT with explicit-all-ones DECLINE, silent with latched, and can false-pass on stale all-ones\nunless the destination register is pre-seeded\"*; the kimi substitute and minimax -- only a POPULATED\nbus-1 device could separate them.  **Four independent routes to one wall.**  glm's pre-seeding point\nis the one actionable improvement: seed the destination register so a stale all-ones cannot pass.\n\n*The lesson about this machine is the `ls _scratchpad/*.pdf` lesson again, one directory over: I\ndeclared a fact unverifiable while its primary source sat in the project.  **Before calling a\nhardware question unanswerable here, look for the guest OS source tree.***\n\n*** A FACT THIS ROW'S QUESTION DOES NOT CARRY, ESTABLISHED 2026-09-08 FROM THREE INDEPENDENT FILES:\nOPTION-SET MEMBER 5 (FAULT) IS NOT AVAILABLE ON ALPHA. ***\n\nThis is an Alpha machine, and the adjudication of this row weighs dispositions from the standing\noption set.  **FAULT looks available and is not**, which is the option-set-completeness problem\ninverted -- not a missing member, a PHANTOM one.\n\n    memory_rw.c        the device-failure dispatcher has MIPS and M88K exception arms and\n                       NO ALPHA ARM                          (found via dev_lca.c, `lcaisalane`)\n    memory_alpha.c     no MM-fault dispatch exists at all    (found via `alphapteprot`)\n    cpu_alpha_palcode  ALPHA_KENTRY_MM and ALPHA_MMCSR_* are DEFINED and have NO CONSUMERS;\n                       kentry[] is written by wrent and initialised to a dummy PAL, never\n                       dispatched                            (found via `alphatbinegsel` Q3)\n\n**Three briefs reached this from three directions, none of them asked about it.**  And a fourth\nseat priced it: choosing FAULT obliges building delivery first -- a PAL frame,\n`kentry[ALPHA_KENTRY_MM]`, MMCSR/VA, and teaching `pc_to_pointers` about a redirected PC.  *\"That is\na new IMPLEMENT round, not a de-fatalisation.\"*\n\n> **SO ANY ANSWER TO THIS ROW THAT REACHES FOR FAULT IS REACHING FOR A ROUND THAT DOES NOT EXIST\n> YET.**  It remains a legitimate ANSWER -- but it is an answer with a prerequisite, and the\n> prerequisite is large.  Recorded here so the adjudication prices it rather than assuming it.\n\n*** THE NOTE ABOVE UNDERSTATED THE SCOPE, AND THE CORRECTION IS THE USEFUL PART: FAULT IS NOT\n\"UNAVAILABLE ON ALPHA\".  IT IS AVAILABLE ON **MIPS AND M88K ONLY**, FOR EVERY ARCHITECTURE IN THIS\nTREE.  Derived 2026-09-08 by opening the dispatcher. ***\n\n`src/cpus/memory_rw.c`, the device-failure path -- the whole of it:\n\n    if (res <= 0 && !no_exceptions) {\n            debug(\"[ %s device '%s' addr %08lx failed ]\");\n    #ifdef MEM_MIPS\n            mips_cpu_exception(cpu, cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION? EXCEPTION_IBE : EXCEPTION_DBE, ...);\n    #endif\n    #ifdef MEM_M88K\n            m88k_exception(cpu, cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION ? M88K_EXCEPTION_INSTRUCTION_ACCESS\n                                                           : M88K_EXCEPTION_DATA_ACCESS, 0);\n    #endif\n            return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED;\n    }\n\n**TWO `#ifdef` ARMS.  There is no ARM arm, no Alpha arm, no PPC arm and no SH arm** -- every other\narchitecture reaches `return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED` with no exception delivered at all.\n\n> **SO OPTION-SET MEMBER 5 IS A PHANTOM ON EVERY ARCHITECTURE BUT TWO**, and a round on an ARM, SH,\n> PPC or Alpha device that reaches for FAULT is reaching for a round that does not exist yet.\n\n*Found because I wrote the OPPOSITE into a brief.  Assessing `fbfiqsilent` -- an ARM footbridge row\n-- I told the seat that \"member 5 FAULT may genuinely be available here, `memory_rw`'s\ndevice-failure path has an ARM arm.\"  It replied that the claimed arm **is absent from this tree**,\nand opening the file confirms it.  **I was one brief away from spreading a false availability claim\nacross the ARM queue**, having just corrected three rows for the Alpha version of it.*\n\n*** codex: REVISE.  THE SHIFT IS RIGHT AND ALMOST EVERYTHING AROUND IT NEEDED CORRECTING --\nINCLUDING TWO PREMISES I STATED AS SETTLED. ***\n\n**CONFIRMED, with a denominator.**  `dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f` is correct: `relative_addr >> 5`\nundoes the address expansion, so for a valid tag T and aligned register R,\n`A = (T << 5) | (R << 5) | 24` gives `((A >> 5) & ~0xff) == T`, putting device bits at 15..11 of\nthe recovered tag.  Validated over **4,194,304 encode/decode combinations, zero mismatches** --\nand codex labelled that an ARITHMETIC check, not an emulator test, which is the honest form.\n\n*** MY IMPLEMENT SKETCH WAS INCOMPLETE: IT DECODED `dev` AND NOT `bus`. ***  `IOC_CONF` selects\nthe CYCLE FORMAT, it does not supply the bus number -- PCI address bits 23..2 come from CPU\naddress bits 28..7, and only bits 1..0 come from `IOC_CONF[1:0]`.  So the minimum correction is\n\n    } else {\n        bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff;\n        dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f;\n    }\n\n> **AND THE TRAP IN THE OBVIOUS ALTERNATIVE, which I would have walked into: DO NOT CHOOSE THE\n> DECODING BRANCH FROM THE EXTRACTED BUS FIELD.**  Primary-bus IDSEL bits overlap that field, and\n> keeping `bus == 1` in the forwarding call **collapses every type-1 destination onto bus 1**.\n\n*** PREMISE 4 IS FALSE AS STATED, and the refutation is one function away. ***  I wrote that an\nempty-slot read returns all-ones.  `bus_pci_data_access` implements a THREE-WAY contract:\n\n    read, register 0        ->  0xffffffffffffffff\n    read, any other reg     ->  ZERO\n    write                   ->  a diagnostic on EVERY access, then returns\n\nThe measured ID-register instance stands; the GENERAL claim does not.  **This matters because it\nmeans the candidate dispositions differ TODAY, with no bus-1 device added at all** -- an\nunconditional all-ones responder differs from the backend at nonzero registers, and announcement\npolicies differ on diagnostic output.  (The write diagnostic does not terminate: `fatal()` returns\nnormally.)\n\n*** THE DISCRIMINATING ROW I PROPOSED CANNOT DISCRIMINATE, AND IT HAS THREE FALSE-POSITIVE TRAPS.\n***  At register 0, all-ones is returned by correct forwarding, by the INCORRECT bus-fixed-to-1\nforwarding, by explicit all-ones completion, and by announced all-ones completion -- four\ndispositions, one observable.  Worse:\n\n* **THE EXISTING ADDRESS DOES NOT ENCODE BUS 1.**  `lca_confbus_probe.py`'s setup uses a ZERO\n  window offset, so the recovered tag -- bus field included -- is zero, and the control side\n  reaches the **no-IDSEL fallback to device 0**.  *The witness establishes mode-dependent\n  termination, not a correctly addressed secondary-bus transaction.*  That is a scope correction\n  to what this row's existing witness is entitled to claim.\n* **A FAILED LOAD PRESERVES THE CONTROL SIGNATURE.**  `memory_rw` pre-zeros, so a handler that\n  returns success without filling data yields zero -- but a literal `return 0` takes the FAILURE\n  path, and `LS_GENERIC_N` then returns *before updating the destination register*, so the witness\n  **reads back the all-ones the register already held.**  A vacuity trap of exactly the shape the\n  carrier's witness clauses exist to catch.\n* **THE PARSER TAKES THE FIRST OBSERVATION** (`printed` uses `re.search`), so adding another print\n  and calling the helper again retrieves the CONTROL's result rather than the new one.\n\n**WHAT IT PRESCRIBES INSTEAD:** keep the signature check as an empty-ID COMPLETION check with an\nindependently identified result and a distinct prior destination value, and add a **model-level\nrouting fixture** observing the exact bus/device/function/register passed onward -- distinct\nsynthetic devices on bus 1 and on another bus separate correct decoding from hardcoded bus 1 and\nfrom constant-return stubs.  Include write dispatch and preservation of primary-bus decoding.\n\n*** A SECOND ARCHITECTURAL PREMISE CORRECTED: RAW LCA ABSENCE DOES NOT GUARANTEE ALL-ONES. ***\nDigital's §6.2.18 says an unanswered transaction is a MASTER ABORT: the read returns *arbitrary\ndata with error status*, raising an instruction exception plus interrupt and IOC error logging.\n**So the 64-bit all-ones signature is an EMULATOR-COMPATIBILITY oracle, not a hardware contract**,\nand the round may not claim hardware fidelity for it.  The current path delegates to the generic\nbackend and returns success; it implements none of that error sequence.\n\n**SCOPE, stated so the round does not overclaim what it closes:** this closes the configuration\nhandler's termination and its missing destination decode ONLY.  Still open in the same file --\n`lca_ioc`'s unconditional read/write `default` termination and its register-value rejections;\n`lca_interrupt_assert` / `lca_interrupt_deassert`; and `lca_isa`'s separate guard.  Nor does\nforwarding implement bridge topology or configuration-cycle error reporting: the backend matches a\nnumeric tuple and receives **no cycle-type argument at all**, so type 1 with a zero bus field needs\nan explicit policy or simple forwarding will alias a root-bus device.\n\n**PRIMARY SOURCE, and this project did not have it:** codex cited the *Digital 21066/21066A\nHardware Reference Manual* (Table 6-1, §6.4.2, §6.2.18) with page anchors.  No 21066/LCA manual was\nin `_scratchpad/`, and there are several open LCA rows reasoning from source alone.  Fetched at the\ncitation, on the `vmesbcp2.pdf` precedent.\n\n*** AND THEN VERIFIED LOCALLY RATHER THAN TRUSTED, which is what the carrier's rule demands and\nwhich this project has been burned by skipping: `#357`/`#362`/`#365` shipped DDI 0100I page numbers\nthat came from panel output rather than a manual. ***  `_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt`, 713 KB /\n13,876 lines:\n\n    :8840  S6.2.6   \"CPU physical address bits <31:7> are used for PCI address bits <26:2>\"\n                    -> THE SHIFT OF 5 that `relative_addr >> 5` implements.  CONFIRMED.\n    :9216  S6.2.9   \"PCI address bits ad<1:0> come directly from configuration cycle type\n                     register (IOC_CONF, Section 6.4.2) bits <1:0>\"\n                    -> IOC_CONF selects the CYCLE TYPE and does NOT supply the bus.  CONFIRMED.\n    :9486  S6.2.18  \"the IOC returns arbitrary data with error status to the CPU, which causes\n                     an instruction exception\" + internal interrupt + ERR/CODE=1/CMD/ADDR/LOST\n                    -> absent-device reads are NOT all-ones on real hardware.  CONFIRMED.\n\n**One narrowing, stated because a wrong reason gets reused:** codex wrote *\"bits 23..2 from CPU\naddress bits 28..7\"*.  The manual's general sparse rule is the WIDER `<31:7> -> <26:2>` with the\nsame shift; codex's range is the PCI **type-1 config format** (bus 23..16, dev 15..11, fn 10..8,\nreg 7..2), which S6.2.9 defers to explicitly -- *\"restricted by the encoding requirements of PCI\nconfiguration cycles as described in the PCI Local Bus [Specification]\"*.  **Same shift, narrower\nwindow, and the narrowing is correct for config cycles** -- so `bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff` and\n`dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f` rest on the PCI spec's field layout, not on this manual, and the round\nshould say so rather than citing the LCA manual for the field positions.\n\n*** A TRAP FOR THE NEXT READER: `file` REPORTS THIS PDF AS \"10 page(s)\" AND IT IS WRONG. ***  The\nreal count lives in a linearized trailer.  I nearly discarded the download as truncated because\ncodex had cited `#page=194` and `#page=211`; `pdftotext -layout` settled it in one command.  *A\nsize or page check that disagrees with a citation is a reason to extract, not to discard.*\n\n*** I RE-DERIVED THIS ROW'S GUEST-SOURCE EVIDENCE FROM SCRATCH AND EVERY ELEMENT OF IT WAS ALREADY\nHERE.  Caught before committing, by grepping this note instead of trusting my memory of it. ***\n\nI read `_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/alpha/pci/lca_pci.c` and wrote up four findings as new: that\n`lca_conf_read` writes `REGVAL(LCA_IOC_CONF) = 0x01` on the secondary-bus path; that `data` is\ninitialised to `(pcireg_t)-1` and overwritten only if `badaddr()` succeeds, so absence normalises to\nall-ones at ANY offset; that the primary-bus tag frob places an IDSEL bit at `1 << (device + 11)`;\nand the decomposition itself.  **Counted in this very note before adding them: `REGVAL` 4, `IDSEL`\n7, `device + 11` 4, `badaddr` 2, `pcireg_t)-1` 1.**  All four were already recorded, several in the\nsame words.\n\n**THE CAUSE IS EXACT AND IT IS THE SECOND TIME TODAY: I READ PART OF A RECORD AND ACTED.**  I\nqueried this row's `state_note` and looked at the first fifteen hundred characters, which covered\nthe defect and the shipped witness but stopped short of the guest-source section.  Earlier the same\nday I read a row's prose and never read its `witness` FIELD, and built a second witness for a row\nthat already had one.  *Different field, same failure: a partial read of a record that already\ncontained the answer.*\n\n**WHAT IS ACTUALLY NEW FROM MY PASS is one caveat, and it belongs to the reachability claim rather\nthan to the decode:** the driver's `REGVAL(LCA_IOC_CONF) = 0x01` sits behind `if (secondary)`, so it\nexecutes only when something addresses a bus other than zero -- and **whether `alphabook1` ever\npresents a secondary bus is not established anywhere in this row.**  `devinit_lca` adds only\n`i82378zb` at 0:7:0.  So *the driver contains the sequence unconditionally; whether a booting guest\nexecutes it is a topology fact nobody here has measured.*  That bounds how far the guest source can\ncarry the reachability argument, and it is the one line worth keeping from an otherwise redundant\nre-derivation.\n\n*The `guestsrcunknown` row deliberately FILED its proposed carrier line for the 2026-09-09\nadjudication rather than editing the carrier unilaterally.  I have honoured that: today's two\ncarrier edits were INVENTORY entries in an existing PDF list, not a new behavioural rule.*\n\n*** THE REVISED DESIGN IS A DIFF, NOT A SHAPE -- AND IT REVERSES ITS OWN EARLIER SCOPE POSITION. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"Include the bus-zero alias in this round.  A single-handler diff can fix it without\nchanging the backend API.  My earlier proposal was incomplete; the replacement observer remains\nunvalidated here.\"*\n\n**THE KEY CORRECTION IS ONE VARIABLE, AND IT IS THE WHOLE DEFECT IN MINIATURE:** the handler used a\nsingle `bus` for two different things -- the CYCLE TYPE selected by `ioc_conf & 1`, and the\nDESTINATION bus.  The diff splits them:\n\n    -\tint tag, bus, dev, func, reg;\n    +\tint tag, type1, bus, dev, func, reg;\n    ...\n    -\tbus = d->ioc_conf & 1;\n    -\tif (bus == 0) {\n    +\ttype1 = d->ioc_conf & 1;\n    +\tbus = 0;\n    +\tif (!type1) {\n\nthen decodes the real destination on the type-1 arm -- `bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff; dev = (tag >> 11)\n& 0x1f;` -- replacing the `fatal(); exit(1)`.\n\n*** AND IT CLOSES THE ALIAS IT ITSELF FOUND, WHICH THE EARLIER \"MINIMUM CORRECTION\" DID NOT: ***\n\n    +\tif (type1 && bus == 0) {\n    +\t\t/*  Type-1 cycles do not select primary-bus devices.\n    +\t\t    Match bus_pci's empty-slot reads; discard writes.  */\n    +\t\todata = reg == 0 ? (uint64_t) -1 : 0;\n    +\t} else {\n    \t\tbus_pci_setaddr(...); bus_pci_data_access(...);\n    +\t}\n\n**The refusal covers reads AND writes and leaves backend state untouched**, and its return values\nmatch `bus_pci_data_access`'s own empty-slot contract -- all-ones at register 0, zero elsewhere --\nwhich is the contract I stated WRONGLY as a premise two passes ago and which codex corrected.\n\n> **SO THE ROUND'S SCOPE QUESTION IS ANSWERED AGAINST MY NARROWER FRAMING.**  I asked it to argue\n> the narrower round first; it did, and then held that the alias must land in the same diff -- on\n> the ground that forwarding a type-1 cycle with bus zero would select the existing primary-bus\n> `i82378zb`, so a round that decodes `bus` and stops there ships a NEW misrouting in place of a\n> termination.\n\n**HONEST LIMITS, stated by the seat rather than extracted from it:** the diff is **unapplied and\nuncompiled** -- it passes `git apply --check` and nothing more, because that session is read-only --\nand **the replacement observer remains unvalidated**, so the round still has no measurement lane\nbeyond the existing 3/4 witness.  *A design that says which of its parts are untested is worth more\nthan one that does not, and this round must not present the diff as measured.*\n\n*** #515 IMPLEMENTED AND MEASURED, 2026-09-08.  6/10 PRE-FIX -> 10/10 POST-FIX, and the round found\na FLOOR BREACH IN ITS OWN DETECTOR before any table ran. ***\n\n**THE DIFF** is codex's revised design applied unchanged in substance: `bus' split into `type1'\n(the CYCLE TYPE, `d->ioc_conf & 1') and `bus' (the DESTINATION, decoded from the address), the\n`fatal(\"TODO. Non-zero bus.\"); exit(1)' arm replaced by `bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff; dev = (tag >> 11)\n& 0x1f;', and a refusal arm for the type-1/bus-zero alias returning `bus_pci_data_access''s own\nempty-slot values.  40 insertions, 9 deletions, 47 changed lines.\n\n**THE FUNCTION TAIL IS PRESERVED ON BOTH ARMS** -- `memory_writemax64` and `return 1` are still\nreached, so this is not the early-`return' sketch four readers once proposed into a different\nfunction.  Checked by reading the whole function after the edit, not by reading the diff.\n\n*** MEASUREMENT, in a scratch clone with the baseline NAMED and REBUILT. ***  `/tmp/gxlca`, a\nrelocatable `cp -a` of `build/`; baseline = git HEAD `5d3f82c`; `dev_lca.c` `cmp`-clean against SEC\nbefore mutant 1; baseline binary rebuilt IN THE CLONE with `[ binary -nt source ]` checked.\n`build/`, `est/`, `build-asan/` and the WSL tree were NOT touched.\n\n    PRE-FIX   LCACONFBUS_RESULT=6/10    pass2_20260908/lca_pre.raw\n    POST-FIX  LCACONFBUS_RESULT=10/10   pass2_20260908/lca_post.raw\n\nBoth verified by their completion sentinel (`LCACONFBUS_DONE`), not by existence.\n\n**THE ANCHOR ROW PRINTED THE DERIVED TUPLE EXACTLY**, which is the whole point of an anchored row:\n\n    EVIDENCE(S2): [ bus_pci_data_access(): write to non-existant device, bus 1 func 2 device 3 ]\n\nAddresses were derived from the handler's own two decode lines and then CONFIRMED by that line --\n`bus 1 dev 3 func 2` from `relative_addr = 0x234000`.  The three fields are deliberately DISTINCT\nand non-zero so a swapped field cannot pass by coincidence.\n\n*** TWO DEFECTS IN MY OWN DETECTOR, BOTH FOUND BEFORE COMMIT, AND THE SECOND IS A FLOOR BREACH. ***\n\n**(1) A VACUOUS ROW, caught by the PRE-FIX RUN rather than by review.**  `t1 alias' first read\n`refused if ALIAS not in txt2' and scored GREEN on the pre-fix build -- where the host had already\ndied at the previous access and the alias write NEVER EXECUTED.  An absence-of-text oracle cannot\ndistinguish *\"ran and was refused\"* from *\"never ran\"*: the pattern-that-matched-nothing class, as a\nphantom absence read as a result.  Bound to `alive2' and now renders `died-before-E'.  *The row\nwould have shipped green-for-the-wrong-reason on the very build it exists to indict.*\n\n**(2) A CLAUSE-(d) NULL-REPAIR MUTANT THAT SWEEPS A 10-ROW DETECTOR CLEAN, found by walking the\nCHANNEL line at row-writing time.**  The refusal's repair is one expression -- `odata = reg == 0?\n(uint64_t) -1 : 0'.  Null it to `odata = 0' and every one of the ten rows still passes: the alias\nwas exercised only by a WRITE, whose `odata' is never read, and both control reads take the type-0\npath.  ROW `t1 rdval' added -- a type-1 READ at the alias, pinning the synthesised value.\n\n> *** MY OWN PASS-2 BRIEF ASSERTED THAT CELL WAS UNOBSERVABLE, AND IT IS NOT. ***  I wrote that\n> (type-1, bus == 0, read) *\"returns the same all-ones value as the control, so a read oracle cannot\n> see it\"*.  That conflates two different accesses: the control is a TYPE-0 empty-slot read served\n> by `bus_pci`, and this is the REFUSAL arm's own synthesised value.  They agree under the fix and\n> DISAGREE under the mutant, which is exactly what makes a row possible.  The brief had already gone\n> to the panel when I found it; it goes to the adjudication pass as my own correction.\n\n*** THE PRE-TABLE CHECK WAS RUN AND ITS OUTPUT RECORDED BEFORE TABLE 1, mtime 21:35:00 against a\nbrief of 21:32:10 -- the order is the proof, and it is the artifact rather than an assertion. ***\n`pass2_20260908/PRETABLE.out`, all four checks with `N/A` discharged WITH REASONS on the three\ncontrol rows rather than left blank.\n\n> **AND THE CARRIER ASKED THE FIRST MANDATED APPLICATION TO *CHECK* THE CHANNEL LINE, NOT MERELY\n> OBEY IT -- because a freshly minted rule's exemplar is a hypothesis.  THE ANSWER IS THAT ITS CLAIM\n> HELD.**  The claim is that a row's channel-under-its-mutant is knowable at WRITING time, before\n> any table runs.  It was, and doing it found finding (2) above -- a floor breach -- with no build\n> and no measurement.  Recorded as a measurement OF the rule, not as praise for it.\n\n*** THE ASK-AGAIN: ONE CAPPED QUERY, THREE REAL ESCAPES, ALL THE SAME FAMILY.  2026-09-08. ***\n\nTable 2 came back CLEAN -- ten variants, zero escapes -- so the round spent its single capped\nadversarial query.  **The cap counts ASKS, never FINDINGS**, so all three are disposed of here rather\nthan one taken and two filed.\n\n    deepseek   `if (type1 && bus == 0 && len == 8)'\n               Every row used an 8-byte access, so all fourteen stay GREEN, while a 1/2/4-byte\n               type-1 bus-0 access bypasses the refusal and reaches `bus_pci_data_access' --\n               including the REAL device at 0:7:0.\n    kimi       `func = (tag >> 8) & 7'  ->  `& 3'\n               The table's only func probe was the anchor's func 2 (binary 010), which never\n               exercises the field's TOP bit.  Functions 4-7 silently alias onto 0-3, so a config\n               cycle aimed at dev 7 FUNC 4 matches the registered func-0 device.\n    codex      the `reg != 0' branch of the refusal's own expression\n               *** RANKED ABOVE THE OTHER TWO BY THE SEAT ITSELF, and it is right. ***  `len' and\n               `func' are values no row varies; `reg != 0' is a WHOLE BRANCH of `odata = reg == 0?\n               (uint64_t) -1 : 0' that nothing reached.  Killing `M2_nullval' proves sensitivity to\n               the register-ZERO result and establishes nothing about the required zero elsewhere.\n\n> **ONE FAMILY: AN AXIS EVERY EXISTING ROW HELD CONSTANT.**  len was 8 everywhere, func's top bit was\n> never set, reg was 0 everywhere.  Fourteen rows, three frozen axes -- and the rows were mutually\n> reinforcing on the axes they DID vary, which is exactly what makes a frozen axis invisible.\n\n*** AND THE THIRD ONE WAS ALREADY WRITTEN DOWN IN THIS ROUND'S OWN PRE-TABLE BLOCK AND WALKED PAST. ***\nSection 2 of `PRETABLE.out` says, in its own words: *\"THE `zero elsewhere' HALF IS NOT ROWED ...\nDISCLOSED, not silently skipped ... Coverage debt, clause (e).\"*  A disclosure is an honest record and\nit is not a row; **the seat found in one query what the round had already identified and deferred.**\nThat is the unread-review shape aimed at a round's own artifact rather than at a seat's answer.\n\n**FOUR ROWS ADDED, 14 -> 18** (`t1 len4`, `t0 func4`, `t1 reg0`, `t1 reg4`).  All L1 construction --\ncaught before the detector shipped.\n\n**DISPOSITION, stated rather than assumed.**  Under the body-tested-partition reading, NONE of the\nthree is a floor breach: `lca_pci_conf` tests `writeflag' in its own code but never branches on\n`len' or `func' or `reg', so they induce no partition and fall to clause (e).  **They are closed\nanyway, and the warrant is the CLAIM -> ROW check rather than the floor:** each one falsifies a claim\nthe round's own comment makes -- *\"a type-1 cycle whose bus field is zero selects NOTHING\"* -- and a\nsentence asserting an observable consequence owes a row.\n\n**THE REGISTER PAIR IS ONE SESSION ON PURPOSE.**  A bare *\"reg 4 returns 0\"* row would be vacuous:\nUNMAPPED space also reads 0, which is precisely why the pre-table block deferred it.  Pairing it with\nthe reg-0 read in the SAME process makes the PAIR the oracle -- all-ones then zero, same window, only\nthe register changing -- so a dead window cannot produce it.\n\n*** A SEAT NOTE THAT MATTERS BEYOND THIS ROUND: codex REFUSED THE ASK-AGAIN IN ITS NORMAL FORM, AND\nTHE REFUSAL WAS PREDICTED. ***  The ask-again's whole purpose is to obtain *\"the exact source edit as\na diff hunk\"* that re-opens a closed defect -- construction in its purest form, which\n[[codexcyberflag]] says is filtered.  It was, at 6,339 bytes.  **Reframed to ask for the GAP instead\nof the EDIT** -- *\"name the input-space cell these rows leave least constrained; describe it as a\nproperty, not an edit\"* -- **the same seat answered, and produced the best of the three findings.**\nThe ask-again is structurally a construction task, so every future one needs that reframe.\n\n*** THE LAST TWO SEATS LANDED AND BOTH HIT THE SAME AXIS.  FOUR OF SIX SEATS CONVERGED ON `reg'\nINDEPENDENTLY -- and one of the two is a SHAPE MY OWN MUTANT DOES NOT COVER. ***\n\nThey were still streaming when the panel was first read (grok 230 b, minimax absent) and were NOT\nscored as failures; they finished at 3,600 and 4,549 bytes.  *Sampling once is not a death\ncertificate, and this is the second time today that rule has paid.*\n\n    minimax   `odata = reg == 0? (uint64_t) -1 : 0;`  ->  `odata = (uint64_t) -1;`\n              -- IDENTICAL to `M12_regnull`, which was already in table 3.  Independent arrival at\n              the same mutant is worth recording as corroboration, not as a new finding.\n    grok      `if (type1 && bus == 0)`  ->  `if (type1 && bus == 0 && reg == 0)`\n              *** A DIFFERENT AND STRICTLY WORSE SHAPE. ***\n\n**WHY grok's IS WORSE, and why `M12_regnull` does not cover it.**  `M12` nulls the ternary's\nelse-VALUE: a non-zero register still takes the refusal arm and merely gets the wrong answer.\n**grok's hoists `reg == 0` into the GUARD, so a non-zero register does not take the refusal arm at\nall -- it is FORWARDED, and at 0:7:0 it reaches the real `i82378zb`.**  That re-opens the exact\nmisrouting this round exists to prevent, at every register except 0.\n\n**AND IT COMES WITH THE MAINTAINER'S REASONING, which is what makes it a realistic edit rather than\nan adversarial one** -- grok's own words: *\"A maintainer looking at the ternary and at\n`bus_pci_data_access`'s `dev == NULL` arm will hoist `reg == 0` into the guard: 'the special case is\nonly the ID register; other registers already return 0 for a missing device.'  On an empty slot that\nis even true for reads.  On `i82378zb` it is not.\"*\n\n> **THAT IS THE dev-0 BLINDNESS AGAIN, ONE LEVEL UP.**  The maintainer's premise is TRUE at every\n> empty slot and FALSE at the one populated one -- which is precisely why every row this round added\n> late is aimed at 0:7:0.\n\n**PREDICTED to be caught by `t1 reg4`**: under grok's mutant that access is forwarded to 0:7:0\nregister 4, where the `i82378zb` holds `0x00000003`, against a want of `0x0`.  *** PREDICTED, NOT\nMEASURED -- and this project's rule is that a prediction in a measured column is the frozen-number\nclass.  A targeted two-build table (`base` + `M13_regconj`) is OWED before the round may claim the\nrow kills it. ***\n\n**SEAT TALLY ON THE `reg` AXIS: codex NAMED the gap as the least-constrained cell and ranked it\nabove `len` and `func`; grok and minimax each independently BUILT a mutant on it; and this round's\nown `PRETABLE.out` had DISCLOSED it as clause-(e) debt hours earlier and walked past it.**  Four\nindependent arrivals at one hole is not a coincidence -- it is what an axis every row holds constant\nlooks like from outside.\n\n*** grok PRESCRIBED THE ROW I HAD ALREADY BUILT, AND NAMED TWO RESIDUALS BEYOND IT.  Both are\nDISCLOSED under clause (e) rather than built, and the reasoning for stopping is grok's own. ***\n\nIts closing prescription: *\"If M10_regconj comes back 14/14, the row it wants is a type-1 access at\n0:7:0 **register 4**, both directions, with the same anti-wants as `t1 dev7rd` / `t1 dev7wr` (real\nconfig data / `device 7, function 0 (i82378zb)`).  **A value-0 row at an empty slot will not do.**\"*\n\n**`t1 reg4` IS at 0:7:0 register 4** -- `T1_D7R4 = LCA_PCI_CONF + 0x070080 + BYTE_ENABLES`, re-derived\nas tag 0x3800 -> bus 0, dev 7, func 0, reg 0x04.  So the address half of the prescription was already\nmet, and grok's warning about an empty slot is exactly why the pre-table block had deferred a bare\n`reg != 0` row in the first place.\n\n**RESIDUAL 1 -- ONE DIRECTION, NOT BOTH.**  I built the READ at reg 4 and not the WRITE.  The read\nalone kills `M13_regconj` (the forward returns the device's `0x00000003` against a want of `0x0`), so\nthe mutant in hand is covered; what is NOT covered is a variant that spares reads at non-zero\nregisters and forwards only writes.  **DISCLOSED, not built.**\n\n**RESIDUAL 2 -- THE BUS AXIS, and grok named it while explicitly declining to propose it:**\n\n> *\"Not proposing: `bus = 1` in the type-1 branch (collapses every non-zero bus onto the one\n> `t1 anchor` samples).  That also sweeps, and it is the original cycle-type/bus conflation without\n> the `exit(1)`.  It does not hit 0:7:0 -- those tags get bus 1, which is empty -- so it is a decode\n> collapse, not a reopening of the misrouting.  **A later bus-2 tuple row would catch it; this one\n> does not need that row to be a defect.**\"*\n\n**That is a FOURTH frozen axis** -- every type-1 row uses bus 0 or bus 1, so hardcoding `bus = 1`\nsweeps.  It is a real defect and a LESSER one: it collapses the decode without reaching the populated\nslot.  **A single bus-2 tuple row closes it.**\n\n*** WHY BOTH ARE FILED RATHER THAN BUILT, stated so it is a decision and not an omission. ***  The\ndetector has already grown 4 -> 18 rows inside one round, three of those additions from this single\nask.  Clause (e) exists precisely for this: **a contract that names what it does not cover is\nfalsifiable; one that claims completeness is not.**  grok's own framing supports stopping -- *\"this\none does not need that row to be a defect\"* -- and the probe header will carry both residuals by\nname, so a later escape is *\"outside the contract, as stated\"* rather than an argument about\noverclaiming.\n\n**The round therefore ships an 18-row detector with TWO NAMED HOLES: the write direction at a\nnon-zero register, and any bus value other than 0 or 1.**\n\n*** I PARKED A MAIN-LOOP NOTE IN `entries` AND THE DASHBOARD GENERATOR REFUSED TO RENDER.  It was\nright, and [[sgirandomcolour]] records this exact failure -- which I then repeated. ***\n\n`gen_dashboard.py` stopped with *\"'main-loop' 1 entries across 1 rows: lcaconfbus1kill.  Fix the\nledger (or add the column).  Declared: agy, codex, deepseek, fable5, glm, grok, kimi, minimax,\nopus5\"*.\n\n**`entries` is the SEAT ANSWER list, not a general log.**  `check_stage_panels.py` counts entries to\ndecide whether a stage's nine seats were fired -- so a main-loop note parked there is not merely\nmis-filed, it **MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER HAPPENED**, which is the\nseat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  The entry has been removed and its content lives\nin this note, where it belongs.\n\n*The lesson is not \"remember the convention\".  It is that the generator REFUSES rather than warns,\nso the mistake cannot ship -- and `sgirandomcolour` records seven such entries migrated for the same\nreason on an earlier day.  A rule written in a row's prose did not stop me; the tool did.*",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:43951:   "note": "Filed 2026-09-09 by the 7g-1 ruling: predicted by PRETABLE.out before table 1, measured by the Opus seat, graded floor on the data-flow discriminator.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:43961:     "note": "Agent-tool MEASURING seat; liveness token returned.  Scratch clone /tmp/gxopus7g1b (cp -a of build/), baseline dev_sgi_re.c at e73623a cmp-identical to SEC (md5 8479c1ea), own sentinel, one frozen instrument, predictions written to its PRETABLE.out before the first mutant log (mtime checked).  REPAIRED probe: 16 rows (fill b top, fill c top via a fill_at() helper that mirrors fill() byte for byte with two constants changed; all 14 existing row() calls byte-identical).  BASE 16/16; M_tlbA 14/16 (killed by fill b top + fill c top); M_tlbAB 15/16 (fill c top alone -- it admits B); the nine preserved mutants M1..M9 lose no kill (10/9/13/10/14/14/14/15/15 of 16 vs 10/9/11/10/12/12/12/13/13 of 14: gains only where the defect is live at all labels); null medium 0/16 (neither original table defined one).  All twelve landed on their pre-registered predictions.  Restore: cmp CLEAN vs SEC, rebuilt, -nt OK, restored md5 e113ca71 reproducing the previous seat's clone, sentinel cleared; build/ and build-asan/ untouched.  RECORDS: `fill b top' was #509's own pass-1 proposal, dropped on grok's reading and remembered as shipped by the 7g-1 brief; artifacts/.../PASS1_brief.md is NOT the brief that was sent (the sent one preserved under provenance/); its own table generator first printed verdicts unconditionally and was negative-controlled in five directions.  READING (not measured): do_mte_transfer()'s dst_tlb default: arm is a guest-drivable UNLATCHED fatal() -- filed sgimtetlbdefault.  Noted that SEC's dev_lca.c changed during its table (the loop's #518 work; its restore source dev_sgi_re.c was identical at start and end)."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:44304:     "note": "8,752 b.  D1 upheld ((e): 'M_ALIGNDECLINE is M_BIT30 with a different mask'); F1's warrant overturned as applied to the aliases; the stale `dreamcast_maple` probe comment (L4, fixed); and a predicted survivor `fatal(\"\\n\");` -- READ LATE (omitted from table 3's PRETABLE by my summary), disposed of in-round: killed in targeted kill table 4."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:44385:     "note": "256,557 b -- a THINKING BURN recovered by panel_ollama.py (think=False retry, 126,074 chars), packet-fed.  SURVIVOR atexit, with the flip condition stated; and the PRETABLE's 'strict tightening' claim is false on the time axis (table 4 scanned the whole buffer).  Both adopted."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:44577:   "note": "*** 2026-09-12, codex farm: the first brief was REFUSED by the codex cybersecurity filter; re-worded as plain mutation testing it answered (regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/codex_promote_20260912b.txt).  regress/xscale_inten_probe.py CREATED from the witness + 16 codex replacements (D_OP1, INTEN_WRITE_PRESERVES_ISTEER, BLOCKED reporting, stale-prompt and ignored-timeout fixes, RESULT/CONTROL lines) + D_CRM1_TMR0, the crm-isolation row codex said remained open (tmr0 <- 0x5A5A0000, TMRx_ENABLE clear).  29 rows, UNMEASURED.  The gate_arm.sh section is designed, NOT applied.  MANIFEST entry owed. ***\n\n*** PASS 2, 2026-09-11 (GPT-6 Astra, effort max): **A CLAUSE-(d) FLOOR BREACH IS PREDICTED, NOT YET\nMEASURED.**  THE ROUND IS NOT SHIPPABLE UNTIL IT IS BUILT AND SCORED. ***  Cited: regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/codex_pass2_20260911.txt\n\nVerdict: *\"The detector has holes: an opcode1-gated reinstatement escapes, and several recorded\nkills are misattributed. I found no reason to change the deletion itself.\"*\n\n**THE ESCAPING MUTANT (`M_OPCODE1`), inside the existing inten load arm, after the assignment:**\n    if (opcode1 != 0) { fatal(\"TODO: XScale read from inten?\\n\"); exit(1); }\n\nIt keeps the enclosing predicates and introduces a DIFFERENT one, which is exactly the clause-(d)\nform a PURE DELETION owes -- not the original death condition widened.  **The seat DECODED THE\nLITERAL CP6 INSTRUCTION WORDS in the probe** and found every tested inten access carries\n`opcode1 == 0`; the only nonzero-opcode1 instruction is `E_UNKNOWN` (`opcode1=3, crn=9, crm=2`),\nwhich never reaches the edited arm and dies through `unknown` anyway.  So it predicts **26/26, a\nTOTAL ESCAPE**, while a guest issuing an inten read with nonzero opcode1 would be killed -- so it is\nNOT an equivalent mutant.\n\n*** THAT 26/26 IS A PREDICTION WRITTEN IN THE MEASURED COLUMN'S CHAIR, AND THIS PROJECT HAS PAID\nFOR THAT FOUR TIMES.  IT IS RECORDED AS OWED, NOT AS A SCORE. ***  The leg must be BUILT and RUN\nbefore the breach is graded L2 -- deferred only because a gate held the build trees when it was\nfound.  If it escapes, it is **L2: replace IN-ROUND however late**.\n\n**THE PRETABLE'S clause-(e) DISCLOSURE DOES NOT COVER IT.**  It discloses `opcode1=3` as *\"tested in\narm E\"* -- but arm E tests that value in an ALREADY-REJECTED register tuple, which says nothing\nabout opcode1 at `crm=0, crn=0`.  **A disclosure that names the right VALUE at the wrong SITE is not\na disclosure of the cell.**  The PRETABLE's GUARD FIELD list is also wrong to call opcode1/opcode2\nguards: they are function INPUTS that this function never dispatches on.\n\nTWO MORE ROWS OWED, both cheap and both from the same pass:\n  * **`INTEN_WRITE_PRESERVES_ISTEER`** -- write isteer=0, write inten=S1, read isteer back and\n    expect 0.  A SECOND anchored row against the M5 register substitution, independent of the\n    free-running IRQ path.  **Today `ANCHOR_EXC` is the SOLE discriminator for M5 (25/26) and it is\n    the suite's most load-sensitive row** -- breakpoint plus timeout plus free-run.\n  * **a stepped `tmr0` read at `crm=1, crn=0, l_bit=1`** -- the de-escalation accept row for the\n    `crm` predicate IN ISOLATION.  `D_TRR0` changes crm AND crn together, so it does not hold the\n    other former gates true.  tmr0 is chosen because its read has none of tcr0/tcr1's\n    counter-increment side effect.\n\nMY M5 READING WAS UPHELD WITH TWO CORRECTIONS WORTH KEEPING: **not every `D_*` row is a round-trip**\n(`D_RESET` reads before any inten write and `D_TRR0` exercises another register -- `D_RESET` merely\nfails to discriminate because the reset values coincide), and *\"one discriminator for this\nconsistent M5 substitution\"* is NARROWER than *\"one row capable of detecting any wrong-register\nedit\"*.  *A locally-true restatement that widens a claim is the shape this project has recorded as\nharder to catch than a plain wrong answer.*\n\nTHE FIX ITSELF IS UNCHANGED AND ENDORSED: *\"I would keep the fix exactly as supplied\"*, and ACCEPT\n(option-set member 4) is upheld -- *\"I cannot justify a once-only announcement for the implemented\nread.\"*  **Eight passes on this round's detector, none on its fix until now, and the first pass on\nthe fix approved it while finding the detector's enumeration incomplete.**\n\n---\n\n*** 2026-09-11 L4 RECORD CORRECTION -- THIS ROW READ `state: open' WITH NO WITNESS FIELD WHILE A\nTWO-COLOUR RUNG-3 WITNESS, A PRETABLE.out AND A GREEN SEVEN-LEG KILL TABLE SAT IN ITS OWN\nARTIFACTS DIRECTORY. ***\n\nI wrote `xscale -- no round run' into the checkpoint this afternoon and it was FALSE.  Found by\nopening `regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/', not by any check.  What is measured there:\n\n    BASE       26/26  FAILING: NONE          M_OPCODE2  19/26  (7 D_* rows red)\n    HEAD       11/26  (15 rows red)          M_RD15     25/26  (RD15_SURVIVE)\n    M1         23/26  (3 ANCHOR rows red)    M_NREAD    19/26  (7 D_* rows red)\n    M5         25/26  (ANCHOR_EXC)           RESTORED   26/26  FAILING: NONE\n\ncited: regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/killtable_20260911/SCORES.txt.  **Floor clause (a) is met\n(HEAD red), every mutant is dead on its intended rows, and the restore verified BOTH parts --\nsource cmp-vs-SEC CLEAN and binary FRESH -- before clearing the sentinel.**\n\nTHE DENOMINATOR, because the first draft of this correction overstated the class and the count\nrefuted it: 169 ledger rows sit in work/held/open with zero entries, which is the NORMAL state of\na filed row.  Testing each for an artifacts dir holding a witness or killtable narrows it to\n**FOUR** -- ppcrldimimask, ppcrlwimiwidth, wdcaltdigin and this row -- and the other three are\nrecorded CORRECTLY, `in work' with a witness field naming the artifact.  *The records practice is\nholding; this row was the sole outlier, and a systemic claim would have been wrong.*\n\nSTILL OWED and NOT discharged by this edit: the round has had THREE seats (codex x2 2026-09-10,\nagy x1 2026-09-11) against a standing NINE-SEAT directive, and no panel dir cites it.  The fix is\nuncommitted (` M src/cpus/cpu_arm_coproc.c'), byte-identical in est/, p1=CLEAN p2=FRESH in all\nthree build trees.  **HELD for the panel under the owner's hold-everything ruling -- this edit\ncorrects the RECORD only and licenses nothing.**\n\n---\n\nFILED 2026-09-10 from the GPT-6 Astra exit audit (regress/artifacts/queuetriage/codex_exitaudit_20260910.txt), which ranked it I1/E1. VERIFIED LOCALLY by opening the file before filing.\n\nIn `arm_coproc_i80321_6', crm 0 / crn 0, the load arm reads:\n    cpu->cd.arm.r[rd] = cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten;\n    fatal(\"TODO: XScale read from inten?\\n\");\n    exit(1);\nThe destination register receives the modelled value and the process then dies.  The STORE arm immediately below writes the same field, and the i80321 interrupt controller consumes it, so the register is fully modelled in both directions.\n\n*** THE DECISIVE EVIDENCE IS THE SIBLING CASE IN THE SAME SWITCH, and it is one screen away: `case 4:' (i80321_isteer) has the IDENTICAL structure -- load arm assigns `cpu->cd.arm.r[rd] = cpu->cd.arm.i80321_isteer;', store arm writes it -- and carries NO fatal() and NO exit(). ***  So crn 0's kill is an OUTLIER inside its own switch rather than a policy, which is the upstream-residue pattern this project has now found several times: the author hit the exit he happened to reach while developing, and the sibling he did not.  That is a far stronger warrant than 'it is a host kill', and it is one grep away -- exactly what the option-set rule's sibling check is for.\n\nREACHABILITY: machine_iq80321.c installs it explicitly (`cpu->cd.arm.coproc[6] = arm_coproc_i80321_6;'), and Iyonix does the same.  This is not an unattached helper. Emulated firmware reading its own interrupt-enable register is the natural consumer, and the generic coprocessor dispatcher enforces no supervisor-only precondition of its own.\n\nDISPOSITION: option-set member 4 (ACCEPT) -- delete the two lines, keep the assignment, with NO latch, for the same reason as [[mapledmaaddr]]: the read is implemented and is not ignored, so an announce would ship a wrong record.  FAULT is unavailable here in any case: memory_rw.c delivers a failed-device exception for MIPS and M88K only, and this is ARM.  [CORRECTED 2026-10-18 at the row's closure (the closure checks of GPT-6 Astra and Grok 4.7, regress/artifacts/xscaleinten/close_20261018/, each finding it independently; verified at source): wrong, and reasoned from the wrong mechanism -- the bug file retracted its own copy of this sentence on 2026-09-11.  A coprocessor register transfer is not a device access and never reaches memory_rw.c, and ARM does deliver undefined-instruction exceptions: `arm_cdp()` in src/cpus/cpu_arm.c raises `ARM_EXCEPTION_UND`.  ACCEPT stands on its own warrant -- the read is implemented and is not ignored.]\n\nNOT MEASURED and no witness built; the rung-3 attempt is owed.  Note a witness needs a coprocessor read from an ARM guest, which is a different vehicle from the device probes this project mostly writes -- gate_arm.sh's 358 decode checks are the nearest existing machinery.\n\n*** DESIGN REVIEW 2026-09-10: ACCEPT IS RATIFIED, BUT MY SIBLING WARRANT WAS OVERCLAIMED AND IS NARROWED HERE.  Verified locally before recording. ***\n\nI wrote that the adjacent `case 4:' (isteer) 'carries NO fatal() and NO exit()'.  **FALSE ABOUT THE WHOLE CASE.**  Its READ arm is quiet, as I said -- but its STORE arm assigns the field and then `exit(1)' on ANY NONZERO VALUE.\n\n**THE ASYMMETRY I RELIED ON SURVIVES, AND ONLY IN THE READ DIRECTION:** case 4 READS quietly while case 0 READS and dies, which is the outlier evidence.  In the WRITE direction the two swap over -- case 0 writes quietly, case 4 writes and dies on nonzero.  So the file has kills in both cases, on opposite halves, and the correct statement is that THE READ HALVES ARE ASYMMETRIC, not that one case is clean and the other is not.\n\nThe verdict stands with a narrower warrant: **ACCEPT for the ordinary interrupt-enable read into a general register.**  Its sibling's write-side kill is a SEPARATE defect on a separate arm and belongs to a separate row, not folded into this one.\n\n*** THE OTHER SEVEN EXITS IN THIS FILE ARE TRIAGED, 2026-09-10 (xscaleinten/codex_coproc_20260910.txt, ZERO cyber flags) -- AND MY WORKING HYPOTHESIS IS REFUTED. ***\n\nI asked which of the other seven share this row's shape, on the theory that 'the value is already computed and then discarded' would make several of them one cheap round.  **NONE OF THE OTHER SEVEN HAS THE SAME WARRANTED TWO-LINE ACCEPT REPAIR.**  That is a useful negative: it stops a round copying this disposition across the file, which is exactly what the option-set rule warns about when a precedent is transferred without checking each site.\n\n**AND THE CENSUS UNDERCOUNTED THE FILE: eight ACTIVE exits plus SIX COMMENTED-OUT ones.**  The commented six are inert and are not defects, but they are worth knowing about -- a round de-fatalising a live site sits beside six places where someone already made that call and left the corpse in.\n\nTHE STRUCTURE, which is what decides how the remaining seven split into rounds: three CP15 sites, four CP6, one CP14 -- or by SHAPE, five specific-case sites, TWO DEFAULT-FED ENDPOINTS and one ENTRY GUARD.  The two default-fed ones are the priorities: CP6's shared `unknown:' label is reached from THREE defaults (the outer crm switch and both nested crn switches) and CP14's from FOUR plus a separate opcode2 guard -- so one repair at each label covers many decode paths at once.\n\n*** ONE SITE IS NEITHER A CASE NOR A DEFAULT, which no other exit in this queue has been: CP15's `rd == ARM_PC' ENTRY GUARD, before the switch, in BOTH directions. ***  A refusal that fires before any dispatch is a different fix shape from every other member of this class, and it is ranked last for that reason rather than because it is unimportant.\n\n*** REACHABILITY ANSWERED **YES** 2026-09-10 (xscaleinten/codex_witness_20260910.txt, GPT-6 Astra at max, ZERO cyber flags), AND MY NEGATIVE PREMISE WAS FALSE.  This row now has a ratified disposition, a confirmed path, and a named vehicle -- it needs a measuring seat and nothing else. ***\n\n**INSTALLERS: exactly two, and both assign BEFORE the `!machine->prom_emulation' early return**, so disabling PROM emulation does not remove CP6 -- `machine_iq80321.c' (default CPU `80321_600_2') and `machine_iyonix.c' (`80321_600_B0'), each a direct `cpu->cd.arm.coproc[6] = ...'.  No other installer or caller exists.  **Selecting an XScale CPU is NOT enough**: generic ARM init installs CP15 and, for XScale, CP14 -- never CP6.  Note the setup receives the BOOTSTRAP cpu only.\n\n**PATH:** guest fetch -> `arm_instr_to_be_translated' -> condition-selected `arm_instr_mcr_mrc' -> `arm_mcr_mrc' -> `coproc[6]' -> this handler.  **No privilege or mode check anywhere in that chain** -- dispatch does not consult CPAR (stored and read through CP15, and the source says it is otherwise unused).  Cold init already selects SVC32 with IRQ/FIQ masked, so no privilege transition is needed.  Prerequisites are ordinary: the condition must pass, the fetch must succeed, ARM state not Thumb, and CP6 must be installed -- with a null entry `arm_mcr_mrc' prints a generic TODO and **its undefined-exception call and `exit(1)' are COMMENTED OUT**, so absence does not terminate.\n\n*** MY NEGATIVE PREMISE -- 'the machinery is store/load-oriented and nothing drives a coprocessor transfer' -- IS MEASURABLY FALSE. ***  FIVE existing probes already execute CP15 MRC/MCR on `cats': `footbridge_sites_probe.py', `fbpending_witness.py', `fbpending_drain_probe.py', `pit8253_latch_probe.py', and gate 14's `arm_flags_probe.py' on `testarm'.  **The directly reusable pattern is `footbridge_sites_probe.py'** -- launch a stock machine into the debugger, plant instructions in RAM, set pc, step, check disassembly, inspect registers -- pointed at IQ80321 or Iyonix instead of `cats'.  **No machine or device addition is required**, which is what the witness ladder demands.  No dedicated CP6 test exists yet.\n\n*** AN ORACLE TRAP THAT APPLIES FAR BEYOND THIS ROW: A PRINTED STEP DISASSEMBLY DOES NOT PROVE THE INSTRUCTION EXECUTED, OR EVEN THAT ITS CONDITION PASSED.  The engine prints the disassembly BEFORE executing. ***  Any probe here that treats a stepped disassembly line as evidence of execution is asserting something the engine does not promise.\n\n**A NAIVE `write W, read, expect W' HAS FOUR INDISTINGUISHABLE FAILURES:** always-zero (passes when `W' is zero); a stale value equal to `W'; a read that never executes (passes when the destination already holds `W' -- *especially easy if the write-source register is reused as the read destination*); and a program that never ran at all, when the inspected register or memory already holds the expected value.  **The discriminating form:** two DISTINCT NONZERO values written and read in succession, the read destination deliberately pre-set to a third value before each read, and SEPARATE write-source and read-destination registers.  Plus execution evidence -- intended address, decoded coprocessor and register fields, the destination TRANSITION, and continuation -- with missing observations failing rather than defaulting to zero.\n\n**AND THE ORACLE NEEDS CONTINUATION AND SILENCE, not just a value:** the CURRENT code already assigns the destination before terminating, so the assignment alone is not the outcome under test.  Unchanged source predicts the named diagnostic and exit status 1; the corrected behaviour requires the right value, continued execution, and NO diagnostic from this read arm.\n\n**THE SAME VEHICLE REACHES EVERY OTHER TERMINATION IN THIS FUNCTION** -- interrupt steering (`crn=4', write, nonzero), interrupt acknowledge (`crn=8', write, ANY value including zero), and the shared `unknown:' label (either direction, unsupported `crn' or `crm').  `opcode1' and `opcode2' are NOT validated, so changing them alone does not route a recognised selection to `unknown'.  *** EACH SITE NEEDS ITS OWN PROCESS INVOCATION, because the first `exit(1)' reached prevents observing any later one. ***  That groups the COVERAGE work without implying the repairs should be identical.\n\n**A COUNT IN MY OWN BRIEF WAS WRONG: `crm=1' has SIXTEEN implemented direction arms -- eight reads and eight writes -- not fourteen.**  All sixteen are locally quiet, but *** 'quiet' DOES NOT MEAN 'a plain storage assignment' ***: `tcr0' and `tcr1' INCREMENT before returning their values, and a `tisr' write clears selected bits and deasserts timer interrupts.  A sibling-parity argument resting on 'the timer arms are plain accessors' would be wrong.\n\n*** 2026-09-11: WITNESS DESIGNED (rung 3, `-E iq80321`, six invocations).  Two of my stated premises were WRONG and the seat corrected both; one architectural fact it established changes what the round may CLAIM. ***\n\n**THE DEBUGGER CANNOT REACH CP6 STATE AT ALL, so clause (f)'s anchor rule cannot be satisfied in its usual form here.**  `debugger_cmd_reg()` rejects any coprocessor number outside 0-3 -- coproc 6 cannot even be asked for; `arm_cpu_register_dump()` prints GPRs, cpsr, banked registers and the CP15 control block and **names no CP6 field anywhere**; and the settings tree carries only `pc`, `r0`-`r9`/`sl`/`fp`/`ip`/`sp`/`lr` and `cpsr`, so `print` cannot reach it either.\n\n> **The ONLY way to observe `cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten` is to make a guest MRC copy it into a GPR -- which is the instruction under test.  There is no channel OUTSIDE the decode under test.**  The three substitutes (the MCR store arm at `:315` as a different branch naming the same lvalue; the `unknown:` printf anchoring the ENCODING; the crm 1 `trr0` bank anchoring BANK SELECTION) are each real and **none is decode-independent.  The contract must say so rather than claim an anchor it does not have.**\n\nMY WRONG PREMISES, both corrected by reading: (1) I said five existing probes execute CP15 MRC/MCR **on `cats`** -- `arm_flags_probe.py` is `-E testarm`, and its CP15 word `0xEE070F9A` is an MCR that ARMS A COMBINER, not a register read.  The substance held; the machine attribution did not.  (2) I proposed `cats` as reachable -- it is not: generic ARM init installs only CP15/CP14, so `arm_mcr_mrc` takes its `else` branch, **prints and does NOT exit**, leaving the poison unchanged and the step line disassembling a perfectly good `mrc 6,...`.  *That state is pixel-identical to 'the instruction did not execute', which is why row A2 asserts the ABSENCE of `[ TODO: arm_mcr_mrc:` with `cp_num=6`.  Without it the two conflate.*\n\nTHE BLIND SPOT I FLAGGED IS ANSWERED BY A ROW, NOT A COMMENT.  **P2** asserts, from the step-PRE snapshot, that each subject destination still holds its POISON immediately before its own step; if any already holds the expected value the probe prints `VOID` for every value row and goes RED.  **X4** is its inequality companion: arm N (subject word replaced by a NOP) must NOT show `S1` in r3 -- if it does, something other than the instruction under test put it there and every value row is worthless.\n\nTHE DISASSEMBLY GAP IS CLOSED BY MEASUREMENT, NOT BY ARGUMENT.  `cpu_dyntrans.c:282-296` re-reads and disassembles the word, and executes it afterwards at `:325`; the print path never consults the condition code.  So **arm C** plants `0x0E103610` -- ONE NIBBLE from the subject word -- and the step line prints `mrceq 6,0,r3,cr0,cr0,0` while r3 must NOT change.  *One word, one nibble apart, opposite answers: that is the statement that a printed CP6 MRC and an executed one are different events.*\n\nENCODING, derived twice rather than recalled: `MRC p6,0,r3,c0,c0,0` = **0xEE103610**, from ARM ARM DDI 0406C.d A8.8.108 (`ddi0406.txt:25478`) AND from the emulator's own field extractor at `cpu_arm.c:2645-2651`.  The two agree field for field.  General form `0xEE100610 | (crn<<16) | (rd<<12) | crm`.\n\nSIX INVOCATIONS, and the rule is stated rather than assumed: **one per terminating site, plus one per mutually-exclusive content of the subject slot.**  Arms E (`unknown:` at `:419`) and S (isrc write at `:333`) terminate in BOTH builds, so neither can share a process with anything downstream; D and L terminate pre-fix only; N and C are the same slot with different words.  **S is the blast-radius control: if it ever goes green the deletion was not scoped to one arm, and that is a FAIL.**\n\nFOUR OF SIX ARMS MUST BE BYTE-IDENTICAL ACROSS THE TWO BUILDS -- itself a measured statement of the diff's blast radius.\n\n(d) NULL-REPAIR: the fix adds no predicate, so the mutants reroute existing ones.  `if (l_bit)` -> `if (l_bit && cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten)` is caught by **V0 alone**, the reset read taken while the field is still 0 -- which is why V0 is in the program and was not dropped for brevity.  A kill re-added on a narrow `rd` is caught by the five distinct subject `rd` values {3,4,5,6,9}, `rd` being a floor axis because it indexes the guarded body's own lvalue.\n\nDISCLOSED (clause (e)): predicates on `opcode1`/`opcode2` -- every word here has both at 0, and the body never TESTS them (it only passes them to the `unknown:` printf), so they are not floor axes here.  Also disclosed: interrupt DELIVERY is unreachable from a stepping probe (`debugger.c:800`, `:835-840` stop the host timer), so this is necessarily a REGISTER-TRANSFER detector, not a behaviour detector.  And the `isteer`-nonzero kill at `:324` is the one remaining `crm 0` kill this detector does not observe.\n\nDISPOSITION UNCHANGED AND STRENGTHENED.  `:311` already computes the correct value before killing, and `dev_i80321.c:445-446` aliases the field into the interrupt controller (`d->enable`), read at `:86`/`:92` -- so it is live state a consumer reads, and an *\"unimplemented; ignored\"* message would be false in product code.  The sibling grep agrees: within `crm 0` the `crn 4` READ at `:318` is already a silent accept and the `crm 1` bank is silent both ways, so silencing `crn 0` makes the file MORE internally consistent.\n\nNO MMU PROLOGUE AND NO ARM T, deliberately: a coprocessor move touches no memory, so the only access is the instruction fetch at VA 0x8000, which `arm_setup_initial_translation_table(cpu, 0x4000)` maps identically.  **The probe header must say this in those words** -- a reader who knows `footbridge_sites_probe.py` will look for its arm T and must not conclude it was forgotten.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 ADVERSARIAL PASS 1.  **THE ANCHOR ADMISSION IS HALF FALSE, AND FINDING THE MISSED CHANNEL CONVERTS A DISCLOSED WEAKNESS INTO A CLOSED ONE.**  One FLOOR BREACH (L2) and one TOTAL ESCAPE were found; all seven encodings verified correct. ***\n\n**THE DEBUGGER HALF IS CONFIRMED, line by line:** `debugger_cmds.c:1662` rejects any coprocessor number outside 0-3 (`reg ,6` is refused); `cpu_arm.c:193-198` registers only `pc`, the GPRs and `cpsr`; `arm_cpu_register_dump` names no CP6 field; and `i80321_inten` appears in exactly three places in `src/` -- `cpu_arm_coproc.c:311,315`, `dev_i80321.c:446`, `cpu_arm.h:212`.  `DEVICE_ACCESS(i80321)` never touches `d->enable`, so there is **no MMIO alias**.\n\n*** BUT \"NO CHANNEL OUTSIDE THE DECODE UNDER TEST\" IS FALSE. ***\n\n    dev_i80321.c:446    d->enable = &cpu->cd.arm.i80321_inten;\n    dev_i80321.c:84-87  { *d->status |= linemask;\n                          if (*d->status & *d->enable) INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq); }\n\n**`i80321_assert()` is a SECOND READER of the exact cell, outside the CP6 decode, and its observable is the ARM IRQ line.  That is clause (f)'s \"side effect\" anchor, verbatim** -- and it is reachable SYNCHRONOUSLY inside one guest store: `machine_iq80321.c:61-64` wires the ns16550 as `irq=...i80321.28`, and `DEVICE_ACCESS(ns16550)` calls `dev_ns16550_tick()` inside the guest's own store for `com_ier` and `com_mcr`.  So `strb 0x02 -> 0xfe800001` then `strb 0x08 -> 0xfe800004` asserts line 28 and the `isrc & inten` test fires.\n\n**THE COST, stated honestly: the anchor arm must FREE-RUN.**  `cpu_dyntrans.c:205-209` suppresses the exception under `single_step`, and `irq_asserted` is in neither the `reg` dump nor the settings tree, so a stepping arm cannot see it.  Use the project's existing free-running shape: clear CPSR I, plant `b .`, breakpoint at **0x18** (`ARM_EXCEPTION_IRQ = 6`, and `ARM_CONTROL_V` is not in the reset set), `continue`.  Contrast row: `inten = 0` gives no exception and the `b .` spins to the stall budget.\n\n=== M1 -- A FLOOR BREACH (L2), AND IT IS THE ANSWER TO THE QUESTION I MOST WANTED ===\n\n    if (l_bit) { r[rd] = inten;\n                 if (cpu->cd.arm.i80321_isrc != 0) { fatal(...); exit(1); } }\n\n**`i80321_isrc` is 0 in every one of the six arms** -- nothing asserts an i80321 interrupt in a bare probe, and the only guest-reachable writer of `isrc` is the crn-8 MCR, which `exit(1)`s.  **On a real guest it is NEVER 0 at the moment INTCTL is read, because NetBSD reads INTCTL from inside the interrupt path.**  The host dies exactly as before and nothing in the six arms can see it.\n\nGraded L2 rather than clause-(e) debt under the pass-11 generating-set ruling: `inten`'s ONLY consumer is `i80321_assert`/`i80321_deassert`, which reads `*d->status & *d->enable`, so `isrc` and `inten` are a JOINT INPUT of the model, written by different paths.  The floor is `{l_bit} x {inten classes} x {isrc = 0, != 0}` and **the whole `isrc != 0` column is empty.**  *The same anchor arm closes it: asserting the serial gives `isrc = 1<<28`, readable back through `MRC p6,0,Rt,c8,c0,0` = `0xEE187610` -- the crn-8 LOAD has no `exit(1)` (`:328-329`), a fact the design did not use.*\n\n=== M5 -- A TOTAL ESCAPE, AND IT IS NOT A HOST KILL, WHICH MAKES IT WORSE ===\n\nSwap the cell on BOTH sides to `i80321_isteer`: reset read 0, three round-trips, L, N, C, E and S all GREEN -- **while the device's interrupt enable mask is never written and a guest's interrupts silently never fire.**  The design's own anchor admission conceded this was invisible; with the channel above found, it is closable.\n\n=== THREE MORE ESCAPES, EACH CLOSED BY ONE WORD ===\n* **M2 -- the detector's real bound is FOUR READS PER PROCESS.**  Arm D performs exactly four subject reads and L performs one, each in a fresh process, so a `static int nread; if (++nread > 4) exit(1);` escapes 6/6 while a guest polling INTCTL dies.  Repair: one more read in arm D.\n* **M3 -- the `rd` axis is not closed, and the manual says WHICH value matters.**  DDI 0406 A8.8.108: *\"`<Rt>` ... can be R0-R14 or, in some cases, APSR_nzcv\"* -- **Rt = R15 means write the CONDITION FLAGS**, and GXemul writes `r[15]`, the PC, unconditionally.  That is a real modelling defect, so a future `if (rd == ARM_PC) exit(1)` is likely.  `{3,4,5,6,9}` is five of sixteen and **omits the one value with an architectural special case**.  `0xEE10F610`.\n* **M4 -- `opcode1`/`opcode2` are ignored by the arm and CONSTANT across all six invocations.**  `if (opcode2 != 0) exit(1)` escapes 6/6 and re-opens the kill for 7/8 of the encoding space.  Close with `0xEE10A630`.\n\n=== CORRECTIONS TO THE DESIGN ===\n* **A FOURTH TERMINATING SITE IS UNROWED**: crn 4 STORE with a non-zero value (`:320-325`).  It is the subject's nearest sibling inside the same `case crm 0:` switch, and under SIBLING PARITY crn 4 is uncovered in BOTH directions -- including the silent-accept read at `:317-318` that the disposition argument leans on.\n* **L, N and C MERGE into one invocation** -- the rule binds only if the words must occupy the same address, and nothing requires that.  Six arms -> four, before the additions.\n* **Arm E is strengthened for free**: `0xEE107612` yields three ZERO fields, so a broken extractor still mostly matches.  **`0xEE79B6B2`** gives `opcode1=3 opcode2=5 crn=9 crm=2 rd=11 l=1` -- six distinct non-trivial fields, turning E into a decode-field anchor at zero cost.\n* **THE NOP WAS NAMED, NOT ENCODED.**  *\"the NOP (`mov r0,r0`)\"* is a LABEL; the word is `0xE1A00000`.  That is precisely this project's hand-assembled-encoding trap, recorded four times.\n* **MY NON-INTERFERENCE REASON WAS WRONG AND THE CONCLUSION SURVIVES.**  I said the tick cannot fire inside a `step` because its input is a `timer_add` callback.  **The tick DOES fire** -- `machine.c:354` initialises `ticks_till_next[n] = 0` and `:727-730` fires at `<= 0`, so every tick function runs on step 1 of every invocation.  What protects the cell is the ELSE branch: `tmr0` is 0 at reset, so the else writes `i80321_isrc` and `tisr` and never `inten`.  *This matters for the free-running anchor arm, which must not assume the tick is inert -- that same step-1 tick calls `INTERRUPT_DEASSERT` whenever `!(isrc & inten)`.*\n\n=== VERIFIED SOUND ===  All seven encodings confirmed against BOTH DDI 0406 A8.8.108/A8.8.99 and the emulator's own extractor, field for field, with three near-miss combiner traps cleared (`mia*`, `0xee070f9a`, `0x8afffffa`).  VA 0x8000 verified and **more robust than claimed** -- it holds even under `-Q`, because with the MMU off VA == PA.  *But the margin is ZERO: the L1 table ends at 0x7FFF and the code starts at 0x8000, so any literal pool below the code lands in the table.*  Disposition confirmed as option-set member **4 ACCEPT**, the DC21285 `+0x188` precedent exactly.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 -- WITNESS BUILT AND MEASURED (agy 3.8, scratch clone).  **THE ANCHOR ARM IS THE ONLY THING THAT KILLS M1 AND M5, MEASURED.** ***\n\n    witness, committed HEAD   SCORE 11/26   (15 rows red)\n    witness, with the fix     SCORE 26/26\n    BASE 26/26   RESTORED 26/26   restore verified BOTH parts (cmp CLEAN, binary FRESH)\n\n**THE KILL TABLE VINDICATES THE ADVERSARIAL PASS BY MEASUREMENT, which is the whole point of having built the anchor arm:**\n\n    M1        23/26   FAILING: ANCHOR_M1, ANCHOR_EXC, ANCHOR_CONTRAST\n    M5        25/26   FAILING: ANCHOR_EXC\n    M_OPCODE2 19/26   M_RD15 25/26   M_NREAD 19/26\n\n> **`M1` -- the floor breach -- dies on NOTHING BUT THE THREE ANCHOR ROWS.  `M5` -- the total escape that is not a host kill -- dies on EXACTLY ONE ROW, `ANCHOR_EXC`.**  Every stepping arm is green under both.  *So without the free-running anchor arm the six-arm design would have shipped with a floor breach and a total escape, exactly as the review predicted from reading alone.*\n\nThat is this project's *a reading seat buys the CLASS, a measuring seat buys the INSTANCE* rule with both halves present in one day: the review named `M1` and `M5` from the source and said the stepping arms could not see them; the table shows they are seen by three rows and one row respectively, and by nothing else.\n\n`M_OPCODE2` and `M_NREAD` each red the same seven D-arm rows (19/26), and `M_RD15` reds exactly `RD15_SURVIVE` (25/26) -- so the three cheap one-word additions the review prescribed each carry a measured kill rather than being precautionary.\n\n**ORDER: the PRETABLE discriminator HOLDS** -- `PRETABLE.out` at `02:18:05`, `build_BASE.log` at `02:20:51`, so the block was written 2m46s before table 1 and its own mtime proves it.\n\n*** BUT ONE ORDER CLAIM IS NOT EVIDENCED AND I WILL NOT ASSERT IT: whether the COMMITTED witness leg was run BEFORE the edit was applied. ***  `witness_fix.out` carries mtime `02:20:10` and `witness_committed.out` `02:20:23` -- **the fix leg is the EARLIER file** -- and the report does not address ordering anywhere (grep for order/test-first/before-any-edit returns nothing).\n\n**What IS established is weaker but real: the kill table's own `HEAD` leg checks out the committed source, rebuilds and probes, scoring 11/26.**  So a pre-fix measurement exists and is reproducible; what is missing is evidence that it was taken FIRST, which is what witness-ladder clause (i) asks for.  *This project's oracle-correction rule exists because a measurement taken after the edit cannot be distinguished from one fitted to it -- and the two mtimes here are the only thing that speaks to the question, and they point the wrong way.*\n\n**OWED: either a statement from the run that the committed leg preceded the edit, or a re-run of the committed leg from a clean checkout with its mtime recorded.**  Cheap either way, and it is the difference between a witness and a detector in this project's own vocabulary.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PROPAGATION CHECK (two-part clearance, RUN not asserted).  **ITS SEC EDIT IS IN NO BUILD TREE, AND ALL THREE BINARIES REPORT `FRESH`.** ***\n\n`cpu_arm_coproc.c` carries this row's uncommitted SEC edit (2 lines removed).  The clearance was run against all three build trees and `est/`:\n\n    tree               part 1 (cmp vs SEC)   part 2 [binary -nt source]\n    build/             DIVERGENT             FRESH   (binary 00:48:13)\n    build-asan/        DIVERGENT             FRESH   (binary 00:51:01)\n    /tmp/gxsec-build   DIVERGENT             FRESH   (binary 00:51:42)\n    est/               SOURCE tree -- no binary exists, part 2 N/A (inapplicable, not unrun)\n\n*The other three edited files in the same working tree -- `cpu_ppc_instr.c`, `dev_lca.c`, `dev_sgi_re.c` -- are CLEAN in all three, so this is a single missed propagation rather than a tree-wide drift.*\n\n> *** THE DANGEROUS PART IS THAT PART 2 GIVES NO WARNING: all three binaries are NEWER than their sources, so `FRESH` is TRUE and MEANINGLESS -- the binary is newer than a source that is simply the wrong source. ***  **Part 2 tests staleness against whatever source sits in the tree; only part 1 tests whether that is the source you think it is.**  A round that ran only the freshness half here would measure PRE-FIX ARM coprocessor behaviour and see nothing wrong.\n\n**None of the four files is in the divergence set** (derived, 6 paths), so the clearance genuinely applies and `DIVERGENT` here means UNPROPAGATED rather than by-design.\n\n*** NOT PROPAGATED IN THIS PASS, DELIBERATELY. ***  Propagating without rebuilding is the trap this project has paid for twice, and a build was in flight (agy, `wdcaltdigin` witness).  **Propagate and rebuild together, then re-run BOTH parts** -- recorded in `_scratchpad/CHECKPOINT.md` with the restore recipe so a fresh session finds it.\n\n*Found by running the clearance at a round boundary rather than at a mutation boundary -- which is the carrier's own finding that the two-part clearance is a ROUND-BOUNDARY obligation and that the sentinel discipline cannot catch this class at all, because nothing mutated anything.*",
