GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:45729:     "note": "REPAIR 13 + KILL TABLE 11, 33 legs, every one ending CRIMEP_DONE.  **THE CLOSING-PASS-7 GAP IS CLOSED AND MEASURED**: K1 187/188, K2 186/188, K3 187/188 and E2 186/188 -- all four were 157/157 ESCAPES before this repair.  **THE ENUMERATION VERIFIES ITSELF, 28 of 28**: 27 generated closure cells killed at their own (len, direction, label) cell, and ONE -- C_ctl4r -- at 188/188, which is the CONTRACT'S OWN NEGATIVE CONTROL PASSING rather than an escape, because the contract lists CONTROL 4R among the EQUIVALENT cells (reads 0 either way).  A closure emitting only kills would never have tested its own equivalence claim.  *** MY PER-ROW PREDICTION FOR K2 WAS WRONG AND THE MEASUREMENT IS RIGHT: PRETABLE predicted four `p revP * pc' rows, it reds TWO (sw, lw), because K2 sits on the `len == 4' ARM and the 8-byte sd/ld cannot reach it. I wrote the prediction from the CELL without carrying the WIDTH the mutant is gated on.  K1/K3/E2 matched exactly. ***  THREE legs carry CRIMEP_CONTROL=FAIL (C_ist4r, C_imk4r, C_tim8r); in each the red rows ARE the cell's own channel so every kill is ATTRIBUTABLE, and the GATE is unaffected because it pins CONTROL=OK on BASE, which is 188/188 OK.  What the aggregate cannot do is separate `the mutant moved a control row' from `this session was invalid' -- FILED as an instrument note, not fixed, since a round takes one site.  Targeting rule: adds-only repair, no existing session's command list changed, so no earlier kill re-run.  Pins 157 -> 188; the score-claims checker caught nine stale claims in the new contract text (seven got era tags, TWO were false N/M parses of a hex value and a run count and were rephrased rather than tagged)."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:45769:     "note": "CLOSES -- `zero surviving latch-SET clean-sweeps in the 28-cell denominator' -- i.e. it holds the floor to the latch-SET half, which is exactly the branch glm and codex dispute.  FOUR records findings, all correct: PRETABLE 11 promised FOUR negative controls and the table could run ONE (`mutants529k.py' has a single equivalent-cell member; the other three were close-7 PROTOTYPE legs); the HOST-DIAGNOSTIC parenthetical names THREE cells against a count of FIVE and its `rather than rowed' is stale now that `p acc ctl msg' counts the CONTROL writes; the brief's WHY block still described pass 7; and *** the FROZEN table-11 instrument still heads the block `DRAFT -- NOT APPLIED' while the live probe was corrected later the same day -- the one finding whose correct disposition is to change NOTHING, since a frozen instrument edited after its run can no longer re-derive its own scores.  Recorded in CORRECTIONS.md. ***  NOTE ON SEAT HEALTH: sampled at 950 bytes mid-answer and finished at 8314 -- the sample-twice rule working."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:45819:     "note": "INTERIM, and **IT DECLINED TO ISSUE A VERDICT ON A THIRD OF THE EVIDENCE** -- the provisional-adjudication rule applied by a seat to itself, which is the right call.  8 of 14 Table A legs scored; its own check_pred8.py reports PREDICTIONS MATCHED 8, WRONG 0; every leg ends CRIMEP_DONE with CONTROL=OK; PREDICT.md (20:04:24) and PREDICT2.md both predate the first build log (20:07:03), so the mtimes prove the order.  *** THE ENUMERATION NAMES FOUR AXES AND THE HANDLER HAS SIX: `relative_addr & 4' (the lane, tested twice inside the len == 4 arm) and `cpu->pc' (the PROM window) are named nowhere.  BOTH MEASURED CLOSED -- the four default-arm lane splits are killed on exactly the rows it predicted, and `W_rev8r_out' 187/188 reddening `p rev ld' ONLY is the POSITIVE CONTROL that makes the two 188/188 window legs mean EQUIVALENCE rather than `nothing looked'; three clean sweeps without it would have proved nothing.  So both are RECORDS defects, not floor gaps. ***  *** THE REAL FINDING IS THE LATCH-CLEAR HALF AND IT IS BIGGER THAN glm SAW: EIGHT of the 46 ACCEPTS entries are driven latch-CLEAR by `latch x5' ALONE, and that session's only three rows are two announce counts and a RAM round trip -- all blind to a silent decline.  Open guest-visible cells: INTSTAT 4R, TIME 4W, TIME 4R.  glm named ONE member; the class has FOUR.  It is the K-class's own argument with CLEAR and SET exchanged, and the contract's amended `catching row runs in the bypass's own latch state' clause fails for them BY ITS OWN WORDS. ***  Second and independent: codex's cell is a GENERATING-SET breach -- every post-latch CONTROL write carries HARD_RESET clear, and the only 0x800 write is pre-latch.  All mutants plus the owed rows (t4clear.py, ctlreset.py) are BUILT AND QUEUED for Table B, where the verdict turns.  It also CORRECTED THE C_ctl4r REASON -- the cell IS driven post-latch by ACCEPTS entry (\"lw\", 0x08, 0) in session 26, by the one session that cannot observe it, so deepseek was right in conclusion and wrong in detail -- and found that killtable11 had NO check_pred.py while PRETABLE 11 claimed one (now written and run: 33 legs, CHECK_PRED11_PASS).  It discharged the P6 register-collision audit MECHANICALLY across all 51 sessions: zero printed registers written by more than one load.  *** STOPPED BY THE OWNER ~20:5x; NO VERDICT ISSUED.  Table B -- where the verdict turns -- NEVER RAN, so closing pass 8 is INCOMPLETE, not negative.  The seat woke, diagnosed the SIGTERM independently before touching anything (a killed wrapper does not imply killed work), and its record and the coordinator's agree on every material fact.  Safe at that moment for a specific reason: Table B is the one that mutates the clone and drops a sentinel, and it had not begun.  Verified: 0 processes, all EIGHT trees no-sentinel / p1 CLEAN / p2 FRESH.  *** FOUR LOGS ARE TRUNCATED AND MUST NEVER BE CITED -- probe_G_lane_all/_ist/_tim and probe_W_rev4r_out stop at 123 lines with NO CRIMEP_DONE, each ending mid-table on an `ok' row, so each LOOKS like a healthy partial result; only tail -1 distinguishes it.  check_pred8.py is safe by construction (it reads lane files, written only after a probe RETURNS) and reports them NOT RUN rather than scoring a partial log. ***  8 legs survive WITH sentinels and are real evidence.  Three owed rows are already BUILT for the resume: t4clear.py (latch-CLEAR gapped TIME), ctlreset.py (HARD_RESET generating set) and ctl4r.py (the post-latch 4-byte CONTROL read whose ABSENCE made C_ctl4r vacuous -- the direct answer to deepseek).  Resuming means RE-RUNNING, not rebuilding.  Full record: STOPPED.md beside the seat's own unedited RESUME.md.  *** AND A NEW SHAPE OF THE CHECK-THAT-CANNOT-FAIL, found while verifying this stop: `ps -ef | grep -c \"[g]xemul\"' returned 1 WITH NOTHING RUNNING -- the bracket trick stops GREP self-matching, but ps prints the `wsl -- bash -c' wrapper's argv, which carries the literal pattern.  READ THE MATCHED LINES, NEVER THE COUNT.  Third distinct form of this trap here: ps -ef is argument-blind for script names (false NEGATIVE, always), pgrep -f matches its own wrapper (false positive), and a bracket-tricked grep -c counts the wrapper (false positive). ***"
