GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:46209:     "note": "PRETABLE FOR REPAIR 14's OWN TABLE (table 12) IS WRITTEN, and its mtime precedes every artifact the table will produce, because none of them exists yet. All four mandated checks are discharged as OUTPUT rather than assertion, including GUARD-FIELD vs BODY-FIELD recorded N/A WITH ITS REASON -- repair 14 edits the probe only, adds no predicate and removes no guard, so there is nothing to compare; an unrun check and an inapplicable one must not render the same. *** THE BLOCK EARNED ITS KEEP BEFORE THE TABLE RAN, WHICH IS THE ARGUMENT FOR MANDATING IT: *** drafting the predicted red rows is what exposed that nothing in the repair moved under either HARD_RESET mutant. THE BASE LEG MUST REPORT 204/204 -- derived as 188 + 16 by counting row() sites with loop templates expanded, PREDICTED here rather than asserted, and it doubles as the check that nothing was added twice (entry 14 adds no row; it moves a count from 3 to 4). *** TARGETING IS NOT ADDS-ONLY AND THE BLOCK SAYS SO: four EXISTING sessions gained accesses, so the targeting rule does NOT exempt the earlier tables' kills and every leg is re-run. *** THE MOST IMPORTANT LINE IN IT IS A PREDICTED FAILURE: M3_timelatch and M5_revlatch are predicted to STILL ESCAPE at 204/204, disclosed in advance rather than discovered afterwards, because their carriers are deliberately not in this diff -- Fable requires them to observe the SUPPRESSED arm and the measuring seat needed four mutants to find that face at all. The probe contract says so in as many words, so they are outside the contract as stated rather than breaches. A pass that predicted only successes would be the one to distrust. Also recorded as owed to the base leg and not settleable statically: six row-name literals are format templates, and whether every expansion is DISTINCT at runtime needs a run -- a duplicate name does not fail loudly, it makes a gate grep ambiguous."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:46218:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/crimewidthkill/repair14_20260916/check_pred.py written 03:04:51, after PRETABLE.out at 02:55:23 and before any driver or log exists",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:46219:     "note": "THE MECHANICAL SCORER FOR TABLE 12 IS WRITTEN **BEFORE** THE TABLE, which is the whole correction: kill table 11 shipped WITHOUT one -- its PRETABLE claimed a checker that did not exist -- so its predictions were compared BY EYE, and that is how its K2 prediction error survived the run and had to be found by reading afterwards. A claimed check that cannot fail is worse than none because it reassures. *** IT DISTINGUISHES A DISCLOSED ESCAPE FROM A BREACH, WHICH IS WHAT CLAUSE (e) IS FOR AND WHICH NO EARLIER CHECKER IN THIS CAMPAIGN DID. *** M3_timelatch and M5_revlatch are PREDICTED to escape at 204/204 -- their carriers are deliberately absent, the probe contract names them, and Fable scoped repair 14 to the accept side -- so the checker reports them as DISCLOSED, not as failures. An UNPREDICTED 204/204 on a mutant repair 14 was written to close is a breach and fails. *** AND IT FAILS IN THE OTHER DIRECTION TOO, which is the part I would not have thought to add a week ago: if a DISCLOSED escape turns out to be KILLED, that is also reported -- because the contract would then be WRONG IN THE SAFE DIRECTION, and a contract that understates coverage is still a wrong record. *** It also carries three guards from defects this campaign actually hit: the FAIL match is ANCHORED to line-end and excludes CRIMEP_ tokens, because an unanchored grep over probe output counts summary lines as rows (it did exactly that on table 7 an hour ago, returning 33 and 67 for 32 and 66); the denominator is checked against 204 so a leg run on the wrong instrument cannot score; and the tally is cross-checked against the extracted red count so a broken extraction cannot pass silently. A MUST_STAY_KILLED set guards the reverse regression -- a leg that was killed before repair 14 and is clean after it."
