GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:46238:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/crimewidthkill/repair14_20260916/kill529r14.sh; PRETABLE.out 02:55:23, check_pred.py 03:04:51, mutants_r14.py, driver last -- the mandated order proven by mtime",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:46239:     "note": "KILL TABLE 12 IS FULLY STAGED AND CAN FIRE THE MOMENT THE MACHINE IS FREE. FIFTEEN legs across FOUR frozen mutators, and the map was DERIVED from each mutator rather than assumed -- which mattered, because mutants14.py does NOT know M5_revlatch or M6_istlatch: those came from the ADJUDICATION seat's own run and live in a separate mutants_adj.py. A driver written from memory would have dispatched two legs to a mutator that has never heard of them, and the failure would have looked like a build error rather than a map error. Verified 15/15 on a read-only dry run, and the driver RE-VERIFIES the map AT RUN TIME before touching the clone -- with a fallback that greps the mutator's own dict, because the two older mutators predate the list action and their silence must not read as a wrong dispatch. IT CARRIES THE GUARDS THIS CAMPAIGN PAID FOR: a clone-local sentinel dropped before the first mutation and cleared only after cmp-clean AND binary-newer-than-source; a restored leg compared byte-for-byte against base; an EQUIVALENT-MUTANT warning when a mutant binary matches base; and a hunk-for-hunk CROSS-CHECK of every rebuilt edit against the diff the earlier passes preserved, because a re-derivation that silently differs would make the whole table a measurement of something else -- the check table 7 is currently passing on every leg. *** AND IT CARRIES AN EXPLICIT REFUSAL TO RACE #530: a header note saying not to start while table 7 runs, with the reason -- this table's gapped rows measure a clock advance across the debugger's own pause, and this project has a recorded gate failure from two concurrent CPU-heavy jobs. *** The staging order is now complete and mtime-proven throughout: PRETABLE, then the mechanical checker, then the missing mutator, then the driver, and no log or build for this table exists yet."
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:46265:     "grader": "grok 4.6 xhigh, the OWED repair-14 pass 2; verified against logs/probe_*.raw (each ends CRIMEP_DONE), the mutant text, PRETABLE.out and check_pred.py",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:46267:     "note": "*** THE PASS DOES NOT CLOSE #529, AND IT REJECTED BOTH READINGS I OFFERED. ***  I asked whether 'killed on their named rows' means AT LEAST ONE or ALL.  grok named a third thing: SPLIT KILL FROM CHANNEL.  'At least one' is the check-that-cannot-fail -- naming all 204 rows would then pass -- and it is the reading that lets me close, so it is refused.  check_pred.py ALREADY implements the third thing (exact set equality), so A PREDICTION MISS IS L4 OF PRETABLE, A WRONG RECORD, FIXED IN THE RECORD AND NOT BY ADDING ROWS.  Q1: G_lane_ctl's sole-carrier at 203/204 is NOT a floor breach.  Q3: my grade (completeness defect, not floor) upheld -- but my REASONING was refuted: I wrote 'whatever it scored would have matched' and check_pred.py puts M1_gotolatch in MUST_STAY_KILLED, so a 204/204 would have printed REGRESSION and CHECK_PRED12_FAIL.  The cannot-fail part is the SET, not the KILL.  *** THE UNASKED FINDING IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS: `acc ctl0c' NEVER REDS UNDER ANY OF THE FIFTEEN LEGS, including the equivalent-cell decline the PAIR exists to observe -- so the DISCRIMINATING half of the pair carries no measured kill.  A row is not discharged by existing.  Do NOT add a row; run a decline that reaches 0x0c, or stop claiming ctl0c as the channel. ***  And a FALSE CONTRACT CLAIM, verified in dev_sgi_ip32.c before recording: the default: arm is NOT host-log-only for acc ctl0c -- the width arm runs BEFORE the switch and 0x0c/8 == 0x08/8, so a 4-byte read takes CONTROL's LOW half and a decline answers 0.  Guest-visible.  Latent hazard recorded though it did not fire: check_pred.py still slices ln[:15], the same padded-column family as #530's bug."
