GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:46604:   "state_note": "*** STATUS 2026-10-02 (GXEMUL [087a90]): CLOSED by #529.  The fix is unchanged since 2026-09-13 (dev_sgi_ip32.c 8c1d1545); the detector is repair 16's 226 rows with the F2 adjudication's text (r17), GPT-6 Astra's r17 audit and the measured ACCEPTS-46 enumeration (r18), one sentence from Astra's second records audit (r19), the flagship's final ruling (r20) and Astra's confirmation of it (r21) and one docstring qualifier the precommit hook required (r22, the committed text); the chain closed on repair 16's F2 pass, and the final ruling of 2026-09-30 was confirmed the same day with two text dispositions overturned.  The note below said \"NOTHING MEASURED ... Next: fire table 12\" -- stale from 2026-09-17 08:53, when table 12 completed. ***\n\nrepair 14 FULLY STAGED -- probe edits, contract, PRETABLE, check_pred.py, mutants_r14.py and a map-verified 15-leg driver, all ordered before any run. Three checkers green. NOTHING MEASURED; #530 table 7 still holds the machine, deliberately unraced. Next: fire table 12, then the INTMASK/CONTROL-write swaps Fable requires MEASURED, then the decline carriers",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:46780:     "note": "#568 F2: PROCEED WITH CHANGES -- GLM 5.3: overturned two of its own positions (D3, D4); `vbr reset` sat wrongly in the CONTROL list; three PRETABLE pins named.",
GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/ledger.json:47709:   "note": "*** 2026-09-12, codex farm: the promotion was NOT done -- regress/ppc_rlwimi_probe.py was a byte-identical copy of the witness (cmp).  Promoted per codex's design and REVIEW (regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth/codex_promote*_20260912.txt, codex_promoreview_20260912.txt; ppcrldimimask/codex_promote_20260912.txt): regress/ppc_rldimi_probe.py (27 rows) with RESULT/CONTROL/DONE lines, a private loader stub, census wants pinned to MEASURED literals (they had been derived from the lists they count), and target-instruction CONTROL rows (without them a no-op instruction still read CONTROL=OK).  gate_ppc.sh sections designed to run BEFORE the NetBSD prerequisite with a strict parse -- PARKED at _scratchpad/gate_ppc.ppcround.sh (two new pins would raise SCORE_CLAIMS 25 -> 27) and MANIFEST entries parked (_scratchpad/ppc_manifest.py apply).  ALL UNMEASURED.  OWED: run both, re-table against frozen copies (rldimi M_NOCIRC by name), gate negative controls. *** [2026-10-18, at the #527 closure (Grok 4.7): DONE before #527 committed -- kill table 3 (killtable3_20260912/), the gate log (gate_ppc_20260912.log, PASS 110 checks) and the gate's eleven negative-control cases, ten expected to FAIL and one GOOD baseline (gate_negctl_20260912.out).  History, not open debt.]\n\nFILED 2026-09-10 from the [[ppcrldiclzero]] witness's sibling audit.  MEASURED, not read.\n\nThe handler rotates with a `while (sh-- != 0)' loop, which ALWAYS exits with sh == -1 because the post-decrement runs once more than the body.  The mask loop then terminates on a condition involving 63 - sh, i.e. 64 -- always.  The architecture requires the mask to END AT 63-SH (the rldimix RTL and its prose, '1 bits from bit MB through bit 63 - SH').\n\nMEASURED with the destination seeded to 0 and an all-ones source:\n    SH=8    got 0xffffffffffffffff   want 0xffffffffffffff00\n    SH=32   got 0xffffffffffffffff   want 0xffffffff00000000\n    SH=0    got 0xffffffffffffffff   want 0xffffffffffffffff   -- the one safe case\n\n**This is the standard bitfield INSERT.**  A compiler using it to splice a field destroys everything to the right of that field.  Live for every SH != 0.\n\n*** THE DURABLE REPAIR IS NOT TO PATCH THE 63 - sh EXPRESSION. ***  That loop idiom is shared by rldicl, rldicr AND rldimi; rldicr survives only because it never reads sh again.  **Any future edit that reaches for sh after the rotate loop in any of the three will be wrong the same way.**  Save sh before the loop instead.\n\nSame reachability and the same gate blind spot as [[ppcrldiclzero]]: 64-bit CPUs only, and no gate instantiates one.\n\n*** DESIGN REVIEW 2026-09-10 (ppcrldimimask/codex_design_20260910.txt, GPT-6 Astra at max, 14,947 bytes, ZERO cyber flags): THE DEFECT PREMISE IS EXACT AND **MY PROPOSED REPAIR IS INSUFFICIENT**. ***\n\nThe post-decrement reading is confirmed exactly: the loop's final test evaluates the old value zero and then decrements to -1, **and this happens even for SH=0**, so the mask always covers architectural bits MB..63.  The rldimix RTL requires MASK(MB, 63-SH).\n\n**BUT 'SAVE sh BEFORE THE LOOP AND KEEP THE MASK LOOP' -- which I filed as the durable repair -- LEAVES TWO MORE DEFECTS.**  Both come from the manual's own mask definition, which makes BOTH endpoints inclusive and WRAPS through bit 63 to bit 0 when the beginning exceeds the end:\n  (1) the existing loop compares the INCREMENTED position against the inclusive endpoint, so where it terminates normally it **stops one bit early**;\n  (2) it **never wraps**, so other operand combinations run past bit 63 and evaluate invalid shift counts -- an undefined-behaviour defect hiding inside the wrong-value one.\n\nTHE PRESCRIBED REPAIR is therefore four coordinated changes rather than one: preserve the decoded `sh', rotate with a separate counter (`int n = sh; while (n-- != 0)'), advance the mask position modulo 64 (`m = (m + 1) & 63'), and terminate on the position AFTER the endpoint, modulo 64 (`m != ((64 - sh) & 63)').\n\n*A round that had taken my one-line framing would have shipped a fix that is still wrong at the endpoint and still undefined on the wrapping cases, and its detector -- built from the same framing -- would very likely have passed it.*\n\n*** THE GATE CLAIM WAS WRONG AND IS CORRECTED HERE.  I WROTE IT INTO THREE ROWS, A CHANGELOG BLOCK AND TWO COMMIT MESSAGES BEFORE A SEAT CHECKED IT (wiringcliff/codex_ppcgate_20260910.txt). ***\n\n**I CLAIMED: 'no gate in this tree instantiates a 64-bit PowerPC CPU at all, so the whole 64-bit-only instruction group is invisible BY CONSTRUCTION.'  THE FIRST HALF IS FALSE.**  Gate 9 (gate_asan_sweep.sh) enumerates EVERY advertised machine type and subtype -- including bareppc, testppc and macppc/g5, all of which default to the PPC970 -- so 64-bit PowerPC CPUs ARE constructed by a gate today, and have been all along.\n\n**THE CORRECT AND NARROWER FINDING: NO GATE CHECKS THEIR INSTRUCTION RESULTS.**  Gate 9 is a CONSTRUCTION check with no PowerPC result oracle; the two PowerPC gates both drive `macppc -e g4', an MPC7400, which is 32-bit, and their probes select it EXPLICITLY rather than by default.  So the defects were invisible because nothing EXECUTES these instructions and inspects the answer -- not because nothing builds the CPU.\n\n*** THAT DISTINCTION IS THIS PROJECT'S OWN WITNESS LADDER, ONE LEVEL UP, AND I WALKED PAST IT. ***  The ladder's whole rung-2 clause says machine CONSTRUCTION proves presence and NOT reachability, and is a reproduction only for defects that construction itself executes.  I applied that rule carefully to witnesses all day and then made exactly the error it warns about when reasoning about GATES.  A gate that constructs a CPU is a rung-2 gate; these defects need a rung-3 one.\n\nTHE PRESCRIBED CLOSURE IS SMALLER THAN MY FRAMING SUGGESTED: wire the existing 19-row regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py into gate_ppc.sh, asserting its score AND its completion sentinel.  That closes the missing instruction-result check.  **It does NOT close all 64-bit PowerPC coverage**, and the seat says so explicitly rather than letting the round claim it does.\n\n*** DESIGNED 2026-09-10 (ppcrldimimask/codex_repair_20260910.txt, GPT-6 Astra at max, ZERO cyber flags).  MY PREMISES HELD AND MY CHARACTERISATION OF THE DEFECT WAS HALF OF IT. ***\n\nCONFIRMED: `sh' is `-1' on exit for EVERY decoded SH including zero (the final test reads the old value, decrements, and exits), the rotate body IS a correct ROTL64, and the mask loop therefore always stops at `m == 64' and selects bits `MB..63'.  The architectural mask is `MASK(MB, 63-SH)', INCLUSIVE of both endpoints and WRAPPING -- RTL at `ppc_pem_32and64_2005.txt:29908' (`m <- MASK(b, ~n)'), endpoint prose at `:29909', mask definition at `:6879'.\n\n*** THE ROW'S TITLE DESCRIBES ONLY THE NON-WRAPPING HALF.  The defect has TWO directions: ***\n  * `MB <= 63-SH' (non-wrapping) -- it OVERWRITES the low SH destination bits that must survive;\n  * `MB > 63-SH' (wrapping) -- it OMITS the required insertion into positions `0..63-SH', leaving\n    those destination bits UNCHANGED.\nA repair tested only on non-wrapping encodings fixes one and leaves the other.\n\n**AND SAVING SH IS NOT ENOUGH -- THREE MORE EDITS ARE NEEDED, which is why my sketch was rejected.**  The comparison happens AFTER the increment, so the stopping position is the endpoint's SUCCESSOR, and the counter must be circular:\n\n        int m, n = sh;                       /*  1: keep SH  */\n        while (n-- != 0) { ... }             /*  2: consume the copy  */\n        do {\n                tmp |= ((uint64_t)1 << (63-m));\n                m = (m + 1) & 63;            /*  3: circular increment  */\n        } while (m != ((64 - sh) & 63));     /*  4: successor, modulo 64  */\n\n**Changing only one of edits 3 and 4 does not repair the loop.**  A partial repair that saves SH and sets the bound to 24 at `MB=40, SH=40' while keeping `m ++' walks 40..63 and then enters the body with `m == 64', evaluating `(uint64_t)1 << -1'.  *** That is UNDEFINED BEHAVIOUR, and the seat declined to call it an infinite loop on the ground that doing so would assign DEFINED behaviour to an undefined program -- which is the correct discipline and is what my brief's question got wrong. ***\n\nWORKED CASE, usable as a row: `rldimi r4,r3,40,40' = instruction word **0x7864422e**, with `r3 = 0xffffffffffffffff' and `r4 = 0': the repaired loop visits 40..63 then 0..23, 48 iterations, mask **0xffffff0000ffffff**, which is also the correct result.  **The original handler produces 0x0000000000ffffff.**\n\nMUTANT, one character, defined result: `64` -> `63` in the repaired bound.  `rldimi r4,r3,0,0' = **0x7864000c**, `r3 = ~0', `r4 = 0' gives correct `0xffffffffffffffff' and mutant `0xfffffffffffffffe' -- it drops the inclusive endpoint, architectural bit 63.\n\nThe seat validated its replacement against an independent bit-membership oracle over **40,960 cases** (every SH/MB pair x ten source/destination pairs), max loop count 64.  **It could NOT compile or run the emulator** -- no native compiler, and WSL returned `E_ACCESSDENIED' -- so this is a READING plus an offline model, not a measurement of this tree.  A measuring seat still owes the build.\n\n**BLOCKER PARTLY CLEARED 2026-09-10:** [[ppcwitnessmaskoracle]]'s `want_rldimi' is corrected -- it was returning an all-zero mask for **2016 of 4096** (MB,SH) pairs, and the manual's own `:6882' (*\"It is not possible to specify an all-zero mask\"*) makes that a flat contradiction rather than a disputed reading.  `mask32' is deliberately left alone pending [[ppcrlwhighhalf]].  A wrapping-encoding row measured RED on the committed build is still owed before either repair is scored.\n\n**THE ORACLE BLOCKER IS CLEARED, 2026-09-11.**  [[ppcwitnessmaskoracle]]'s two corrected helpers were verified against PowerPC Book I -- endpoints, wrap direction, the `+32` offsets, the `& 63`, the all-zero-mask assertion's normative standing, and both partition counts.  **PASS.**  A measuring seat can now score this round without the oracle being the thing that is wrong.\n\n*** MEASURED AND REPAIRED 2026-09-11 BY THE agy SEAT (owner-approved use of the measurement reserve).  WITNESS TEST-FIRST, FOUR-EDIT REPAIR APPLIED, KILL TABLE WITH **ZERO ESCAPES**. ***\n\n**THE WITNESS IS TEST-FIRST AND THE ORDER IS PROVEN BY MTIME**, not asserted: `opus_ppc_rldimi_witness_20260911.py` and `witness_committed.out` at 23:02, `witness_fix.out` at 23:06, the repair after.\n\n    committed build (HEAD)   17/27   -- 10 failing rows, 431 of 495 census cells wrong\n    repaired build           27/27   -- 0 failing rows, 0 of 495 census cells wrong\n\n**THE REPAIR IS THE FOUR-EDIT FORM, BYTE-FOR-BYTE AS DESIGNED AND AS I VERIFIED IT OFFLINE OVER ALL 4096 (MB,SH) PAIRS** -- `int m, n = sh;` / `while (n-- != 0)` / `m = (m + 1) & 63;` / `while (m != ((64 - sh) & 63));`.\n\n*** KILL TABLE, `killtable_20260911/SCORES.txt`, frozen instrument, one clone, sentinel dropped before mutant 1 and CLEARED ONLY AFTER BOTH CLEARANCE PARTS (source `cmp` CLEAN, binary FRESH): ***\n\n    BASE           27/27      M_NOKEEP_SH    16/27\n    M_ORIG         17/27      M_NOCIRC       19/27\n    M_MUT63        14/27      M_NO_MERGE     24/27\n    M_NOWRAP       21/27      HEAD           17/27\n    RESTORED       27/27\n\n**ZERO ESCAPES, AND EACH OF THE FOUR EDITS HAS ITS OWN MUTANT AND EACH IS KILLED** -- `M_NOKEEP_SH` for edits 1-2, `M_NOCIRC` for edit 3, `M_MUT63` and `M_NOWRAP` for edit 4.  *That is the sole-killer discipline applied to a repair's parts rather than to a detector's rows, and it is what makes \"four coordinated edits\" a measured claim rather than a description.*\n\n**TWO CONTROLS WORTH NAMING:** `HEAD` scores exactly `M_ORIG`'s 17/27 with the identical failing set, confirming the committed build IS the defect and the mutator reproduces it; and `M_NO_MERGE` reds ONLY the three preservation rows (`pres nowrap`, `pres wrap`, `pres sh0`), which is those rows earning their place.\n\n*** APPARATUS NOTE, AND IT IS THE REASON THIS REPORT NEARLY WENT UNREAD: agy's `--print-timeout 30m` EXPIRED WHILE ITS BACKGROUND TASKS WERE STILL RUNNING. ***  The print session returned 8.8 KB of progress narration and no report, with stderr reading *\"root agent idle; waiting for 2 background task(s)\"*.  **The report existed on disk the whole time** -- `opus_measure_20260911.md`, 13 KB -- and was found only by looking in the artifacts directory rather than at the seat's stdout.  *A build-plus-kill-table does not fit a 30-minute print budget; size the timeout to the job, and when an agy run ends with background tasks outstanding, GO AND READ THE ARTIFACTS.  Its stderr is the tell and it is not the documented 111-byte quota wall.*\n\nSTILL OWED BEFORE THIS SHIPS: **pass 2 on the diff**, per the repair-chain rule -- one pass per floor-bound diff, closing on the first pass that finds zero breaches.\n\n*** PASS 2 (PARTIAL -- ONE SEAT, AND THE MISS IS NAMED): NO INCORRECT RESULT FOUND, ZERO FLOOR BREACHES, TWO CONCRETE CLAUSE-(e) ITEMS. ***  `ppcrldimimask/codex_pass2_20260911.txt`.\n\n**THE INERT-REPAIR CHECK PASSES, AND IT WAS THE ONE I MOST WANTED.**  Yesterday an adopted design in this same file turned out inert because a loop mutated the variable its guard tested before the guard ran.  Here the dataflow is `decode sh -> initialise n from sh -> decrement n during rotation -> read the ORIGINAL sh in the mask bound`, with **no intervening assignment, increment, decrement or address escape**.  The bound depends on the preserved `sh`, not the exhausted `n`.  **The repair is ACTIVE.**\n\n**TERMINATION IS PROVED FOR EVERY ENCODING, including the case I flagged.**  At `(MB,SH) = (0,0)` the bound is `0` and the mask loop runs **exactly 64 times** -- a full circuit, which is correct, because `MASK(0,63)` is all-ones and a `do/while` treats initial equality as a complete circuit rather than zero iterations.  Generally the loop runs 1..64 times, the increment stays in `0..63`, and no mask-bit shift has an invalid count.\n\n*** TWO CLAUSE-(e) ITEMS, BOTH CONCRETE, AND THE FIRST IS THE KIND A VALUE ORACLE CANNOT SEE BY CONSTRUCTION: ***\n  1. **A UB rewrite that produces every expected value.**  Replacing the rotation loop with `s = (s << sh) | (s >> (64 - sh));` invokes undefined behaviour at `SH = 0`, where the right shift has count 64 -- **and a compiler that recognises the idiom and emits a hardware rotate yields every correct test value anyway.**  *A value-checking instrument cannot distinguish correct output from undefined behaviour that happens to produce correct output.*\n  2. **Register aliasing.**  Computing the mask first and delaying the source load until after the destination is cleared preserves behaviour when `RA != RS` and breaks `RA == RS`.  That is the same aliasing axis named for the `rlwinm` work, and it applies here too.\n\n**AND A COVERAGE QUALIFICATION ON MY OWN TABLE:** `M_NOKEEP_SH` removes edits 1 and 2 TOGETHER, so that leg does **not** establish separate runtime kills for the two textual changes.  My claim that *\"each of the four edits has its own mutant\"* is therefore true of edits 3 and 4 and loose about 1 and 2.\n\n*** THIS IS A PARTIAL PASS AND I AM RECORDING IT AS ONE. ***  It was a SINGLE SEAT, fired because the measuring seat was mid-build on another round and a nine-seat panel would have contended with it.  The repair-chain rule closes a chain on the first pass finding zero breaches **provided the pass is valid -- roster complete or its misses named**.  The misses are named here: eight seats unfired.  **The round owes its full panel before it ships**, and the seat itself noted it was not given the probe rows and so cannot claim the instrument lacks alias checks.\n\n*** CORRECTION 2026-09-11: **the existing `regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py` has 24 ROWS, NOT 19.**  THE \"19-ROW\" FIGURE IS WRONG and it appears verbatim in this row and in its sibling. ***\n\nMeasured three ways: `git show e777ff0:regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py | grep -c '^row('` = **24**; HEAD = 24; worktree = 24; and `git log --follow` shows no change since `e777ff0`.  `ppcwitnessmaskoracle` calls the same instrument a **\"24-row detector\"**, so the ledger contradicted itself.\n\n**19 is a SCORE, lifted from different artifacts** -- `regress/artifacts/ppcrldiclzero/witness_committed.out` reads `SCORE 19/27`, and the superseded `pass2_20260910/opus_probe_BASE.raw` reads `SCORE 19/19`.  *A score transcribed into a row-count column is the frozen-number class in a new coat, and this one is load-bearing: it is the prescribed gate-wiring closure, so a future round wiring \"19 rows\" into a gate would pin a denominator five short and the check would pass while testing less than it claims.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11: CLAUSE (c) NOW GENUINELY DISCHARGED -- AN HONEST IDENTITY NULL MEDIUM, AND IT IS STRICTLY SHARPER THAN THE PRE-FIX LEG IT REPLACES. ***\n\n`killtable_nullmedium_20260911/` (sentinel `NULLMEDIUM_DONE`), built by agy 3.8 in `/tmp/gxrldimi_clone` against the frozen instrument:\n\n    NULL_MEDIUM (identity X(rldimi) body)   SCORE 13/27   -- FOURTEEN rows RED\n    RESTORED                                SCORE 27/27\n    Clearance: Part 1 (cmp vs SEC)=CLEAN    Part 2 (binary -nt source)=FRESH\n\nThe reds cover every substantive family -- `nowrap` x5, `wrap` x4, `pres` x3 -- **and both census rows (`census wrong`, `census zero`) also redden**, so they are not the pure bookkeeping I had predicted they might be.  *A prediction I wrote into the brief, refuted by the measurement, which is the right way round.*\n\n**THE COMPARISON IS THE FINDING, AND IT IS THE SECOND INSTANCE OF ONE PATTERN:**\n\n    ppcrldimimask   identity 13/27 (14 red)   pre-fix HEAD leg 17/27 (10 red)\n    ppcrlwimiwidth  identity 12/28 (16 red)   pre-fix HEAD leg 14/28 (14 red)\n\n**Both rounds, independently: the identity medium reddens MORE rows than the pre-fix medium the convention substitutes for it.**  That is what a sharper clause-(c) leg looks like, measured rather than argued -- and it is now carried to the flagship seat as question 7n-1, which asks whether *a strictly sharper form was available and cheap* converts an established house convention into a floor breach across the ~16 tables that use it.\n\n*The old discharge was `PRETABLE.out:69`, \"(c) null medium -> HEAD leg (whole-file pre-fix)\", with the PRETABLE itself predicting that leg identical to `M_ORIG` and the run confirming both at 17/27 -- two legs measuring one thing.  That sentence is superseded, not deleted: the leg's score was real; what was false was its NAME.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PASS 2 (codex GPT-6 Astra at max).  **Both algorithms VERIFIED; THE PROBE COVERS NEITHER FIX, AND THESE ARE TWO COMMITS NOT ONE.** ***\n\n*VERDICT: Both local algorithms check out; mode integration is unverified, and the probe covers neither fix.*\n\n**THE ARITHMETIC IS CONFIRMED, and by exhaustion rather than by argument:** independent in-memory models found **no mismatches across all 4,096 `rldimi` mask combinations and all 1,024 `rlwimi` mask pairs**, plus operand checks covering every word-rotate encoding in BOTH destination widths.  *Stated honestly as arithmetic checks, not compiled-C or emulator runs.*\n\n**`rldimi`: `(64 - sh) & 63` IS CORRECT.**  Book I's RTL is `m <- MASK(b, !n)` with `n` six bits, so `!n` is `63-SH` and the loop must stop once `m` has advanced past it.  `:4315` names that endpoint in prose.  *The pre-fix `63 - sh` read `sh` AFTER `while (sh-- != 0)` had left it at -1.*\n\n=== *** THE FINDING THAT CHANGES THE ROUND: NO DETECTOR COVERS EITHER FIX *** ===\n> *'There is no shared functioning detector here -- the permitted probe detects NEITHER repair.'*\n\n`ppc_rldicl_probe.py`'s two expected-value helpers exist but implement DISTINCT instruction formulas.  **So both fixes would ship with nothing that fails on the pre-fix build** -- vacuity floor clause (a) unmet, for both.  **Each commit owes instruction-specific observations that kill its own effect-nullifying mutant, including a SEPARATE WIDTH-SENSITIVE observation for `rlwimi`.**\n\n*** AND THEY ARE TWO COMMITS, NOT ONE.  The scope rule's join needs SAME SEMANTIC CLASS **AND ONE FIX DESIGN**; the class matches and the designs do not: *** `rldimi` preserves the shift amount and builds a correct inclusive circular mask; `rlwimi` narrows the rotation SOURCE and implements a duplicated high-word insertion.  *Even granting that the existing helpers satisfy 'no new oracle', the one-fix-design half fails.*\n\n=== THE 32-BIT MODE QUESTION: MY WORRY WAS HALF RIGHT, AND THE HALF THAT SURVIVES IS THE SHARPER ONE ===\nThe truncation is **sound as a representation argument, not an accident**: assignment to a 32-bit `MODE_uint_t` discards the upper bits by defined unsigned-conversion semantics, the shift itself happens at 64-bit width, and the loop then performs the low-word insertion.\n\n> *** BUT Book I S1.2 (`:342`): both execution modes affect ALL 64 BITS when an instruction sets a 64-bit register, and the register result is MODE-INDEPENDENT; `:1989` describes every GPR as 64 bits.  **So a 64-bit processor running in 32-bit MODE still owes the wrapping high-word write.** ***\n\n**The permitted files do not define `MODE_uint_t` or `reg`, or establish which implementation selects each compiled variant, so the seat declined to certify it** -- *'If a 64-bit processor's 32-bit execution mode reaches a variant that loses the architectural high word, that remains wrong.'*  **Filed rather than guessed.**\n\n**L4 CORRECTION APPLIED IN PLACE:** the one-line comment *'A wrapping MASK(MB+32, ME+32) includes the high word'* invited the reading that architectural 32-bit mode removes half the register.  Replaced with the seat's more accurate wording plus the open question and its Book I citations.  *This file's idiom is `MODE32` with `#else` (see `extsb`/`extsh`) and carries no `MODE64` conditional, so a future guard would be `#ifndef MODE32` -- recorded so the next round does not invent the opposite convention.*\n\n**STATUS: arithmetic green, DETECTOR OWED for both, and the round splits in two.**  Landing is held by owner ruling until the full panel reports.",
